window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["gurugram-2026-ma"] = {"id": "gurugram-2026-ma", "title": "Gurugram 2026 M.A.", "fullTitle": "Gurugram University 2026, M.A. Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper, official key)", "sub": "70 real questions · official answer key · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Korsakoff syndrome results mostly from which kind of amnesia ?", "options": {"a": "Transient global amnesia", "b": "Defensive amnesia", "c": "Drug and alcohol amnesia", "d": "Primary degenerative amnesia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: Korsakoff syndrome is an alcohol-related amnesia, so of the types listed it belongs under drug and alcohol amnesia. Sergei Korsakoff (1887), a Russian psychiatrist, described it in people with long-term heavy drinking. The direct cause is a lack of thiamine (vitamin B1). Heavy drinkers eat poorly and absorb thiamine badly, and brain cells that depend on it die. The damage falls on the mammillary bodies and the mediodorsal (dorsomedial) thalamus, parts of the circuit that lays down new memories. It often follows Wernicke's encephalopathy, described by Carl Wernicke (1881): sudden confusion, an unsteady gait (ataxia) and jerky eye movements (nystagmus). Together they form the Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. The memory picture has three parts. Dense anterograde amnesia means new facts cannot be stored. Retrograde amnesia is patchy and worse for recent years than for distant ones. Confabulation means the person sincerely fills memory gaps with invented stories. Attention, old skills and general intelligence are fairly well kept. Example: Arjun, 52, has drunk heavily for twenty years. He cannot recall his doctor's name ten minutes after meeting her, yet calmly describes a trip to the market that morning that never happened. Early thiamine injections can stop the Wernicke stage from becoming permanent Korsakoff amnesia. Option c is right because only drug and alcohol amnesia names the cause of Korsakoff syndrome. The stem is loosely worded (the syndrome does not result from an amnesia; it is one), but the intended link is alcohol. Exam trap: examiners set confabulation beside lying or malingering. Confabulation is honest gap filling, not deliberate faking. A second trap is calling Korsakoff a degenerative dementia; it is a nutritional brain injury that mainly hits memory.", "topic": "Korsakoff syndrome and alcohol"}, {"q": "In which year was tip of the tongue phenomenon given by Brown and McNeill ?", "options": {"a": "1966", "b": "1976", "c": "1986", "d": "1956"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Roger Brown and David McNeill reported their study of the tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon in 1966. The tip-of-the-tongue (TOT) state is the feeling that a word is stored in memory and about to come, yet it will not come out. Roger Brown and David McNeill (1966) created this state in the laboratory. They read Harvard students the dictionary definitions of rare words, such as \"a navigational instrument used to measure angles of the sun and stars\" (sextant). Whenever a student felt the word was on the tip of the tongue, they wrote down whatever they could. Students often knew the first letter, the number of syllables and where the stress fell. They also offered similar-sounding words (secant, sextet) or similar-meaning ones (astrolabe, compass). So the word was partly accessible: some of its sound and meaning features were available, but the full form could not be retrieved. The study showed that much forgetting is a failure of retrieval, not a loss of storage. It also links to metamemory, knowledge about one's own memory, named by John Flavell (1971), because people in a TOT state have a strong feeling of knowing. Example: Meera sees a photo of a film actor, knows his name starts with R and has three syllables, but recalls it only on the bus home. Option a is right because the paper, \"The tip of the tongue phenomenon\", appeared in the Journal of Verbal Learning and Verbal Behavior in 1966. Exam trap: the year sits close to other famous memory dates. Roger Brown's flashbulb memory paper with James Kulik is from 1977, and George Miller's \"magical number seven\" paper is from 1956, the option examiners use to catch students who guess at a well-known memory year.", "topic": "Tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon"}, {"q": "The concept of episodic memory was given by :", "options": {"a": "Quillian", "b": "Tulving", "c": "Pavlov", "d": "Nickerson"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Endel Tulving (1972), an Estonian-Canadian psychologist, introduced the concept of episodic memory. In a chapter titled \"Episodic and semantic memory\", he split long-term declarative memory into two systems. Episodic memory stores personal events tied to a time and place: what happened, where and when. Semantic memory stores general facts and meanings, with no record of when they were learned. Tulving (1985) later added that episodic recall carries autonoetic consciousness, a sense of mentally travelling back in time and re-living the moment, while semantic knowing is noetic, simply knowing. Procedural memory, a third long-term system, holds skills such as riding a bicycle. Evidence for the split comes from brain damage. Tulving's patient K.C., injured in a motorcycle accident in 1981, kept his general knowledge and language but could not recall a single personal episode from his life. Example: Kabir remembers that on his first day of college he spilled tea on his shirt in the canteen; that is episodic memory. Kabir knows that Delhi is the capital of India; that is semantic memory. Option b is right because the terms episodic and semantic memory were coined and defined by Tulving. Exam trap: semantic memory is linked to Ross Quillian, whose hierarchical network model (1968, tested with Allan Collins in 1969) described how facts are stored, so students who remember \"Quillian\" and \"memory\" together may pick him. Quillian modelled semantic knowledge; he did not propose the episodic system. Another trap is crediting Tulving with levels of processing, which belongs to Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972); Tulving only tested it later with Craik (1975).", "topic": "Episodic memory and Tulving"}, {"q": "Memory interference resulting from activities subsequent to the events you are trying to remember is called :", "options": {"a": "Retroactive interference", "b": "Proactive interference", "c": "Engrams", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: retroactive interference is forgetting caused by new learning that comes after the material you are trying to remember. Interference theory holds that much forgetting happens because memories compete, not because traces simply fade. John McGeoch (1932) argued that interference, not disuse, explains forgetting, and that it is strongest when old and new material are similar. Direction matters. In retroactive interference, new learning works backwards (\"retro\") and disrupts recall of old learning. In proactive interference, old learning works forwards (\"pro\") and disrupts recall of new learning. A classic study is John Jenkins and Karl Dallenbach (1924). Students learned nonsense syllables and were tested after one to eight hours spent either asleep or awake. Recall was better after sleep, because fewer new experiences came in to interfere. The standard laboratory design is simple. The experimental group learns list A, then list B, then is tested on A. The control group learns A, rests, then is tested on A. Poorer recall of A in the experimental group shows retroactive interference. Example: Riya changes her phone number. A month later she tries to give her old number to the bank and keeps saying the new one; the new number has interfered with the old. Option a is right because the stem describes interference from activities subsequent to (after) the events being remembered, which is the definition of retroactive interference. Exam trap: students mix up the two directions. Ask which memory is lost. If the old memory is lost, the interference is retroactive; if the new memory is lost, it is proactive. Riya forgetting her new number because the old one keeps intruding would be proactive interference.", "topic": "Retroactive and proactive interference"}, {"q": "Sandhya, a child of 6 years, was bit by rabbits and due to that was taken to a doctor's office to get injection. Sandhya after that became afraid of rabbits and doctors. On Christmas eve, she went to a park where she saw Santa Claus and started panicking and crying. What kind of learning process best describes her fear of Santa Claus ?", "options": {"a": "Phobia", "b": "Stimulus Discrimination", "c": "Behaviour Modification", "d": "Stimulus Generalization"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Sandhya's fear of Santa Claus is stimulus generalisation, the spread of a conditioned response to new stimuli that resemble the original conditioned stimulus. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927), a neutral stimulus paired with an unconditioned stimulus comes to trigger a conditioned response on its own. Pavlov found that a dog conditioned to one tone also salivated, less strongly, to similar tones. The more a new stimulus resembles the original, the stronger the response; this is the generalisation gradient. John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) showed the same for fear with Little Albert. An 11-month-old boy was shown a white rat while a steel bar was struck loudly behind him. After a few pairings he cried at the rat alone. He then also showed distress at a rabbit, a dog, a fur coat, cotton wool and a Santa Claus mask with a white beard. In Sandhya's case, the bite and the injection were painful unconditioned stimuli; rabbits and doctors became conditioned stimuli for fear. Santa Claus shares features with both: white fur trim, a fluffy white beard and an adult stranger who comes close. Her fear spread to him without any new painful event. Option d is right because the question asks for the learning process that explains fear of a new stimulus that was never paired with pain but resembles feared ones. Exam trap: \"Phobia\" is tempting because the fear seems irrational, but a phobia is a clinical outcome or label, not a learning process. Generalisation is the process; a phobia may be the result. Its opposite, discrimination, is learning to respond to one stimulus but not to similar ones.", "topic": "Stimulus generalisation in fear"}, {"q": "Who among the following was the pioneering for positive reinforcement principle studies ?", "options": {"a": "E. L. Thorndike", "b": "Ivan Pavlov", "c": "B. F. Skinner", "d": "H. M. Jenkins"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: B. F. Skinner (1938) pioneered the systematic study of positive reinforcement in operant conditioning. Operant conditioning is learning in which a behaviour becomes more or less frequent because of its consequences. Burrhus Frederic Skinner defined reinforcement by its effect: any consequence that makes a response more likely is a reinforcer. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant after the response (food, praise, money). Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a loud noise stops when a lever is pressed). Both increase behaviour; punishment decreases it. Skinner built the operant chamber, later called the Skinner box. A hungry rat pressed a lever, a food pellet dropped, and a cumulative recorder drew its rate of responding over time. In The Behavior of Organisms (1938) and Schedules of Reinforcement with Charles Ferster (1957), he showed how the timing of reinforcers shapes behaviour. He applied these ideas to teaching machines, token economies and shaping, which builds new behaviour by reinforcing successive approximations. Example: a teacher gives Arjun a star each time he hands in homework on time, and his on-time submissions rise; that is positive reinforcement. Option c is right because Skinner built the operant framework, separated positive from negative reinforcement and made reinforcement the centre of a whole research programme. Exam trap: Edward Thorndike came first, with his puzzle-box cats (1898) and the law of effect (1911), so he is often called the father of instrumental learning. But Thorndike spoke of \"satisfying\" consequences and did not frame positive reinforcement as Skinner did. Key note: some books call Thorndike the pioneer because the law of effect is the root idea behind reinforcement. The stem asks about positive reinforcement principle studies, which are Skinner's, so the official key stands.", "topic": "Skinner and positive reinforcement"}, {"q": "In a signal detection task, an observer's sensitivity remains constant, but they adopt a highly conservative response criterion. What is the expected outcome ?", "options": {"a": "Hits and False Alarms will both increase", "b": "Hits and False Alarms will both decrease", "c": "Hits will increase while False Alarms decrease", "d": "Hits will decrease while False Alarms increase"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: with a highly conservative criterion and unchanged sensitivity, the observer says \"yes\" less often, so both hits and false alarms fall. Signal detection theory (SDT), brought into psychology by David Green and John Swets (1966), separates two things that the old threshold idea mixed together. Sensitivity (d prime) is how well the observer can tell signal from noise; it depends on signal strength and the observer's senses. The response criterion (beta or c) is how much evidence the observer demands before saying \"yes, a signal was there\"; it depends on expectations, motives and payoffs. Each trial has four possible outcomes: hit (signal present, \"yes\"), miss (present, \"no\"), false alarm (absent, \"yes\") and correct rejection (absent, \"no\"). A conservative observer sets the bar high. Saying \"yes\" only when quite sure gives fewer false alarms but also fewer hits, and so more misses and more correct rejections. A liberal observer does the reverse: more hits and more false alarms. Moving the criterion slides the observer along one receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve; only a change in sensitivity moves them onto a different curve. Example: Dr Meera reads chest X-rays for tuberculosis. After a complaint about unnecessary treatment, she decides to report TB only when the shadow is very clear. Her eyes have not changed, but she now misses some real cases (fewer hits) and wrongly flags fewer healthy patients (fewer false alarms). Option b is right because a pure criterion shift moves hits and false alarms in the same direction. Exam trap: option c (more hits, fewer false alarms) is what better sensitivity looks like, not a criterion change. Hits and false alarms move in opposite directions only when d prime itself changes.", "topic": "Signal detection response criterion"}, {"q": "An individual fails to solve a complex puzzle because they keep applying a rule that worked on previous, simpler problems, even though a shorter alternative is available. This illustrates :", "options": {"a": "Functional fixedness", "b": "Negative transfer due to mental set", "c": "Retroactive interference", "d": "Proactive confirmation bias"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: repeating a rule that worked on earlier, simpler problems when a shorter route is available is negative transfer caused by a mental set. A mental set (German Einstellung) is a readiness to attack a problem in the way that worked before. Transfer of training is the effect of earlier learning on new learning: positive when it helps, negative when it gets in the way. A mental set produces negative transfer when the old method no longer suits the new problem. The classic study is the water-jar experiment of Abraham Luchins (1942). Participants used three jars of different sizes (A, B and C) to measure out an exact amount of water. A run of early problems could be solved only by the long formula B minus A minus 2C. Later problems could also be solved by a much simpler formula, A minus C or A plus C. Most people who had practised the long formula kept using it, and many failed a problem that could be solved only by the short one. A control group that skipped the practice problems found the short solutions easily. Example: Kabir solves ten worksheet problems by long division. Problem eleven only needs halving, but he does long division again and runs out of time. Option b is right because the stem describes a previously successful rule blocking a simpler solution, which is exactly Luchins's Einstellung effect. Exam trap: functional fixedness is mental set's close cousin. Both are blocks to problem solving that come from past experience, but functional fixedness concerns an object's usual use, while mental set concerns a habitual method or rule. The stem talks about a rule, not an object, so the answer is mental set.", "topic": "Mental set and negative transfer"}, {"q": "Based on the Schachter-Singer Two-Factor Theory of emotion, if an individual is covertly injected with epinephrine and placed in a room with an angry confederate, how will they interpret their physiological arousal ?", "options": {"a": "They will feel calm due to cognitive appraisal", "b": "They will attribute their arousal to the injection", "c": "They will experience and report feeling angry", "d": "They will feel a pure, undifferentiated sense of euphoria"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the Schachter-Singer theory, an uninformed person aroused by epinephrine and placed with an angry companion labels the arousal as anger. Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962) proposed the two-factor theory. Emotion needs two ingredients: physiological arousal and a cognitive label for that arousal. Arousal on its own is general, much the same for fear, anger or joy, so we decide which emotion we feel by searching the situation for a reason. In their experiment, male students were told they were testing a vitamin called \"Suproxin\". Most were actually injected with epinephrine (adrenaline), which raises heart rate and causes trembling and flushing. Some were correctly told what side effects to expect (informed); others were told nothing (uninformed). A placebo group got a saline injection. Each then waited with a confederate who acted either euphoric or angry, the angry one complaining loudly about an insulting questionnaire. Uninformed participants had arousal with no explanation, so they took on the confederate's mood and acted more euphoric or more angry. Informed participants blamed the injection and were little affected. In the anger condition the effect showed more clearly in behaviour than in self-ratings, and later replications (Gary Marshall and Philip Zimbardo, 1979) were mixed, but the theory's prediction is clear. Example: Riya drinks three strong coffees without realising it and sits beside a furious colleague; she finds herself feeling irritated too. Option c is right because the injection was covert, so the person has no explanation for the arousal and uses the angry confederate as the cue. Exam trap: option b is what the informed group did; they attributed their arousal to the injection. Always check whether the injection was covert or disclosed.", "topic": "Schachter-Singer two-factor theory"}, {"q": "Which component of Robert Sternberg's Triarchic Theory of Intelligence is primarily responsible for the executive processes involved in planning, monitoring and evaluating problem-solving strategies ?", "options": {"a": "Performance components", "b": "Knowledge-acquisition components", "c": "Contextual adaptations", "d": "Metacomponents"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: metacomponents are the executive processes in Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory (1985) that plan, monitor and evaluate problem solving. In Beyond IQ, Sternberg argued that intelligence has three parts, or subtheories. The componential (analytical) subtheory describes the mental machinery inside the head. The experiential (creative) subtheory covers how we handle novel tasks and how practice makes skills automatic. The contextual (practical) subtheory covers how we adapt to, shape or select our environment. The componential part contains three kinds of components. Metacomponents are higher-order processes: recognising what the problem is, choosing a strategy, allocating time, monitoring progress and judging the result. Performance components carry out the chosen strategy, for example encoding the terms of a problem, inferring relations and applying them. Knowledge-acquisition components learn new information by selecting what is relevant, combining it and comparing it with what is already known. Sternberg likened metacomponents to a manager who directs the workers (performance components) and the trainers (knowledge-acquisition components). Example: before an exam, Meera decides to answer the short questions first, keeps an eye on the clock and switches strategy when one question takes too long; those are metacomponents at work. Writing the actual answers uses performance components. Option d is right because planning, monitoring and evaluating are exactly what Sternberg assigned to metacomponents. Exam trap: \"executive\" points to metacomponents, not to performance components, even though performance components seem to \"perform\" the task. A second trap is mixing levels: contextual adaptation belongs to the practical subtheory and is about fitting the outside world, not internal executive control. Metacomponents overlap with metacognition, the knowledge and control of one's own thinking described by John Flavell (1979).", "topic": "Sternberg's triarchic metacomponents"}, {"q": "According to the investment theory of creativity by Sternberg and Lubart, creative individuals are best described as those who ?", "options": {"a": "Buy high and sell low by pursuing ideas that are traditional, then continuing once they are accepted", "b": "Focus exclusively on high-risk, low-payoff artistic ventures", "c": "Buy low and sell high by pursuing ideas that are novel but unpopular, then moving on once they are accepted", "d": "Totally avoid structured intellectual investments"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the investment theory of creativity, creative people \"buy low and sell high\" in the world of ideas. Robert Sternberg and Todd Lubart (1991), and later their book Defying the Crowd (1995), borrowed a stock-market metaphor. A smart investor buys shares when they are cheap and unpopular and sells them once their value has risen. A creative person does the same with ideas. They take up an idea that is new and out of favour, one that others dismiss or even mock (buying low). They develop it and persuade others of its worth. Once the idea is accepted and popular, they move on to the next unpopular idea (selling high). Creativity, on this view, is partly a decision to defy the crowd. The theory says creativity needs six resources working together: intellectual abilities (seeing problems in new ways, judging which ideas are worth pursuing and selling them to others), knowledge, thinking styles (a liking for thinking in new ways), personality (willingness to take sensible risks, tolerance of ambiguity, persistence), intrinsic motivation and a supportive environment. Example: Kabir, a psychology student, studies mental health apps in 2015 while his professors call them a fad. By 2022 the field is crowded and respected, so he shifts to a new, unfashionable question. Option c is right because it matches both halves of the metaphor: novel but unpopular ideas, then moving on once they gain acceptance. Exam trap: option a reverses the metaphor. Buying high, chasing ideas that are already popular, is conformity, not creativity. Also do not confuse this with Raymond Cattell's (1971) investment theory of intelligence, in which fluid intelligence is \"invested\" in learning and becomes crystallised intelligence.", "topic": "Investment theory of creativity"}, {"q": "In Cattell's trait theory of personality, source traits represent :", "options": {"a": "Underlying structural variables that form the core dimensions of personality", "b": "Unconscious motives that cannot be measured via factor analysis", "c": "Visible behavioral clusters that are highly unstable across time", "d": "Purely situational adaptations unique to an individual"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: in Raymond Cattell's trait theory, source traits are the underlying structures that form the core dimensions of personality. Raymond Cattell (1946, 1950) built his theory from data rather than clinical hunches. He started from the lexical work of Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936), who listed about 18,000 English words that describe people. Cattell reduced this list and gathered three kinds of data: L-data (life records and ratings by others), Q-data (questionnaires) and T-data (objective tests). He then used factor analysis, a statistical method that finds which measures vary together. Surface traits are clusters of observable behaviours that seem to go together, such as being talkative, smiling and joining groups. They are easy to see but less stable, and each may arise from several deeper causes. Source traits are the underlying factors that produce those surface clusters. They are more stable and form the building blocks of personality. Cattell identified 16 source traits and measured them with the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949), whose factors include A (warmth), C (emotional stability) and E (dominance). He also divided source traits into constitutional ones, rooted in heredity, and environmental-mould ones, shaped by experience. Example: Arjun chats with strangers, cracks jokes and organises outings; these surface traits may all spring from one source trait, warmth. Option a is right because it describes source traits as underlying structural variables, the core dimensions revealed by factor analysis. Exam trap: examiners describe surface traits (visible behaviour clusters) and label them source traits. Remember: the surface is what you see; the source is where it comes from. And source traits are found through factor analysis, not beyond its reach.", "topic": "Cattell's source traits"}, {"q": "According to the Whorfian hypothesis (Linguistic Relativity), how does language influence human cognition ?", "options": {"a": "Language determines the structural anatomy of the visual cortex", "b": "The specific structure of a language shapes or constraints the ways in which its speakers think and perceive the world", "c": "Thought operates entirely independently of linguistic constraints in all cultures", "d": "Language serves merely as a passive storage unit for innate pre-formed concepts"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Whorfian hypothesis holds that the structure of a person's language shapes or constrains how they think about and perceive the world. It is named after Benjamin Lee Whorf (1940), a fire-insurance inspector and amateur linguist, and his teacher Edward Sapir (1929), so it is also called the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis. Whorf noticed that workers smoked carelessly around \"empty\" petrol drums because the word \"empty\" suggested no danger, though the drums held explosive vapour. He also argued that the Hopi language treats time differently from English, so Hopi speakers must think about time differently. There are two versions. The strong version, linguistic determinism, says language determines thought, so we cannot think what our language cannot express. The weak version, linguistic relativity, says language influences thought, making some distinctions easier or faster. Evidence has gone against the strong version. Eleanor Rosch (publishing as Eleanor Heider, 1972) found that the Dani of New Guinea, who have only two basic colour terms, still remembered focal colours better than non-focal ones, just like English speakers. The weak version has support: Russian speakers, who have separate basic words for light and dark blue, tell those shades apart faster (Jonathan Winawer and colleagues, 2007). Example: Meera speaks Hindi, which separates chacha (father's younger brother) from mama (mother's brother), so she may notice which side of the family an uncle belongs to more readily than an English speaker who just says \"uncle\". Option b is right because it states the core claim: language structure shapes thought. Exam trap: linguistic relativity belongs to Whorf, not to Noam Chomsky (1957; universal grammar) or Jean Piaget (1923; thought comes before language). It is also a claim about thinking, never about brain anatomy.", "topic": "Linguistic relativity (Whorf)"}, {"q": "A subject is asked to name the ink color of the word 'BLUE' printed in red ink. The delay in response time observed is known as the Stroop effect which primarily demonstrates the automaticity of :", "options": {"a": "Color naming over reading", "b": "Reading text over color naming", "c": "Spatial mapping over lexical access", "d": "Auditory monitoring over speech"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Stroop effect shows that reading is automatic and takes priority over colour naming. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people to name the ink colour of colour words printed in a conflicting ink, such as RED printed in green. Naming the inks took much longer than naming plain colour patches, about 74 per cent longer in his study, and errors rose. Yet reading the words aloud while ignoring the ink was hardly slowed at all. The explanation lies in automatic versus controlled processing (Walter Schneider and Richard Shiffrin, 1977). Automatic processes are fast, effortless, need little attention and start on their own once triggered. Controlled processes are slow, effortful and need attention. For a literate adult, reading a familiar word is automatic: the meaning of BLUE arrives whether you want it or not. Naming an ink colour is less practised and needs control. So the automatic word response competes with the colour response, and resolving the conflict costs time. This is why children who are only beginning to read show a smaller effect, and why the task is now widely used to measure inhibition and executive function. Example: Kabir sees the word BLUE printed in red ink and must say \"red\". He starts to say \"blue\", stops himself, then says \"red\"; that delay is the Stroop effect. Option b is right because the interference runs from the stronger, automatic process (reading) to the weaker, controlled one (colour naming). Exam trap: option a reverses the direction. Because the task asks people to name colours, students assume colour naming must be the automatic process. In fact colour naming is the slowed, effortful task, and reading is what intrudes on it.", "topic": "Stroop effect and automaticity"}, {"q": "Under a Variable Ratio schedule of reinforcement, how does the pattern of operant responding typically appear ?", "options": {"a": "Low rate with a long post-reinforcement pause", "b": "Scalloped pattern with rapid responding only at the end of an interval", "c": "Extremely low, erratic bursts of responding", "d": "High and steady rate of responding with little or no post-reinforcement pause"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: a variable ratio (VR) schedule produces a high, steady rate of responding with little or no pause after each reinforcer. Schedules of reinforcement were mapped by Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957). In continuous reinforcement every response is rewarded; in partial (intermittent) reinforcement only some are, and there are four basic schedules. Ratio schedules reward a number of responses; interval schedules reward the first response after some time has passed. Fixed means the number or time stays the same; variable means it changes around an average. On a VR 10 schedule, a reward comes on average after every 10 responses, but sometimes after 2 and sometimes after 20. Because the very next response could be the winning one, there is no reason to pause, and because more responses bring more rewards, the rate is high. VR also gives the greatest resistance to extinction, since a long run without reward looks like an ordinary unlucky streak. Slot machines and lottery tickets work this way, which helps explain gambling. A fixed ratio (FR) schedule also gives a high rate, but with a pause after each reward (\"break and run\"). A fixed interval (FI) schedule gives a scalloped pattern: little responding just after a reward, speeding up as the interval ends. A variable interval (VI) schedule gives a moderate, steady rate. Example: Arjun keeps opening loot boxes in a mobile game because a rare prize turns up after an unpredictable number of boxes. Option d is right because the high, steady, pause-free pattern is the signature of VR. Exam trap: the post-reinforcement pause belongs to fixed schedules (FR and FI), not variable ones, and the scallop belongs to FI only.", "topic": "Variable ratio schedule"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the core assertion of the James-Lange theory of emotion ?", "options": {"a": "Emotional experience and physiological arousal occur simultaneously via thalamic routing", "b": "Cognitive appraisal of a situation precedes both physiological arousal and emotional feeling", "c": "Emotional experience is our conscious awareness of our distinct physiological and behavioral reactions to a stimulus", "d": "Emotion is determined solely by the localized activation of the prefrontal cortex"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the James-Lange theory says that emotional experience is our awareness of the body's own physiological and behavioural reactions to a stimulus. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) reached the idea independently. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid, and then run and tremble. James reversed the order: we see the bear, the body reacts (the heart races, we run), and the feeling of fear is our perception of those bodily changes. In his famous phrase, we \"feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble\". Each emotion should therefore have its own distinct pattern of bodily change. Evidence for the theory is partial. Paul Ekman and colleagues (1983) found that posing facial expressions produced small emotion-specific changes in heart rate and skin temperature. The facial feedback hypothesis, tested by Fritz Strack and colleagues (1988) with pens held in the mouth, suggests body signals can colour feelings. Walter Cannon (1927) criticised the theory: visceral changes are slow, similar across emotions, and cutting the nerves from the viscera in animals did not abolish emotional behaviour. Example: Riya hears a loud crash at night; her heart pounds and her hands shake, and only then does she notice that she feels frightened. Option c is right because it puts the bodily reaction first and defines the feeling as awareness of that reaction. Exam trap: option a describes the Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon, 1927; Philip Bard, 1928), in which the thalamus sends signals to the cortex and to the body at the same time, so feeling and arousal are simultaneous. Students who remember only \"arousal and emotion\" mix up the two theories.", "topic": "James-Lange theory of emotion"}, {"q": "According to Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs, which need must be substantially satisfied before an individual becomes motivated by self-actualization needs ?", "options": {"a": "Physiological needs only", "b": "Safety needs only", "c": "Esteem needs", "d": "Belongingness needs only"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Abraham Maslow's hierarchy, esteem needs sit just below self-actualisation, so they must be substantially satisfied first. Abraham Maslow (1943; expanded in Motivation and Personality, 1954) arranged human needs in a hierarchy, usually drawn as a pyramid. From the bottom up come physiological needs (food, water, sleep), safety needs (security, stability, freedom from fear), belongingness and love needs (friendship, family, intimacy), esteem needs (self-respect, achievement, recognition from others) and, at the top, self-actualisation (becoming everything one is capable of becoming). The first four are deficiency needs (D-needs): when unmet they create tension, and once met they stop motivating. Self-actualisation is a growth need (B-need, for being) that keeps motivating as it is fed. Maslow said a higher need emerges when lower ones are relatively, not completely, satisfied; his rough figures for an average person were about 85 per cent of physiological needs met but only about 50 per cent of love needs. Later reviews, such as Mahmoud Wahba and Lawrence Bridwell (1976), found little support for a strict order, and Maslow later added self-transcendence above self-actualisation. Example: Kabir has a stable job, a safe home and close friends. Once he also feels competent and respected at work, he starts painting seriously simply because it fulfils him. Option c is right because esteem is the level immediately beneath self-actualisation, and reaching it implies the lower levels are reasonably met as well. Exam trap: the word \"only\" in options a, b and d. Each of those levels must be met, but none of them alone is enough; the question wants the need directly below the top. Students also swap belongingness and esteem; belongingness comes first.", "topic": "Maslow's hierarchy of needs"}, {"q": "Which basic component of language refers to the smallest distinctive sound units that can distinguish one word from another ?", "options": {"a": "Morphemes", "b": "Phonemes", "c": "Syntax", "d": "Semantics"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: phonemes are the smallest distinctive sound units in a language, the units that can change one word into another. Linguists describe language at several levels: phonology deals with sounds, morphology with word parts, syntax with sentence structure, semantics with meaning and pragmatics with use in social settings. A phoneme carries no meaning by itself, but swapping one changes the word: /p/ and /b/ separate \"pat\" from \"bat\", and /k/ and /g/ separate \"cot\" from \"got\". English has about 40 to 44 phonemes, and the world's languages range from about a dozen to over a hundred. Phonemes combine into morphemes, the smallest units that carry meaning. \"Cats\" has two morphemes: the free morpheme \"cat\" and the bound morpheme \"-s\", meaning plural. Charles Hockett (1960) called this two-layer system duality of patterning: meaningless sounds build meaningful units. Which sounds count as separate phonemes depends on the language. In Hindi, unaspirated /k/ and aspirated /kh/ are different phonemes, so kal (yesterday or tomorrow) and khal (skin) are different words; in English the same difference never changes meaning. Infants can hear almost all such contrasts, but by about 10 to 12 months they lose sensitivity to contrasts their home language does not use (Janet Werker and Richard Tees, 1984). Example: when Riya teaches Hindi to her English-speaking friend, he hears kal and khal as the same word until he learns the contrast. Option b is right because the stem asks for the smallest distinctive sound unit, which is the definition of a phoneme. Exam trap: morpheme versus phoneme. The smallest unit of meaning is the morpheme; the smallest unit of sound that distinguishes words is the phoneme.", "topic": "Phonemes and morphemes"}, {"q": "Concepts that are fussy around the edges have no fixed or readily specified set of defining features are called :", "options": {"a": "Natural concepts", "b": "Logical concepts", "c": "Schemas", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: concepts that are fuzzy around the edges, with no fixed or readily specified set of defining features, are natural concepts. A concept is a mental category that groups objects, events or ideas that share properties. Psychologists divide concepts into two kinds. Logical (artificial) concepts are defined by a clear rule: a triangle is any closed figure with three straight sides, and anything that meets the rule belongs, with no in-between cases. Natural concepts are the categories we build in everyday life, such as 'bird', 'game' or 'furniture'. They have no neat checklist. Their members share a family resemblance, a phrase from the philosopher Ludwig Wittgenstein (1953), and some members are better examples than others. Eleanor Rosch (1973) showed that we judge membership by similarity to a prototype, the most typical member: people verify 'a robin is a bird' faster than 'a penguin is a bird'. That graded, blurry boundary is what 'fuzzy around the edges' means. For example, Meera calls a sparrow a bird at once, but hesitates over an ostrich or a bat, because her concept 'bird' works by resemblance to a typical bird, not by a strict definition. Option a is right because the stem is the standard textbook definition of natural concepts, which are contrasted with logical concepts that can be clearly defined by a set of rules. Exam trap: the printed word 'fussy' is a misprint for 'fuzzy', so do not let it throw you. Also, do not confuse natural concepts with schemas: a schema is a broad knowledge framework about a situation, not a category with blurry edges. Jerome Seymour Bruner, Jacqueline Goodnow and George Austin (1956) studied logical concepts through hypothesis testing; Rosch studied natural ones.", "topic": "Natural versus logical concepts"}, {"q": "Events that are perceived dimly with us beings vaguely aware of them are at the :", "options": {"a": "Focus", "b": "Margin", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: events that we perceive dimly, being only vaguely aware of them, lie at the margin of attention (the margin of consciousness). Classical psychology describes the field of consciousness as having two zones. The focus is the small central part we attend to fully: its contents are clear, vivid and detailed. The margin, also called the fringe, surrounds the focus: its contents are present in awareness but faint and unclear, and they can move into the focus the moment attention shifts. Wilhelm Wundt (1896) described this as the difference between the Blickpunkt, the focal point where a content is clearly grasped (apperception), and the wider Blickfeld, the field of consciousness where contents are merely perceived. William James (1890) spoke of the 'fringe' of consciousness, a halo of dim feelings and relations around the clear centre of the stream of thought. Edward Bradford Titchener (1908) described attention as levels of clearness, with the focus at the top and the margin below. For example, while Arjun reads his textbook, the words are in focus; the hum of the fan, the feel of the chair and the chatter from the next room sit at the margin. He is vaguely aware of them, and if someone in that chatter says his name, it jumps into focus at once. Option b is right because 'perceived dimly' and 'vaguely aware' are the defining features of the margin, while the focus is the opposite: clear and vivid. Exam trap: examiners offer 'Both (A) and (B)' because focus and margin together make up the field of attention. But the stem asks only about the dim zone, and one event cannot be clear and dim at the same moment. Also, do not confuse the margin with the unconscious: marginal events are still within awareness, only faintly.", "topic": "Focus and margin of attention"}, {"q": "Tendency to assume that more closely an item resembles typical examples of a concept, it is more likely that it belong to the concept is :", "options": {"a": "Availability heuristc", "b": "Adjustment heuristics", "c": "Achoring heuristic", "d": "Respresentativeness heuristics"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the tendency to judge that the more closely an item resembles typical examples of a category, the more likely it belongs to it, is the representativeness heuristic. A heuristic is a mental shortcut, a rule of thumb that gives quick, usually adequate answers without careful calculation. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1972, 1974) described three classic judgement heuristics. With representativeness we ask, 'How much does this look like my picture of a typical member?' and treat that resemblance as probability. It often works, but it makes people neglect base rates, the real frequency of each category. In their engineer-lawyer study (1973), people judged a man described as cautious and fond of puzzles to be an engineer, whether engineers made up 30 or 70 of the 100 people in the sample. In the Linda problem (Tversky and Kahneman, 1983), most people rated 'feminist bank teller' as likelier than 'bank teller', the conjunction fallacy, because Linda resembled a feminist. For example, Kabir meets a quiet man with glasses who loves books and guesses he is a librarian, not a salesman, though salesmen vastly outnumber librarians. Kabir judged by resemblance, not by numbers. Option d is right because the stem describes judging category membership by similarity to typical examples, which is the definition of representativeness. Exam trap: the availability heuristic sounds close but rests on ease of recall: an event seems common because examples come to mind easily, as flying feels dangerous after news of a crash. Representativeness is about resemblance; availability is about memory. Also note that anchoring and adjustment are not two separate heuristics: Tversky and Kahneman (1974) named one heuristic, anchoring and adjustment, so options b and c point to the same idea. The paper's misspellings ('heuristc', 'Achoring', 'Respresentativeness') do not change the answer.", "topic": "Representativeness heuristic"}, {"q": "A study uses a 2 × 2 × 3 mixed factorial design. The first factor is within-subjects, the second is between-subjects, and the third is between-subjects. How many distinct F-statistics must be computed to test all main effects and interactions ?", "options": {"a": "3", "b": "4", "c": "6", "d": "7"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a three-factor design needs 7 F-statistics, one for each main effect and each interaction, whatever the mix of within-subjects and between-subjects factors. In analysis of variance (ANOVA), developed by Ronald Aylmer Fisher (1925), every effect is tested with an F-ratio: the variance that effect explains divided by a suitable error variance. The label '2 × 2 × 3' tells you two things. It has three numerals, so there are three factors (call them A, B and C), and multiplying them gives 12 cells. The number of testable effects depends only on the number of factors. Count them: three main effects (A, B and C); three two-way interactions (A × B, A × C and B × C); and one three-way interaction (A × B × C). That is 3 + 3 + 1 = 7. A quick rule is 2 to the power k, minus 1, where k is the number of factors: 2 cubed is 8, and 8 minus 1 is 7. For example, Riya studies recall with word type (concrete or abstract) as a within-subjects factor, gender as a between-subjects factor, and three age groups as another between-subjects factor. She will report seven F-tests. Option d is right because three factors always yield seven effects to test. What 'mixed' changes is not the number of F-tests but their error terms: effects that involve the within-subjects factor (A, A × B, A × C, A × B × C) are tested against a within-subjects error term, while B, C and B × C are tested against the between-subjects error. Exam trap: do not count levels or cells. Adding the levels (2 + 2 + 3) also gives 7 here only by coincidence, and multiplying gives 12 cells, not 12 tests.", "topic": "Effects in factorial ANOVA"}, {"q": "Under what condition is it imperative to use a non-parametric alternative like the Kruskal-Wallis test instead of a one-way ANOVA ?", "options": {"a": "When the dependent variable is ordinal and the assumption of normality is severely violated", "b": "When the dependent variable is measured on a continuous interval scale", "c": "When the sample sizes are large and variances are equal", "d": "When there are exactly two independent groups"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: a non-parametric test such as the Kruskal-Wallis test is called for when the dependent variable is ordinal and the normality assumption is severely violated. One-way ANOVA, developed by Ronald Aylmer Fisher (1925), compares the means of three or more independent groups. It is a parametric test: it assumes interval or ratio data, roughly normal scores within each group, and similar variances across groups (homogeneity of variance). When these assumptions fail, especially with ranks, ratings or badly skewed scores in small samples, the F-test can mislead. The Kruskal-Wallis H test, published by William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952), is the 'one-way ANOVA by ranks'. It pools all the scores, ranks them from lowest to highest, and asks whether the average rank differs between the groups. Because it uses only order, it does not assume normality and suits ordinal data. The H statistic is read against the chi-square distribution with k minus 1 degrees of freedom, where k is the number of groups. For example, Meera compares pain ratings on a 1 to 5 scale among patients given three different therapies. The ratings are ordinal and bunched at one end, with only 12 patients per group, so Kruskal-Wallis is the right choice. Option a is right because ordinal data plus a severe breach of normality are the two classic reasons to leave ANOVA for its rank-based alternative. Exam trap: option d sounds plausible because it describes a setting where rank tests are common, but two independent groups call for the Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947), or a t-test, not Kruskal-Wallis, which is built for three or more groups. Also remember that large samples and equal variances (option c) are the very conditions that make ANOVA robust, so they argue for keeping ANOVA, not abandoning it.", "topic": "Kruskal-Wallis versus one-way ANOVA"}, {"q": "A researcher notes a Pearson correlation coefficient between job satisfaction and employee well-being, organizational stress, emotions and self-efficacy. Which among the following shows the highest correlation ?", "options": {"a": "-1.66", "b": "0.61", "c": "1.02", "d": "-0.66"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: of the four values, -0.66 shows the highest (strongest) correlation, because -1.66 and 1.02 are impossible and 0.66 in size beats 0.61. The Pearson product-moment correlation coefficient, r, developed by Karl Pearson (1896) from the work of Francis Galton (1888), measures the strength and direction of a straight-line relationship between two variables. It can only range from -1.00 to +1.00. The sign shows direction: a plus means the two variables rise together, and a minus means one rises as the other falls. The size, ignoring the sign (the absolute value), shows strength: 0 means no linear relationship, and the closer to 1 in either direction, the stronger it is. So r = -0.66 and r = +0.66 are equally strong; they simply point in opposite directions. Applying this: -1.66 lies outside the possible range, so it can only be a calculation error, and 1.02 also exceeds +1 and is impossible. That leaves 0.61 and -0.66, whose sizes are 0.61 and 0.66, so -0.66 is the stronger correlation. For example, Arjun finds that job satisfaction correlates -0.66 with organisational stress and +0.61 with self-efficacy. Stress is more strongly related to satisfaction, even though the relationship is negative. Option d is right because it has the largest absolute value among the values a correlation can actually take. Exam trap: students pick 1.02 because it is the 'largest number', or -1.66 because its size is the biggest, forgetting that r can never go beyond plus or minus 1. Others pick 0.61 because a positive number looks 'higher' than a negative one. In correlation, 'highest' means strongest, and strength is judged by size, not by sign.", "topic": "Strength of Pearson's r"}, {"q": "What is the primary conceptual difference between a quasi-experimental design and a true experimental design ?", "options": {"a": "Quasi-experiments do not require any post-test measurements", "b": "True experiments lack a clear dependent variable", "c": "Quasi-experiments lack random assignment of participants to experimental conditions", "d": "True experiments are conducted exclusively within naturalistic settings"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the defining difference is that quasi-experiments lack random assignment of participants to conditions. A true experiment has three features: the researcher manipulates an independent variable, controls extraneous variables, and assigns participants to groups at random. Random assignment, a principle set out by Ronald Aylmer Fisher (1935), gives every participant an equal chance of landing in any condition, so the groups start out equivalent on age, ability, motivation and everything else, measured or not. Any later difference can then be credited to the treatment. Donald Thomas Campbell and Julian C. Stanley (1963) popularised the term quasi-experiment ('quasi' means 'as if') for designs that still apply or compare a treatment and still measure outcomes, but use intact or pre-existing groups such as classes, wards or villages. Common forms are the nonequivalent control group design, the interrupted time-series design and the regression discontinuity design. Because the groups may differ before the study begins, selection becomes a threat to internal validity, and causal conclusions are weaker. For example, Kabir wants to test a mindfulness programme. If he randomly assigns 60 students to the programme or a waiting list, he runs a true experiment. If he gives the programme to Section A and uses Section B for comparison because the timetable allows it, he runs a quasi-experiment, since Section A might already be calmer or keener. Option c is right because random assignment is the single feature that separates the two designs. Exam trap: do not confuse random assignment with random selection. Random selection (sampling) decides who enters the study and supports external validity; random assignment decides who receives which condition and protects internal validity. A quasi-experiment can use a random sample and still lack random assignment.", "topic": "Quasi versus true experiments"}, {"q": "Which ethical violation occurs when a researcher alters or selectively omits experimental data to artificially confirm a favored hypothesis ?", "options": {"a": "Plagiarism", "b": "Falsification", "c": "Fabrication", "d": "Uninformed deception"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: altering or selectively omitting real data so that the results support a favoured hypothesis is falsification. Research misconduct is usually defined by three acts, often abbreviated FFP, as in the United States federal policy on research misconduct (2000). Fabrication is making up data or results that were never collected. Falsification is manipulating research materials, equipment or procedures, or changing or omitting data or results, so that the research record no longer represents what really happened. Plagiarism is using another person's ideas, words or results without giving credit. The key difference between the first two is whether the data exist. In falsification, real data were collected, but the researcher tampers with them: dropping awkward participants without a stated rule, deleting outliers only when they hurt the hypothesis, or reporting only the conditions that worked. In fabrication, the numbers are invented outright, as in the case of the Dutch social psychologist Diederik Stapel (2011), who was found to have made up data for dozens of published studies. For example, Riya runs a study on music and memory. Her real results show no effect, so she quietly removes six participants whose scores spoil the pattern, and the effect becomes significant. Every number she reports was collected, but she has falsified the record. Option b is right because the stem says the researcher 'alters or selectively omits' existing data, which is the exact wording of falsification. Exam trap: fabrication and falsification are often swapped. Remember: fabrication fabricates (creates from nothing); falsification falsifies (distorts what exists). Selective omission counts as falsification even when no single number is changed. Honest error and honest differences of opinion are not misconduct.", "topic": "Research misconduct: falsification"}, {"q": "Which measure of central tendency is the most appropriate and psychometrically stable for a highly skewed pay distribution ?", "options": {"a": "Median", "b": "Mean", "c": "Mode", "d": "Geometric Mean"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: for a highly skewed distribution such as pay, the median is the most appropriate and stable measure of central tendency. A measure of central tendency gives one value that represents the typical score. The mean is the arithmetic average: add all the scores and divide by how many there are. The median is the middle score once all the scores are put in order, so half fall above it and half below. The mode is the most frequent score. The mean uses the actual size of every score, so a few extreme values drag it towards the tail. The median uses only the order of scores, so extreme values barely move it; statisticians call it resistant, or robust, to outliers. Pay is the classic positively skewed variable: most people earn modest amounts and a few earn huge sums, giving a long tail to the right. In a positive skew the usual order is mode, then median, then mean, with the mean pulled furthest towards the high earners. For example, in Arjun's small firm nine staff earn between Rs 20,000 and Rs 40,000 a month, and the owner takes Rs 10 lakh. The mean pay comes out above Rs 1 lakh, a figure that describes nobody, while the median stays near Rs 30,000, a fair picture of a typical employee. This is why surveys and governments report median household income. Option a is right because the median is unaffected by the size of extreme values, so it stays stable and representative when a distribution is skewed. Exam trap: the mean is the most stable measure from sample to sample when data are normal, and later statistics need it, so students choose it out of habit. Stability across samples is not the same as resistance to outliers; with heavy skew, the median wins.", "topic": "Central tendency for skewed data"}, {"q": "A researcher studies the effects of sleep deprivation on reaction time in a group of participants. In this study, reaction time consitutes the :", "options": {"a": "Independent variable", "b": "Dependent variable", "c": "Confounding variable", "d": "Extraneous variable"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: reaction time is the dependent variable, because it is the outcome measured to see whether sleep deprivation changes it. In an experiment the researcher deliberately changes one thing and measures its effect on another. The independent variable (IV) is the factor the researcher manipulates or varies: here, sleep deprivation, for instance a full night's sleep versus 24 hours awake. The dependent variable (DV) is the behaviour measured afterwards, whose value is expected to depend on the IV: here, reaction time in milliseconds. A handy test is to fit the study into the phrase 'the effect of ___ on ___'. The first blank is the IV and the second is the DV, so 'the effects of sleep deprivation on reaction time' gives the answer at once. These terms became standard in psychology through the textbook Experimental Psychology by Robert Sessions Woodworth (1938). Other variables must be kept out of the way. Extraneous variables are any other factors that could affect the DV, such as caffeine or age. When an extraneous variable changes systematically along with the IV, it becomes a confounding variable and makes the result impossible to interpret. For example, Kabir keeps one group awake all night and lets another group sleep, then measures how fast each person presses a button when a light flashes. If the sleepless group also drank more tea, tea would be a confound. Option b is right because reaction time is what is measured, and its value is expected to change as a result of sleep deprivation. Exam trap: students sometimes reverse the two, especially when the measured variable sounds active or is mentioned first. Always ask which variable the researcher controls (the IV) and which one the researcher records (the DV).", "topic": "Independent and dependent variables"}, {"q": "According to Classical Test Theory (CTT), if a test has a reliability coefficient of 0.81, what percentage of the total observed score variance is attributable to random measurement error ?", "options": {"a": "81%", "b": "29%", "c": "9%", "d": "19%"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: with a reliability coefficient of 0.81, 19 per cent of the observed-score variance is due to random measurement error. Classical test theory, built on the work of Charles Spearman (1904), assumes that every observed score (X) is a true score (T) plus random error (E): X = T + E. Because error is random and unrelated to the true score, the variances add as well: observed variance = true variance + error variance. The reliability coefficient is defined as the proportion of observed variance that is true-score variance. So a reliability of 0.81 means that 81 per cent of the differences between people's scores reflect real differences in the trait, and the rest, 1 - 0.81 = 0.19, or 19 per cent, reflects random error such as fatigue, lucky guessing or a noisy room. For example, Meera's anxiety scale has a test-retest reliability of 0.81. When scores vary across her sample, about four-fifths of that variation is genuine anxiety and about one-fifth is measurement noise. Option d is right because error variance is simply one minus the reliability coefficient, and 1 - 0.81 = 0.19. Exam trap: do not square the coefficient or take its square root here. A correlation between two different variables is squared to get shared variance (r = 0.81 would share about 66 per cent), and the square root of reliability (0.90) is the index of reliability, the correlation between observed and true scores. A reliability coefficient, however, is already a ratio of variances, so it is read directly as a percentage. Option a gives the true-score share, not the error share. The standard error of measurement uses the same quantity: SEM equals the standard deviation multiplied by the square root of (1 - r).", "topic": "Reliability and error variance"}, {"q": "A psychometrist wishes to assess the internal consistency of a test which has multi-point, non-dichotomous items (such as a 5-point Likert scale). Which statistical metric should be calculated ?", "options": {"a": "Kuder-Richardson Formula 20 (KR-20)", "b": "Cronbach's Alpha", "c": "Cohen's Kappa", "d": "Spearman-Brown coefficient"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: for internal consistency on multi-point items such as a 5-point Likert scale, the right statistic is Cronbach's alpha. Internal consistency asks whether all the items of a test measure the same thing, using a single administration. The earliest method was split-half reliability: score two halves of the test, correlate them and correct for length. Because the result depends on how the test is split, G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) derived formulas from item statistics instead. Their KR-20 uses p and q, the proportions passing and failing each item, so it works only for dichotomous items scored right or wrong, yes or no. Lee Joseph Cronbach (1951) generalised KR-20 into coefficient alpha by replacing pq with each item's variance, so alpha works for items with any number of score points, such as Likert ratings from 1 to 5. Alpha equals k divided by (k - 1), multiplied by one minus the sum of item variances divided by the total-score variance. It equals the mean of all possible split-half coefficients, and for dichotomous items it gives exactly the same value as KR-20. Values of about 0.70 or above are usually treated as acceptable for research. For example, Kabir writes a 20-item exam-stress questionnaire rated from 'never' (1) to 'always' (5). To check whether the items hang together, he computes Cronbach's alpha, not KR-20. Option b is right because alpha is the internal consistency coefficient designed for items with more than two score points. Exam trap: KR-20 and alpha are both internal consistency coefficients, so KR-20 is the near miss. The deciding detail is item format: dichotomous items mean KR-20; multi-point items mean alpha. Cohen's kappa also sounds like a reliability index, but it measures agreement between raters, not consistency among items.", "topic": "Cronbach's alpha for Likert items"}, {"q": "If a researcher wants to eliminate the impact of speed or time limits on a test to assess an examinee's maximum level of performance on increasingly difficult items, they should construct a :", "options": {"a": "Speed test", "b": "Mastery test", "c": "Power test", "d": "Situational judgement test"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: a test that removes the effect of time limits and measures how far a person can go on items of rising difficulty is a power test. Tests are classified by how time and difficulty are combined. A speed test contains easy items of about equal difficulty and a time limit so tight that almost nobody finishes; the score reflects how quickly a person works, as in a clerical checking or letter-cancellation task. A power test allows generous time, or no limit at all, but its items climb steadily in difficulty until some are too hard for anyone. The score shows the hardest level a person can master, that is, their maximum ability, not their pace. Most real ability tests are speeded power tests, a blend of the two. Anne Anastasi (1954) stressed that the distinction also matters for reliability: split-half and internal consistency estimates are falsely inflated on pure speed tests, so they belong with power tests. Raven's Progressive Matrices, devised by John Carlyle Raven (1938) and given untimed in its standard form, is a classic power test. For example, Meera takes an untimed reasoning test whose puzzles grow harder page by page. She stops at puzzle 34 because the later ones are beyond her, not because the clock ran out, so her score reflects her power, the ceiling of her ability. Option c is right because removing time pressure and grading items from easy to hard is the definition of a power test. Exam trap: examiners reverse the definitions. It is power tests that have items of increasing difficulty; speed tests have uniformly easy items. A mastery test sounds similar because it also concerns what a person can do, but it checks whether a fixed standard has been reached, not the maximum level a person can attain.", "topic": "Speed versus power tests"}, {"q": "Which ethical principle is violated if a psychologist administers a highly specialized clinical test battery without having advanced training or certification in its interpretation ?", "options": {"a": "Informed consent", "b": "Competence", "c": "Confidentiality", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: giving a specialised clinical test battery without the training to interpret it violates the ethical principle of competence. Competence means that psychologists work only within the limits of their education, training, supervised experience and professional credentials. The American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016) sets this out in Standard 2 (Competence), and its assessment standards add that tests must be used only in ways a psychologist's training supports and must not be handed to unqualified persons. In India, the Rehabilitation Council of India, under the RCI Act (1992), registers clinical psychologists and expects the same. Test publishers enforce competence through user qualification levels. Level A tests, such as achievement tests, need only the manual; Level B tests, such as group intelligence and aptitude tests, need training in test construction and statistics; Level C tests, such as individual intelligence tests, clinical personality inventories and projective techniques, need an advanced degree and supervised experience. The risk is real: an untrained user may score a battery wrongly, misread a profile and label a client with a disorder or brain damage that is not there. For example, Arjun, a counsellor with only a bachelor's degree, buys a neuropsychological battery online and tells a client she has early dementia. Even though she agreed to testing and her results stayed private, Arjun acted outside his competence. Option b is right because the violation lies in the psychologist's lack of training and qualification, the core of the competence standard. Exam trap: informed consent and confidentiality are also central duties in testing, so they look tempting. But neither is breached by lack of training: a psychologist can obtain full consent and protect privacy and still be unfit to interpret the test.", "topic": "Competence in test use"}, {"q": "What psychometric challenge occurs when an individual consistently responds to self-report personality items presenting a favorable, idealized image of themselves rather than reflecting their true behaviour ?", "options": {"a": "Social desirability bias", "b": "Acquiescence response set", "c": "Malingering", "d": "Extreme response bias"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: consistently answering self-report items so as to present a favourable, idealised image, rather than one's true behaviour, is social desirability bias. Self-report inventories assume that people describe themselves honestly, but answers can be shaped by how people want to look. Lee Joseph Cronbach (1946) showed that habitual ways of answering, called response sets, shift scores regardless of item content. Allen L. Edwards (1957) found that how socially desirable an item sounds strongly predicts whether people endorse it. Delroy Paulhus (1984) later split social desirability into two parts: impression management, a conscious effort to look good to others, and self-deceptive enhancement, an honest but inflated view of oneself. Test makers control it in several ways: forced-choice items matched for desirability, as in the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule; lie scales such as the L scale of the MMPI; and separate measures such as the Marlowe-Crowne Social Desirability Scale of Douglas Crowne and David Marlowe (1960). For example, Riya, applying for a job, fills in a personality questionnaire. She marks 'never' for 'I sometimes lose my temper' and 'always' for 'I finish every task on time', though neither is true. Her answers paint the ideal employee, not the real Riya. Option a is right because the stem describes a favourable, idealised self-presentation on self-report items, the definition of social desirability bias, also called faking good. Exam trap: malingering is the mirror image. Both distort the picture, but malingering is deliberately presenting oneself as worse (faking bad) for an external gain such as compensation or avoiding duty. Acquiescence and extreme responding are also response styles, but they depend on the format of an item (agreeing, or choosing the end points), not on how desirable an answer looks.", "topic": "Social desirability response bias"}, {"q": "Which neuropsychological test is specifically designed to assess executive functioning, abstract reasoning, and set-shifting capabilities by requiring participants to match cards based on changing rules ?", "options": {"a": "Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test", "b": "Thematic Apperception Test", "c": "Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale", "d": "Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST)"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is the neuropsychological test of executive functioning, abstract reasoning and set-shifting that asks people to match cards by rules that change. Executive functions are the higher control processes, centred on the prefrontal cortex, that let us plan, form concepts, hold a goal in mind and switch strategy when circumstances change. The WCST was devised by David Grant and Esta Berg (1948), and Robert Heaton (1981) wrote its standard clinical manual. Four stimulus cards are laid out, differing in colour, shape and number of symbols. The person sorts a deck of response cards under them but is never told the rule; the examiner says only 'right' or 'wrong'. After a run of ten correct sorts the rule changes without warning, say from colour to shape. A healthy adult notices the string of 'wrong' feedback, works out the new rule and shifts. The key score is perseverative errors: going on sorting by the old rule despite being told it is wrong. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral frontal lobe excisions made many perseverative errors, tying the test to the frontal lobes. It is widely used in schizophrenia, head injury and frontal lobe damage. For example, after a road accident Kabir keeps sorting by colour even after hearing 'wrong' ten times in a row. His difficulty in shifting set points to frontal injury. Option d is right because matching cards by changing, unannounced rules is the signature of the WCST. Exam trap: the Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test also sits in neuropsychological batteries, so it is a tempting near miss, but it measures visual-motor integration by copying designs, not set-shifting. Card sorting plus shifting rules always means the WCST.", "topic": "Wisconsin Card Sorting Test"}, {"q": "Which neuroimaging technique provides exceptional temporal resolution by tracking electrical activity down to the millisecond, but offers relatively limited spatial localization ?", "options": {"a": "Functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging (FMRI)", "b": "Positron Emission Tomography (PET)", "c": "Electroencephalography (EEG)", "d": "Computed Tomography (CT)"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: electroencephalography (EEG) tracks the brain's electrical activity millisecond by millisecond, giving excellent temporal resolution but poor spatial localisation. Brain-measurement methods are judged on two kinds of resolution. Temporal resolution is precision about when activity happens; spatial resolution is precision about where it happens. The German psychiatrist Hans Berger (1929) first recorded EEG from the human scalp. Electrodes on the scalp pick up the summed electrical activity of millions of cortical neurons as it happens, so changes can be timed to within a millisecond. Averaging the EEG over many repeated stimuli gives event-related potentials, such as the P300 wave that appears about 300 milliseconds after a rare target stimulus. The weakness is location: the signal is blurred by the skull and scalp, and each electrode sums activity over a wide area, so EEG can only say roughly where, usually within centimetres, and it barely detects deep structures. By contrast, fMRI and PET measure blood flow or metabolism, which change over seconds, so they locate activity well but time it poorly. For example, Meera wants to know how quickly the brain tells a happy face from an angry one. She uses EEG, because the difference appears within a few hundred milliseconds; if she wanted to know which brain region does the job, she would choose fMRI. Option c is right because EEG combines millisecond timing with limited spatial precision, exactly as the stem describes. Exam trap: fMRI sounds the most modern and has good spatial resolution, so students choose it, but blood-flow signals lag behind neural events by several seconds. CT is purely structural and shows no activity at all. Magnetoencephalography (MEG) shares EEG's millisecond timing with better localisation, but it is not offered here.", "topic": "EEG temporal resolution"}, {"q": "An individual is born with an extra copy of chromosome 21, resulting in a total of 47 chromosomes. This specific chromosomal anomaly can be seen in :", "options": {"a": "Down syndrome", "b": "Turner syndrome", "c": "Klinefelter syndrome", "d": "Huntington's disease"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: an extra copy of chromosome 21, giving 47 chromosomes in place of the usual 46, is the karyotype of Down syndrome (trisomy 21). Humans normally carry 23 pairs of chromosomes: 22 pairs of autosomes plus one pair of sex chromosomes (XX or XY). When a pair fails to separate during meiosis (nondisjunction), an egg or sperm carries two copies of a chromosome, and the zygote ends up with three. John Langdon Down (1866) described the clinical picture: intellectual disability, a flat facial profile, upward-slanting eyes, a single palmar crease, low muscle tone, heart defects and an early onset of Alzheimer's disease. Jérôme Lejeune (1959) then showed the extra chromosome 21 under the microscope. About 95 per cent of cases are free trisomy 21; the rest are translocation or mosaic forms. The risk rises steeply with maternal age. Down syndrome is the commonest genetic cause of intellectual disability, while fragile X is the commonest inherited one. Example: Meera's newborn son has low muscle tone and a heart murmur; his karyotype reads 47,XY,+21, which is Down syndrome. Option a is right because only Down syndrome is defined by three copies of chromosome 21. Exam trap: Klinefelter syndrome also has 47 chromosomes, so a student who reads only the number may pick c; the extra chromosome there is an X (47,XXY), not chromosome 21. Turner syndrome is the reverse error, a missing X (45,X), and Huntington's disease has a normal chromosome count with one faulty gene. Always check which chromosome is extra, not just the total.", "topic": "Down syndrome, trisomy 21"}, {"q": "What physiological change is uniquely a characteristic of REM sleep when compared to slow-wave sleep ?", "options": {"a": "Profound muscular atonia combined with rapid eye movements and low-voltage", "b": "High-amplitude delta waves on EEG", "c": "Desynchronized EEG patterns", "d": "Extreme stability in heart and respiration"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the combination of profound muscle atonia, rapid eye movements and a low-voltage EEG is found only in REM sleep. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered REM sleep by watching sleepers' eyes dart under closed lids, and William Dement and Nathaniel Kleitman (1957) linked these periods to vivid dreaming. Sleep has two broad states. Non-REM sleep runs from light N1 and N2 to N3, slow-wave sleep, where the EEG shows high-amplitude delta waves (0.5 to 4 Hz), the body is still but keeps some muscle tone, and heart rate and breathing are slow and regular. REM sleep is called paradoxical sleep because the brain looks awake while the body is limp: the EEG is fast, low voltage and desynchronised, the eyes move rapidly, and the large skeletal muscles are almost paralysed (REM atonia, produced by circuits in the pons that inhibit spinal motor neurons). Heart rate and breathing become irregular. REM makes up 20 to 25 per cent of adult sleep and lengthens toward morning. Example: Kabir's sleep lab record at 5 a.m. shows a flat chin muscle trace, sawtooth EEG waves and bursts of eye movement; he is in REM. Option a is right because no other state, waking or non-REM, joins atonia with rapid eye movements and a low-voltage EEG. Exam trap: delta waves (b) belong to slow-wave sleep, REM brings irregular rather than stable heart rate and breathing (d), and a desynchronised EEG (c) is real in REM but also marks waking and N1. Key note: Some candidates argue for c, since the REM EEG really is desynchronised compared with slow-wave sleep. But the stem asks what is unique to REM, and only the combination in option a (which already includes the low-voltage EEG) meets that test, so the official key stands.", "topic": "REM sleep features"}, {"q": "Which subcortical structure acts as the primary sensory relay station, filtering and routing all incoming sensory inputs (except olfaction) to their respective cortical destinations ?", "options": {"a": "Hypothalamus", "b": "Amygdala", "c": "Thalamus", "d": "Pons"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the thalamus is the brain's main sensory relay station, passing every sense except smell on to the cortex. The thalamus is a paired, egg-shaped mass of grey matter in the diencephalon, sitting on top of the brainstem on either side of the third ventricle. It is made of separate nuclei, each linked to its own patch of cortex. The lateral geniculate nucleus relays vision to the occipital lobe, the medial geniculate nucleus relays hearing to the temporal lobe (a handy memory aid: lateral for light, medial for music), and the ventral posterior nucleus relays touch, pain, temperature, body position and taste to the parietal cortex. Smell is the exception: the olfactory bulb sends fibres straight to the olfactory cortex, reaching the thalamus only later. The thalamus is not a passive switchboard. Through its reticular nucleus and two-way loops with the cortex it gates which signals pass, so it shapes attention, sleep and consciousness. Damage can cause loss of sensation or severe central pain, as Joseph Dejerine and Gustave Roussy (1906) described in thalamic pain syndrome. Example: when Riya hears her name in a noisy canteen, the sound is relayed through her medial geniculate nucleus before her auditory cortex recognises it. Option c is right because the thalamus is the single structure that receives and routes all the non-olfactory senses. Exam trap: the hypothalamus sits just below the thalamus and has a similar name, but it controls drives, hormones and body temperature rather than relaying sensation.", "topic": "Thalamus as sensory relay"}, {"q": "The primary mechanism of action for Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors (SSRIs) involves :", "options": {"a": "Inhibiting monoamine oxidase enzymes from degrading serotonin", "b": "Increasing the rate of exocytosis in the axon terminal", "c": "Blocking the pre-synaptic reuptake transporter proteins, increasing serotonin availability in the synaptic cleft", "d": "Stimulating post-synaptic serotonin receptors directly"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: SSRIs work by blocking the presynaptic serotonin reuptake transporter, so more serotonin stays in the synaptic cleft. Normally a neuron releases serotonin (5-HT) into the synapse, the transmitter binds to receptors on the next cell, and then a transporter protein on the presynaptic terminal (SERT) pumps it back inside for reuse. This reuptake ends the signal. A selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor such as fluoxetine or sertraline binds to SERT and blocks the pump, so serotonin lingers in the cleft and keeps acting on its receptors. In drug terms an SSRI is an indirect agonist: it boosts serotonin's effect without itself activating a receptor. 'Selective' means it largely spares the noradrenaline and dopamine transporters, which is why SSRIs cause fewer side effects than the older tricyclics. Fluoxetine (Prozac) was described by David Wong and colleagues (1974) at Eli Lilly and approved in the United States in 1987. Although the blockade happens within hours, mood lifts only after 2 to 4 weeks, which suggests the benefit comes from slower changes such as receptor down-regulation and neuroplasticity. Example: Arjun starts sertraline for depression; his sleep and appetite improve in the third week, though the drug blocked reuptake from the first dose. Option c is right because blocking the reuptake transporter is exactly what 'reuptake inhibitor' means. Exam trap: option a describes MAO inhibitors, which also raise serotonin but by stopping its breakdown, not its reuptake; examiners swap the two because both are antidepressants that raise monoamines.", "topic": "SSRI mechanism of action"}, {"q": "Which major neurotransmitter is widely implicated as the primary inhibitor within the central nervous system ?", "options": {"a": "Glutamate", "b": "Acetylcholine", "c": "Norepinephrine", "d": "Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA)"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. A neurotransmitter is inhibitory when it makes the next neuron less likely to fire. GABA does this by opening channels that let negative chloride ions in (the fast GABA-A receptor) or let positive potassium ions out (the slower GABA-B receptor). Either way the inside of the cell becomes more negative, a hyperpolarisation called an inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP), and the neuron moves further from its firing threshold. Eugene Roberts and Sam Frankel (1950) identified GABA in the brain; it is made from glutamate by the enzyme glutamic acid decarboxylase, and perhaps a third of brain synapses use it. The balance between glutamate excitation and GABA inhibition matters clinically. Too little inhibition can cause seizures and anxiety, which is why benzodiazepines, barbiturates and alcohol, all of which enhance the GABA-A receptor, calm anxiety and stop fits. Example: when Meera takes a prescribed benzodiazepine before a dental procedure, it strengthens the effect of her own GABA, and her racing worry quietens. Option d is right because GABA is the chief inhibitory transmitter throughout the central nervous system. Exam trap: glutamate, the main excitatory transmitter, is the classic decoy, since GABA is built from it and the two are always taught together. Note also that glycine, not GABA, is the main inhibitory transmitter of the spinal cord, so a question that asks specifically about the spinal cord has a different answer.", "topic": "GABA, main inhibitory transmitter"}, {"q": "Which sensory receptor cells located within the retina are specialized for capturing color vision and responsible for light adaptation ?", "options": {"a": "Cones", "b": "Rods", "c": "Ganglion cells", "d": "Bipolar cells"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: cones are the retinal photoreceptors that give colour vision and work in bright light, so they carry vision when the eye is light-adapted. The retina has two kinds of light-sensitive receptor. Rods (about 120 million) contain a single pigment, rhodopsin, are extremely sensitive and serve scotopic, night vision in shades of grey. Cones (about 6 to 7 million) are packed into the fovea, need more light and serve photopic vision: sharp detail and colour. Colour depends on three cone types, most sensitive to short, medium and long wavelengths, the idea proposed by Thomas Young (1802) and developed by Hermann von Helmholtz (1852) as the trichromatic theory. Light adaptation is the eye's adjustment when you move from darkness into bright light: the pupil narrows, the rods saturate and drop out, and the cones take over, within about a minute. Dark adaptation is the slower reverse, about 20 to 30 minutes, ending with the rods. Example: when Kabir walks out of a dark cinema into afternoon sun he is dazzled, then within a minute his cones take over and he reads the red and blue signboards clearly. Option a is right because cones are the only receptors that capture colour and the ones that operate in light-adapted vision. Exam trap: rods are the decoy because they are the famous adaptation receptors, but their role is dark adaptation and night vision; reversing rods and cones is the standard trick in this question.", "topic": "Cones and colour vision"}, {"q": "Which hormone released by the adrenal cortex in response to chronic psychological stress and can impair hippocampal functioning over time ?", "options": {"a": "Cortisol", "b": "Epinephrine", "c": "Thyroxine", "d": "Insulin"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: cortisol, the main glucocorticoid of the adrenal cortex, is released in chronic stress and in excess can damage the hippocampus. The body has two stress systems. The fast one, the sympathetic-adrenal-medullary (SAM) system, sends nerve signals to the adrenal medulla, which releases epinephrine (adrenaline) within seconds. The slower one is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: the hypothalamus releases CRH, the anterior pituitary releases ACTH into the blood, and the adrenal cortex responds with cortisol within minutes. Cortisol raises blood sugar and damps inflammation, and normally switches itself off through negative feedback, partly via receptors in the hippocampus. Hans Selye (1936) described how prolonged stress wears the body down in his general adaptation syndrome. Robert Sapolsky, Lewis Krey and Bruce McEwen (1986) proposed the glucocorticoid cascade hypothesis: long-term high cortisol shrinks hippocampal dendrites and can kill neurons, a damaged hippocampus brakes the HPA axis less well, cortisol climbs further, and memory suffers. Brain scans in Cushing's syndrome, long-standing depression and PTSD often show a smaller hippocampus. Example: Riya, caring for her ill father for two years while working night shifts, has raised cortisol and keeps forgetting appointments. Option a is right because cortisol is the adrenal cortex hormone that rises in chronic stress and harms the hippocampus. Exam trap: epinephrine is also an adrenal stress hormone, but it comes from the adrenal medulla and drives the fast, short-term fight-or-flight response, so the words 'cortex' and 'chronic' rule it out.", "topic": "Cortisol and the hippocampus"}, {"q": "According to Kurt Lewin's Field Theory, human behaviour is a dynamic function of the interaction between which two core vectors ?", "options": {"a": "Biological drives and unconscious defense mechanisms", "b": "The person and their immediate psychological environment", "c": "Past reinforcement history and future expectations", "d": "Social norms and institutional constraints"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: Kurt Lewin's field theory holds that behaviour is a function of the person and the psychological environment, written B = f(P, E). Kurt Lewin (1936), a Gestalt-trained psychologist, set this out in Principles of Topological Psychology. He borrowed the idea of a field from physics: just as many forces in a magnetic field act at once on a particle, many forces act together on a person at a given moment. The person and the environment together form the life space, the totality of facts that determine behaviour at that moment. Crucially, the environment is psychological, the world as the person perceives it, not the objective physical setting. Lewin mapped the life space with regions (possible activities), boundaries (barriers), valences (attractions and repulsions) and vectors (forces pushing towards or away from goals). Because behaviour depends on the present field, he stressed contemporaneity: past events matter only as they exist in the present life space. His approach led on to group dynamics and action research and earned him the title of father of modern social psychology. Example: Arjun freezes during a group discussion at college. Field theory explains this through his anxiety and goals (P) together with how he sees the room, such as a stern-looking professor (E), not through either alone. Option b is right because B = f(P, E) names exactly the person and the psychological environment. Exam trap: option c sounds scientific but brings in reinforcement history, which is behaviourism; Lewin deliberately focused on the present field rather than past learning.", "topic": "Lewin's field theory"}, {"q": "According to the Elaboration Likelihood Model (ELM) of persuasion, under which specific condition will an individual process a message using the central route ?", "options": {"a": "When they are highly distracted and lack cognitive capacity", "b": "When the source of the message is exceptionally attractive or famous", "c": "When the message relies heavily on emotional fear appeals", "d": "When they possess both high motivation and the cognitive ability to scrutinize the argument's core merits"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: in the Elaboration Likelihood Model, people take the central route when they have both the motivation and the ability to think carefully about a message. Richard Petty and John Cacioppo (1986) proposed the ELM to explain why the same message persuades some people and not others. Elaboration means thinking about the arguments in a message. When elaboration likelihood is high, people take the central route: they scrutinise argument quality, and strong arguments produce attitude change that lasts, resists counter-attack and predicts behaviour. Motivation comes from personal relevance and a high need for cognition; ability needs time, knowledge and freedom from distraction. If either is missing, people take the peripheral route and are swayed by surface cues such as an attractive or famous source, the sheer number of arguments or a good mood; this change is quicker but weaker and short-lived. In a classic study, Richard Petty, John Cacioppo and Rachel Goldman (1981) found that students judged a proposed comprehensive examination on argument quality when it would start next year (high relevance) but on source expertise when it was a decade away. Example: Meera, choosing health insurance for her parents, reads every clause and compares claim ratios: high motivation and ability, so central route. Her cousin picks a plan because a film star endorses it: peripheral route. Option d is right because the central route needs motivation and ability together. Exam trap: options a, b and c each describe a peripheral route condition (distraction, an attractive source, emotional appeal); examiners count on students confusing 'persuasive' with 'central'.", "topic": "Elaboration likelihood model"}, {"q": "According to Harold Kelley's Covariation Model of attribution, an observer will make an external attribution when a target's behavior exhibits :", "options": {"a": "Low distinctiveness, low consensus, and high consistency", "b": "High distinctiveness, low consensus, and low consistency", "c": "Low distinctiveness, high consensus, and low consistency", "d": "High distinctiveness, high consensus, and high consistency"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: Harold Kelley's covariation model predicts an external attribution when consensus, distinctiveness and consistency are all high. Harold Kelley (1967) asked how we decide whether a behaviour is caused by the person, the stimulus or the circumstances. His answer is that we act like intuitive scientists: across many observations we credit the cause that varies together, or covaries, with the behaviour. Three kinds of information matter. Consensus asks whether other people react to the same stimulus in the same way. Distinctiveness asks whether this person reacts differently to other stimuli. Consistency asks whether this person reacts the same way to this stimulus on other occasions. High consensus, high distinctiveness and high consistency point outward, to the stimulus or situation. Low consensus, low distinctiveness and high consistency point inward, to the person's disposition. Low consistency points to passing circumstances. Leslie McArthur (1972) tested the model with written vignettes and found that people do use all three cues, though they underuse consensus. Example: Kabir laughs at a stand-up comedian. Everyone else in the hall laughs too (high consensus), Kabir rarely laughs at other comedians (high distinctiveness) and he laughs at this one every time (high consistency). We conclude the comedian is genuinely funny: an external cause. Option d is right because only the all-high pattern points to the stimulus. Exam trap: option a is the mirror image, the classic pattern for an internal attribution; consistency is high in both patterns, and examiners rely on students forgetting that consensus and distinctiveness flip.", "topic": "Kelley's covariation model"}, {"q": "According to Jean Piaget's cognitive development theory, which cognitive limitation explains why a preoperational child believes that a tall, narrow glass holds more liquid than a short, wide glass, even after watching the liquid poured directly from one to the other ?", "options": {"a": "Inability to assimilate new information", "b": "Lack of object permanence", "c": "Centration and lack of reversibility", "d": "Failure of formal deductive operations"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: the preoperational child fails liquid conservation because of centration and a lack of reversibility. Jean Piaget (1952), in The Child's Conception of Number, showed that young children do not grasp conservation, the understanding that a quantity stays the same when only its shape or arrangement changes. In the liquid task a child agrees that two identical short, wide glasses hold the same amount of juice. One is then poured, in full view, into a tall, narrow glass. A four- or five-year-old now says the tall glass has more. Two limits of preoperational thought (about 2 to 7 years) explain this. Centration is focusing on one striking feature, here the height of the liquid, while ignoring another, the narrower width. Lack of reversibility is the inability to run the action backwards in the mind and see that pouring the juice back would give equal glasses again. Around 7, in the concrete operational stage, children conserve and justify it with three arguments: identity (nothing was added or taken away), compensation (taller but thinner) and reversibility (pour it back and it is the same). Example: Riya, aged 4, insists her brother's tall glass of lassi has more than hers, though their mother poured both from the same jug; at 8 she laughs at the idea. Option c is right because centration plus irreversibility is Piaget's own explanation of conservation failure. Exam trap: lack of object permanence (b) sounds like losing track of things, but it is an infant limitation, solved by about 2 years, long before this task.", "topic": "Conservation, centration and reversibility"}, {"q": "Which of the following developmental milestones is uniquely characteristic of the transition into the formal operational stage of cognitive development ?", "options": {"a": "Systematic hypothetical-deductive reasoning and abstract thought", "b": "Mastery of conservation of mass and volume", "c": "The emergence of basic symbolic play", "d": "The initial tracking of objects out of view"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: systematic hypothetico-deductive reasoning and abstract thought are the signature of the formal operational stage. In the account of Bärbel Inhelder and Jean Piaget (1958), cognitive development runs through four stages: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (from about 11 or 12). Concrete thinkers reason logically, but only about real, picturable things. Formal thinkers can reason about possibilities, about abstractions such as justice or infinity, and about propositions whose conclusions follow from their logical form. The core skill is hypothetico-deductive reasoning: starting from a general idea, deducing hypotheses and testing them systematically, one variable at a time. Inhelder and Piaget showed this with the pendulum problem in The Growth of Logical Thinking from Childhood to Adolescence. Children had to find what controls how fast a pendulum swings: string length, weight, height of release or force of push. Concrete operational children changed several things at once and drew muddled conclusions; formal operational adolescents varied one factor while holding the others constant and found that only string length matters. Example: Arjun, 14, tests why his plants are wilting by changing only the watering in one pot while keeping light and soil the same; that is formal operational reasoning. Option a is right because hypothetical, abstract and systematic reasoning first appears at the formal operational stage. Exam trap: conservation (b) is the most famous Piagetian milestone, so students grab it, but it marks the concrete operational stage; formal operations are about thinking beyond the concrete.", "topic": "Formal operational thought"}, {"q": "According to Paul Baltes' framework of life-span developmental psychology, successful aging is optimized through which of the following behavioural management process ?", "options": {"a": "Sublimation, projection, and defense integration", "b": "Continuous physical deceleration", "c": "Age-segregated institutionalization", "d": "Selective Optimization with Compensation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: the model of successful ageing in Paul Baltes' framework is selective optimisation with compensation (SOC). Paul Baltes and Margret Baltes (1990) proposed SOC as a general strategy for ageing well. It grew out of the lifespan view Paul Baltes (1987) had set out: development is lifelong, multidirectional and always a mix of gains and losses, and in late life the losses grow. SOC describes three processes for managing this. Selection means narrowing one's goals to the domains that matter most, either by choice or because of a loss. Optimisation means investing time, effort and practice to keep performing well in those chosen domains. Compensation means using new means, such as aids, other people or new strategies, when old means are lost. The Baltes couple's classic illustration was the pianist Arthur Rubinstein, who in his eighties played fewer pieces (selection), practised them more (optimisation) and slowed down before fast passages so they sounded quicker by contrast (compensation). Alexandra Freund and Paul Baltes (1998) found that older adults who reported using SOC strategies had higher well-being. Example: Kabir's grandmother, 78, gives up her large garden but keeps her favourite herb bed (selection), spends more time tending it (optimisation) and uses a kneeling stool and long-handled tools for her stiff knees (compensation). Option d is right because SOC is precisely the behavioural management process Paul Baltes named. Exam trap: option a looks technical because it lists defence mechanisms, but sublimation and projection are psychoanalytic ideas from Sigmund Freud (1894) and Anna Freud (1936), not lifespan psychology.", "topic": "Selective optimisation with compensation"}, {"q": "According to Urie Bronfenbrenner's ecological systems theory, the direct interaction between a child's family and their school teachers falls under which system layer ?", "options": {"a": "Microsystem", "b": "Mesosystem", "c": "Exosystem", "d": "Macrosystem"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: links and interactions between two of a child's microsystems, such as family and school, make up the mesosystem. Urie Bronfenbrenner (1979), in The Ecology of Human Development, pictured the child at the centre of nested environmental systems, like Russian dolls, each influencing the child and influenced in turn. The microsystem is the innermost layer: settings the child takes part in directly and face to face, such as home, classroom, peer group and neighbourhood. The mesosystem is the set of connections between those microsystems, such as parent-teacher meetings, help with homework or ties between a child's friends and parents. It is a relationship between settings, not a setting. The exosystem contains settings the child is not part of that still affect the child, such as a parent's workplace, the school management committee or local health services. The macrosystem is the overarching culture: values, laws, customs and the economic system. Bronfenbrenner later added the chronosystem, the dimension of time and life transitions, and renamed the approach the bioecological model. A strong mesosystem helps: children do better when home and school share expectations and communicate. Example: Meera's parents meet her class teacher every month, and the teacher sends home reading tips they follow; this home-school link is the mesosystem. Option b is right because the stem describes an interaction between two microsystems, which is the definition of the mesosystem. Exam trap: microsystem (a) tempts because the family and the teachers each belong to a microsystem, but the question asks about the link between them; the prefix meso (middle) marks that in-between layer.", "topic": "Bronfenbrenner's mesosystem"}, {"q": "The cognitive limitation wherein a young child is unable to take or understand another person's spatial or psychological perspective is called :", "options": {"a": "Centration", "b": "Animism", "c": "Egocentrism", "d": "Abstract thought"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: egocentrism is a young child's inability to take another person's point of view, whether spatial or psychological. In the theory of Jean Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder (1956), egocentrism is the hallmark of the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years). It does not mean selfishness. It means the child assumes that others see, think and feel what the child does, because the child cannot yet separate their own perspective from someone else's. Piaget and Inhelder tested it with the three mountains task. Children sat in front of a model of three mountains, one with snow, one with a house and one with a red cross, and chose the picture showing what a doll seated elsewhere would see. Four-year-olds usually picked their own view; correct choices became common only around 7 to 8. Egocentrism also shows in speech (talking without checking whether the listener understands) and in hiding games, where a toddler covers their own eyes and believes they cannot be seen. Later research, such as the policeman doll task of Martin Hughes (1975), showed that with familiar, meaningful tasks children take others' perspectives much earlier than Piaget thought. Example: Kabir, 3, holds up his drawing to show his grandmother on a video call with the paper facing himself, sure she can see it. Option c is right because failure to take another's spatial or psychological perspective is the definition of egocentrism. Exam trap: centration (a) is a close cousin, since both are preoperational limits, but centration is focusing on one feature of an object or problem, not failing to take another person's viewpoint.", "topic": "Preoperational egocentrism"}, {"q": "The dynamic process of biological growth and development that progresses naturally according to a genetic blueprint over time is called :", "options": {"a": "Learning", "b": "Socialization", "c": "Habituation", "d": "Maturation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: maturation is the natural unfolding of growth and development according to a genetic timetable, with little dependence on practice. Arnold Gesell (1928) built his maturational theory on this idea. Watching hundreds of children at Yale, he found that motor and other milestones appear in a fixed sequence and at fairly predictable ages, and he published age norms for them. In his view development is driven from within, and teaching cannot push a child past what maturation allows. Readiness is therefore the key idea: learning works best once maturation has prepared the body and brain. Arnold Gesell and Helen Thompson (1929) tested this with the method of co-twin control. One identical twin, T, was trained in stair climbing for six weeks from 46 weeks of age; her sister, C, began training only at 53 weeks, yet caught up within about two weeks. Practice added little before the body was ready. Walking, puberty and the arrival of teeth are classic examples of maturation. Learning, by contrast, is a relatively permanent change in behaviour that results from experience and practice. Example: Riya's parents worry that she is not walking at 10 months, but most babies walk between 9 and 15 months, so the doctor reassures them that her maturational timetable will bring it. Option d is right because the stem describes growth guided by a genetic blueprint, which is the definition of maturation. Exam trap: learning (a) is the usual decoy, since learning and maturation always work together, but learning depends on experience, while maturation depends on biology and time.", "topic": "Maturation"}, {"q": "A client presents with an extreme, irrational fear of being evaluated or scrutinized by others in social situations, leading to severe avoidance of public speaking and group meetings. Under the DSM-5, this profile warrants a diagnosis of :", "options": {"a": "Agoraphobia", "b": "Social Anxiety Disorder", "c": "Generalized Anxiety Disorder", "d": "Panic Disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: an intense, irrational fear of being evaluated or scrutinised by others, with avoidance of speaking and meetings, is social anxiety disorder in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013). Social anxiety disorder, formerly called social phobia, is marked fear or anxiety about one or more social situations in which the person may be watched or judged: conversations, eating or drinking in front of others, giving a speech. The core fear is negative evaluation: being humiliated, embarrassed or rejected, or offending others. The situations almost always provoke fear and are avoided or endured with intense distress, the fear is out of proportion to the real threat, and it typically lasts six months or more with clear impairment. A performance only specifier covers people whose fear is confined to speaking or performing in public. The disorder usually begins in the early teens. David M. Clark and Adrian Wells (1995) explained why it persists: anxious people turn attention inward to monitor how they come across, and use safety behaviours such as avoiding eye contact, which stop them learning that disaster does not happen. Cognitive behavioural therapy with exposure is the first-line treatment, and SSRIs also help. Example: Arjun, a capable engineer, refuses a promotion because it involves presenting to clients; he dreads that others will see his hands shake and think him incompetent. Option b is right because fear of scrutiny and negative evaluation is the defining feature of social anxiety disorder. Exam trap: agoraphobia (a) also involves avoiding crowds and public places, but the agoraphobic fears being unable to escape or get help if panic strikes, not being judged by other people.", "topic": "Social anxiety disorder"}, {"q": "The 'cognitive triad' in Beck's Cognitive Therapy for depression consists of a patient's dysfunctional, negative views regarding which three dimensions ?", "options": {"a": "The self, the world, and the future", "b": "Past achievements, current status, and biological health", "c": "The mother, the father, and the siblings", "d": "Unconscious desires, societal constraints, and ego ideals"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: Aaron Beck's cognitive triad is a depressed person's negative view of the self, the world and the future. Aaron Beck (1967), a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, set out the model in Depression: Clinical, Experimental and Theoretical Aspects. Studying depressed patients' dreams and thoughts, he expected to find anger turned inward, as Freudian theory predicted, but found instead themes of loss, defeat and worthlessness. He proposed that depression is maintained by a pattern of negative thinking with three parts. The negative view of the self says 'I am worthless, inadequate, unlovable'. The negative view of the world, or of current experience, says 'everything is against me, nothing works out'. The negative view of the future says 'it will never get better', which links to hopelessness and suicide risk. These views are kept alive by automatic thoughts, by underlying schemas formed in childhood, and by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation and selective abstraction. In cognitive therapy, Aaron Beck and colleagues (1979) taught patients to catch these thoughts, test them against evidence through collaborative empiricism and Socratic questioning, and replace them with balanced ones. Example: after failing one exam, Meera thinks 'I am stupid' (self), 'my teachers are unfair and nobody helps me' (world) and 'I will never get a job' (future): the full triad. Option a is right because self, world and future are the three domains Aaron Beck named. Exam trap: students mix up the triad with past, present and future, or with the CBT triangle of thoughts, feelings and behaviours; the cognitive triad is self, world, future.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive triad"}, {"q": "Which therapeutic modality, developed by Fritz Perls, emphasizes immediate, here-and-now awareness, integrating fragmented parts of the self, and using techniques like the 'empty chair' ?", "options": {"a": "Gestalt Therapy", "b": "Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT)", "c": "Psychoanalysis", "d": "Logotherapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the therapy Fritz Perls developed, built on here-and-now awareness, integrating disowned parts of the self and the empty chair, is Gestalt therapy. Frederick Salomon (Fritz) Perls (1893 to 1970), working with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman, set out the approach in Gestalt Therapy (Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman, 1951). It is a humanistic, experiential therapy. Its central idea is that distress comes from unfinished business (old feelings never fully expressed) and from parts of the self that have been split off and disowned. Talking about the past is discouraged; the client is asked to notice, right now, what he feels, does and avoids (\"What are you aware of now?\"). Change comes from awareness and contact, not from the therapist's interpretation. In the empty chair technique the client speaks to an imagined person, or to a disowned part of himself, placed in an empty chair, and may then switch chairs and answer back. In the related two-chair dialogue, the critical 'top dog' and the excuse-making 'underdog' argue until the client owns both sides. For example, Kabir, who never grieved his father, tells the empty chair what he could not say at the funeral and then answers as his father; the anger and sadness he had pushed away become part of him again. Option a is right because every feature in the stem (Perls, here-and-now awareness, integration of fragments, the empty chair) is a signature of Gestalt therapy. Exam trap: do not confuse Gestalt therapy with Gestalt psychology, the school of perception founded by Max Wertheimer (1912); they share only the word Gestalt (whole). Role play with chairs also appears in the psychodrama of Jacob Moreno (1921), but the empty chair as a here-and-now awareness technique is Perls's.", "topic": "Gestalt therapy and Fritz Perls"}, {"q": "A client undergoes an intense behaviour therapy procedure where they are continuously exposed to their maximum feared stimulus in a safe setting without any possibility of escape until their anxiety completely subsides. This is an example of :", "options": {"a": "Systematic desensitization", "b": "Flooding", "c": "Aversive conditioning", "d": "Modeling"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: continuous exposure to the most feared stimulus at full intensity, with escape blocked until anxiety subsides, is flooding. Flooding is an exposure-based behaviour therapy. It rests on extinction: in classical conditioning, a conditioned fear weakens when the feared cue keeps appearing without the harm it once predicted. Avoidance normally prevents this, because each escape brings relief, so escape is negatively reinforced and the person never discovers that the fear was false. Flooding removes that exit. The client is exposed, in real life (in vivo) or in imagination, to the top of the fear hierarchy at once, and stays until arousal peaks and falls on its own, often within 30 to 90 minutes. Thomas Stampfl's implosive therapy (1967) is an imaginal version that adds exaggerated, psychodynamic themes. Flooding works quickly but is very distressing, so it needs informed consent and a client able to tolerate it. For example, Meera, who fears lifts, rides a lift with her therapist floor after floor for an hour; her panic rises sharply, then fades, and the lift loses its power over her. Option b is right because the stem gives flooding's three defining marks: the maximum feared stimulus, no possibility of escape, and staying until anxiety subsides. Exam trap: systematic desensitisation, from Joseph Wolpe (1958), also treats phobias through exposure, so examiners use it as the near miss. The difference is the dose and the method: desensitisation climbs a graded hierarchy step by step while the client is relaxed (reciprocal inhibition), whereas flooding starts at the top with no relaxation and no escape.", "topic": "Flooding versus desensitisation"}, {"q": "When a client in psychoanalysis unconsciously directs the intense emotional feelings, conflicts, or expectations they originally held toward their parents onto the therapist, it is called :", "options": {"a": "Resistance", "b": "Countertransference", "c": "Transference", "d": "Catharsis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the unconscious redirection onto the therapist of feelings, conflicts and expectations originally held towards parents is transference. Sigmund Freud (1905) recognised it in the case of Dora, a young patient who broke off treatment abruptly; he realised she had been reacting to him as if he were the men who had hurt her. Transference happens because early relationship patterns live on unconsciously, and the analyst's neutral, fairly anonymous stance gives them a blank screen to land on. It can be positive (affection, idealisation, love) or negative (hostility, suspicion). Freud first saw it as an obstacle, then as the main tool of cure: when the patient's old conflict is relived with the analyst (the transference neurosis), it can be interpreted and worked through in the present. For example, Arjun, whose father was harsh and critical, becomes sure that his calm therapist is secretly disappointed in him and starts arriving late to avoid her 'judgement'; exploring this shows him how he treats every authority figure. Option c is right because the stem describes the client's feelings about parents being directed onto the therapist, which is the textbook definition of transference. Exam trap: countertransference runs the other way: it is the therapist's emotional reaction to the client. Freud (1910) named it as a blind spot arising from the therapist's own unresolved conflicts, and since Paula Heimann (1950) the term also covers all the therapist's feelings, used as clues about the client. If the stem says the client feels something towards the therapist, choose transference; if the therapist does, choose countertransference.", "topic": "Transference in psychoanalysis"}, {"q": "Which of the following describes 'psychological resilience' in clinical psychology ?", "options": {"a": "The absolute absence of any physiological stress response during trauma", "b": "The complete suppression of negative emotional memories", "c": "A permanent state of manic defense", "d": "The capacity of an individual to adapt successfully and maintain stable psychological functioning in the face of severe adversity or trauma"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: psychological resilience is the capacity to adapt successfully and keep stable psychological functioning in the face of severe adversity or trauma. Resilience research began with children who did well against the odds. Emmy Werner (1982), with Ruth Smith, followed every child born on the Hawaiian island of Kauai in 1955 for decades; about one third of the high-risk children (poverty, family conflict, birth problems) grew into competent, caring adults. Protective factors included an easy temperament, at least one stable caring adult, good problem-solving skills and support outside the family. Ann Masten (2001) called resilience 'ordinary magic' because it comes from common human resources, not rare gifts. George Bonanno (2004) showed that after loss or trauma the commonest path in adults is resilience: a short dip, then stable healthy functioning, rather than lasting disorder. Resilient people still feel pain and still show stress responses; what marks them out is recovery and adaptation. For example, Riya loses her home in a flood. She grieves, sleeps badly for a few weeks and feels afraid, but she leans on her cousins, returns to college and within months is functioning well again: that is resilience. Option d is right because it captures the two essentials of the definition: significant adversity, and positive adaptation despite it. Exam trap: resilience is not invulnerability or the absence of distress. Options that say 'absolute absence' or 'complete suppression' describe impossible states or unhealthy avoidance; resilience is bending and bouncing back, not never bending. Do not confuse it with hardiness, the commitment, control and challenge of Suzanne Kobasa (1979), a personality style that can support resilience.", "topic": "Psychological resilience"}, {"q": "A client reports experiencing sudden, un-triggered episodes of intense terror, racing heart, chest pain, and shortness of breath, accompanied by a persistent fear of having future attacks. This clinical presentation points to :", "options": {"a": "Generalized Anxiety Disorder", "b": "Panic Disorder", "c": "Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder", "d": "Specific Phobia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: recurrent, unexpected surges of terror with racing heart, chest pain and breathlessness, plus persistent fear of further attacks, is panic disorder. A panic attack is an abrupt surge of intense fear that peaks within minutes, with at least 4 of 13 symptoms in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013): palpitations, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, choking, chest pain, nausea, dizziness, chills or heat, numbness, derealisation, fear of losing control and fear of dying. Attacks occur in many disorders, so an attack alone is not a diagnosis. Panic disorder needs recurrent unexpected attacks (out of the blue, with no obvious trigger), with at least one followed by 1 month or more of persistent worry about further attacks or their consequences (\"I will have a heart attack\"), or a maladaptive change in behaviour such as avoiding exercise. Donald Klein (1964) helped separate panic from general anxiety when he found that imipramine stopped the attacks but did not ease background worry. David Clark (1986) explained attacks cognitively, as catastrophic misinterpretation of normal bodily sensations. For example, Kabir's heart suddenly pounds while he watches television; he feels he is dying and rushes to hospital, where his ECG is normal. Weeks later he still dreads the next attack. Option b is right because the stem gives both halves of the diagnosis: un-triggered attacks and persistent fear of future attacks. Exam trap: specific phobia and social anxiety disorder can also produce panic attacks, but those attacks are cued by the feared object or situation. 'Un-triggered' points to panic disorder; 'whenever he sees a dog' points to a phobia.", "topic": "Panic disorder diagnosis"}, {"q": "According to the Diathesis-Stress Model, a psychological disorder develops when :", "options": {"a": "A biological vulnerability interacts with environmental stress factors", "b": "Environmental stressors occur in the absence of any pre-existing vulnerability", "c": "Genetic mutations completely override environmental safety barriers", "d": "An individual experiences a single high-stress milestone"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the diathesis-stress model a disorder develops when a pre-existing vulnerability, often biological, interacts with environmental stress. A diathesis is a predisposition. It may be genetic (a family history of schizophrenia), biological (an over-reactive stress system) or psychological (a negative thinking style, early loss). Paul Meehl (1962) used the idea to explain schizophrenia: an inherited neural defect (schizotaxia) produces a vulnerable personality, but only certain life experiences tip it into illness. Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) generalised it as the vulnerability-stress model: everyone has a threshold, and illness appears when stress exceeds that person's threshold. The two parts interact, so neither alone is usually enough. A highly vulnerable person may break down under modest stress, while a person with little vulnerability may cope with severe stress. Twin studies fit this: when one identical twin has schizophrenia, the other develops it in only about half of cases, so genes load the gun and the environment pulls the trigger. For example, Arjun and Kabir both lose their jobs in the same month. Arjun, whose mother and grandmother had recurrent depression, falls into a major depressive episode; Kabir, with no such loading, is upset but recovers. Option a is right because it states the model's core: vulnerability and stress acting together. Exam trap: options that make one factor sufficient on its own (stress alone, genes alone, a single life event) describe one-dimensional models, the very views the diathesis-stress model was built to replace. Note too that the diathesis need not be biological; a cognitive vulnerability counts as well.", "topic": "Diathesis-stress model"}, {"q": "A psychologist coordinates an immediate intervention for a community the following a major natural disaster to stabilize acute trauma symptoms and provide immediate coping resources. This falls under :", "options": {"a": "Traditional psychoanalysis", "b": "Long-term cognitive restructuring", "c": "Crisis intervention", "d": "Group dynamic debriefing"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: an immediate, short-term intervention after a disaster to stabilise acute trauma reactions and supply coping resources is crisis intervention. A crisis, as Gerald Caplan (1964) defined it, arises when a person meets an obstacle to important life goals that cannot, for the time being, be overcome by his usual problem-solving, and a period of disorganisation follows. Crises usually resolve within about 4 to 6 weeks, for better or worse, so early help can tip the outcome. The approach grew from Erich Lindemann's (1944) study of the bereaved after the Cocoanut Grove nightclub fire in Boston, which suggested that timely brief help could prevent lasting grief reactions. Crisis intervention is brief, active and directive. It works in the here and now: ensuring safety, calming acute distress, meeting practical needs, restoring problem-solving and linking people to support and services. Its goal is a return to the pre-crisis level of functioning, not a change of personality. After disasters it is often delivered as psychological first aid. For example, after a cyclone in Odisha, a psychologist named Meera sets up relief-camp teams that check who is at risk, teach simple grounding and breathing, reunite families and refer the most distressed people for care. Option c is right because the stem stresses immediacy, a community after a disaster, stabilising acute symptoms and providing coping resources, all defining features of crisis intervention. Exam trap: one-session group 'debriefing' after trauma sounds similar, but it is a single technique, not the umbrella approach, and the review by Suzanna Rose and colleagues (2002) found that single-session debriefing does not prevent PTSD and may even slow recovery.", "topic": "Crisis intervention after disaster"}, {"q": "What is the primary diagnostic difference between a manic episode and a hypomanic episode under the DSM-5 criteria ?", "options": {"a": "Manic episodes do not involve elevated mood", "b": "Hypomanic episodes are longer in duration than manic episodes", "c": "Manic episodes cause marked impairment in social or occupational functioning or require hospitalization; hypomanic episodes do not", "d": "Hypomanic episodes always feature flagrant delusions and hallucinations"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: under DSM-5 the main difference is severity: a manic episode causes marked impairment in social or occupational functioning, needs hospitalisation or has psychotic features, whereas a hypomanic episode does not. DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) gives both episodes the same core: a distinct period of abnormally elevated, expansive or irritable mood with persistently increased activity or energy, plus at least 3 of 7 symptoms (4 if the mood is only irritable). The symptoms are inflated self-esteem or grandiosity, decreased need for sleep, pressured speech, flight of ideas or racing thoughts, distractibility, increased goal-directed activity or agitation, and risky pleasure-seeking. The differences are duration and severity. Mania lasts at least 1 week (any duration if hospitalised); hypomania at least 4 consecutive days. In hypomania the change is clear and observable to others, but not severe enough to cause marked impairment or need hospitalisation, and there are no psychotic features; if any of these appear, the episode is by definition manic. The line matters because one manic episode makes bipolar I disorder, while bipolar II needs hypomania plus a major depressive episode. For example, Riya sleeps 4 hours a night for five days, talks fast and starts three projects, yet keeps up at work: hypomania. Her cousin Meera stops sleeping for a week, spends her savings, believes God has chosen her and is admitted: mania. Option c is right because impairment, hospitalisation and psychosis are the criteria DSM-5 uses to separate the two. Exam trap: do not reverse the durations. Mania needs the longer minimum (7 days) and hypomania the shorter (4 days), and psychotic features always mean mania, never hypomania.", "topic": "Mania versus hypomania"}, {"q": "The mental health diagnostic system published by the World Health Organization (WHO) that covers both physical and mental disorders globally is known as the :", "options": {"a": "DSM-5", "b": "ICD-11", "c": "MMPI-2", "d": "PDM-2"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the World Health Organization's classification that covers both physical and mental disorders worldwide is ICD-11. The International Classification of Diseases (ICD) began as a list of causes of death drawn up by Jacques Bertillon (1893), and WHO took it over in 1948. ICD-6 (1948) was the first edition to include mental disorders. ICD-10 (endorsed 1990, published 1992) placed them in Chapter V (F codes), still common in Indian case files. ICD-11 was adopted by the World Health Assembly in May 2019 and came into effect on 1 January 2022. It covers every kind of health condition, from infections and cancers to injuries, with mental, behavioural and neurodevelopmental disorders in Chapter 6 (codes such as 6A20 for schizophrenia). Its mental disorders come with Clinical Descriptions and Diagnostic Requirements, which are more flexible than DSM checklists, and it is free to use and available in many languages, which suits low-resource countries. For example, when Dr Meera records a patient's diabetes and depression in a government hospital in Delhi, both diagnoses are coded from the same ICD system. Option b is right because ICD is the only option published by WHO and the only one covering physical as well as mental disorders. Exam trap: DSM-5 is the best-known diagnostic manual, so examiners offer it first, but it is published by the American Psychiatric Association (2013; text revision DSM-5-TR, 2022) and covers mental disorders only. Remember the pairing: WHO and all diseases means ICD; APA and mental disorders only means DSM.", "topic": "ICD-11 and WHO"}, {"q": "Who is recognized as the historical founder of psychoanalysis, introducing concepts such as unconscious mind, ego, and defense mechanisms ?", "options": {"a": "Carl Jung", "b": "Sigmund Freud", "c": "Alfred Adler", "d": "B. F. Skinner"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Sigmund Freud is recognised as the founder of psychoanalysis, the theory and therapy built on the unconscious mind, the ego and defence mechanisms. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) was a Viennese neurologist. Working with Josef Breuer, he published Studies on Hysteria (1895), based partly on Breuer's patient 'Anna O.', whose symptoms eased when she talked through forgotten memories (the 'talking cure'). Freud first used the word psychoanalysis in 1896 and replaced hypnosis with free association. In The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) he argued that much of mental life is unconscious and that dreams are the 'royal road' to it. In The Ego and the Id (1923) he divided personality into the id (instinctual drives, pleasure principle), the ego (reality principle, the mediator) and the superego (conscience). Defence mechanisms, such as repression and projection, are the ego's unconscious ways of keeping anxiety-provoking impulses out of awareness. For example, when Arjun keeps 'forgetting' his dental appointment, a Freudian would see repression of anxiety at work. Option b is right because Freud founded psychoanalysis and introduced all three concepts named in the stem. Exam trap: his daughter Anna Freud wrote The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936), which listed and systematised the defences, so examiners sometimes credit her; but the idea of defence began with Sigmund Freud's 1894 paper on the neuro-psychoses of defence. Carl Jung (1913) and Alfred Adler (1911) were early colleagues who broke away to found their own schools, so neither is the founder.", "topic": "Freud, founder of psychoanalysis"}, {"q": "The psychological process of dealing with internal or external demands that are appraised as taxing or exceeding personal resources is known as :", "options": {"a": "Stress appraisal", "b": "Homeostasis", "c": "Resilience", "d": "Coping"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the process of managing internal or external demands appraised as taxing or exceeding one's resources is coping. The stem is almost word for word the definition given by Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984): coping is 'constantly changing cognitive and behavioural efforts to manage specific external and/or internal demands that are appraised as taxing or exceeding the resources of the person'. It belongs to their transactional model of stress, in which stress is not just an event but a relationship between person and environment. First comes primary appraisal (is this harmful, threatening or a challenge?), then secondary appraisal (what can I do about it?), and then coping, the actual effort. Lazarus and Folkman divided coping into problem-focused coping (changing the situation: making a plan, seeking information) and emotion-focused coping (managing the feelings: reappraising, seeking comfort, distraction). Problem-focused coping tends to help when the situation can be changed; emotion-focused coping helps when it cannot. Coping is a process, not a fixed trait, and it is defined by effort, not by success. For example, Kabir learns his board exam is in two weeks and he is behind. He sees a threat (primary appraisal), decides he can still revise two chapters a day (secondary appraisal), and then draws up a timetable and calls a friend when he panics: both kinds of coping. Option d is right because coping is the name for the efforts that follow when demands are appraised as exceeding resources. Exam trap: the stem contains the word 'appraised', which tempts students towards 'stress appraisal'. But appraisal is the judgement that comes before coping; coping is what you then do about the demand.", "topic": "Coping (Lazarus and Folkman)"}, {"q": "Which specific source of diagnostic unreliability is primarily minimized by using structured clinical interview for DSM-5 ?", "options": {"a": "Subject variance", "b": "Temporal variance", "c": "Criterion variance", "d": "Information variance"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: a structured clinical interview such as the SCID mainly reduces information variance, the unreliability that arises when clinicians gather different information about the same patient. Robert Spitzer, Jean Endicott and Eli Robins (1975) named five sources of disagreement between diagnosticians. Subject variance: the patient really has different conditions at different times (intoxicated on one visit, sober on the next). Occasion variance: the patient is seen in different stages of the same illness. Information variance: clinicians have different information, because they ask different questions, cover different areas or use different informants. Observation variance: given the same information, clinicians notice or interpret it differently. Criterion variance: clinicians use different rules for turning the same data into a diagnosis. An earlier study by Clyde Ward, Aaron Beck and colleagues (1962) found that criterion variance caused most disagreements. The two problems got different fixes. Explicit operational criteria (the Feighner criteria of John Feighner and colleagues in 1972, then DSM-III in 1980) reduced criterion variance. Structured interviews such as the SCID (Robert Spitzer and colleagues, from the 1980s) reduced information variance by making every clinician cover the same questions in the same order, so no area is skipped. For example, Dr Riya and Dr Kabir see the same patient; Riya asks about past manic spells and Kabir does not, so they disagree. With the SCID, both must work through the mania module. Option d is right because standardising what is asked is exactly what a structured interview does. Exam trap: criterion variance is the near miss. It is cured mainly by explicit diagnostic criteria, the job of the DSM itself; the SCID applies those criteria, but its own contribution is standardising the information gathered.", "topic": "Sources of diagnostic unreliability"}, {"q": "In the context of Rorschach inkblot test, what was the primary objective of John Exner's Comprehensive system ?", "options": {"a": "Eliminating projection", "b": "Shortening administration", "c": "Enhancing face validity", "d": "Standardizing scoring"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the main aim of John Exner's Comprehensive System was to standardise the administration and scoring of the Rorschach. Hermann Rorschach (1921) published his ten inkblots but died a year later without a full scoring system. In the United States five rival systems grew up from the 1930s, led by Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Marguerite Hertz, Zygmunt Piotrowski and David Rapaport, each with its own codes and instructions. As a result, research done with one system could not be compared with research done with another, and reliability was poor. John E. Exner (1974) combined the elements of each system that had the best empirical support into one Comprehensive System. It fixed the seating, instructions and inquiry, required at least 14 responses for a valid record, defined each code (location, determinants, form quality, content, special scores) and gathered the codes into a Structural Summary of ratios and indices compared against norms. Examples are Lambda, the Experience Balance (EB) and constellations such as the depression index (DEPI) and the suicide constellation (S-CON). After Exner's death, Gregory Meyer and colleagues built the Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS, 2011) with the same aim. For example, if Dr Meera in Bengaluru and a psychologist in Chicago both score Arjun's protocol with the Comprehensive System, they should reach the same codes and compare them with the same norms. Option d is right because Exner's purpose was a single, standardised, empirically based way to give and score the test. Exam trap: standardising the Rorschach did not change its nature. It is still a projective, performance-based test, so 'eliminating projection' is wrong; Exner stressed the perceptual problem-solving side of responses but did not remove projection.", "topic": "Exner's Comprehensive System"}, {"q": "During behavioural observation, the phenomenon where a individual's behaviour changes just because they are being watched is known as :", "options": {"a": "Expectancy effect", "b": "Habituation", "c": "Observer drift", "d": "Reactivity"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: when a person's behaviour changes simply because he knows he is being watched, the effect is called reactivity. In behavioural assessment, a clinician counts and records what a person actually does: in natural settings (home, school, ward), in analogue settings (a simulated situation in the clinic) or through self-monitoring. Each method assumes the record shows typical behaviour, and reactivity threatens that assumption. A child may behave better when a stranger sits in the classroom, or a patient may smoke less on the days he records each cigarette. Reactivity usually fades as the person gets used to the observer, so clinicians allow a settling-in period, observe unobtrusively (one-way mirrors, video, or people already present such as teachers) or use records that need no observer. In research the same problem appears as the Hawthorne effect, named after the Western Electric Hawthorne studies (1924 to 1932) associated with Elton Mayo (1933), where output changed when workers knew they were being studied. Reactivity can even be used on purpose in therapy, since recording a habit often reduces it. For example, Arjun, aged 8, is referred for classroom disruption. On the first two days, with the psychologist sitting at the back, he sits quietly; by the fourth day, used to her presence, he is out of his seat again. Option d is right because reactivity is precisely the change in behaviour caused by the act of observation itself. Exam trap: observer drift sounds similar but concerns the observer, not the observed: over time, observers gradually change how they apply the behaviour definitions, so their records become less accurate. It is corrected by retraining and by checking agreement between observers.", "topic": "Reactivity in observation"}, {"q": "Which component of the mental status exam specifically assesses the 'process' of thought rather than the 'content' of thought ?", "options": {"a": "Linearity", "b": "Phobias", "c": "Suicidality", "d": "Obsessions"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: linearity describes how ideas connect, so it belongs to thought process (form of thought), whereas phobias, suicidality and obsessions are all thought content. In the mental status examination (MSE), the clinician separates how a person thinks from what he thinks about. Thought process, also called form of thought, asks whether ideas follow one another logically towards a goal. Normal thinking is described as linear, logical and goal-directed. Disordered process includes circumstantiality (long detours but finally reaching the point), tangentiality (drifting away and never returning), flight of ideas (rapid jumps on chance links, typical of mania), loosening of associations or derailment (ideas without logical links, which Eugen Bleuler (1911) made central to schizophrenia), thought blocking, perseveration and word salad. Thought content covers themes and beliefs: delusions, overvalued ideas, obsessions, phobias, preoccupations, and suicidal or homicidal ideas. The clinician rates process by listening to the structure of speech, and content by asking what occupies the person's mind. For example, Meera answers 'How did you come here today?' with a straight, connected account: her process is linear and goal-directed. Yet she says she cannot stop thinking her hands are contaminated (an obsession) and is terrified of lifts (a phobia): those go under content. Option a is right because linearity describes the flow and connection of ideas, the defining concern of thought process. Exam trap: obsessions feel like a 'process' because they repeat, but the MSE files them under content, since they describe what the person thinks. Likewise suicidal ideation is content, even though it is also assessed separately in risk assessment.", "topic": "MSE thought process versus content"}, {"q": "There are two girls Rita and Gita who are both five years old. One has an IQ of 140 and the other has an IQ of 80. What are their mental ages ?", "options": {"a": "7 and 4 respectively", "b": "6 and 5 respectively", "c": "8 and 5 respectively", "d": "6 and 4 respectively"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: with a chronological age of 5, an IQ of 140 gives a mental age of 7 and an IQ of 80 gives a mental age of 4. This uses the ratio IQ. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905, revised 1908) grouped test items by the age at which typical children pass them, so a child's mental age (MA) is the age level of the items he or she passes. William Stern (1912) divided mental age by chronological age (CA), and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied the result by 100 in the Stanford-Binet: IQ = (MA ÷ CA) × 100. To find mental age, rearrange it: MA = (IQ × CA) ÷ 100. If Rita is the girl with IQ 140, her mental age is 140 × 5 ÷ 100 = 7, so she performs like an average seven-year-old. If Gita has IQ 80, her mental age is 80 × 5 ÷ 100 = 4, like an average four-year-old. A quick sense check: an IQ above 100 always means MA above CA, and an IQ below 100 always means MA below CA. The ratio IQ works for children, whose abilities grow each year, but fails for adults, whose mental age stops rising in the late teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15). Option a is right because 7 and 4, in the order the IQs are given (140, then 80), is the only pair that satisfies the formula. Exam trap: the usual slip is to invert the formula and divide CA by MA, or to add or subtract years instead of multiplying. Always check by working back: 7 ÷ 5 × 100 = 140 and 4 ÷ 5 × 100 = 80.", "topic": "Ratio IQ and mental age"}, {"q": "Arrange the prominent personalities in clinical psychology in ascending order :", "options": {"a": "St. Augustine - Plato - Johann Weyer - William Harvey", "b": "Plato - St. Augustine - William Harvey - Johann Weyer", "c": "Plato - St. Augustine - Johann Weyer - William Harvey", "d": "Plato - Johann Weyer - St. Augustine - William Harvey"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: in chronological (ascending) order the four are Plato, St. Augustine, Johann Weyer and William Harvey. The question asks you to place figures from the early history of ideas about mental illness on a timeline. Plato (about 427 to 347 BC), the Greek philosopher, saw mental disturbance partly as a loss of harmony in the soul, held that the disturbed should not be judged like others for their acts, and urged in the Laws that families care for them at home. St. Augustine of Hippo (AD 354 to 430) wrote the Confessions (about AD 397 to 400), a searching examination of his own feelings, conflicts and anguish that historians treat as a forerunner of introspection and even of psychoanalytic self-analysis. Johann Weyer (1515 to 1588), a Rhineland physician, argued in De praestigiis daemonum (1563) that many women accused of witchcraft were mentally ill and needed treatment, not burning; Gregory Zilboorg (1941) called him the founder of modern psychiatry. William Harvey (1578 to 1657), the English physician who described the circulation of the blood in De motu cordis (1628), also noted that emotions such as fear and anger disturb the heart, an early psychosomatic idea. A memory hook: Greek philosophy (BC), a Church Father of the late Roman Empire, the Renaissance protest against witch hunts (sixteenth century), then the scientific revolution (seventeenth century). For example, Kabir makes a timeline card: fourth century BC, about AD 400, 1563, 1628. Option c is right because it is the only sequence that follows these dates. Exam trap: Weyer and Harvey were both early modern physicians, so options swap them; but Weyer's book (1563) appeared before Harvey was even born (1578).", "topic": "History of abnormal psychology"}]};
window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["gurugram-2026-pdcp"] = {"id": "gurugram-2026-pdcp", "title": "Gurugram 2026 PDCP", "fullTitle": "Gurugram University 2026, Professional Diploma in Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper, official key)", "sub": "60 real questions · official answer key · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "A researcher divides a city into geographical zones and randomly selects three zones. Every household in the selected zones is included in the study. Which sampling technique is being used ?", "options": {"a": "Stratified sampling", "b": "Cluster sampling", "c": "Systematic sampling", "d": "Quota sampling"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: dividing the city into zones, randomly picking three zones and then including every household inside them is cluster sampling. Cluster sampling is a probability method in which the population is first split into naturally occurring groups, called clusters, such as zones, villages, schools or hospital wards. The researcher then randomly selects some whole clusters and studies them, instead of picking individuals from a full list of the population. When every member of each chosen cluster is included, as here, it is one-stage cluster sampling; when individuals are sampled again inside the chosen clusters, it becomes two-stage or multistage sampling, the method used in large surveys such as India's National Family Health Survey. Its main advantage is cost and convenience: no list of every household in the city is needed, only a list of zones, and field workers travel to just a few areas. Its weakness is precision. Households in one zone tend to resemble each other in income, housing and community, so a cluster sample usually carries a larger sampling error than a simple random sample of the same size. Example: Meera wants to survey family stress in Pune. She splits the city into 20 zones, draws three by lottery and interviews every household in those three. She never needed a city-wide household list. Option b is right because the unit chosen at random is the whole zone (the cluster), and everyone inside the selected clusters is studied. Exam trap: students confuse clusters with strata. In stratified sampling the population is also divided into groups, but the researcher samples from every group; in cluster sampling only some groups are chosen, and those are taken whole. Strata should be internally similar and different from one another, while each cluster should ideally be a small mixed copy of the whole population.", "topic": "Cluster sampling"}, {"q": "A psychologist investigates whether childhood trauma is associated with adult anxiety by studying individuals who already experienced trauma. This is an example of :", "options": {"a": "Experimental research", "b": "Ex post facto research", "c": "Longitudinal research", "d": "Correlational experiment"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: studying people who have already experienced childhood trauma, to see whether it is linked to adult anxiety, is ex post facto research. Ex post facto is Latin for 'after the fact'. Fred N. Kerlinger (1964) described it as research in which the independent variable has already occurred, or cannot ethically or practically be manipulated, so the researcher starts with pre-existing groups or with the outcome and looks back for possible causes. It is also called causal-comparative research. Here trauma is the independent variable. No psychologist could ever assign children to experience trauma, so the only route is to compare adults who were traumatised with adults who were not, and measure their anxiety. Example: Arjun, a researcher in Chennai, recruits 60 adults with documented childhood abuse and 60 without, and gives both groups an anxiety inventory. If the first group scores higher, Arjun has found an association, but he cannot be sure that trauma caused it, because the groups may also differ in poverty, parental mental illness or genes. Option b is right because the presumed cause happened before the study began and was not manipulated by the researcher; the researcher simply selected people who already differed on it. The two main weaknesses of the design are self-selection (people are not randomly placed in the groups) and the post hoc fallacy (assuming that whatever came first must be the cause). Its strength is that it lets researchers study causes that could never be ethically manipulated, such as abuse, bereavement, brain injury or smoking. Exam trap: ex post facto studies look like experiments because they compare groups, but there is no manipulation and no random assignment, so they cannot establish cause and effect. Ex post facto research is non-experimental.", "topic": "Ex post facto research"}, {"q": "Rejecting a true null hypothesis is known as :", "options": {"a": "Type II error", "b": "Sampling error", "c": "Type I error", "d": "Measurement error"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true is a Type I error, a false positive. Every significance test starts from the null hypothesis (H0), which states that there is no effect or no difference. After testing, the researcher either rejects H0 or fails to reject it. Because the decision rests on a sample, two kinds of mistake are possible. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1928, 1933) framed testing as a decision with fixed error rates and gave us the two error types. A Type I error means claiming an effect that does not exist. Its probability is alpha, the significance level chosen in advance, usually .05; so at alpha .05 a true null will be wrongly rejected about 5 times in 100. A Type II error is the opposite: failing to reject a null hypothesis that is false, so a real effect is missed. Its probability is beta, and the power of a test is 1 minus beta. Example: Kabir tests a new relaxation app on anxious students. In truth the app does nothing, but by chance his sample improves, p comes out at .03, and he announces that the app works. Kabir has made a Type I error. Lowering alpha to .01 reduces Type I errors, but with the same sample size it raises the risk of Type II errors; a larger sample reduces both. Option c is right because 'rejecting a true null' is the textbook definition of a Type I error. Exam trap: the two errors are easy to swap. Remember 'Type I is a false alarm (reject a true H0); Type II is a miss (keep a false H0)'. Examiners also offer 'accepting a false null' as a description of Type I, which is the reverse.", "topic": "Type I error"}, {"q": "The asymptotic tails of a normal distribution indicate that :", "options": {"a": "The curve never touches the X-axis", "b": "The curve intersects the X-axis at two points", "c": "The distribution is positively skewed", "d": "Scores cannot be negative"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: asymptotic tails mean that the normal curve comes closer and closer to the X-axis (the baseline) as it moves away from the mean, but never actually touches it. An asymptote is a line that a curve approaches without ever meeting. The normal probability curve, first derived by Abraham de Moivre (1733) and later tied to errors of observation by Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809), is bell-shaped, symmetrical and unimodal, with the mean, median and mode at the same central point. In theory it stretches from minus infinity to plus infinity. However far you move from the mean, a tiny probability always remains, so the curve stays just above the baseline. About 68.26 percent of cases lie within 1 standard deviation (SD) of the mean, 95.44 percent within 2 SD and 99.74 percent within 3 SD, which leaves 0.26 percent beyond 3 SD on either side: small, but not zero. Example: Riya's class takes an IQ test with a mean of 100 and an SD of 15. An IQ of 160, 4 SD above the mean, is extremely rare, but the curve says it is not impossible; the tail keeps going. The total area under the curve is 1 (100 percent), so areas can be read directly as probabilities. Option a is right because 'asymptotic' means exactly this: the tails approach the X-axis indefinitely without touching or crossing it. Exam trap: real data sets have a lowest and a highest score, so a drawn histogram does end, but the theoretical normal curve does not. Do not confuse asymptotic tails with skewness either: the normal curve has tails of equal length on both sides, and both are asymptotic.", "topic": "Normal curve asymptotic tails"}, {"q": "A distribution with a longer tail on the right side is described as :", "options": {"a": "Negatively skewed", "b": "Positively skewed", "c": "Symmetrical", "d": "Mesokurtic"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a distribution whose longer tail stretches to the right, towards the high scores, is positively skewed. Skewness describes how far a distribution departs from symmetry, and the direction of skew is named after the tail, not the hump. In a positively (right) skewed distribution most scores pile up at the low end, and a few very high scores drag the tail out to the right. Those extreme scores pull the mean upwards, so the usual order is mean greater than median greater than mode. In a negatively (left) skewed distribution the tail points towards the low scores and the order reverses: mode greater than median greater than mean. Karl Pearson (1895) gave a simple index of skewness: 3 times (mean minus median), divided by the standard deviation. A positive value means right skew, a negative value left skew. Example: Arjun records monthly family income in a Delhi neighbourhood. Most families earn modest amounts, but two business owners earn many times more. The graph rises steeply on the left and trails off to the right: a positively skewed distribution, in which the median describes a typical family better than the mean. Reaction times and the number of hospital visits per year are other common right-skewed variables, and a very hard test, on which most students score low, is also positively skewed. Option b is right because the side of the longer tail names the skew, and here it is the right, or positive, side. Exam trap: students look at where the scores are bunched (on the left) and call the distribution 'left skewed'. The bunch is on the left precisely because the tail runs to the right. A very easy test gives the opposite pattern, negative skew.", "topic": "Positive skewness"}, {"q": "Which of the following represents an interval level of measurement ?", "options": {"a": "Gender", "b": "Socio-economic status", "c": "Temperature measured in Celsius", "d": "Rank in a competition"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: temperature measured in degrees Celsius is the classic example of an interval scale. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, remembered as NOIR: nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio. A nominal scale only names categories (gender, blood group). An ordinal scale puts things in order, but the gaps between ranks need not be equal (first, second and third in a race). An interval scale has order and equal distances between adjacent values, but its zero point is arbitrary, not a true absence of the quantity. A ratio scale adds a true zero (height, weight, reaction time, temperature in Kelvin). Celsius fits interval perfectly. The difference between 10 and 20 degrees equals the difference between 30 and 40 degrees, so adding and subtracting make sense. But 0 degrees Celsius is simply the freezing point of water, not 'no heat', so ratios do not make sense: 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20 degrees. Example: Meera notes that Delhi was at 40 degrees and Shimla at 20 degrees. She can say that Delhi was 20 degrees warmer, but not that it was twice as hot. In psychology, IQ scores and summed Likert-scale scores are usually treated as interval data, which is why means, standard deviations and t-tests are used with them. Option c is right because Celsius has equal units but an arbitrary zero, the defining pair of features of interval measurement. Exam trap: examiners offer 'rank' or 'socio-economic status' because they involve numbers or order. Order alone makes a scale ordinal; equal intervals are what make it interval, and a true zero is what would make it ratio.", "topic": "Interval scale of measurement"}, {"q": "A researcher measures the stress levels of the same participants before and after a mindfulness program. Which t-test is appropriate ?", "options": {"a": "Independent samples t-test", "b": "One-sample t-test", "c": "Matched (Paired) samples t-test", "d": "Z-test"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: when the same participants are measured twice, before and after a programme, the right test is the matched (paired, related or dependent) samples t-test. The t-test was worked out by William Sealy Gosset (1908), a chemist at the Guinness brewery who published under the pen name 'Student', to compare means from small samples. It has three forms. The one-sample t-test compares one sample mean with a known or hypothesised value. The independent samples t-test compares the means of two separate groups of people. The paired samples t-test compares two sets of scores that come in linked pairs: the same person tested twice, or two people deliberately matched (twins, or patients matched on age and severity). The paired test works on each person's difference score (after minus before) and asks whether the mean difference is significantly different from zero, with degrees of freedom equal to the number of pairs minus 1. Because each person serves as his or her own control, stable individual differences (some people are always more stressed than others) are removed, so the paired test is usually more powerful than an independent test on the same number of scores. Example: Kabir runs an eight-week mindfulness course for 25 nurses in Bengaluru and measures each nurse's stress before and after. He computes 25 difference scores and tests their mean with 24 degrees of freedom. Option c is right because the before and after scores belong to the same people, so the samples are related, not independent. Exam trap: two sets of scores tempt students to pick the independent samples test. The deciding question is not 'how many sets of scores?' but 'do they come from the same or matched people?'. If yes, use the paired test; its non-parametric alternative is the signed-rank test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945).", "topic": "Paired samples t-test"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a primary method of data collection ?", "options": {"a": "Observation", "b": "Interview", "c": "Questionnaire", "d": "Standard deviation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: standard deviation is not a method of data collection at all; it is a descriptive statistic used after the data have been collected. Primary data are gathered first-hand by the researcher for the present study. The main primary methods are observation (watching and recording behaviour as it happens, in natural or controlled settings), the interview (face-to-face or telephone questioning, which may be structured, semi-structured or unstructured), the questionnaire (a written set of questions that respondents answer on their own, on paper or online) and psychological tests. Secondary data, by contrast, were collected by someone else for another purpose and are re-used: census records, hospital files, archives and published surveys. The standard deviation (SD), a term coined by Karl Pearson (1894), measures how widely scores spread around the mean; it is the square root of the average squared deviation from the mean. It is a tool of analysis, used to summarise data that observation, interviews or questionnaires have already produced. Example: Riya studies screen time among teenagers in Jaipur. She observes students in the school canteen, interviews ten parents and gives a questionnaire to 200 students. Those three are her methods of data collection. Later she calculates that mean screen time is 4 hours a day with an SD of 1.5 hours; the SD describes her data but collected none of it. Option d is right because observation, interview and questionnaire are all primary data-collection methods, while standard deviation is a measure of variability. Exam trap: in 'not' questions, examiners often slip in an item from a different stage of research, here analysis rather than collection. Ask which option belongs to a different category, not which method seems weakest.", "topic": "Primary data collection methods"}, {"q": "A histogram is most appropriate for displaying :", "options": {"a": "Nominal data", "b": "Continuous frequency distribution", "c": "Correlation coefficients", "d": "Factor loadings"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a histogram is the graph designed for a continuous frequency distribution, such as test scores grouped into class intervals. A frequency distribution shows how often each score, or each class interval of scores, occurs. A histogram displays it as a set of adjoining rectangles: the class intervals run along the X-axis, frequency runs up the Y-axis, and the area of each bar is proportional to the frequency of its interval. The term was introduced by Karl Pearson (1891). The bars touch because the variable is continuous: one interval ends exactly where the next begins, so there is no gap on the scale. If you join the midpoints of the bar tops and bring the line down to the baseline at both ends, you get a frequency polygon, a related graph for the same kind of data. The shape of a histogram also shows at a glance whether a distribution is symmetrical, skewed or bimodal. Example: Arjun gives an anxiety scale to 120 college students in Kolkata and groups the scores into intervals of 10 to 19, 20 to 29, 30 to 39 and so on. He plots a histogram with touching bars and sees that most students cluster in the middle, with a long tail to the right. Option b is right because the histogram is built for continuous data grouped into intervals, which is exactly a continuous frequency distribution. Exam trap: the bar graph looks similar, but it is meant for nominal or categorical data (religion, diagnosis, gender), and its bars are separated by gaps to show that the categories are distinct. Touching bars mean a continuous scale; separated bars mean categories.", "topic": "Histogram"}, {"q": "In criterion-related validity, the measure against which a test is compared is called the :", "options": {"a": "Internal criterion", "b": "External criterion", "c": "Parallel criterion", "d": "Construct criterion"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: in criterion-related validity the test is compared with an external criterion, an independent measure of the behaviour the test is meant to predict or reflect. Validity asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure. Criterion-related validity answers by correlating test scores with a criterion: a direct, outside measure of the same quality, such as job performance ratings, examination marks, a psychiatric diagnosis or later success in training. The resulting correlation is called the validity coefficient. There are two kinds. In concurrent validity the criterion is measured at about the same time as the test, as when a new depression scale is checked against a psychiatrist's diagnosis made the same week. In predictive validity the criterion comes later, as when an entrance test is checked against first-year university marks. The criterion must be external because a test cannot validate itself: agreement among its own items shows only consistency. A good criterion is relevant, reliable, free from bias and available at reasonable cost. If the people rating the criterion already know the test scores, the correlation is inflated artificially, a flaw called criterion contamination. Example: Kabir designs a clerical aptitude test for a bank in Mumbai. To check its predictive validity he correlates applicants' test scores with their supervisors' ratings after six months on the job. Those supervisor ratings are the external criterion. Option b is right because the yardstick lies outside the test itself and measures the real-world behaviour of interest. Exam trap: 'internal criterion' is a real term, but it belongs to item analysis and internal consistency, where each item is checked against the total score of the same test. That shows homogeneity, not criterion-related validity.", "topic": "Criterion-related validity"}, {"q": "Which of the following correlation coefficients indicates the strongest linear relationship, irrespective of direction ?", "options": {"a": "+ 0.45", "b": "-0.92", "c": "+ 0.76", "d": "-0.58"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a correlation of -0.92 shows the strongest linear relationship of the four, because strength depends on the size of the coefficient, not its sign. The Pearson product-moment correlation coefficient, r, developed by Karl Pearson (1896), runs from -1.00 to +1.00. Its sign gives the direction: positive means the two variables rise together, negative means one rises as the other falls. Its absolute value (the number with the sign ignored) gives the strength: 0 means no linear relationship, and 1 means that all the points fall exactly on a straight line. So rank the options by absolute value: 0.92 is larger than 0.76, 0.58 and 0.45. Squaring r gives the coefficient of determination, the proportion of variance the two variables share. -0.92 squared is about 0.85, so about 85 percent of the variation in one variable is accounted for by the other, against about 58 percent for +0.76 and only 20 percent for +0.45. Example: Meera, a counsellor in Lucknow, finds that among her clients the hours of sleep per night correlate -0.92 with scores on a fatigue scale. As sleep goes up, fatigue goes down, and very consistently. That is a stronger relationship than a +0.76 correlation between study hours and marks. Option b is right because, irrespective of direction, -0.92 is the closest of the four to 1 in absolute value. Exam trap: many students read the negative sign as 'weaker' or 'less', and choose the largest positive number (+0.76). The sign only tells direction; -0.92 is exactly as strong as +0.92. Remember also that r measures only linear relationships: a strong curved relationship can give an r near zero.", "topic": "Strength of correlation"}, {"q": "The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula is primarily used to estimate :", "options": {"a": "Item validity", "b": "Reliability after changing test length", "c": "Standard error", "d": "Predictive validity"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula estimates how the reliability of a test changes when the test is made longer or shorter. Charles Spearman (1910) and William Brown (1910) published it independently in the same issue of the British Journal of Psychology. Its logic is that a longer test samples behaviour more fully, so random errors tend to cancel out and reliability rises, provided the added items are of the same quality as the old ones. The general formula is: new reliability = nr divided by (1 + (n minus 1)r), where r is the present reliability and n is the factor by which the length changes (2 for doubling, 0.5 for halving). Its commonest use is in split-half reliability. The test is split into two halves, often odd and even items, and the halves are correlated. That half-test correlation underestimates the reliability of the full test, so the formula with n = 2 corrects it: full reliability = 2r divided by (1 + r). Example: Arjun splits his 40-item self-esteem scale into odd and even items and finds that the halves correlate 0.60. Corrected, the full-test reliability is 1.20 divided by 1.60, which is 0.75. If he doubled the scale to 80 similar items, the same formula predicts a reliability of about 0.86. It can also be run backwards to find how many items are needed to reach a target reliability. Option b is right because the 'prophecy' is exactly a prediction of reliability after the length of the test is changed. Exam trap: the formula concerns reliability, not validity, item quality or the standard error. Students also confuse it with the formula 20 of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) and the alpha of Lee Cronbach (1951), which estimate internal consistency directly without splitting.", "topic": "Spearman-Brown prophecy formula"}, {"q": "The nomothetic approach emphasizes :", "options": {"a": "Individual uniqueness", "b": "Universal laws governing behaviour", "c": "Clinical case studies", "d": "Projective assessment"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the nomothetic approach looks for universal or general laws of behaviour that hold for people in general. The terms come from the German philosopher Wilhelm Windelband (1894), who divided sciences into nomothetic ones (from the Greek nomos, law), which seek general laws, and idiographic ones (from idios, one's own), which seek to understand a single, unique case. Gordon Allport (1937) brought the pair into personality psychology. A nomothetic researcher studies many people on the same dimensions, using large samples, standardised tests and statistics, and places each person against group norms. The factor-analytic trait work of Raymond Cattell (1946), the dimensions of Hans Eysenck (1947) and the Big Five are classic nomothetic research, as are experiments that seek laws of learning and memory true of everyone. The idiographic approach instead studies one person in depth, through case studies, diaries, letters and open interviews, to grasp his or her unique pattern. Allport preferred it, famously analysing 301 letters written by one woman in Letters from Jenny (1965). Example: Kabir wants to know whether extraversion predicts the size of friendship networks among Indian college students, so he tests 500 students on a standard scale and computes a correlation. That is nomothetic. If instead he spent months studying the life story of his friend Rohan, that would be idiographic. Option b is right because nomothetic research aims at general, law-like statements about behaviour. Exam trap: the two words are often swapped. Link 'nomo' with norms and numbers (many people, general laws) and 'idio' with the individual. Also, Allport did not coin the terms; Windelband did, and Allport applied them to personality.", "topic": "Nomothetic versus idiographic"}, {"q": "Who introduced the first Intelligence Quotient (IQ) formula based on Mental Age and Chronological Age ?", "options": {"a": "Binet", "b": "Binet Simon", "c": "Binet Stanford", "d": "Stanford"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the IQ formula based on mental age and chronological age came into testing through the Stanford-Binet, which is what 'Binet Stanford' refers to. The story has three steps. First, Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) published the first practical intelligence test in Paris, to identify children who needed special help at school. In the 1908 revision they grouped items by age level, so a child's performance could be expressed as a mental age (MA): the age level of the items the child passes. Second, the German psychologist William Stern (1912) divided mental age by chronological age (CA), giving a 'mental quotient'. Third, Lewis Terman (1916) at Stanford University revised and standardised the Binet scale as the Stanford-Binet, multiplied the quotient by 100 to remove decimals, and made the term intelligence quotient, or IQ, famous: IQ = (MA divided by CA) multiplied by 100. Example: Riya is 10 years old (CA 10) and passes items up to the 12-year level (MA 12). Her ratio IQ is 12 divided by 10, multiplied by 100, which is 120. If her MA were 8, her IQ would be 80. The ratio IQ was later replaced by the deviation IQ of David Wechsler (1939), because mental age stops growing in adulthood. Option c is right because the MA/CA formula reached the world through the 1916 Stanford-Binet. Exam trap: students pick 'Binet' because the test carries his name, but Binet worked with mental level only and never computed a quotient. Key note: strictly, William Stern proposed the mental quotient MA/CA in 1912, and Lewis Terman multiplied it by 100 and made 'IQ' famous in the 1916 Stanford-Binet, which is what 'Binet Stanford' points to. Alfred Binet himself never used a quotient, so a and b are wrong, and Stern is not offered; the official key stands.", "topic": "Origin of the IQ formula"}, {"q": "The Army Alpha Test was first developed during :", "options": {"a": "World War II", "b": "World War I", "c": "Korean War", "d": "Vietnam War"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Army Alpha test was developed during World War I, in 1917, when the United States entered the war. The US Army needed a quick way to sort about 1.75 million recruits by mental ability, to screen out the unfit and pick out likely officers. Individual tests like the Binet scale took about an hour per person with a trained examiner, which was impossible on that scale. A committee of the American Psychological Association, chaired by its president Robert Yerkes (1917), built the first mass group intelligence tests, drawing heavily on the group items of Arthur Otis (1917), a student of Lewis Terman (1916). Two forms were made. The Army Alpha was a verbal, paper-and-pencil test for literate, English-speaking recruits, with subtests such as following directions, arithmetic problems, analogies, number series and general information. The Army Beta was a non-verbal, pictorial test for recruits who could not read or did not know English, given largely through gestures and demonstrations. Example: Arjun, revising testing history, fixes the order as the Binet-Simon scale in 1905 (an individual test), the Stanford-Binet in 1916, then Army Alpha and Beta in 1917 (the first large group tests). After the war the Army tests were adapted for schools and industry, and group testing spread rapidly in the 1920s. Option b is right because Army Alpha and Beta were created in 1917 for the American army in World War I. Exam trap: the best-known army test of World War II was a different one, the Army General Classification Test (1940). Do not confuse Army Alpha either with the Personal Data Sheet of Robert Woodworth (1917), the first personality inventory, which was made in the same war to screen recruits for emotional instability.", "topic": "Army Alpha test"}, {"q": "The General Aptitude Test Battery (GATB) was developed primarily to assess :", "options": {"a": "Personality traits", "b": "Occupational aptitudes", "c": "Emotional intelligence", "d": "Clinical symptoms"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the General Aptitude Test Battery (GATB) was built to assess occupational aptitudes, so that job seekers could be matched to suitable kinds of work. An aptitude is a capacity to learn a skill with training, as opposed to achievement, which is what has already been learned. A multiple aptitude battery measures several such capacities in one package, giving a profile of strengths and weaknesses instead of a single IQ. The GATB was developed by the United States Employment Service and released in 1947, after more than a decade of research, for use in public employment offices. Its 12 tests (8 paper-and-pencil and 4 apparatus tests) yield nine aptitudes: general learning ability (G), verbal aptitude (V), numerical aptitude (N), spatial aptitude (S), form perception (P), clerical perception (Q), motor coordination (K), finger dexterity (F) and manual dexterity (M). Each occupation was given a pattern of minimum aptitude scores, so a counsellor could see which jobs suited a person's profile. Example: Kabir, aged 19, visits an employment counsellor in Hyderabad. On a GATB-type battery he scores high on spatial aptitude, form perception and manual dexterity but low on clerical perception. The counsellor suggests training as a draughtsman or technician rather than an office clerk. A school-focused relative is the Differential Aptitude Tests of George Bennett, Harold Seashore and Alexander Wesman (1947), with eight subtests for educational and vocational guidance. Option b is right because the GATB was made for vocational counselling and job placement, which rest on occupational aptitudes. Exam trap: the GATB has nine aptitudes and the DAT eight subtests, and students mix the two numbers. Both measure aptitudes, not personality, interests or clinical problems.", "topic": "General Aptitude Test Battery"}, {"q": "The Wechsler Deterioration Quotient is primarily used to estimate :", "options": {"a": "Academic achievement", "b": "Intellectual decline", "c": "Emotional maturity", "d": "Occupational aptitude"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Wechsler Deterioration Quotient (also called the deterioration index) estimates intellectual decline, that is, how much cognitive ability a person has lost through ageing, brain disease or severe psychiatric illness. David Wechsler (1939), who built the Wechsler-Bellevue scale and later the WAIS, noticed that some subtests resist ageing and brain damage while others drop sharply. The resistant ones he called 'hold' tests: on the WAIS, Vocabulary, Information, Picture Completion and Object Assembly, which rely on long-stored knowledge. The sensitive ones he called 'don't hold' tests: Digit Span, Similarities, Digit Symbol and Block Design, which need speed, working memory and fresh problem solving. The hold tests give an estimate of the person's earlier, premorbid level; the don't hold tests show current functioning. The quotient is (hold minus don't hold) divided by hold, usually multiplied by 100 to give a percentage loss. Wechsler treated a loss beyond about 10 percent as possible deterioration and beyond about 20 percent as definite deterioration, over and above what is expected for age. Example: Meera, a 60-year-old retired teacher in Kochi, still has an excellent vocabulary and general knowledge, but she has become slow on Digit Symbol and Block Design. Her hold scores are far above her don't hold scores, giving a high quotient that suggests decline and calls for a full neuropsychological work-up. Option b is right because the whole point of comparing hold and don't hold subtests is to measure loss from a former level. Exam trap: the quotient is not a measure of current IQ or achievement. Today it is largely replaced by better premorbid estimates, such as the National Adult Reading Test of Hazel Nelson (1982), and by full neuropsychological batteries.", "topic": "Wechsler Deterioration Quotient"}, {"q": "According to Murray's theory used in the TAT, hunger and thirst are examples of :", "options": {"a": "Psychogenic needs", "b": "Viscerogenic needs", "c": "Social motives", "d": "Ego needs"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: in Henry Murray's theory, hunger and thirst are viscerogenic needs, the primary needs that arise from the body's tissues and organs. Henry Murray (1938), in Explorations in Personality, defined a need as a force in the brain that organises perception, thinking and action to change an unsatisfying situation. He divided needs into two classes. Viscerogenic (primary) needs come from bodily processes and lead to physical satisfaction: the needs for food, water, air, sex, urination, and the avoidance of heat, cold and harm. Psychogenic (secondary) needs are psychological and learned, not tied to any organ: achievement, affiliation, dominance, autonomy, aggression, nurturance, order and many more. Murray added press, the forces in the environment that help or block a need. The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), published by Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935), grew out of this theory. The person tells stories about ambiguous pictures, and the examiner identifies the hero, the hero's needs and the press the hero faces, assuming that storytellers project their own needs onto the hero. David McClelland (1953) later used TAT stories to score the need for achievement. Example: Arjun tells a TAT story in which a boy skips lunch to finish a model plane so he can win a science prize. The skipped lunch touches a viscerogenic need (hunger), but the main theme, striving to win, reveals the psychogenic need for achievement. Option b is right because Murray placed the needs for food and water in the viscerogenic class, as they arise from the body. Exam trap: TAT scoring centres on psychogenic needs, so students assume that all of Murray's needs are psychogenic. Remember that viscera means the internal organs, so viscerogenic means 'born of the body'.", "topic": "Murray's viscerogenic needs"}, {"q": "The Stanford Prison Experiment demonstrated that :", "options": {"a": "Intelligence determines aggression", "b": "Social roles can strongly influence behaviour", "c": "Personality is genetically fixed", "d": "Leadership depends only on intelligence"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Stanford Prison Experiment is the classic demonstration that social roles can strongly shape behaviour, often overriding personality. Philip Zimbardo, Craig Haney and Curtis Banks (1971) built a mock prison in the basement of the psychology building at Stanford University. They screened volunteers, chose 24 healthy, stable male college students and randomly assigned them to be guards or prisoners. Prisoners were 'arrested' at home, given smocks and known only by numbers; guards wore uniforms and mirrored sunglasses and carried batons. Within days many guards became harsh and humiliating, while several prisoners became passive, distressed and helpless, and some had to be released early. The study, planned for two weeks, was stopped after only six days, after Christina Maslach (1971), then a graduate student, objected to the conditions. Because the participants had been screened as normal and were randomly assigned, Zimbardo argued that their behaviour came from the roles and the prison setting, not from 'bad apples'. He later applied this idea to the abuse at Abu Ghraib in The Lucifer Effect (2007). Example: Kabir, a mild and polite student, is made a hostel warden for a week. Within days he is shouting at juniors and enforcing petty rules, behaviour that fades once the role ends. Option b is right because random assignment to the guard or prisoner role, not prior personality, predicted how people behaved. Exam trap: this study is about the power of roles and situations. It is not about obedience to one authority figure (Stanley Milgram, 1963) or conformity to a group (Solomon Asch, 1951). It has also been heavily criticised for its ethics and for possible coaching of guards, and the BBC Prison Study of Stephen Reicher and Alexander Haslam (2006) found that guards did not turn tyrannical on their own.", "topic": "Stanford Prison Experiment"}, {"q": "The Johari Window is mainly used to improve :", "options": {"a": "Memory", "b": "Self-awareness and interpersonal communication", "c": "Intelligence", "d": "Leadership only"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Johari Window is a model for improving self-awareness and interpersonal communication, by mapping how much of ourselves is known to us and to others. It was created by Joseph Luft and Harrington Ingham (1955), and its name joins their first names, Joe and Harry. The window has four panes, made by crossing two questions: is this known to me, and is it known to others? The open area (arena) holds what both I and others know, such as my job or my cheerful manner. The blind area holds what others see but I do not, such as a habit of interrupting. The hidden area (facade) holds what I know but keep from others, such as private fears. The unknown area holds what neither I nor others know yet, such as untapped talents or buried feelings. Trust and communication grow as the open area widens. It widens through self-disclosure, which moves hidden material into the open, and through asking for and accepting feedback, which shrinks the blind area; shared discovery can also shrink the unknown area. The model is used in counselling, group work, team building and interpersonal skills training. Example: in a group session, Riya learns from feedback that she often sounds dismissive in meetings, which she had never noticed, so her blind area shrinks. She also shares that she is anxious about public speaking, so her hidden area shrinks. Her open area grows, and her team understands her better. Option b is right because the whole purpose of the model is to increase self-awareness and improve communication through disclosure and feedback. Exam trap: the Johari Window is often used in leadership training, which makes 'Leadership only' tempting, but the word 'only' is the trap: its core aim is self-knowledge and communication in any relationship.", "topic": "Johari Window"}, {"q": "Edward T. Hall introduced the concept of :", "options": {"a": "Social facilitation", "b": "Proxemics", "c": "Attribution theory", "d": "Cognitive mapping"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: Edward T. Hall introduced proxemics, the study of how people use physical space and interpersonal distance when they communicate. Edward T. Hall (1963), an American anthropologist, coined the word and developed it in The Hidden Dimension (1966). He argued that distance is a silent channel of nonverbal communication, as meaningful as gaze or tone of voice, and that every culture has its own unwritten rules for it. Among middle-class Americans he described four zones. Intimate distance (touching to about 45 cm) is kept for partners, children and close friends. Personal distance (about 45 cm to 1.2 m) suits friends in conversation. Social distance (about 1.2 to 3.6 m) is used for formal and business dealings. Public distance (beyond about 3.6 m) is the space of lectures and speeches. Hall noted that contact cultures, such as Arab and Latin American ones, stand closer than northern European ones, so the same distance can feel warm to one person and pushy to another. In Beyond Culture (1976) he added the contrast between high-context cultures, where much of the meaning lies in the setting and the relationship (India, Japan), and low-context cultures, where meaning is carried by explicit words. For example, when a stranger sits right beside Meera on an almost empty bus bench, she feels uneasy and shifts away: her personal zone has been invaded. Option b is right because proxemics is the term Hall himself created and the idea his name is tied to in every textbook. Exam trap: other famous Edwards and Halls sit nearby in the syllabus. Edward C. Tolman (1948) gave us cognitive maps, and G. Stanley Hall (1892) founded the American Psychological Association; do not let a shared first name or surname pull you towards option d.", "topic": "Hall and proxemics"}, {"q": "Self-serving bias refers to the tendency to :", "options": {"a": "Attribute success internally and failure externally", "b": "Attribute all failures internally", "c": "Attribute success to luck", "d": "Ignore situational influences"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: self-serving bias is the tendency to attribute our own successes to internal causes (our ability or effort) and our failures to external causes (bad luck, an unfair task, other people). It is one of the classic attributional biases, the systematic tilts in how people explain behaviour. Attribution theory began with Fritz Heider (1958), who separated dispositional (internal) from situational (external) causes. Dale Miller and Michael Ross (1975) reviewed the early evidence and asked whether the bias was real or a by-product of people's expectations; later work, including a large meta-analysis by Amy Mezulis and colleagues (2004), confirmed that it is strong and found in almost every group studied, though weaker in collectivist cultures such as Japan. The usual explanation is motivational: crediting ourselves for success and blaming circumstances for failure protects self-esteem. A cognitive account adds that we expect to succeed, so success feels caused by us while failure feels like a surprise from outside. For example, when Arjun tops his statistics test he says, 'I worked really hard', but when he fails the next one he says, 'The paper was unfair and the hall was noisy'. Option a is right because it captures both halves of the bias: internal causes for success, external causes for failure. Exam trap: do not confuse it with the fundamental attribution error of Lee Ross (1977), which is about explaining other people's behaviour by their dispositions while underrating the situation, or with the actor-observer effect of Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett (1971). The self-serving bias is about our own outcomes, and the direction of the attribution flips depending on whether the outcome was good or bad.", "topic": "Self-serving bias"}, {"q": "Night terrors are most commonly observed during :", "options": {"a": "REM sleep", "b": "Stage 1 NREM sleep", "c": "Deep NREM (Stage 3) sleep", "d": "Dream sleep"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: night terrors (sleep terrors) arise from deep non-REM sleep, Stage 3 or N3, also called slow-wave sleep. Sleep runs in cycles of about 90 minutes. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered REM sleep, and the non-REM stages are now labelled N1, N2 and N3 by the American Academy of Sleep Medicine (2007), which merged the old Stages 3 and 4 into N3. N3 is dominated by slow, high-amplitude delta waves, is the hardest stage to wake from, and is concentrated in the first third of the night. Roger Broughton (1968) showed that sleep terrors, sleepwalking and confusional arousals are 'disorders of arousal': the sleeper is partly jolted out of N3, so the body and the emotion systems wake while awareness stays asleep. In a sleep terror the person, usually a child, sits up suddenly, screams, sweats, has a racing heart and wide pupils, is hard to console, and remembers little or nothing next morning. For example, about two hours after falling asleep, five-year-old Kabir screams and stares in terror, does not recognise his mother, then settles back to sleep; at breakfast he recalls nothing. Option c is right because the timing (early in the night), the poor recall and the difficulty waking all point to deep slow-wave sleep. Exam trap: nightmares and night terrors are different. Nightmares are frightening dreams from REM sleep, mostly late in the night, and the dreamer wakes fully and recalls the story vividly. Because the body is paralysed in REM (REM atonia), screaming, sitting up and walking are not normal REM events; acting out dreams in REM is a separate condition, REM sleep behaviour disorder.", "topic": "Night terrors and sleep stages"}, {"q": "The amygdala plays a crucial role in :", "options": {"a": "Balance", "b": "Emotional processing, especially fear", "c": "Speech production", "d": "Visual perception"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the amygdala, an almond-shaped cluster of nuclei deep in each temporal lobe, is central to emotional processing, especially the detection of threat and the learning and expression of fear. It belongs to the limbic system, named by Paul MacLean (1952). The first clue came from Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy (1937, 1939): after removal of both temporal lobes, including the amygdalae, their monkeys became tame and fearless, approached snakes and put objects in their mouths, the Klüver-Bucy syndrome. Joseph LeDoux (1996) traced fear conditioning in rats and described two routes from the thalamus: a fast, crude 'low road' straight to the amygdala, which triggers freezing and a racing heart within milliseconds, and a slower 'high road' through the cortex that checks what the stimulus really is. In humans, patient S.M., whose amygdalae were destroyed by Urbach-Wiethe disease, could not recognise fear in faces (Ralph Adolphs and colleagues, 1994) and showed almost no fear of snakes, spiders or horror films. The amygdala also makes emotional events more memorable, working with stress hormones such as noradrenaline. For example, when Riya jumps back from what looks like a snake on a dark path before she has consciously seen that it is only a rope, her amygdala's low road has fired first. Option b is right because fear and threat processing is the amygdala's best established role. Exam trap: the hippocampus sits right beside it, and examiners swap the two. The hippocampus stores new facts and events, as the amnesia of Henry Molaison (H.M., operated on in 1953) showed; the amygdala adds emotional colour to them. Also, the limbic system as a whole is the classic 'seat of emotion', while the amygdala is its fear hub.", "topic": "Amygdala and fear"}, {"q": "The occipital lobe is primarily responsible for :", "options": {"a": "Hearing", "b": "Vision", "c": "Taste", "d": "Touch"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, is primarily responsible for vision. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes: frontal, parietal, temporal and occipital. The occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or area 17 on the map of Korbinian Brodmann, 1909), which lines the calcarine sulcus on the inner surface of the lobe. Signals travel from the retina along the optic nerve, through the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, and along the optic radiations to V1. The map is retinotopic: neighbouring points on the retina project to neighbouring points of cortex, and the left half of the visual field is processed by the right occipital lobe. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959, 1962) recorded single cells in the visual cortex of cats and found simple and complex cells tuned to lines of a particular orientation, work that won them the Nobel Prize in 1981. From V1, information flows forward along two streams described by Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982): a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe for recognising objects and faces, and a dorsal 'where' stream into the parietal lobe for location and movement. Damage to V1 causes cortical blindness in the matching part of the visual field, even though the eyes are healthy. For example, after a stroke in her right occipital lobe, Meera keeps bumping into things on her left, because she has lost the left half of her visual field. Option b is right because vision is the defining function of the occipital lobe. Exam trap: each primary sense has its own cortex, and examiners rotate them. Hearing is temporal, touch is parietal and taste is in the insula; only vision belongs to the occipital lobe.", "topic": "Occipital lobe and vision"}, {"q": "Which of the following correctly represents the sequence of events during an action potential ?", "options": {"a": "Resting → Repolarization → Depolarization → Hyperpolarization", "b": "Resting → Threshold → Depolarization → Repolarization → Hyperpolarization → Resting", "c": "Threshold → Resting → Hyperpolarization → Depolarization", "d": "Resting → Hyperpolarization → Depolarization → Repolarization"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: an action potential runs resting, threshold, depolarisation, repolarisation, hyperpolarisation, and back to resting. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked out this sequence by recording from the giant axon of the squid, work that won them the Nobel Prize in 1963. At rest the inside of a neuron is about -70 mV, held there by the sodium-potassium pump and by potassium leaking out. Incoming excitatory signals nudge the membrane upward; if their sum reaches threshold (about -55 mV in most texts, about -65 mV in some), voltage-gated sodium channels fly open. Sodium rushes in and the membrane depolarises sharply, overshooting to about +40 or +50 mV. The sodium channels then inactivate while voltage-gated potassium channels open, so potassium flows out and the membrane repolarises. These potassium channels close slowly, so the membrane briefly dips below its resting level, the hyperpolarisation or undershoot, before the pump and leak channels restore -70 mV. The whole spike lasts about 1 millisecond and obeys the all-or-none law: below threshold nothing fires, above it the spike is always the same size. During the absolute refractory period no stimulus can fire another spike; during the relative refractory period, which overlaps the hyperpolarisation, only a stronger stimulus can. For example, when Kabir touches a hot pan, each sensory neuron in his finger runs this exact cycle many times a second. Option b is right because it is the only order in which each step causes the next: reaching threshold opens sodium channels, sodium entry depolarises, potassium exit repolarises, and slow potassium channels cause the undershoot. Exam trap: hyperpolarisation comes last, after repolarisation, never before depolarisation; and threshold is a point on the rising phase reached from rest, not a starting state that comes before rest.", "topic": "Action potential sequence"}, {"q": "The myelin sheath surrounding many axons is primarily responsible for :", "options": {"a": "Producing neurotransmitters", "b": "Regulating hormone secretion", "c": "Increasing the speed of nerve impulse conduction", "d": "Initiating action potentials at the dendrites"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the myelin sheath is a fatty insulating wrap around many axons, and its main job is to increase the speed of nerve impulse conduction. Myelin is made by glial cells: oligodendrocytes in the brain and spinal cord, and Schwann cells (named after Theodor Schwann, 1839) in the peripheral nerves. It is laid down in segments with small gaps between them, the nodes of Ranvier, described by Louis-Antoine Ranvier (1878). Because myelin blocks ion flow across the membrane, the action potential can be regenerated only at the nodes, where voltage-gated sodium channels are packed. Current spreads quickly and passively under the myelin, so the spike seems to leap from node to node: saltatory conduction, from the Latin saltare, to jump. Ichiji Tasaki (1939) demonstrated this jumping conduction in single frog nerve fibres. An unmyelinated axon must regenerate the spike at every point along its length, so it is slow, about 1 m per second in thin fibres, while large myelinated axons conduct at up to about 100 m per second. Myelin also saves energy, because fewer ions need pumping back. Its fat makes it white, which is why tracts of myelinated axons form the brain's white matter. When myelin is destroyed, as in multiple sclerosis, conduction slows or fails, causing weakness, numbness and blurred vision. For example, Arjun pulls his hand off a hot stove almost before he feels the heat, because the myelinated fibres of his reflex arc carry the signal so fast. Option c is right because faster, more efficient conduction is the defining function of myelin. Exam trap: myelin speeds conduction but does not create the action potential; the spike starts at the axon hillock (initial segment), not at the dendrites, and transmitters are made by the neuron itself, not by its sheath.", "topic": "Myelin and conduction speed"}, {"q": "Which of the following neurotransmitters primarily functions as the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the central nervous system ?", "options": {"a": "Dopamine", "b": "Glutamate", "c": "GABA", "d": "Acetylcholine"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter of the central nervous system. It is an amino acid transmitter, made from glutamate in a single step by the enzyme glutamic acid decarboxylase, and it was identified in the brain by Eugene Roberts and Sam Frankel (1950). Up to about a third of brain synapses may use it. GABA acts on two main receptor types. The GABA-A receptor is ionotropic: it is a chloride channel, and when GABA binds, chloride ions flow in and hyperpolarise the neuron, producing a fast inhibitory postsynaptic potential that makes firing less likely. The GABA-B receptor is metabotropic, working through G proteins to open potassium channels. Inhibition is not a minor job: it keeps excitation in check, shapes the timing of neural activity and stops runaway firing. Too little GABA activity is linked to seizures and anxiety. Many calming drugs act by enhancing the GABA-A receptor: benzodiazepines such as diazepam, barbiturates and alcohol. This is why they reduce anxiety, cause sleepiness and can stop seizures, and why mixing them is dangerous. For example, when Meera is given lorazepam before surgery to calm her panic, the drug boosts GABA's inhibitory effect and her racing worry quietens. Option c is right because GABA is the brain's principal inhibitory transmitter. Exam trap: glycine is the main inhibitory transmitter of the spinal cord and brainstem, so if a question says 'spinal cord', choose glycine; if it says 'brain' or 'central nervous system' in general, choose GABA. Also remember that glutamate and GABA are partners: glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter and is the raw material from which GABA is made.", "topic": "GABA, main inhibitory transmitter"}, {"q": "Glutamate primarily functions as a(n) :", "options": {"a": "Inhibitory neurotransmitter", "b": "Excitatory neurotransmitter", "c": "Hormone", "d": "Neuromodulator only"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: glutamate is primarily an excitatory neurotransmitter, in fact the most common excitatory transmitter in the brain and spinal cord. It is an amino acid. Takashi Hayashi (1954) found that glutamate applied to the brain caused convulsions, and David Curtis and Jeffrey Watkins (1960) showed that it excites spinal neurons directly. When glutamate binds its ionotropic receptors, positive ions (sodium, and for some receptors calcium) flow in, depolarising the next neuron and making it more likely to fire. The main ionotropic receptors are AMPA, kainate and NMDA; there are also metabotropic glutamate receptors that act more slowly through G proteins. The NMDA receptor is special: it opens only when glutamate binds and the cell is already depolarised, so it detects coincident activity, and the calcium it lets in strengthens the synapse. This is the basis of long-term potentiation, discovered by Tim Bliss and Terje Lømo (1973) in the rabbit hippocampus and widely seen as a cellular model of learning and memory. Too much glutamate is harmful: in a stroke, glutamate floods out and over-excites neurons until they die, a process called excitotoxicity. For example, as Riya practises a new raga every day, glutamate synapses in her hippocampus and cortex are repeatedly activated and strengthened. Option b is right because glutamate's defining action is to depolarise and excite the neurons it reaches. Exam trap: glutamate and GABA are opposites but chemical relatives. GABA, the main inhibitory transmitter, is made from glutamate in one enzyme step, so a question about the precursor of GABA also wants glutamate; do not let the family link tempt you into calling glutamate inhibitory.", "topic": "Glutamate as excitatory transmitter"}, {"q": "Dopamine is strongly associated with :", "options": {"a": "Hearing", "b": "Reward and motivation", "c": "Digestion", "d": "Blood clotting"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: dopamine is strongly associated with reward and motivation, as well as with movement. Dopamine is a catecholamine made from the amino acid tyrosine by way of L-dopa. Its reward role runs mainly through the mesolimbic pathway, from the ventral tegmental area (VTA) of the midbrain to the nucleus accumbens, with links to the prefrontal cortex through the mesocortical pathway. The story began with James Olds and Peter Milner (1954), whose rats pressed a lever thousands of times an hour to receive electrical stimulation along the medial forebrain bundle, a route that carries dopamine fibres: the so-called pleasure centres. Later, Wolfram Schultz (1997) recorded VTA dopamine neurons in monkeys and found that they fire for unexpected rewards, stay quiet for fully expected ones, and dip when a promised reward fails to arrive: a reward prediction error signal that drives learning. Kent Berridge and Terry Robinson (1998) argued that dopamine creates 'wanting' (incentive salience, the urge to pursue a reward) more than 'liking' (the pleasure itself). Almost every addictive drug raises dopamine in the nucleus accumbens; cocaine blocks its reuptake and amphetamine increases its release. A separate pathway, the nigrostriatal one, controls movement, and its loss causes Parkinson's disease. For example, each notification on Kabir's phone gives a small dopamine burst that keeps him checking the app, even when he does not particularly enjoy what he finds. Option b is right because reward, motivation and reinforcement are dopamine's best known functions. Exam trap: dopamine is about wanting and about learning what predicts reward, not simply about pleasure; and too much mesolimbic dopamine is linked to the positive symptoms of schizophrenia, while too little nigrostriatal dopamine causes the tremor and rigidity of Parkinson's disease.", "topic": "Dopamine, reward and motivation"}, {"q": "An end plate potential occurs at the :", "options": {"a": "Synapse between two neurons", "b": "Neuromuscular junction", "c": "Retina", "d": "Cerebellum"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: an end plate potential (EPP) occurs at the neuromuscular junction, the synapse between a motor neuron and a skeletal muscle fibre. The muscle side of this synapse is a folded patch of membrane called the motor end plate, which gives the potential its name. When an action potential reaches the motor neuron's terminal, calcium enters and triggers the release of acetylcholine (ACh). ACh crosses the narrow cleft and binds nicotinic receptors on the end plate, which are ion channels. Sodium flows in and the end plate depolarises: this local, graded depolarisation is the end plate potential. Unlike a typical excitatory postsynaptic potential between neurons, which is tiny and must be summed with many others, the EPP is very large, so a single nerve impulse almost always pushes the muscle fibre past threshold. This built-in margin is called the safety factor. The muscle then fires its own action potential and contracts, while the enzyme acetylcholinesterase quickly breaks ACh down and ends the signal. Paul Fatt and Bernard Katz (1951, 1952) recorded EPPs in frog muscle and found tiny spontaneous 'miniature end plate potentials', each caused by one packet of ACh. This led to the quantal theory of transmitter release, and Katz shared the Nobel Prize in 1970. Curare blocks nicotinic receptors and paralyses muscle; in myasthenia gravis, antibodies attack the receptors, so EPPs shrink and muscles tire quickly. For example, when Arjun decides to lift his pen, each motor neuron impulse produces an EPP that makes his finger muscles contract. Option b is right because the EPP is, by definition, the postsynaptic potential of the neuromuscular junction. Exam trap: the term is specific; the equivalent potentials between two neurons are called EPSPs and IPSPs, not end plate potentials.", "topic": "End plate potential"}, {"q": "Lev Vygotsky's concept of the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) refers to :", "options": {"a": "The difference between chronological and mental age", "b": "The level of development achieved without assistance", "c": "The gap between what a learner can do independently and what can be achieved with guidance", "d": "The period during which language develops most rapidly"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: Lev Vygotsky's zone of proximal development (ZPD) is the gap between what a learner can do independently and what the learner can achieve with guidance from an adult or a more capable peer. Lev Vygotsky (1934), the Russian psychologist, set out the idea in his last lectures and in Thought and Language, and it reached most English readers through Mind in Society (1978). His sociocultural theory holds that higher mental functions first appear between people, in shared activity, and only later inside the child. He illustrated the ZPD with two children who both solve problems set for eight-year-olds when working alone. With hints, one solves problems set for twelve-year-olds, the other only those for nine-year-olds. Their actual developmental level is the same, but their zones differ, so a single test score hides real differences in readiness to learn. Teaching works best inside the zone: on tasks the child cannot yet do alone but can do with help. David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) later called this kind of adjustable support scaffolding: the helper gives hints, models steps or breaks the task down, then withdraws support as competence grows. For example, Meera cannot solve long division alone, but when her older brother asks guiding questions she gets each step right; long division sits inside her ZPD, while algebra is still beyond it. Option c is right because it states the definition exactly: the distance between independent and assisted performance. Exam trap: the ZPD is often confused with the actual developmental level, which is what the child does alone (option b); and the word scaffolding belongs to Wood, Bruner and Ross, not to Vygotsky himself, although it describes how to teach within his zone.", "topic": "Zone of proximal development"}, {"q": "According to Lawrence Kohlberg's theory, an individual who obeys laws because they maintain social order is functioning at which stage of moral development ?", "options": {"a": "Obedience and Punishment Orientation", "b": "Instrumental Purpose Orientation", "c": "Good Interpersonal Relationships", "d": "Law and Order Orientation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: obeying laws because they maintain social order is Stage 4, the law-and-order orientation, in Lawrence Kohlberg's theory. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) built his theory by presenting boys with moral dilemmas, the best known being the Heinz dilemma: should a man steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife? He scored not the yes or no answer but the reasoning behind it, and found three levels, each with two stages. At the preconventional level, right is judged by the consequences to oneself: Stage 1, the punishment-and-obedience orientation (avoid punishment), and Stage 2, the instrumental purpose orientation (what is in it for me, fair deals). At the conventional level, reached by most adolescents and where most adults stay, right means conforming to social rules: Stage 3, good interpersonal relationships, or the 'good boy, nice girl' orientation (win the approval of people close to you), and Stage 4, law and order (do your duty, respect authority, keep society running). At the postconventional level, reached by a minority, laws are judged against principles: Stage 5, social contract, and Stage 6, universal ethical principles. For example, Kabir says Heinz should not steal because 'if everyone broke the law whenever they felt it was justified, society would fall apart'; his concern is the whole social system, so he is reasoning at Stage 4. Option d is right because the stem's reason, keeping social order, is the hallmark of Stage 4. Exam trap: Stage 1 also involves obeying, but out of fear of punishment, not respect for the social system; and Stage 3 involves conformity too, but to please family and friends, not to preserve society as a whole. Ask why the person obeys, not whether they obey.", "topic": "Kohlberg's law-and-order stage"}, {"q": "According to Erikson, the major psychosocial crisis during adolescence is :", "options": {"a": "Trust vs. Mistrust", "b": "Identity vs. Role Confusion", "c": "Industry vs. Inferiority", "d": "Integrity vs. Despair"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: in Erik Erikson's theory, the major psychosocial crisis of adolescence is identity versus role confusion. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, extended the psychosexual stages of Sigmund Freud (1905) into eight psychosocial stages that span the whole lifespan. Each stage poses a crisis, a turning point between two outcomes, shaped by the person's widening social world, and a good balance yields a basic strength or virtue. The eight are: trust versus mistrust (infancy, hope), autonomy versus shame and doubt (about 1 to 3, will), initiative versus guilt (about 3 to 6, purpose), industry versus inferiority (6 to puberty, competence), identity versus role confusion (about 12 to 18, fidelity), intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood, love), generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood, care) and ego integrity versus despair (late adulthood, wisdom). In adolescence, puberty, new thinking skills and looming choices push the young person to fuse childhood identifications, values, skills and future roles into one answer to 'Who am I?'. Erikson described a psychosocial moratorium, a socially permitted delay in which adolescents try out roles before committing. James Marcia (1966) later turned this into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. For example, Riya, 16, switches between wanting to be a doctor, a musician and a lawyer, and argues with her parents about her clothes and friends; Erikson would see her exploring an identity, which is healthy if it leads to commitment. Option b is right because identity versus role confusion is the fifth stage, the one Erikson tied to adolescence. Exam trap: industry versus inferiority (school age, roughly 6 to 12) sits just before adolescence and is the usual near-miss, while intimacy versus isolation, the stage just after, belongs to young adults, not teenagers.", "topic": "Erikson: identity versus role confusion"}, {"q": "A zygote is formed immediately after :", "options": {"a": "Implantation", "b": "Fertilization", "c": "Gastrulation", "d": "Cleavage"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: a zygote is formed immediately after fertilisation, the union of a sperm and an ovum. Each sex cell (gamete) is made by meiosis and carries 23 chromosomes; when a sperm penetrates the ovum, usually in the fallopian tube, the two nuclei fuse into a single cell with the full 46 chromosomes, in 23 pairs. That single fertilised cell is the zygote, the starting point of prenatal development. Developmental psychologists divide prenatal life into three periods. The germinal period covers about the first two weeks: the zygote begins dividing by mitosis (cleavage) as it travels down the tube, becomes a solid ball of cells, the morula (about day 4), then a hollow blastocyst (about day 5), and finally implants in the wall of the uterus at about 6 to 10 days. The embryonic period, weeks 3 to 8, opens with gastrulation, when the inner cells form three germ layers: ectoderm (skin and nervous system), mesoderm (muscle, bone and blood) and endoderm (gut and lungs). This is when the organs form and when teratogens do most damage. The fetal period runs from week 9 to birth. Since every ovum carries an X chromosome, the father's sperm (X or Y) decides the baby's sex, and if a zygote splits in two the result is identical (monozygotic) twins. For example, if an ovum is fertilised by a Y-bearing sperm, the zygote is XY from that moment and will develop as a boy, as happened when Aditi's baby brother was conceived. Option b is right because fertilisation is the event that creates the zygote; every other option happens to it afterwards. Exam trap: the order matters. Fertilisation comes first, then cleavage, then implantation, then gastrulation; each distractor is a real stage, just a later one.", "topic": "Fertilisation and the zygote"}, {"q": "An employee was called in by their employer for submitting the project assignment later than the submission day. The employee starts throwing a temper tantrum in front of their boss, and bangs the door on their way out. What is the defense mechanism being shown here ?", "options": {"a": "Reaction formation", "b": "Regression", "c": "Suppression", "d": "Denial"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: throwing a temper tantrum and banging the door is regression, a retreat to the behaviour of an earlier, more childlike stage of development when faced with stress or frustration. Defence mechanisms are unconscious strategies the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety. Sigmund Freud (1894) introduced the idea of defence, and Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, catalogued the defences, regression among them. In psychoanalytic theory, a person under threat slips back towards a stage where they once felt safe or where part of their libido stayed fixated. Tantrums, sulking, crying to get one's way, thumb sucking or baby talk in an adult are classic signs. A famous study by Roger Barker, Tamara Dembo and Kurt Lewin (1941) let children play with attractive toys, then put the toys behind a wire screen where they could be seen but not reached. Frustrated, the children played in a less mature way, much like children about a year and a half younger. In the stem, an adult employee is criticised for missing a deadline. Instead of an adult response, such as explaining or apologising, the employee shouts, stamps and slams the door like a small child: that is regression. Similarly, when Kabir is scolded by his manager and responds by sulking and refusing to speak to anyone all day, he too is regressing. Option b is right because the employee's behaviour is a return to a childlike way of handling frustration. Exam trap: do not confuse regression with displacement (taking anger out on a safer target, such as shouting at a younger sibling after a bad day) or acting out (expressing an impulse in action to avoid feeling it). Neither is offered here, so the childlike tantrum points to regression.", "topic": "Regression as a defence"}, {"q": "A psychologist concludes that a client's depressive symptoms are maintained by dysfunctional core beliefs. This formulation is most consistent with :", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalytic therapy", "b": "Cognitive therapy", "c": "Behaviour therapy", "d": "Humanistic therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: a formulation in which depression is maintained by dysfunctional core beliefs is the hallmark of cognitive therapy, developed by Aaron T. Beck (1967), whose treatment manual Cognitive Therapy of Depression appeared in 1979. Beck, a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that his depressed patients' thinking was full of themes of loss and failure that showed up as conscious, automatic thoughts. He proposed the cognitive model: it is not events themselves that cause emotion, but the way they are interpreted. Cognition works at three levels. Core beliefs, or schemas, are deep, rigid, global beliefs about the self, others and the world, usually formed in childhood ('I am unlovable', 'I am a failure'). From them grow intermediate beliefs: rules and assumptions ('If I am not perfect, I will be rejected'). When a situation activates these, negative automatic thoughts pop up ('She has not replied; she must hate me') and drive low mood. In depression they produce the cognitive triad: negative views of the self, the world and the future. Therapy is structured and collaborative: the client learns to catch automatic thoughts, test them against evidence (Socratic questioning, thought records, behavioural experiments) and later to modify the core beliefs beneath them, for example with the downward arrow technique. Judith S. Beck (1995) mapped this in her cognitive conceptualisation diagram. For example, Arjun, who believes deep down 'I am incompetent', reads every small mistake at work as proof and feels hopeless; his therapist helps him gather evidence against the belief. Option b is right because core beliefs and the distorted thinking they feed are the central concepts of Beck's cognitive therapy. Exam trap: Albert Ellis (1962), in rational emotive behaviour therapy, also targets beliefs, but he calls them irrational beliefs and uses the ABC model; the words 'core beliefs' and 'schemas' point to Beck.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive therapy, core beliefs"}, {"q": "According to the diathesis-stress model, psychological disorders develop because :", "options": {"a": "Genetic vulnerability alone is sufficient", "b": "Environmental stress alone is sufficient", "c": "Vulnerability interacts with environmental stressors", "d": "Learned behavior completely explains psychopathology"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: the diathesis-stress model holds that psychological disorders develop when a pre-existing vulnerability (the diathesis) interacts with environmental stressors; usually neither is enough alone. The diathesis may be genetic, biological (such as a birth complication or neurotransmitter dysregulation), psychological (such as a negative thinking style) or social (early adversity). The stress may be a major life event, such as bereavement or migration, or the build-up of daily hassles. Paul Meehl (1962) first proposed a version for schizophrenia: an inherited neural defect he called schizotaxia would lead to clinical illness only in combination with a harmful environment. Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) broadened it into the vulnerability-stress model: everyone has some threshold, and illness appears when stress exceeds a person's level of vulnerability. A highly vulnerable person may break down under mild stress, while a person with low vulnerability may need extreme stress. Twin studies support the model: even the identical twin of a person with schizophrenia has only about a 50 percent chance of developing it, so genes alone cannot be the whole explanation. For example, Kabir and his cousin both lose their jobs in the same layoff. Kabir, whose mother and grandfather had depression and who tends to blame himself, slides into a major depressive episode; his cousin, with no family history and good support, feels low for a few weeks and recovers. Option c is right because the word 'interacts' captures the model's core: vulnerability and stress acting together. Exam trap: options that say 'alone is sufficient' or 'completely explains' describe single-cause models, and the diathesis-stress model was defined against them. A later refinement, differential susceptibility (Jay Belsky, 1997), adds that the same sensitivity can also help people thrive in good environments.", "topic": "Diathesis-stress model"}, {"q": "A therapist repeatedly reinforces adaptive behaviours while ignoring maladaptive behaviours. This intervention is based on :", "options": {"a": "Classical conditioning", "b": "Operant conditioning", "c": "Social learning theory", "d": "Cognitive restructuring"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: reinforcing adaptive behaviours while ignoring maladaptive ones is an operant conditioning intervention, based on learning through consequences. Edward Thorndike (1898) first stated the law of effect: responses followed by satisfying consequences become more likely, and those followed by discomfort less likely. B. F. Skinner (1938) developed this into operant conditioning, in which behaviour operates on the environment and is shaped by what follows it. Reinforcement, positive (adding something pleasant, such as praise) or negative (removing something unpleasant), increases a behaviour. Extinction means withholding the reinforcer that has been maintaining a behaviour, so the behaviour gradually fades; planned ignoring of attention-seeking misbehaviour is extinction when attention was the reinforcer. Therapists often combine the two in differential reinforcement: the problem behaviour is put on extinction while a better alternative is reinforced (differential reinforcement of alternative or incompatible behaviour, DRA or DRI). These techniques are used in parent training, classrooms, token economies (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) and applied behaviour analysis for autism. Therapists warn parents about the extinction burst: when reinforcement stops, the behaviour first gets worse before it fades, and giving in at that point teaches the child that bigger tantrums pay. For example, a therapist advises Meera's parents to praise her each time she asks calmly for a toy and to stop responding when she whines; within weeks calm requests rise and whining drops. Option b is right because the procedure works purely through consequences: reinforcement to strengthen one behaviour and extinction to weaken another. Exam trap: social learning theory also uses reinforcement, but its key mechanism is learning by watching a model (Albert Bandura, 1977), and classical conditioning pairs stimuli before a response rather than changing the consequences that follow it.", "topic": "Operant conditioning in therapy"}, {"q": "Which feature best distinguishes dementia from delirium ?", "options": {"a": "Presence of memory impairment", "b": "Gradual onset and progressive cognitive decline", "c": "Impaired attention", "d": "Presence of hallucinations"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: gradual onset and progressive cognitive decline best distinguish dementia from delirium. Delirium is an acute confusional state: a disturbance of attention and awareness (clouded consciousness) that develops over hours to days, fluctuates through the day, often worsens in the evening, and brings disorientation, memory problems and perceptual disturbances such as visual hallucinations. It always has a physical cause, such as infection, drugs, alcohol withdrawal, a metabolic upset or surgery, and it is often reversible once the cause is treated. Zbigniew Lipowski (1980) did much to define it as acute brain failure, a medical emergency rather than a psychiatric curiosity. Dementia, which DSM-5 (2013) calls major neurocognitive disorder, is a decline from a previous level in memory, language, executive function and other domains, serious enough to cost independence. Its commonest form, Alzheimer's disease, first described by Alois Alzheimer (1906), begins insidiously over months or years and worsens steadily, while consciousness and attention stay fairly clear until the later stages. For example, Arjun's grandfather has been slowly forgetting names and getting lost in his own colony over two years: that pattern suggests dementia. If instead he became confused and agitated overnight after a urinary infection, seeing insects on the wall and alert one hour but drowsy the next, that would suggest delirium. Option b is right because onset and course, slow and progressive versus sudden and fluctuating, are the clearest dividing line. Exam trap: memory impairment, impaired attention and hallucinations can all occur in both conditions, so none of them separates the two; dementia also raises the risk of delirium, so they often coexist. Dementia with Lewy bodies has fluctuations and vivid visual hallucinations too, which is why onset and course are the safer guide.", "topic": "Dementia versus delirium"}, {"q": "Which of the following is classified as a Specific Phobia rather than an Anxiety Disorder involving social situations ?", "options": {"a": "Social Anxiety Disorder", "b": "Agoraphobia", "c": "Acrophobia", "d": "Generalized Anxiety Disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: acrophobia, an intense fear of heights, is a specific phobia, not an anxiety disorder centred on social or public situations. A phobia is a marked, persistent fear of a particular object or situation that is out of proportion to the real danger, is avoided or endured with dread, and in DSM-5 (2013) usually lasts 6 months or more. Phobia names join a Greek word to phobos (fear): akron means peak or height, so acrophobia is fear of heights. DSM-5 groups specific phobias into five types: animal, natural environment (heights, storms, water), blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, flights, enclosed places) and other. Acrophobia belongs to the natural environment type. Martin Seligman (1970) explained why fears of heights, snakes and darkness are so common: through preparedness, evolution has readied us to learn fears of things that once threatened survival. Example: Kabir happily speaks on stage and travels alone, but on a glass-floored viewing deck his legs shake and he crawls back to the lift. His fear is tied to one cue, height, and has nothing to do with being watched or judged, so it is a specific phobia. Option c is right because acrophobia is defined by a single, circumscribed trigger, while the other three are separate DSM-5 anxiety diagnoses: social anxiety disorder (fear of scrutiny), agoraphobia (fear of places where escape is hard) and generalised anxiety disorder (diffuse worry). Exam trap: acrophobia and agoraphobia look alike on the page. Agoraphobia, literally fear of the marketplace, is not a specific phobia in DSM-5; it is its own diagnosis needing fear in two or more of five situations, such as crowds and public transport. Acrophobia is simply the fear of heights.", "topic": "Specific phobia: acrophobia"}, {"q": "Which of the following best distinguishes stress from anxiety ?", "options": {"a": "Stress is always pathological, whereas anxiety is always normal.", "b": "Stress is typically a response to an identifiable external stressor, whereas anxiety may occur even in the absence of an immediate external threat.", "c": "Stress and anxiety are synonymous terms.", "d": "Anxiety always precedes stress."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: stress is usually a reaction to an identifiable external demand, while anxiety can arise even when no immediate external threat is present. Stress has been defined as a stimulus, as a response and as a transaction. Hans Selye (1936) described the general adaptation syndrome, a body-wide response to any demand that moves from alarm to resistance to exhaustion. Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984) added the transactional model: stress arises when a person appraises a demand (the stressor) as exceeding their resources. Either way, stress is tied to a stressor that can usually be named: an exam, a move, an illness. Anxiety is a state of apprehension, tension and worry about possible future danger. DSM-5 (2013) contrasts fear, the response to a threat that is present now, with anxiety, the anticipation of a threat that may come. Because anxiety is future-oriented and generated from within, it can persist after the stressor has gone, or appear with no clear cause at all, as in generalised anxiety disorder. Example: Arjun feels tense and sleepless in the week before his board exams, and the feeling lifts once the exams end; that is stress. His cousin Meera wakes with a racing heart and a sense of dread on ordinary days when nothing is wrong; that is anxiety. Option b is right because the presence of an identifiable external stressor is the textbook dividing line between the two states. Exam trap: neither state is automatically pathological or automatically normal. Selye (1974) later coined eustress for helpful stress, and anxiety becomes a disorder only when it is excessive, persistent and impairing, so absolute words such as \"always\" signal a wrong option.", "topic": "Stress versus anxiety"}, {"q": "From a psychodynamic perspective, individuals with PTSD often protect themselves from overwhelming traumatic memories through :", "options": {"a": "Intellectualization only", "b": "Dissociation and repression", "c": "Sublimation and humor", "d": "Reaction formation and compensation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: psychodynamic theory holds that people with PTSD shield themselves from overwhelming traumatic memories mainly through dissociation and repression. A defence mechanism is an unconscious manoeuvre of the ego that keeps unbearable thoughts and feelings out of awareness. Pierre Janet (1889) first proposed that memories too terrible to integrate are split off from ordinary consciousness, a process he called dissociation; they live on as fragments that return as flashbacks and nightmares. Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer (1895), in Studies on Hysteria, described repression, the pushing of painful memories into the unconscious. Mardi Horowitz (1976) built on both in his stress response model: the survivor swings between intrusion (the memory breaking through) and denial or numbing (the defences shutting it out), and recovery comes as the memory is gradually worked through. Modern diagnosis reflects this. DSM-5 (2013) lists inability to recall an important part of the trauma, typically due to dissociative amnesia, as a PTSD symptom, and offers a dissociative subtype with depersonalisation and derealisation. Example: after a bus accident, Riya cannot remember the minutes between the crash and the ambulance, and when reminded of it she feels as if she is watching herself from outside her body. The blank is repression and dissociative amnesia; the unreal feeling is dissociation. Option b is right because both defences keep the trauma out of conscious memory, which is exactly what the stem describes. Exam trap: examiners offer mature defences such as sublimation and humour, which George Vaillant (1977) linked to good adjustment, or add the word \"only\" to make a partly plausible defence too narrow. Trauma calls up the primitive, memory-blocking defences.", "topic": "Psychodynamic defences in PTSD"}, {"q": "World Mental Health Day is observed every year on :", "options": {"a": "September 10", "b": "October 5", "c": "October 10", "d": "November 10"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: World Mental Health Day is observed every year on 10 October. The day was started in 1992 by the World Federation for Mental Health, an international non-governmental body founded in 1948, to raise awareness of mental health and to fight stigma. The World Health Organization supports it, and since 1994 each year has carried a theme; the 2024 theme, for example, was mental health at work. Schools, hospitals and universities use the day for awareness talks, free screening camps and campaigns that make help-seeking normal. India has used the date for policy too: Tele-MANAS, the national tele-mental health helpline (14416), was launched on 10 October 2022. Several other days fall close to it, and examiners like to mix them up. 10 September is World Suicide Prevention Day, organised since 2003 by the International Association for Suicide Prevention with the World Health Organization. 5 October is World Teachers' Day, set by UNESCO in 1994. 10 November is UNESCO's World Science Day for Peace and Development. A quick memory aid is \"10/10\": the tenth day of the tenth month. Option c is right because 10 October is the date that has been World Mental Health Day since its first observance in 1992. Exam trap: World Suicide Prevention Day (10 September) shares the number 10 and the mental health theme, so it is the near miss most students pick. Keep the two apart by month: suicide prevention in September, mental health in October.", "topic": "World Mental Health Day"}, {"q": "Difficulty in handwriting, spelling, punctuation and written expression is most commonly associated with :", "options": {"a": "Dyslexia", "b": "Dysgraphia", "c": "Dyscalculia", "d": "Apraxia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: difficulty in handwriting, spelling, punctuation and written expression is the picture of dysgraphia. Dysgraphia is a specific learning difficulty in writing. DSM-5 (2013) folded the old separate learning disorders into one diagnosis, specific learning disorder, and asks the clinician to specify the area: impairment in reading, in written expression or in mathematics. The written expression specifier covers spelling accuracy, grammar and punctuation accuracy, and clarity or organisation of written work; dysgraphia is the everyday name for it. Like every specific learning disorder, it needs difficulties lasting at least 6 months despite targeted help, achievement well below age level on standardised tests, onset in the school years, and no better explanation in intellectual disability, sensory loss or poor teaching. Intelligence is typically average. In India, the RPwD Act 2016 lists dysgraphia among specific learning disabilities, which are certified from age 8. Example: Arjun, aged 9, explains a science chapter fluently aloud, but his written answers are cramped and hard to read, words like \"becuase\" recur, full stops are missing and his ideas come out in a jumble. His reading and arithmetic are fine. Option b is right because the stem names writing on every count: the motor act (handwriting), the mechanics (spelling and punctuation) and the composition (written expression). Exam trap: spelling problems also appear in dyslexia, so examiners hope you will grab the more famous term. Dyslexia is defined by trouble reading words accurately and fluently; when the list is all about producing written text, the answer is dysgraphia. Do not confuse it with dyspraxia or apraxia, which are problems of motor planning.", "topic": "Dysgraphia: writing disorder"}, {"q": "Which sequence correctly represents the WHO classification of Intellectual Disability from least severe to most severe ?", "options": {"a": "Moderate → Mild → Severe → Profound", "b": "Mild → Moderate → Severe → Profound", "c": "Mild → Severe → Moderate → Profound", "d": "Profound → Severe → Moderate → Mild"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the WHO grades intellectual disability, from least to most severe, as mild, moderate, severe and profound. Intellectual disability means significant limitation in intellectual functioning and in adaptive behaviour, beginning in the developmental period. ICD-10 (1992), the WHO classification most exam questions draw on, sets the four levels by IQ: mild 50 to 69, moderate 35 to 49, severe 20 to 34 and profound below 20. ICD-11 (2019) renamed the condition disorders of intellectual development and kept the same four levels, now defined by standard deviations below the mean on intelligence and adaptive behaviour: mild about 2 to 3 SD below, moderate about 3 to 4, and severe and profound beyond 4, told apart by adaptive skills. DSM-5 (2013) also keeps the four labels but grades them by adaptive functioning, since that decides how much support a person needs. About 85 percent of cases are mild and are often first noticed at school; severe and profound levels usually show as delayed milestones in the first two years. Example: Meera, aged 10, has an IQ of 45; she can dress and feed herself, speaks in short sentences and needs supervision outside home, so she falls in the moderate band. Her classmate with an IQ of 62, who reads simple text, sits in the mild band. Option b is right because it is the only option that rises steadily in severity: mild, then moderate, then severe, then profound. Exam trap: d is the same list reversed (most to least severe), and a and c scramble the middle of the scale. Read the direction asked for, and remember that moderate always sits between mild and severe.", "topic": "Intellectual disability severity levels"}, {"q": "Which neuroimaging technique is preferred for repeated brain imaging in research participants because it does not expose them to ionizing radiation ?", "options": {"a": "PET", "b": "CT", "c": "MRI", "d": "SPECT"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: MRI is preferred for repeated scans of research participants because it uses no ionising radiation. Ionising radiation, as in X-rays and radioactive tracers, carries enough energy to damage DNA, and the risk adds up with each exposure, so ethics committees limit how often healthy volunteers can be scanned with it. Magnetic resonance imaging works differently. The person lies in a strong magnetic field that lines up the hydrogen nuclei in the body's water; pulses of radio waves knock them out of line, and as they relax they give off signals that differ between grey matter, white matter and fluid. A computer turns these signals into detailed slices with excellent spatial resolution. Paul Lauterbur (1973) made the first MR images, and he shared the 2003 Nobel Prize with Peter Mansfield. Functional MRI adds the BOLD signal described by Seiji Ogawa (1990), which tracks oxygenated blood flowing to active regions, so the same scanner shows activity as well as structure. Example: Kabir joins a two-year study of brain development; he can be scanned every 6 months, structurally and with fMRI, with no cumulative dose. The main cautions are metal implants, pacemakers and claustrophobia, not radiation. Option c is right because MRI relies only on magnetism and radio waves, so repeat imaging carries no radiation risk. Exam trap: PET and SPECT are functional scans, as is fMRI, so students sometimes assume all functional methods are alike. PET and SPECT need injected radioactive tracers and CT uses X-rays; only MRI and fMRI avoid ionising radiation.", "topic": "MRI: no ionising radiation"}, {"q": "Which of the following correctly matches the substance with its characteristic hallucination ?", "options": {"a": "Cocaine - Visual hallucinations", "b": "LSD - Tactile hallucinations", "c": "Cannabis - Visual hallucinations", "d": "Alcohol withdrawal - Gustatory hallucinations"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: of the four pairings offered, cannabis with visual hallucinations is the one that matches. A hallucination is a perception without an external stimulus, experienced as real. Different drugs tend to produce hallucinations in different senses, and clinicians use the sense involved as a clue. Cannabis acts through delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) on cannabinoid receptors. At ordinary doses it brings euphoria, slowed time and sharpened colours and sounds; at high doses, or with potent forms such as charas, it can cause visual distortions and frank visual hallucinations, and DSM-5 (2013) has a specifier, cannabis intoxication with perceptual disturbances. The other pairings are mismatched. Cocaine and amphetamines are known for tactile hallucinations: formication, the feeling of insects crawling under the skin (\"cocaine bugs\"). LSD, whose effects Albert Hofmann (1943) discovered, is the classic visual hallucinogen, giving colours, trails and geometric patterns in clear consciousness. Alcohol withdrawal delirium (delirium tremens) brings vivid visual hallucinations of small animals or insects, often with tactile ones, while gustatory hallucinations suggest temporal lobe epilepsy. Example: after a strong bhang lassi at Holi, Arjun sees the coloured powder leave glowing trails and the walls seem to ripple. Option c is right because visual perceptual disturbance is a recognised effect of high-dose cannabis, while each other option links a substance to the wrong sense. Exam trap: options a and b swap the hallmarks of cocaine (tactile) and LSD (visual), so read each pairing carefully. Key note: cannabis more often distorts perception than causes true hallucinations, and cocaine can sometimes cause visual ones, so c is best read as the least wrong pairing rather than a textbook hallmark. The official key stands.", "topic": "Substance-specific hallucinations"}, {"q": "Phenylketonuria (PKU) primarily results from :", "options": {"a": "Excess insulin secretion", "b": "Inability to metabolize phenylalanine", "c": "Iron deficiency", "d": "Vitamin D deficiency"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: phenylketonuria results from an inability to metabolise the amino acid phenylalanine. Ivar Asbjørn Følling (1934), a Norwegian physician, discovered PKU when he found phenylpyruvic acid in the urine of two intellectually disabled siblings. PKU is an autosomal recessive single-gene disorder: the child inherits a faulty copy of the gene for the liver enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase from each parent. Without the enzyme, phenylalanine from dietary protein cannot be converted into tyrosine. Phenylalanine and its by-products build up in the blood and damage the developing brain, causing intellectual disability, seizures, a musty body odour and pale skin and hair (tyrosine is needed to make melanin). Carriers with one faulty copy are healthy, and two carrier parents face a one in four risk in each pregnancy. PKU is the classic example of a genetic disorder whose outcome depends on the environment. Robert Guthrie (1963) introduced the heel-prick blood test that made newborn screening possible, and a low-phenylalanine diet started in the first weeks prevents almost all of the brain damage. Example: Meera's newborn daughter shows a raised phenylalanine level on her heel-prick screen; she is started on a special formula, later avoids high-protein foods and the sweetener aspartame, and grows up with normal intelligence. Option b is right because the core defect is metabolic: the body cannot break down phenylalanine. Exam trap: PKU is genetic but it is not chromosomal like Down syndrome (trisomy 21); it is a single recessive gene causing an enzyme deficiency. Remember the chain: phenylalanine hydroxylase missing, phenylalanine high, tyrosine low.", "topic": "Phenylketonuria (PKU)"}, {"q": "In psychopathology, obsessions are primarily considered a disorder of :", "options": {"a": "Perception", "b": "Thought content", "c": "Intelligence", "d": "Attention"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: obsessions are classed as a disorder of thought content. Descriptive psychopathology, the method Karl Jaspers (1913) laid down in General Psychopathology, describes thinking under four headings: stream (speed and flow, as in pressure of thought), form (how ideas link, as in flight of ideas or derailment), content (what the person thinks about) and possession (whether thoughts feel like one's own). Content covers delusions, overvalued ideas, preoccupations, phobias and obsessions. An obsession is a recurrent, intrusive and unwanted thought, image or urge that the person recognises as a product of their own mind, finds senseless or distressing, and tries to resist. Aubrey Lewis (1936) stressed this inner resistance as the mark that separates an obsession from a delusion, which is held with conviction and without struggle. Obsessions are ego-dystonic, meaning they clash with the person's values, and in OCD they usually drive compulsions that briefly lower the anxiety. Example: Meera keeps thinking \"my hands are covered in germs\" after touching a door handle. She knows the thought is her own and excessive, tries to push it away, and washes her hands for twenty minutes. The theme of the thought, contamination, is a problem of content. Option b is right because an obsession is defined by what is thought, a fixed intrusive theme, rather than by how perception, intellect or attention works. Exam trap: some Indian case formats file obsessions under possession of thought, because control of thinking is lost, and thought insertion is a possession disorder in which the thought feels alien. Possession is not offered here, so content is the answer, and the patient's insight is what separates an obsession from a delusion.", "topic": "Obsessions as thought content"}, {"q": "The hallmark feature of anorexia nervosa is :", "options": {"a": "Excessive eating", "b": "Fear of gaining weight despite being underweight", "c": "Frequent panic attacks", "d": "Persistent hallucinations"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the hallmark of anorexia nervosa is an intense fear of gaining weight even while the person is underweight. William Gull (1873) in London named the illness anorexia nervosa, and Ernest-Charles Lasègue (1873) in Paris described the same picture as anorexie hystérique. The name misleads: anorexia means loss of appetite, but most patients are hungry and are driven by a fear of fatness. DSM-5 (2013) sets three criteria: restriction of energy intake leading to a significantly low body weight; intense fear of gaining weight or becoming fat, or persistent behaviour that blocks weight gain, even at a low weight; and a disturbance in how weight or shape is experienced, with self-worth resting on them or denial of how serious the low weight is. There are two subtypes, restricting and binge-eating/purging, and adult severity is graded by body mass index, from mild (17 or above) to extreme (below 15). Hilde Bruch (1978), in The Golden Cage, described the typical patient as a compliant, high-achieving girl who feels ineffective and finds in control of her body the one thing that is truly hers. Anorexia has one of the highest death rates of any psychiatric disorder. Example: Riya, 16, weighs far less than she should for her height, counts every calorie, runs for hours and still pinches her stomach in the mirror, saying she is fat. Option b is right because fear of weight gain despite low weight is the core criterion that defines the disorder. Exam trap: DSM-5 dropped amenorrhoea as a criterion, and purging does not rule out anorexia; it defines a subtype. Excessive eating belongs to bulimia nervosa and binge-eating disorder, not anorexia.", "topic": "Anorexia nervosa core feature"}, {"q": "A therapist helps a client identify irrational beliefs such as \"I always fail\" and replace them with more realistic thoughts. This approach reflects :", "options": {"a": "Behaviour therapy", "b": "Cognitive therapy", "c": "Client-centered therapy", "d": "Psychoanalysis"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: helping a client identify irrational thoughts such as \"I always fail\" and replace them with realistic ones is cognitive therapy. Cognitive therapy rests on the idea that it is not events themselves but the way we interpret them that shapes how we feel and act. Aaron Beck (1967) developed it after noticing that his depressed patients had a stream of quick, negative automatic thoughts about themselves, the world and the future, which he called the cognitive triad. These thoughts carry cognitive distortions: \"I always fail\" is overgeneralisation, drawing a sweeping rule from a few events. In therapy, client and therapist treat such thoughts as hypotheses to be tested, an approach called collaborative empiricism. Socratic questions (\"What is the evidence? Is there another way to see this?\") and the dysfunctional thought record help the client spot the thought, weigh the evidence and write a balanced alternative; this is cognitive restructuring. Albert Ellis (1955) built a parallel approach, rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), which disputes irrational beliefs such as \"I must succeed at everything\". Example: Kabir fails one mathematics test and tells himself, \"I always fail.\" His therapist asks him to list his recent results; he sees he passed four of five, and rewrites the thought as \"I failed this test, and I can prepare differently for the next one.\" His low mood eases. Option b is right because the stem describes changing thoughts, the central method of cognitive therapy, not changing behaviour directly or exploring the unconscious. Exam trap: \"irrational beliefs\" is Ellis's phrase, which can make students hesitate, but REBT is itself a cognitive therapy, so the cognitive option is still correct. Behaviour therapy targets actions, not beliefs.", "topic": "Cognitive therapy: restructuring thoughts"}, {"q": "The memory disorder characterized by severe anterograde amnesia and confabulation due to thiamine deficiency is most commonly seen in individuals with chronic :", "options": {"a": "Opioid dependence", "b": "Alcohol dependence", "c": "Nicotine dependence", "d": "Sedative dependence"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the thiamine-deficiency memory disorder with severe anterograde amnesia and confabulation, Korsakoff's syndrome, is most commonly seen in chronic alcohol dependence. Thiamine (vitamin B1) is needed for brain cells to use glucose. Long-term heavy drinkers run short of it for three reasons: poor diet, poor absorption from the gut and poor storage in a damaged liver. Carl Wernicke (1881) described the acute stage, Wernicke's encephalopathy, with a triad of confusion, ataxia (unsteady gait) and eye movement problems such as nystagmus; it is an emergency treated with thiamine. Untreated, many patients pass into the chronic stage described by Sergei Korsakoff (1887): a dense inability to form new memories (anterograde amnesia), patchy loss of older memories (retrograde amnesia) and confabulation, filling memory gaps with invented stories told without any intent to deceive. Attention and general intelligence are relatively spared. Together the two stages form the Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, and post-mortem studies show damage to the mammillary bodies and the dorsomedial thalamus. Example: Rakesh, 58, has drunk heavily for thirty years. On the ward he greets the doctor warmly, cannot recall meeting her an hour earlier, and says he spent the morning at the bank, though he has not left his bed. Option b is right because chronic alcohol use is by far the commonest cause of the thiamine deficiency behind this syndrome. Exam trap: do not confuse Wernicke's encephalopathy (alcohol, thiamine, eye signs) with Wernicke's aphasia (fluent but meaningless speech after damage to the left temporal lobe). Also, giving glucose before thiamine can tip a vulnerable drinker into Wernicke's encephalopathy.", "topic": "Korsakoff's syndrome and alcohol"}, {"q": "In the Bollywood movie 'Bhool Bhulaiya', what is the disorder of the main character ?", "options": {"a": "Dissociative Fugue", "b": "Conversion Disorder", "c": "Dissociative Identity Disorder", "d": "Dissociative Hysteria"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Bhool Bhulaiyaa (2007), the main character's disorder is dissociative identity disorder. In the film, directed by Priyadarshan and remade from the Malayalam Manichitrathazhu (1993), Avni, played by Vidya Balan, moves into an old palace and begins to take on the identity of Manjulika, a dancer from the palace's legend. As Manjulika she speaks and dances differently and turns violent; as Avni she has no memory of these episodes. The psychiatrist, Dr Aditya, played by Akshay Kumar, explains this as a split personality. DSM-5 (2013) defines dissociative identity disorder (DID), called multiple personality disorder until DSM-IV (1994), by two core criteria. First, a disruption of identity into two or more distinct personality states, or an experience of possession, with changes in behaviour, memory and sense of self. Second, recurrent gaps in memory for everyday events or personal information beyond ordinary forgetting. The usual identity is the host, the others are alters, and changing between them is a switch. Corbett Thigpen and Hervey Cleckley (1957) made the disorder famous with The Three Faces of Eve. DSM-5 also notes that in many cultures the alternate identity appears as a possessing spirit or person, which is exactly how Manjulika presents. Option c is right because the film shows separate identities with amnesia between them, the core of DID, not a single episode of flight or a bodily symptom. Exam trap: \"dissociative hysteria\" sounds plausible because older Indian texts used hysteria for dissociative states, but it is not a current diagnosis, and a fugue involves sudden travel with loss of identity, not alternating identities.", "topic": "DID in Bhool Bhulaiyaa"}, {"q": "The cocktail party phenomenon primarily illustrates :", "options": {"a": "Focused attention", "b": "Selective attention", "c": "Sustained attention", "d": "Span of attention"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the cocktail party phenomenon illustrates selective attention. Selective attention is the ability to focus on one source of information while filtering out competing ones. Colin Cherry (1953), a British engineer, named the cocktail party problem: how do we follow one conversation in a room full of voices? He tested it with dichotic listening, playing a different message to each ear and asking people to repeat one aloud, which is called shadowing. They recalled almost nothing of the meaning of the unattended message but noticed physical changes, such as a male voice turning female. Neville Moray (1959) then showed that about a third of listeners noticed their own name in the ignored ear, the effect most people mean by the cocktail party phenomenon. This troubled Donald Broadbent's (1958) filter model, which blocked unattended input completely, and led Anne Treisman (1964) to propose an attenuator that only turns ignored input down, so highly relevant words such as one's name can still break through. Example: at a crowded sangeet, Riya listens to her cousin's story and hears the music only as noise, yet she turns at once when someone across the room says \"Riya\". Option b is right because the phenomenon shows us picking one stream out of many and filtering the rest, which is the definition of selective attention. Exam trap: sustained attention is about duration and span of attention is about how many items we take in at once; neither involves picking one voice from many. Key note: some textbooks, notably Michael Eysenck and Mark Keane's Cognitive Psychology (1990), use \"focused attention\" as another name for selective attention and discuss the cocktail party problem under that heading, so a has a defence. Selective attention remains the standard label, and the official key stands.", "topic": "Cocktail party: selective attention"}, {"q": "Piaget's Three-Mountain Task was primarily designed to assess :", "options": {"a": "Conservation", "b": "Egocentrism", "c": "Object permanence", "d": "Moral reasoning"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the three mountains task of Jean Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder (1956) was designed to assess egocentrism. They described it in The Child's Conception of Space. A child sat in front of a model of three mountains, one capped with snow, one with a house and one with a red cross. A doll was placed at another side of the table, and the child chose, from a set of pictures, the view the doll would see. Four-year-olds usually picked the picture of their own view, and correct choices became common only around 7 to 8 years. Piaget read this as egocentrism: the preoperational child (about 2 to 7) cannot yet take another person's point of view and assumes everyone sees the world as they do. Egocentrism also shows in collective monologues, in which children talk past each other. Later work softened the finding. Martin Hughes (1975) set a game in which a boy doll had to hide from policemen dolls; about 90 percent of children aged 3.5 to 5 succeeded, because the task made human sense. Margaret Donaldson (1978) argued that Piaget's tasks underestimated young children. Example: four-year-old Arjun, asked what his grandmother sees from across the table, points to the picture of the mountain with the house, the one facing him. Option b is right because the task tests whether a child can take another person's visual perspective, and failing it is the classic sign of egocentrism. Exam trap: do not confuse it with conservation tasks, such as the beakers of water, which come from the same preoperational stage but test the understanding that quantity stays the same when appearance changes.", "topic": "Three mountains task: egocentrism"}, {"q": "Which of the following theorist - animal pairs is incorrectly matched ?", "options": {"a": "Edward C. Tolman - Rat", "b": "Edward L. Thorndike - Cat", "c": "Ivan Pavlov - Dog", "d": "Edward C. Tolman - Chimpanzee"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Edward Chace Tolman (1930) studied rats, not chimpanzees; the chimpanzee belongs to Wolfgang Köhler (1917). This is a \"not\" question, so three pairings are true and one is false. Ivan Pavlov (1897) used dogs: by measuring their salivation he found that a neutral sound paired with food came to trigger salivation on its own, the basis of classical conditioning. Edward Lee Thorndike (1898) used hungry cats in puzzle boxes: escape times fell gradually across trials, which he explained by the law of effect, the idea that rewarded responses are stamped in. Tolman used rats in mazes: in the latent learning study with Charles Honzik (1930), rats that explored a maze unrewarded for ten days ran it as well as rewarded rats the day after food appeared, showing they had formed a cognitive map. So a, b and c are correct. The chimpanzee studies belong to Köhler, a Gestalt psychologist working on Tenerife. His chimpanzee Sultan joined two sticks to reach a banana outside the cage, solving the problem suddenly, which Köhler called insight learning. Example: Meera remembers the pairs as a small zoo: Pavlov's dog salivates, Thorndike's cat escapes the box, Tolman's rat maps the maze, and Köhler's chimp stacks boxes for the banana. Option d is right because Tolman never based his theory on chimpanzees; the pairing mixes up Tolman's rats with Köhler's apes. Exam trap: when the same theorist appears in two options, one of the two must be the wrong pairing, so ask which animal he really used. Also do not confuse Köhler with Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), the moral development theorist, or with Harry Harlow (1949), whose monkeys showed learning sets.", "topic": "Learning theorists and animals"}, {"q": "Which of the following forms of intelligence is most likely to decrease with normal aging, while accumulated knowledge and vocabulary remain relatively stable ?", "options": {"a": "Fluid intelligence", "b": "Crystallized intelligence", "c": "Spatial intelligence", "d": "Emotional intelligence"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: fluid intelligence is the ability most likely to decline with normal ageing, while accumulated knowledge and vocabulary stay stable. Raymond Cattell (1963) split general intelligence into two parts, and his student John Horn (1966) developed the idea with him. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve new problems on the spot, independent of what one has learned: seeing patterns, holding information in mind and working out rules. Raven's Progressive Matrices is its classic test. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills built up through education and experience, measured by vocabulary and general information tests. The two follow different paths across the lifespan. Fluid ability peaks in the early twenties and then falls steadily, partly because of what Timothy Salthouse (1996) called a general slowing of processing speed. Crystallised ability keeps growing or holds steady into the sixties and seventies. K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) showed that cross-sectional studies exaggerate the decline, yet the pattern of fluid loss and crystallised stability remains. Example: Arjun's grandfather, 72, struggles to learn a new phone app and is slow on a puzzle he has never seen, yet he still beats the family at crosswords and knows the meaning of rare words. Option a is right because fluid reasoning and speed are the abilities that age most, while the knowledge and vocabulary the stem calls stable are crystallised. Exam trap: students often reverse the two; crystallised is the stable one. Also, Raymond Cattell, who proposed fluid and crystallised intelligence, is not James McKeen Cattell (1890), who coined the term \"mental test\".", "topic": "Fluid intelligence and ageing"}, {"q": "In the context of problem solving, what is the moment when the problem's solution comes to us suddenly ?", "options": {"a": "Orientation", "b": "Illumination", "c": "Elaboration", "d": "Action-orientation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the moment when a solution suddenly comes to mind is called illumination. Graham Wallas (1926), in The Art of Thought, described four stages of creative problem solving. In preparation the person defines the problem, gathers information and works on it consciously, often without success. In incubation the problem is set aside, and work is thought to go on outside awareness. In illumination the answer arrives suddenly, the familiar \"aha\" or \"eureka\" moment, often while the person is doing something else. In verification the idea is checked, tested and refined. Wallas drew partly on the mathematician Henri Poincaré (1908), who described the solution to a problem that had defeated him arriving as he stepped onto a bus. The same sudden restructuring was seen in Wolfgang Köhler's (1917) chimpanzees and is called insight. Janet Metcalfe and David Wiebe (1987) showed that on insight problems, people's ratings of how close they are to a solution stay flat until just before the answer appears, unlike the steady rise on routine algebra. Example: Meera spends an evening stuck on a logic puzzle and gives up; the next morning, while brushing her teeth, the whole answer flashes into her mind. Option b is right because illumination is the stage defined by the sudden arrival of the solution. Exam trap: incubation, the stage just before, is when the problem is set aside, and examiners may offer it in place of illumination. The order is preparation, incubation, illumination, verification; remember \"Please Incubate Ideas Very carefully\".", "topic": "Wallas: illumination stage"}, {"q": "A small child is unable to think of various ways in which a screwdriver can be used. What is the mental set being shown here ?", "options": {"a": "Functional fixedness", "b": "Barnum effect", "c": "Hindsight bias", "d": "Availability heuristic"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: being unable to think of other ways to use a screwdriver is functional fixedness. Functional fixedness is the tendency to see an object only in terms of its usual function, which blocks creative uses in problem solving. It is a specific kind of mental set, a readiness to approach a problem in one fixed way. Karl Duncker (1945) showed it with the candle problem: people given a candle, matches and a box of drawing pins had to fix the candle to a wall. Few thought of emptying the box and pinning it up as a shelf, but many more did when the box was handed over empty, because then it no longer looked like a container. Abraham Luchins (1942) showed the broader mental set, or Einstellung, with water jar problems: people drilled in one long formula kept using it when a far simpler one would do. Example: six-year-old Kabir needs to open a tin of paint and is handed a screwdriver; he insists it is only for screws and never thinks of using it as a lever, a stirrer or a paperweight. Interestingly, Tim German and Margaret Defeyter (2000) found that 5-year-olds were less fixed than 6 to 7-year-olds, because the idea of what an object is for develops with age. Option a is right because the child's block is about seeing only one use for an object, which is the definition of functional fixedness. Exam trap: the stem says \"mental set\", which may send students looking for Einstellung, but functional fixedness is the form of mental set that concerns objects, and it is the only option here that blocks problem solving at all.", "topic": "Functional fixedness"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"gurugram-2026-ma": [{"q": "Korsakoff syndrome results mostly from which kind of amnesia ?", "concept": "The answer is c: Korsakoff syndrome is an alcohol-related amnesia, so of the types listed it belongs under drug and alcohol amnesia. Sergei Korsakoff (1887), a Russian psychiatrist, described it in people with long-term heavy drinking. The direct cause is a lack of thiamine (vitamin B1). Heavy drinkers eat poorly and absorb thiamine badly, and brain cells that depend on it die. The damage falls on the mammillary bodies and the mediodorsal (dorsomedial) thalamus, parts of the circuit that lays down new memories. It often follows Wernicke's encephalopathy, described by Carl Wernicke (1881): sudden confusion, an unsteady gait (ataxia) and jerky eye movements (nystagmus). Together they form the Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. The memory picture has three parts. Dense anterograde amnesia means new facts cannot be stored. Retrograde amnesia is patchy and worse for recent years than for distant ones. Confabulation means the person sincerely fills memory gaps with invented stories. Attention, old skills and general intelligence are fairly well kept. Example: Arjun, 52, has drunk heavily for twenty years. He cannot recall his doctor's name ten minutes after meeting her, yet calmly describes a trip to the market that morning that never happened. Early thiamine injections can stop the Wernicke stage from becoming permanent Korsakoff amnesia. Option c is right because only drug and alcohol amnesia names the cause of Korsakoff syndrome. The stem is loosely worded (the syndrome does not result from an amnesia; it is one), but the intended link is alcohol. Exam trap: examiners set confabulation beside lying or malingering. Confabulation is honest gap filling, not deliberate faking. A second trap is calling Korsakoff a degenerative dementia; it is a nutritional brain injury that mainly hits memory.", "traps": {"a": "Transient global amnesia is a sudden, temporary loss of the ability to form new memories that lasts hours and then clears fully, often after exertion or emotional stress in older adults; it has nothing to do with alcohol or thiamine.", "b": "Defensive (psychogenic or dissociative) amnesia is forgetting of stressful personal information for psychological reasons, as in dissociative amnesia; Korsakoff amnesia has a physical cause, brain damage from thiamine deficiency.", "d": "Primary degenerative amnesia points to progressive brain diseases such as Alzheimer's disease, where memory loss is part of a spreading dementia; Korsakoff syndrome comes from alcohol-linked nutritional injury and does not keep progressing once drinking stops and thiamine is given."}}, {"q": "In which year was tip of the tongue phenomenon given by Brown and McNeill ?", "concept": "The answer is a: Roger Brown and David McNeill reported their study of the tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon in 1966. The tip-of-the-tongue (TOT) state is the feeling that a word is stored in memory and about to come, yet it will not come out. Roger Brown and David McNeill (1966) created this state in the laboratory. They read Harvard students the dictionary definitions of rare words, such as \"a navigational instrument used to measure angles of the sun and stars\" (sextant). Whenever a student felt the word was on the tip of the tongue, they wrote down whatever they could. Students often knew the first letter, the number of syllables and where the stress fell. They also offered similar-sounding words (secant, sextet) or similar-meaning ones (astrolabe, compass). So the word was partly accessible: some of its sound and meaning features were available, but the full form could not be retrieved. The study showed that much forgetting is a failure of retrieval, not a loss of storage. It also links to metamemory, knowledge about one's own memory, named by John Flavell (1971), because people in a TOT state have a strong feeling of knowing. Example: Meera sees a photo of a film actor, knows his name starts with R and has three syllables, but recalls it only on the bus home. Option a is right because the paper, \"The tip of the tongue phenomenon\", appeared in the Journal of Verbal Learning and Verbal Behavior in 1966. Exam trap: the year sits close to other famous memory dates. Roger Brown's flashbulb memory paper with James Kulik is from 1977, and George Miller's \"magical number seven\" paper is from 1956, the option examiners use to catch students who guess at a well-known memory year.", "traps": {"b": "1976 is ten years too late; no tip-of-the-tongue landmark comes from that year, and Roger Brown's other famous memory paper, on flashbulb memory with James Kulik, is from 1977.", "c": "1986 is twenty years too late; it is the year of David Rubin's work on the reminiscence bump in autobiographical memory, a different phenomenon.", "d": "1956 is the year of George Miller's paper on the \"magical number seven, plus or minus two\" in short-term memory, ten years before Brown and McNeill."}}, {"q": "The concept of episodic memory was given by :", "concept": "The answer is b: Endel Tulving (1972), an Estonian-Canadian psychologist, introduced the concept of episodic memory. In a chapter titled \"Episodic and semantic memory\", he split long-term declarative memory into two systems. Episodic memory stores personal events tied to a time and place: what happened, where and when. Semantic memory stores general facts and meanings, with no record of when they were learned. Tulving (1985) later added that episodic recall carries autonoetic consciousness, a sense of mentally travelling back in time and re-living the moment, while semantic knowing is noetic, simply knowing. Procedural memory, a third long-term system, holds skills such as riding a bicycle. Evidence for the split comes from brain damage. Tulving's patient K.C., injured in a motorcycle accident in 1981, kept his general knowledge and language but could not recall a single personal episode from his life. Example: Kabir remembers that on his first day of college he spilled tea on his shirt in the canteen; that is episodic memory. Kabir knows that Delhi is the capital of India; that is semantic memory. Option b is right because the terms episodic and semantic memory were coined and defined by Tulving. Exam trap: semantic memory is linked to Ross Quillian, whose hierarchical network model (1968, tested with Allan Collins in 1969) described how facts are stored, so students who remember \"Quillian\" and \"memory\" together may pick him. Quillian modelled semantic knowledge; he did not propose the episodic system. Another trap is crediting Tulving with levels of processing, which belongs to Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972); Tulving only tested it later with Craik (1975).", "traps": {"a": "Ross Quillian built the hierarchical semantic network model (1968; with Allan Collins, 1969), in which concepts are linked nodes; it describes how general knowledge is organised, not the episodic system.", "c": "Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning with salivating dogs in the early 1900s; his work is on learning by stimulus pairing, not on systems of long-term memory.", "d": "Raymond Nickerson is known for the penny-recognition study with Marilyn Adams (1979), showing how poorly we encode familiar objects, and for a major review of confirmation bias (1998); he did not propose episodic memory."}}, {"q": "Memory interference resulting from activities subsequent to the events you are trying to remember is called :", "concept": "The answer is a: retroactive interference is forgetting caused by new learning that comes after the material you are trying to remember. Interference theory holds that much forgetting happens because memories compete, not because traces simply fade. John McGeoch (1932) argued that interference, not disuse, explains forgetting, and that it is strongest when old and new material are similar. Direction matters. In retroactive interference, new learning works backwards (\"retro\") and disrupts recall of old learning. In proactive interference, old learning works forwards (\"pro\") and disrupts recall of new learning. A classic study is John Jenkins and Karl Dallenbach (1924). Students learned nonsense syllables and were tested after one to eight hours spent either asleep or awake. Recall was better after sleep, because fewer new experiences came in to interfere. The standard laboratory design is simple. The experimental group learns list A, then list B, then is tested on A. The control group learns A, rests, then is tested on A. Poorer recall of A in the experimental group shows retroactive interference. Example: Riya changes her phone number. A month later she tries to give her old number to the bank and keeps saying the new one; the new number has interfered with the old. Option a is right because the stem describes interference from activities subsequent to (after) the events being remembered, which is the definition of retroactive interference. Exam trap: students mix up the two directions. Ask which memory is lost. If the old memory is lost, the interference is retroactive; if the new memory is lost, it is proactive. Riya forgetting her new number because the old one keeps intruding would be proactive interference.", "traps": {"b": "Proactive interference runs the other way: earlier learning disrupts recall of later material (Benton Underwood, 1957, showed it explains much forgetting of word lists); the stem says the disrupting activities come after the events.", "c": "An engram is the hypothetical physical memory trace in the brain, a term coined by Richard Semon (1904) and searched for by Karl Lashley (1950); it is a storage idea, not a type of interference.", "d": "None of these cannot be right because retroactive interference matches the stem's definition exactly."}}, {"q": "Sandhya, a child of 6 years, was bit by rabbits and due to that was taken to a doctor's office to get injection. Sandhya after that became afraid of rabbits and doctors. On Christmas eve, she went to a park where she saw Santa Claus and started panicking and crying. What kind of learning process best describes her fear of Santa Claus ?", "concept": "The answer is d: Sandhya's fear of Santa Claus is stimulus generalisation, the spread of a conditioned response to new stimuli that resemble the original conditioned stimulus. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927), a neutral stimulus paired with an unconditioned stimulus comes to trigger a conditioned response on its own. Pavlov found that a dog conditioned to one tone also salivated, less strongly, to similar tones. The more a new stimulus resembles the original, the stronger the response; this is the generalisation gradient. John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) showed the same for fear with Little Albert. An 11-month-old boy was shown a white rat while a steel bar was struck loudly behind him. After a few pairings he cried at the rat alone. He then also showed distress at a rabbit, a dog, a fur coat, cotton wool and a Santa Claus mask with a white beard. In Sandhya's case, the bite and the injection were painful unconditioned stimuli; rabbits and doctors became conditioned stimuli for fear. Santa Claus shares features with both: white fur trim, a fluffy white beard and an adult stranger who comes close. Her fear spread to him without any new painful event. Option d is right because the question asks for the learning process that explains fear of a new stimulus that was never paired with pain but resembles feared ones. Exam trap: \"Phobia\" is tempting because the fear seems irrational, but a phobia is a clinical outcome or label, not a learning process. Generalisation is the process; a phobia may be the result. Its opposite, discrimination, is learning to respond to one stimulus but not to similar ones.", "traps": {"a": "A phobia is an intense, irrational fear that can become a clinical disorder (specific phobia in DSM-5); it names the outcome, not the learning process that spread the fear to Santa Claus.", "b": "Stimulus discrimination, also from Ivan Pavlov's work, is learning to respond only to the conditioned stimulus and not to similar ones; had Sandhya discriminated, she would have stayed calm with Santa Claus.", "c": "Behaviour modification is the applied use of conditioning principles, built on B. F. Skinner's operant work, to change behaviour; it is a therapy approach, not the process that created her fear."}}, {"q": "Who among the following was the pioneering for positive reinforcement principle studies ?", "concept": "The answer is c: B. F. Skinner (1938) pioneered the systematic study of positive reinforcement in operant conditioning. Operant conditioning is learning in which a behaviour becomes more or less frequent because of its consequences. Burrhus Frederic Skinner defined reinforcement by its effect: any consequence that makes a response more likely is a reinforcer. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant after the response (food, praise, money). Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a loud noise stops when a lever is pressed). Both increase behaviour; punishment decreases it. Skinner built the operant chamber, later called the Skinner box. A hungry rat pressed a lever, a food pellet dropped, and a cumulative recorder drew its rate of responding over time. In The Behavior of Organisms (1938) and Schedules of Reinforcement with Charles Ferster (1957), he showed how the timing of reinforcers shapes behaviour. He applied these ideas to teaching machines, token economies and shaping, which builds new behaviour by reinforcing successive approximations. Example: a teacher gives Arjun a star each time he hands in homework on time, and his on-time submissions rise; that is positive reinforcement. Option c is right because Skinner built the operant framework, separated positive from negative reinforcement and made reinforcement the centre of a whole research programme. Exam trap: Edward Thorndike came first, with his puzzle-box cats (1898) and the law of effect (1911), so he is often called the father of instrumental learning. But Thorndike spoke of \"satisfying\" consequences and did not frame positive reinforcement as Skinner did. Key note: some books call Thorndike the pioneer because the law of effect is the root idea behind reinforcement. The stem asks about positive reinforcement principle studies, which are Skinner's, so the official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Edward Thorndike (1898, 1911) studied cats escaping puzzle boxes and stated the law of effect, the forerunner of reinforcement; he pioneered instrumental learning, but the operant study of positive reinforcement is Skinner's.", "b": "Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning, in which one stimulus is paired with another; he used \"reinforcement\" for the unconditioned stimulus, but his work is about stimulus pairing, not consequences of behaviour.", "d": "Herbert M. Jenkins, with Paul Brown (1968), discovered autoshaping: pigeons began pecking a lit key that merely predicted food, a classical conditioning effect inside an operant setting; he did not pioneer positive reinforcement."}}, {"q": "In a signal detection task, an observer's sensitivity remains constant, but they adopt a highly conservative response criterion. What is the expected outcome ?", "concept": "The answer is b: with a highly conservative criterion and unchanged sensitivity, the observer says \"yes\" less often, so both hits and false alarms fall. Signal detection theory (SDT), brought into psychology by David Green and John Swets (1966), separates two things that the old threshold idea mixed together. Sensitivity (d prime) is how well the observer can tell signal from noise; it depends on signal strength and the observer's senses. The response criterion (beta or c) is how much evidence the observer demands before saying \"yes, a signal was there\"; it depends on expectations, motives and payoffs. Each trial has four possible outcomes: hit (signal present, \"yes\"), miss (present, \"no\"), false alarm (absent, \"yes\") and correct rejection (absent, \"no\"). A conservative observer sets the bar high. Saying \"yes\" only when quite sure gives fewer false alarms but also fewer hits, and so more misses and more correct rejections. A liberal observer does the reverse: more hits and more false alarms. Moving the criterion slides the observer along one receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve; only a change in sensitivity moves them onto a different curve. Example: Dr Meera reads chest X-rays for tuberculosis. After a complaint about unnecessary treatment, she decides to report TB only when the shadow is very clear. Her eyes have not changed, but she now misses some real cases (fewer hits) and wrongly flags fewer healthy patients (fewer false alarms). Option b is right because a pure criterion shift moves hits and false alarms in the same direction. Exam trap: option c (more hits, fewer false alarms) is what better sensitivity looks like, not a criterion change. Hits and false alarms move in opposite directions only when d prime itself changes.", "traps": {"a": "Hits and false alarms both rising is the signature of a liberal criterion, an observer who says \"yes\" readily, like a smoke alarm; it is the mirror image of the conservative case in the stem.", "c": "More hits with fewer false alarms means the observer separates signal from noise better, that is, sensitivity (d prime) has risen; the stem says sensitivity stays constant.", "d": "Fewer hits with more false alarms means the observer has become worse at telling signal from noise, a fall in sensitivity; a criterion shift alone cannot produce this pattern."}}, {"q": "An individual fails to solve a complex puzzle because they keep applying a rule that worked on previous, simpler problems, even though a shorter alternative is available. This illustrates :", "concept": "The answer is b: repeating a rule that worked on earlier, simpler problems when a shorter route is available is negative transfer caused by a mental set. A mental set (German Einstellung) is a readiness to attack a problem in the way that worked before. Transfer of training is the effect of earlier learning on new learning: positive when it helps, negative when it gets in the way. A mental set produces negative transfer when the old method no longer suits the new problem. The classic study is the water-jar experiment of Abraham Luchins (1942). Participants used three jars of different sizes (A, B and C) to measure out an exact amount of water. A run of early problems could be solved only by the long formula B minus A minus 2C. Later problems could also be solved by a much simpler formula, A minus C or A plus C. Most people who had practised the long formula kept using it, and many failed a problem that could be solved only by the short one. A control group that skipped the practice problems found the short solutions easily. Example: Kabir solves ten worksheet problems by long division. Problem eleven only needs halving, but he does long division again and runs out of time. Option b is right because the stem describes a previously successful rule blocking a simpler solution, which is exactly Luchins's Einstellung effect. Exam trap: functional fixedness is mental set's close cousin. Both are blocks to problem solving that come from past experience, but functional fixedness concerns an object's usual use, while mental set concerns a habitual method or rule. The stem talks about a rule, not an object, so the answer is mental set.", "traps": {"a": "Functional fixedness, shown by Karl Duncker (1945) in the candle problem, is failing to see an object in any role but its usual one (a box only as a container); the stem is about a problem-solving rule, not an object.", "c": "Retroactive interference is a memory effect in which new learning disrupts recall of older learning (John Jenkins and Karl Dallenbach, 1924); it explains forgetting, not a failure to find a shorter solution.", "d": "\"Proactive confirmation bias\" is not a recognised term; confirmation bias, studied by Peter Wason (1960), is seeking evidence that supports one's belief, a reasoning error rather than the habit of reusing an old method."}}, {"q": "Based on the Schachter-Singer Two-Factor Theory of emotion, if an individual is covertly injected with epinephrine and placed in a room with an angry confederate, how will they interpret their physiological arousal ?", "concept": "The answer is c: in the Schachter-Singer theory, an uninformed person aroused by epinephrine and placed with an angry companion labels the arousal as anger. Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962) proposed the two-factor theory. Emotion needs two ingredients: physiological arousal and a cognitive label for that arousal. Arousal on its own is general, much the same for fear, anger or joy, so we decide which emotion we feel by searching the situation for a reason. In their experiment, male students were told they were testing a vitamin called \"Suproxin\". Most were actually injected with epinephrine (adrenaline), which raises heart rate and causes trembling and flushing. Some were correctly told what side effects to expect (informed); others were told nothing (uninformed). A placebo group got a saline injection. Each then waited with a confederate who acted either euphoric or angry, the angry one complaining loudly about an insulting questionnaire. Uninformed participants had arousal with no explanation, so they took on the confederate's mood and acted more euphoric or more angry. Informed participants blamed the injection and were little affected. In the anger condition the effect showed more clearly in behaviour than in self-ratings, and later replications (Gary Marshall and Philip Zimbardo, 1979) were mixed, but the theory's prediction is clear. Example: Riya drinks three strong coffees without realising it and sits beside a furious colleague; she finds herself feeling irritated too. Option c is right because the injection was covert, so the person has no explanation for the arousal and uses the angry confederate as the cue. Exam trap: option b is what the informed group did; they attributed their arousal to the injection. Always check whether the injection was covert or disclosed.", "traps": {"a": "Feeling calm through appraisal would need a calming cue; appraisal theories (Magda Arnold, 1960; Richard Lazarus, 1966) stress interpretation, but here the arousal is real and the only cue available is an angry person.", "b": "Attributing the arousal to the injection is what Schachter and Singer's informed group did, because they had been told the side effects; the stem says the injection was covert, so no such explanation is available.", "d": "Euphoria was the label adopted by uninformed participants paired with a euphoric confederate; with an angry confederate the cue points to anger, and the theory denies that arousal alone produces any specific feeling."}}, {"q": "Which component of Robert Sternberg's Triarchic Theory of Intelligence is primarily responsible for the executive processes involved in planning, monitoring and evaluating problem-solving strategies ?", "concept": "The answer is d: metacomponents are the executive processes in Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory (1985) that plan, monitor and evaluate problem solving. In Beyond IQ, Sternberg argued that intelligence has three parts, or subtheories. The componential (analytical) subtheory describes the mental machinery inside the head. The experiential (creative) subtheory covers how we handle novel tasks and how practice makes skills automatic. The contextual (practical) subtheory covers how we adapt to, shape or select our environment. The componential part contains three kinds of components. Metacomponents are higher-order processes: recognising what the problem is, choosing a strategy, allocating time, monitoring progress and judging the result. Performance components carry out the chosen strategy, for example encoding the terms of a problem, inferring relations and applying them. Knowledge-acquisition components learn new information by selecting what is relevant, combining it and comparing it with what is already known. Sternberg likened metacomponents to a manager who directs the workers (performance components) and the trainers (knowledge-acquisition components). Example: before an exam, Meera decides to answer the short questions first, keeps an eye on the clock and switches strategy when one question takes too long; those are metacomponents at work. Writing the actual answers uses performance components. Option d is right because planning, monitoring and evaluating are exactly what Sternberg assigned to metacomponents. Exam trap: \"executive\" points to metacomponents, not to performance components, even though performance components seem to \"perform\" the task. A second trap is mixing levels: contextual adaptation belongs to the practical subtheory and is about fitting the outside world, not internal executive control. Metacomponents overlap with metacognition, the knowledge and control of one's own thinking described by John Flavell (1979).", "traps": {"a": "Performance components are the lower-order processes that execute a strategy, such as encoding and comparing; they do the work but do not plan or monitor it.", "b": "Knowledge-acquisition components are the processes used to learn new information (selective encoding, combination and comparison); they gather knowledge rather than direct problem solving.", "c": "Contextual adaptation belongs to Sternberg's contextual or practical subtheory, adjusting to the environment alongside shaping and selecting it; it concerns real-world fit, not internal executive control."}}, {"q": "According to the investment theory of creativity by Sternberg and Lubart, creative individuals are best described as those who ?", "concept": "The answer is c: in the investment theory of creativity, creative people \"buy low and sell high\" in the world of ideas. Robert Sternberg and Todd Lubart (1991), and later their book Defying the Crowd (1995), borrowed a stock-market metaphor. A smart investor buys shares when they are cheap and unpopular and sells them once their value has risen. A creative person does the same with ideas. They take up an idea that is new and out of favour, one that others dismiss or even mock (buying low). They develop it and persuade others of its worth. Once the idea is accepted and popular, they move on to the next unpopular idea (selling high). Creativity, on this view, is partly a decision to defy the crowd. The theory says creativity needs six resources working together: intellectual abilities (seeing problems in new ways, judging which ideas are worth pursuing and selling them to others), knowledge, thinking styles (a liking for thinking in new ways), personality (willingness to take sensible risks, tolerance of ambiguity, persistence), intrinsic motivation and a supportive environment. Example: Kabir, a psychology student, studies mental health apps in 2015 while his professors call them a fad. By 2022 the field is crowded and respected, so he shifts to a new, unfashionable question. Option c is right because it matches both halves of the metaphor: novel but unpopular ideas, then moving on once they gain acceptance. Exam trap: option a reverses the metaphor. Buying high, chasing ideas that are already popular, is conformity, not creativity. Also do not confuse this with Raymond Cattell's (1971) investment theory of intelligence, in which fluid intelligence is \"invested\" in learning and becomes crystallised intelligence.", "traps": {"a": "Buying high and selling low by pursuing traditional ideas reverses the metaphor; following ideas that are already accepted is conformity, the opposite of what Sternberg and Lubart describe.", "b": "The theory says creative people take sensible risks on ideas they judge to be valuable, in every field, not only art; deliberately chasing high-risk, low-payoff ventures would be poor investing, not creativity.", "d": "Sternberg and Lubart describe creativity as an investment of intellectual resources (abilities, knowledge, thinking styles), so avoiding investment contradicts the theory's central idea."}}, {"q": "In Cattell's trait theory of personality, source traits represent :", "concept": "The answer is a: in Raymond Cattell's trait theory, source traits are the underlying structures that form the core dimensions of personality. Raymond Cattell (1946, 1950) built his theory from data rather than clinical hunches. He started from the lexical work of Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936), who listed about 18,000 English words that describe people. Cattell reduced this list and gathered three kinds of data: L-data (life records and ratings by others), Q-data (questionnaires) and T-data (objective tests). He then used factor analysis, a statistical method that finds which measures vary together. Surface traits are clusters of observable behaviours that seem to go together, such as being talkative, smiling and joining groups. They are easy to see but less stable, and each may arise from several deeper causes. Source traits are the underlying factors that produce those surface clusters. They are more stable and form the building blocks of personality. Cattell identified 16 source traits and measured them with the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949), whose factors include A (warmth), C (emotional stability) and E (dominance). He also divided source traits into constitutional ones, rooted in heredity, and environmental-mould ones, shaped by experience. Example: Arjun chats with strangers, cracks jokes and organises outings; these surface traits may all spring from one source trait, warmth. Option a is right because it describes source traits as underlying structural variables, the core dimensions revealed by factor analysis. Exam trap: examiners describe surface traits (visible behaviour clusters) and label them source traits. Remember: the surface is what you see; the source is where it comes from. And source traits are found through factor analysis, not beyond its reach.", "traps": {"b": "Unconscious motives belong to psychoanalytic theory (Sigmund Freud) and to Henry Murray's needs, not to Cattell; his source traits are, by definition, discovered and measured through factor analysis.", "c": "Visible behaviour clusters describe Cattell's surface traits, the observable layer; source traits are the stable factors beneath them, so this option describes the wrong level.", "d": "Purely situational adaptations reflect situationism, Walter Mischel's (1968) challenge to trait theory; Cattell's source traits are stable dispositions that hold across situations, the opposite idea."}}, {"q": "According to the Whorfian hypothesis (Linguistic Relativity), how does language influence human cognition ?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Whorfian hypothesis holds that the structure of a person's language shapes or constrains how they think about and perceive the world. It is named after Benjamin Lee Whorf (1940), a fire-insurance inspector and amateur linguist, and his teacher Edward Sapir (1929), so it is also called the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis. Whorf noticed that workers smoked carelessly around \"empty\" petrol drums because the word \"empty\" suggested no danger, though the drums held explosive vapour. He also argued that the Hopi language treats time differently from English, so Hopi speakers must think about time differently. There are two versions. The strong version, linguistic determinism, says language determines thought, so we cannot think what our language cannot express. The weak version, linguistic relativity, says language influences thought, making some distinctions easier or faster. Evidence has gone against the strong version. Eleanor Rosch (publishing as Eleanor Heider, 1972) found that the Dani of New Guinea, who have only two basic colour terms, still remembered focal colours better than non-focal ones, just like English speakers. The weak version has support: Russian speakers, who have separate basic words for light and dark blue, tell those shades apart faster (Jonathan Winawer and colleagues, 2007). Example: Meera speaks Hindi, which separates chacha (father's younger brother) from mama (mother's brother), so she may notice which side of the family an uncle belongs to more readily than an English speaker who just says \"uncle\". Option b is right because it states the core claim: language structure shapes thought. Exam trap: linguistic relativity belongs to Whorf, not to Noam Chomsky (1957; universal grammar) or Jean Piaget (1923; thought comes before language). It is also a claim about thinking, never about brain anatomy.", "traps": {"a": "Whorf made a claim about thinking and perception, not brain anatomy; no version of linguistic relativity says language builds the structure of the visual cortex.", "c": "Thought running entirely free of language is the universalist opposite of Whorf, argued for example by Steven Pinker (1994), who proposed a language-independent \"mentalese\"; it denies the Whorfian claim.", "d": "Language as a passive store for innate, ready-made concepts is close to Jerry Fodor's language of thought (1975) and other nativist views; it reverses Whorf's direction, in which language actively shapes concepts."}}, {"q": "A subject is asked to name the ink color of the word 'BLUE' printed in red ink. The delay in response time observed is known as the Stroop effect which primarily demonstrates the automaticity of :", "concept": "The answer is b: the Stroop effect shows that reading is automatic and takes priority over colour naming. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people to name the ink colour of colour words printed in a conflicting ink, such as RED printed in green. Naming the inks took much longer than naming plain colour patches, about 74 per cent longer in his study, and errors rose. Yet reading the words aloud while ignoring the ink was hardly slowed at all. The explanation lies in automatic versus controlled processing (Walter Schneider and Richard Shiffrin, 1977). Automatic processes are fast, effortless, need little attention and start on their own once triggered. Controlled processes are slow, effortful and need attention. For a literate adult, reading a familiar word is automatic: the meaning of BLUE arrives whether you want it or not. Naming an ink colour is less practised and needs control. So the automatic word response competes with the colour response, and resolving the conflict costs time. This is why children who are only beginning to read show a smaller effect, and why the task is now widely used to measure inhibition and executive function. Example: Kabir sees the word BLUE printed in red ink and must say \"red\". He starts to say \"blue\", stops himself, then says \"red\"; that delay is the Stroop effect. Option b is right because the interference runs from the stronger, automatic process (reading) to the weaker, controlled one (colour naming). Exam trap: option a reverses the direction. Because the task asks people to name colours, students assume colour naming must be the automatic process. In fact colour naming is the slowed, effortful task, and reading is what intrudes on it.", "traps": {"a": "Colour naming over reading reverses the effect; colour naming is the slower, controlled process that suffers interference, while reading the word is the automatic one.", "c": "Spatial mapping over lexical access fits spatial compatibility effects such as the Simon effect (J. Richard Simon, 1969); the Stroop task involves no spatial conflict.", "d": "Auditory monitoring over speech relates to dichotic listening and shadowing studies (Colin Cherry, 1953), not to the visual colour-word conflict of the Stroop task."}}, {"q": "Under a Variable Ratio schedule of reinforcement, how does the pattern of operant responding typically appear ?", "concept": "The answer is d: a variable ratio (VR) schedule produces a high, steady rate of responding with little or no pause after each reinforcer. Schedules of reinforcement were mapped by Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957). In continuous reinforcement every response is rewarded; in partial (intermittent) reinforcement only some are, and there are four basic schedules. Ratio schedules reward a number of responses; interval schedules reward the first response after some time has passed. Fixed means the number or time stays the same; variable means it changes around an average. On a VR 10 schedule, a reward comes on average after every 10 responses, but sometimes after 2 and sometimes after 20. Because the very next response could be the winning one, there is no reason to pause, and because more responses bring more rewards, the rate is high. VR also gives the greatest resistance to extinction, since a long run without reward looks like an ordinary unlucky streak. Slot machines and lottery tickets work this way, which helps explain gambling. A fixed ratio (FR) schedule also gives a high rate, but with a pause after each reward (\"break and run\"). A fixed interval (FI) schedule gives a scalloped pattern: little responding just after a reward, speeding up as the interval ends. A variable interval (VI) schedule gives a moderate, steady rate. Example: Arjun keeps opening loot boxes in a mobile game because a rare prize turns up after an unpredictable number of boxes. Option d is right because the high, steady, pause-free pattern is the signature of VR. Exam trap: the post-reinforcement pause belongs to fixed schedules (FR and FI), not variable ones, and the scallop belongs to FI only.", "traps": {"a": "A low rate with a long pause after each reward fits lean fixed schedules, such as a long fixed interval or a very large fixed ratio; VR removes the pause because any response could be rewarded.", "b": "The scalloped pattern, slow just after a reward and accelerating towards the end of the interval, is the hallmark of a fixed interval schedule, where the timing of reward is predictable.", "c": "Extremely low, erratic bursts describe no standard schedule and look more like responding during extinction, after reinforcement has stopped; VR produces the highest and most stable rates."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the core assertion of the James-Lange theory of emotion ?", "concept": "The answer is c: the James-Lange theory says that emotional experience is our awareness of the body's own physiological and behavioural reactions to a stimulus. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) reached the idea independently. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid, and then run and tremble. James reversed the order: we see the bear, the body reacts (the heart races, we run), and the feeling of fear is our perception of those bodily changes. In his famous phrase, we \"feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble\". Each emotion should therefore have its own distinct pattern of bodily change. Evidence for the theory is partial. Paul Ekman and colleagues (1983) found that posing facial expressions produced small emotion-specific changes in heart rate and skin temperature. The facial feedback hypothesis, tested by Fritz Strack and colleagues (1988) with pens held in the mouth, suggests body signals can colour feelings. Walter Cannon (1927) criticised the theory: visceral changes are slow, similar across emotions, and cutting the nerves from the viscera in animals did not abolish emotional behaviour. Example: Riya hears a loud crash at night; her heart pounds and her hands shake, and only then does she notice that she feels frightened. Option c is right because it puts the bodily reaction first and defines the feeling as awareness of that reaction. Exam trap: option a describes the Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon, 1927; Philip Bard, 1928), in which the thalamus sends signals to the cortex and to the body at the same time, so feeling and arousal are simultaneous. Students who remember only \"arousal and emotion\" mix up the two theories.", "traps": {"a": "Simultaneous experience and arousal routed through the thalamus is the Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon, 1927; Philip Bard, 1928), proposed as a criticism of James-Lange.", "b": "Appraisal first, then arousal and feeling, is the cognitive appraisal view of Magda Arnold (1960) and Richard Lazarus (1966, 1991); James-Lange puts the bodily reaction first, with no appraisal step before it.", "d": "No classic theory says emotion is caused solely by the prefrontal cortex; that region helps regulate emotion, but James-Lange locates emotion in feedback from the body, not in one brain area."}}, {"q": "According to Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs, which need must be substantially satisfied before an individual becomes motivated by self-actualization needs ?", "concept": "The answer is c: in Abraham Maslow's hierarchy, esteem needs sit just below self-actualisation, so they must be substantially satisfied first. Abraham Maslow (1943; expanded in Motivation and Personality, 1954) arranged human needs in a hierarchy, usually drawn as a pyramid. From the bottom up come physiological needs (food, water, sleep), safety needs (security, stability, freedom from fear), belongingness and love needs (friendship, family, intimacy), esteem needs (self-respect, achievement, recognition from others) and, at the top, self-actualisation (becoming everything one is capable of becoming). The first four are deficiency needs (D-needs): when unmet they create tension, and once met they stop motivating. Self-actualisation is a growth need (B-need, for being) that keeps motivating as it is fed. Maslow said a higher need emerges when lower ones are relatively, not completely, satisfied; his rough figures for an average person were about 85 per cent of physiological needs met but only about 50 per cent of love needs. Later reviews, such as Mahmoud Wahba and Lawrence Bridwell (1976), found little support for a strict order, and Maslow later added self-transcendence above self-actualisation. Example: Kabir has a stable job, a safe home and close friends. Once he also feels competent and respected at work, he starts painting seriously simply because it fulfils him. Option c is right because esteem is the level immediately beneath self-actualisation, and reaching it implies the lower levels are reasonably met as well. Exam trap: the word \"only\" in options a, b and d. Each of those levels must be met, but none of them alone is enough; the question wants the need directly below the top. Students also swap belongingness and esteem; belongingness comes first.", "traps": {"a": "Physiological needs (hunger, thirst, sleep) form the base of the pyramid; they must be met first, but meeting them alone leaves safety, belongingness and esteem needs still pressing.", "b": "Safety needs (security, stability) are the second level; with only these met, the person would still be driven by belongingness and esteem, not yet by self-actualisation.", "d": "Belongingness and love needs are the third level, below esteem; with only these met, the person would next be driven by esteem needs, not by self-actualisation."}}, {"q": "Which basic component of language refers to the smallest distinctive sound units that can distinguish one word from another ?", "concept": "The answer is b: phonemes are the smallest distinctive sound units in a language, the units that can change one word into another. Linguists describe language at several levels: phonology deals with sounds, morphology with word parts, syntax with sentence structure, semantics with meaning and pragmatics with use in social settings. A phoneme carries no meaning by itself, but swapping one changes the word: /p/ and /b/ separate \"pat\" from \"bat\", and /k/ and /g/ separate \"cot\" from \"got\". English has about 40 to 44 phonemes, and the world's languages range from about a dozen to over a hundred. Phonemes combine into morphemes, the smallest units that carry meaning. \"Cats\" has two morphemes: the free morpheme \"cat\" and the bound morpheme \"-s\", meaning plural. Charles Hockett (1960) called this two-layer system duality of patterning: meaningless sounds build meaningful units. Which sounds count as separate phonemes depends on the language. In Hindi, unaspirated /k/ and aspirated /kh/ are different phonemes, so kal (yesterday or tomorrow) and khal (skin) are different words; in English the same difference never changes meaning. Infants can hear almost all such contrasts, but by about 10 to 12 months they lose sensitivity to contrasts their home language does not use (Janet Werker and Richard Tees, 1984). Example: when Riya teaches Hindi to her English-speaking friend, he hears kal and khal as the same word until he learns the contrast. Option b is right because the stem asks for the smallest distinctive sound unit, which is the definition of a phoneme. Exam trap: morpheme versus phoneme. The smallest unit of meaning is the morpheme; the smallest unit of sound that distinguishes words is the phoneme.", "traps": {"a": "Morphemes are the smallest units of meaning (the free \"cat\" and the bound \"-s\" in \"cats\"); they are built from phonemes but are defined by meaning, not by sound.", "c": "Syntax is the set of rules for ordering words into phrases and sentences, the level Noam Chomsky (1957) analysed in transformational grammar; it works on words, not single sounds.", "d": "Semantics is the study of meaning in words and sentences; it is the meaning level of language, not the sound level."}}, {"q": "Concepts that are fussy around the edges have no fixed or readily specified set of defining features are called :", "concept": "The answer is a: concepts that are fuzzy around the edges, with no fixed or readily specified set of defining features, are natural concepts. A concept is a mental category that groups objects, events or ideas that share properties. Psychologists divide concepts into two kinds. Logical (artificial) concepts are defined by a clear rule: a triangle is any closed figure with three straight sides, and anything that meets the rule belongs, with no in-between cases. Natural concepts are the categories we build in everyday life, such as 'bird', 'game' or 'furniture'. They have no neat checklist. Their members share a family resemblance, a phrase from the philosopher Ludwig Wittgenstein (1953), and some members are better examples than others. Eleanor Rosch (1973) showed that we judge membership by similarity to a prototype, the most typical member: people verify 'a robin is a bird' faster than 'a penguin is a bird'. That graded, blurry boundary is what 'fuzzy around the edges' means. For example, Meera calls a sparrow a bird at once, but hesitates over an ostrich or a bat, because her concept 'bird' works by resemblance to a typical bird, not by a strict definition. Option a is right because the stem is the standard textbook definition of natural concepts, which are contrasted with logical concepts that can be clearly defined by a set of rules. Exam trap: the printed word 'fussy' is a misprint for 'fuzzy', so do not let it throw you. Also, do not confuse natural concepts with schemas: a schema is a broad knowledge framework about a situation, not a category with blurry edges. Jerome Seymour Bruner, Jacqueline Goodnow and George Austin (1956) studied logical concepts through hypothesis testing; Rosch studied natural ones.", "traps": {"b": "Logical (artificial) concepts are defined by a clear rule of necessary and sufficient features, such as 'a square has four equal sides and four right angles', so their boundaries are sharp, the opposite of fuzzy.", "c": "A schema, the term made famous by Frederic Bartlett (1932), is an organised framework of knowledge about an object or event that guides memory and expectation; it is not defined by blurry category boundaries.", "d": "'None of these' fails because the stem quotes the standard definition of natural concepts, which is offered as option a."}}, {"q": "Events that are perceived dimly with us beings vaguely aware of them are at the :", "concept": "The answer is b: events that we perceive dimly, being only vaguely aware of them, lie at the margin of attention (the margin of consciousness). Classical psychology describes the field of consciousness as having two zones. The focus is the small central part we attend to fully: its contents are clear, vivid and detailed. The margin, also called the fringe, surrounds the focus: its contents are present in awareness but faint and unclear, and they can move into the focus the moment attention shifts. Wilhelm Wundt (1896) described this as the difference between the Blickpunkt, the focal point where a content is clearly grasped (apperception), and the wider Blickfeld, the field of consciousness where contents are merely perceived. William James (1890) spoke of the 'fringe' of consciousness, a halo of dim feelings and relations around the clear centre of the stream of thought. Edward Bradford Titchener (1908) described attention as levels of clearness, with the focus at the top and the margin below. For example, while Arjun reads his textbook, the words are in focus; the hum of the fan, the feel of the chair and the chatter from the next room sit at the margin. He is vaguely aware of them, and if someone in that chatter says his name, it jumps into focus at once. Option b is right because 'perceived dimly' and 'vaguely aware' are the defining features of the margin, while the focus is the opposite: clear and vivid. Exam trap: examiners offer 'Both (A) and (B)' because focus and margin together make up the field of attention. But the stem asks only about the dim zone, and one event cannot be clear and dim at the same moment. Also, do not confuse the margin with the unconscious: marginal events are still within awareness, only faintly.", "traps": {"a": "The focus is the centre of the field of attention, where events are perceived clearly and vividly; the stem describes the dim, vague zone, which is the opposite.", "c": "Focus and margin together form the field of attention, but they are opposite zones of it: one event cannot be both clearly perceived (focus) and dimly perceived (margin) at the same time.", "d": "'None of these' fails because the margin (or fringe) is the standard term for dimly perceived events, and it is offered as option b."}}, {"q": "Tendency to assume that more closely an item resembles typical examples of a concept, it is more likely that it belong to the concept is :", "concept": "The answer is d: the tendency to judge that the more closely an item resembles typical examples of a category, the more likely it belongs to it, is the representativeness heuristic. A heuristic is a mental shortcut, a rule of thumb that gives quick, usually adequate answers without careful calculation. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1972, 1974) described three classic judgement heuristics. With representativeness we ask, 'How much does this look like my picture of a typical member?' and treat that resemblance as probability. It often works, but it makes people neglect base rates, the real frequency of each category. In their engineer-lawyer study (1973), people judged a man described as cautious and fond of puzzles to be an engineer, whether engineers made up 30 or 70 of the 100 people in the sample. In the Linda problem (Tversky and Kahneman, 1983), most people rated 'feminist bank teller' as likelier than 'bank teller', the conjunction fallacy, because Linda resembled a feminist. For example, Kabir meets a quiet man with glasses who loves books and guesses he is a librarian, not a salesman, though salesmen vastly outnumber librarians. Kabir judged by resemblance, not by numbers. Option d is right because the stem describes judging category membership by similarity to typical examples, which is the definition of representativeness. Exam trap: the availability heuristic sounds close but rests on ease of recall: an event seems common because examples come to mind easily, as flying feels dangerous after news of a crash. Representativeness is about resemblance; availability is about memory. Also note that anchoring and adjustment are not two separate heuristics: Tversky and Kahneman (1974) named one heuristic, anchoring and adjustment, so options b and c point to the same idea. The paper's misspellings ('heuristc', 'Achoring', 'Respresentativeness') do not change the answer.", "traps": {"a": "The availability heuristic (Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman, 1973) judges frequency or probability by how easily examples come to mind, such as thinking more words begin with K; it is about ease of recall, not resemblance to a typical case.", "b": "Adjustment is half of the anchoring and adjustment heuristic (Tversky and Kahneman, 1974): we start from an initial value and adjust too little away from it; it concerns numerical estimates, not resemblance to a category.", "c": "Anchoring is the other half of the same heuristic: estimates stay close to an arbitrary starting number, as when guesses of the share of African nations in the United Nations were pulled towards a number from a spun wheel; it has nothing to do with typical examples of a concept."}}, {"q": "A study uses a 2 × 2 × 3 mixed factorial design. The first factor is within-subjects, the second is between-subjects, and the third is between-subjects. How many distinct F-statistics must be computed to test all main effects and interactions ?", "concept": "The answer is d: a three-factor design needs 7 F-statistics, one for each main effect and each interaction, whatever the mix of within-subjects and between-subjects factors. In analysis of variance (ANOVA), developed by Ronald Aylmer Fisher (1925), every effect is tested with an F-ratio: the variance that effect explains divided by a suitable error variance. The label '2 × 2 × 3' tells you two things. It has three numerals, so there are three factors (call them A, B and C), and multiplying them gives 12 cells. The number of testable effects depends only on the number of factors. Count them: three main effects (A, B and C); three two-way interactions (A × B, A × C and B × C); and one three-way interaction (A × B × C). That is 3 + 3 + 1 = 7. A quick rule is 2 to the power k, minus 1, where k is the number of factors: 2 cubed is 8, and 8 minus 1 is 7. For example, Riya studies recall with word type (concrete or abstract) as a within-subjects factor, gender as a between-subjects factor, and three age groups as another between-subjects factor. She will report seven F-tests. Option d is right because three factors always yield seven effects to test. What 'mixed' changes is not the number of F-tests but their error terms: effects that involve the within-subjects factor (A, A × B, A × C, A × B × C) are tested against a within-subjects error term, while B, C and B × C are tested against the between-subjects error. Exam trap: do not count levels or cells. Adding the levels (2 + 2 + 3) also gives 7 here only by coincidence, and multiplying gives 12 cells, not 12 tests.", "traps": {"a": "3 is only the number of main effects, one per factor; it leaves out the three two-way interactions and the three-way interaction, each of which needs its own F-test.", "b": "4 counts the three main effects plus the three-way interaction but forgets the three two-way interactions (A × B, A × C and B × C).", "c": "6 counts the three main effects and the three two-way interactions but omits the three-way interaction, A × B × C, which is also tested."}}, {"q": "Under what condition is it imperative to use a non-parametric alternative like the Kruskal-Wallis test instead of a one-way ANOVA ?", "concept": "The answer is a: a non-parametric test such as the Kruskal-Wallis test is called for when the dependent variable is ordinal and the normality assumption is severely violated. One-way ANOVA, developed by Ronald Aylmer Fisher (1925), compares the means of three or more independent groups. It is a parametric test: it assumes interval or ratio data, roughly normal scores within each group, and similar variances across groups (homogeneity of variance). When these assumptions fail, especially with ranks, ratings or badly skewed scores in small samples, the F-test can mislead. The Kruskal-Wallis H test, published by William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952), is the 'one-way ANOVA by ranks'. It pools all the scores, ranks them from lowest to highest, and asks whether the average rank differs between the groups. Because it uses only order, it does not assume normality and suits ordinal data. The H statistic is read against the chi-square distribution with k minus 1 degrees of freedom, where k is the number of groups. For example, Meera compares pain ratings on a 1 to 5 scale among patients given three different therapies. The ratings are ordinal and bunched at one end, with only 12 patients per group, so Kruskal-Wallis is the right choice. Option a is right because ordinal data plus a severe breach of normality are the two classic reasons to leave ANOVA for its rank-based alternative. Exam trap: option d sounds plausible because it describes a setting where rank tests are common, but two independent groups call for the Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947), or a t-test, not Kruskal-Wallis, which is built for three or more groups. Also remember that large samples and equal variances (option c) are the very conditions that make ANOVA robust, so they argue for keeping ANOVA, not abandoning it.", "traps": {"b": "A continuous interval-scale dependent variable is exactly what parametric ANOVA is designed for, so it is a reason to use ANOVA, not to switch to a rank test.", "c": "Large samples and equal variances make ANOVA robust (the central limit theorem protects it against mild non-normality), so these conditions favour ANOVA rather than Kruskal-Wallis.", "d": "With exactly two independent groups the rank-based choice is the Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947), or an independent t-test for normal data; Kruskal-Wallis is meant for three or more groups."}}, {"q": "A researcher notes a Pearson correlation coefficient between job satisfaction and employee well-being, organizational stress, emotions and self-efficacy. Which among the following shows the highest correlation ?", "concept": "The answer is d: of the four values, -0.66 shows the highest (strongest) correlation, because -1.66 and 1.02 are impossible and 0.66 in size beats 0.61. The Pearson product-moment correlation coefficient, r, developed by Karl Pearson (1896) from the work of Francis Galton (1888), measures the strength and direction of a straight-line relationship between two variables. It can only range from -1.00 to +1.00. The sign shows direction: a plus means the two variables rise together, and a minus means one rises as the other falls. The size, ignoring the sign (the absolute value), shows strength: 0 means no linear relationship, and the closer to 1 in either direction, the stronger it is. So r = -0.66 and r = +0.66 are equally strong; they simply point in opposite directions. Applying this: -1.66 lies outside the possible range, so it can only be a calculation error, and 1.02 also exceeds +1 and is impossible. That leaves 0.61 and -0.66, whose sizes are 0.61 and 0.66, so -0.66 is the stronger correlation. For example, Arjun finds that job satisfaction correlates -0.66 with organisational stress and +0.61 with self-efficacy. Stress is more strongly related to satisfaction, even though the relationship is negative. Option d is right because it has the largest absolute value among the values a correlation can actually take. Exam trap: students pick 1.02 because it is the 'largest number', or -1.66 because its size is the biggest, forgetting that r can never go beyond plus or minus 1. Others pick 0.61 because a positive number looks 'higher' than a negative one. In correlation, 'highest' means strongest, and strength is judged by size, not by sign.", "traps": {"a": "-1.66 lies outside the possible range of Pearson's r (-1 to +1), so it signals a computing error, not a very strong negative correlation.", "b": "0.61 is a valid, moderately strong positive correlation, but its absolute value is smaller than that of -0.66, so it is weaker; a plus sign does not make a correlation higher.", "c": "1.02 exceeds +1.00, which is a perfect positive correlation, so it is impossible for a Pearson coefficient."}}, {"q": "What is the primary conceptual difference between a quasi-experimental design and a true experimental design ?", "concept": "The answer is c: the defining difference is that quasi-experiments lack random assignment of participants to conditions. A true experiment has three features: the researcher manipulates an independent variable, controls extraneous variables, and assigns participants to groups at random. Random assignment, a principle set out by Ronald Aylmer Fisher (1935), gives every participant an equal chance of landing in any condition, so the groups start out equivalent on age, ability, motivation and everything else, measured or not. Any later difference can then be credited to the treatment. Donald Thomas Campbell and Julian C. Stanley (1963) popularised the term quasi-experiment ('quasi' means 'as if') for designs that still apply or compare a treatment and still measure outcomes, but use intact or pre-existing groups such as classes, wards or villages. Common forms are the nonequivalent control group design, the interrupted time-series design and the regression discontinuity design. Because the groups may differ before the study begins, selection becomes a threat to internal validity, and causal conclusions are weaker. For example, Kabir wants to test a mindfulness programme. If he randomly assigns 60 students to the programme or a waiting list, he runs a true experiment. If he gives the programme to Section A and uses Section B for comparison because the timetable allows it, he runs a quasi-experiment, since Section A might already be calmer or keener. Option c is right because random assignment is the single feature that separates the two designs. Exam trap: do not confuse random assignment with random selection. Random selection (sampling) decides who enters the study and supports external validity; random assignment decides who receives which condition and protects internal validity. A quasi-experiment can use a random sample and still lack random assignment.", "traps": {"a": "Quasi-experiments do take post-test measures; the nonequivalent control group design, for example, measures both groups after the treatment, just as a true experiment does.", "b": "True experiments always have a clearly defined, measured dependent variable; that is part of what makes them experiments, so this reverses a basic requirement.", "d": "True experiments are usually run in controlled settings such as laboratories (field experiments exist but are not the rule); naturalistic settings are where quasi-experiments and natural experiments are common, so this reverses the usual pattern."}}, {"q": "Which ethical violation occurs when a researcher alters or selectively omits experimental data to artificially confirm a favored hypothesis ?", "concept": "The answer is b: altering or selectively omitting real data so that the results support a favoured hypothesis is falsification. Research misconduct is usually defined by three acts, often abbreviated FFP, as in the United States federal policy on research misconduct (2000). Fabrication is making up data or results that were never collected. Falsification is manipulating research materials, equipment or procedures, or changing or omitting data or results, so that the research record no longer represents what really happened. Plagiarism is using another person's ideas, words or results without giving credit. The key difference between the first two is whether the data exist. In falsification, real data were collected, but the researcher tampers with them: dropping awkward participants without a stated rule, deleting outliers only when they hurt the hypothesis, or reporting only the conditions that worked. In fabrication, the numbers are invented outright, as in the case of the Dutch social psychologist Diederik Stapel (2011), who was found to have made up data for dozens of published studies. For example, Riya runs a study on music and memory. Her real results show no effect, so she quietly removes six participants whose scores spoil the pattern, and the effect becomes significant. Every number she reports was collected, but she has falsified the record. Option b is right because the stem says the researcher 'alters or selectively omits' existing data, which is the exact wording of falsification. Exam trap: fabrication and falsification are often swapped. Remember: fabrication fabricates (creates from nothing); falsification falsifies (distorts what exists). Selective omission counts as falsification even when no single number is changed. Honest error and honest differences of opinion are not misconduct.", "traps": {"a": "Plagiarism is presenting someone else's ideas, words or results as your own without credit; it concerns authorship, not tampering with your own experimental data.", "c": "Fabrication is inventing data or results that were never collected at all; the stem describes altering or omitting data that do exist, which is falsification.", "d": "'Uninformed deception' is not a standard category of research misconduct; deception in research means misleading participants about a study's purpose (as in Stanley Milgram's 1963 obedience study), which concerns the treatment of participants, not the integrity of data."}}, {"q": "Which measure of central tendency is the most appropriate and psychometrically stable for a highly skewed pay distribution ?", "concept": "The answer is a: for a highly skewed distribution such as pay, the median is the most appropriate and stable measure of central tendency. A measure of central tendency gives one value that represents the typical score. The mean is the arithmetic average: add all the scores and divide by how many there are. The median is the middle score once all the scores are put in order, so half fall above it and half below. The mode is the most frequent score. The mean uses the actual size of every score, so a few extreme values drag it towards the tail. The median uses only the order of scores, so extreme values barely move it; statisticians call it resistant, or robust, to outliers. Pay is the classic positively skewed variable: most people earn modest amounts and a few earn huge sums, giving a long tail to the right. In a positive skew the usual order is mode, then median, then mean, with the mean pulled furthest towards the high earners. For example, in Arjun's small firm nine staff earn between Rs 20,000 and Rs 40,000 a month, and the owner takes Rs 10 lakh. The mean pay comes out above Rs 1 lakh, a figure that describes nobody, while the median stays near Rs 30,000, a fair picture of a typical employee. This is why surveys and governments report median household income. Option a is right because the median is unaffected by the size of extreme values, so it stays stable and representative when a distribution is skewed. Exam trap: the mean is the most stable measure from sample to sample when data are normal, and later statistics need it, so students choose it out of habit. Stability across samples is not the same as resistance to outliers; with heavy skew, the median wins.", "traps": {"b": "The mean is pulled towards the long tail by extreme values, so in a skewed pay distribution it overstates what a typical person earns; it suits roughly symmetrical interval or ratio data.", "c": "The mode is the most frequent value; it is unstable (small changes in the data can shift it, and there may be several modes) and is mainly used for nominal data, so it is not the most stable choice.", "d": "The geometric mean (the nth root of the product of n values) suits rates and ratios such as growth rates; it is not the standard summary for a skewed pay distribution, and it fails if any value is zero."}}, {"q": "A researcher studies the effects of sleep deprivation on reaction time in a group of participants. In this study, reaction time consitutes the :", "concept": "The answer is b: reaction time is the dependent variable, because it is the outcome measured to see whether sleep deprivation changes it. In an experiment the researcher deliberately changes one thing and measures its effect on another. The independent variable (IV) is the factor the researcher manipulates or varies: here, sleep deprivation, for instance a full night's sleep versus 24 hours awake. The dependent variable (DV) is the behaviour measured afterwards, whose value is expected to depend on the IV: here, reaction time in milliseconds. A handy test is to fit the study into the phrase 'the effect of ___ on ___'. The first blank is the IV and the second is the DV, so 'the effects of sleep deprivation on reaction time' gives the answer at once. These terms became standard in psychology through the textbook Experimental Psychology by Robert Sessions Woodworth (1938). Other variables must be kept out of the way. Extraneous variables are any other factors that could affect the DV, such as caffeine or age. When an extraneous variable changes systematically along with the IV, it becomes a confounding variable and makes the result impossible to interpret. For example, Kabir keeps one group awake all night and lets another group sleep, then measures how fast each person presses a button when a light flashes. If the sleepless group also drank more tea, tea would be a confound. Option b is right because reaction time is what is measured, and its value is expected to change as a result of sleep deprivation. Exam trap: students sometimes reverse the two, especially when the measured variable sounds active or is mentioned first. Always ask which variable the researcher controls (the IV) and which one the researcher records (the DV).", "traps": {"a": "The independent variable is the one the researcher manipulates, which here is sleep deprivation; reaction time is not manipulated but measured.", "c": "A confounding variable is an uncontrolled factor that varies along with the IV (such as tea drinking differing between groups) and offers a rival explanation; reaction time is the planned outcome, not a rival cause.", "d": "An extraneous variable is any outside influence on the DV that the researcher tries to control, such as age or practice; reaction time is the very thing being measured, so it cannot be extraneous."}}, {"q": "According to Classical Test Theory (CTT), if a test has a reliability coefficient of 0.81, what percentage of the total observed score variance is attributable to random measurement error ?", "concept": "The answer is d: with a reliability coefficient of 0.81, 19 per cent of the observed-score variance is due to random measurement error. Classical test theory, built on the work of Charles Spearman (1904), assumes that every observed score (X) is a true score (T) plus random error (E): X = T + E. Because error is random and unrelated to the true score, the variances add as well: observed variance = true variance + error variance. The reliability coefficient is defined as the proportion of observed variance that is true-score variance. So a reliability of 0.81 means that 81 per cent of the differences between people's scores reflect real differences in the trait, and the rest, 1 - 0.81 = 0.19, or 19 per cent, reflects random error such as fatigue, lucky guessing or a noisy room. For example, Meera's anxiety scale has a test-retest reliability of 0.81. When scores vary across her sample, about four-fifths of that variation is genuine anxiety and about one-fifth is measurement noise. Option d is right because error variance is simply one minus the reliability coefficient, and 1 - 0.81 = 0.19. Exam trap: do not square the coefficient or take its square root here. A correlation between two different variables is squared to get shared variance (r = 0.81 would share about 66 per cent), and the square root of reliability (0.90) is the index of reliability, the correlation between observed and true scores. A reliability coefficient, however, is already a ratio of variances, so it is read directly as a percentage. Option a gives the true-score share, not the error share. The standard error of measurement uses the same quantity: SEM equals the standard deviation multiplied by the square root of (1 - r).", "traps": {"a": "81 per cent is the share of observed variance that is true-score variance, which is what the reliability coefficient itself means; the error share is what remains, 19 per cent.", "b": "29 per cent does not follow from any classical test theory formula; it is an arithmetic slip for 1 - 0.81, which equals 0.19.", "c": "9 per cent comes from a slip such as subtracting 0.81 from 0.90 (the index of reliability, its square root) instead of from 1; error variance is 1 - 0.81, which is 19 per cent."}}, {"q": "A psychometrist wishes to assess the internal consistency of a test which has multi-point, non-dichotomous items (such as a 5-point Likert scale). Which statistical metric should be calculated ?", "concept": "The answer is b: for internal consistency on multi-point items such as a 5-point Likert scale, the right statistic is Cronbach's alpha. Internal consistency asks whether all the items of a test measure the same thing, using a single administration. The earliest method was split-half reliability: score two halves of the test, correlate them and correct for length. Because the result depends on how the test is split, G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) derived formulas from item statistics instead. Their KR-20 uses p and q, the proportions passing and failing each item, so it works only for dichotomous items scored right or wrong, yes or no. Lee Joseph Cronbach (1951) generalised KR-20 into coefficient alpha by replacing pq with each item's variance, so alpha works for items with any number of score points, such as Likert ratings from 1 to 5. Alpha equals k divided by (k - 1), multiplied by one minus the sum of item variances divided by the total-score variance. It equals the mean of all possible split-half coefficients, and for dichotomous items it gives exactly the same value as KR-20. Values of about 0.70 or above are usually treated as acceptable for research. For example, Kabir writes a 20-item exam-stress questionnaire rated from 'never' (1) to 'always' (5). To check whether the items hang together, he computes Cronbach's alpha, not KR-20. Option b is right because alpha is the internal consistency coefficient designed for items with more than two score points. Exam trap: KR-20 and alpha are both internal consistency coefficients, so KR-20 is the near miss. The deciding detail is item format: dichotomous items mean KR-20; multi-point items mean alpha. Cohen's kappa also sounds like a reliability index, but it measures agreement between raters, not consistency among items.", "traps": {"a": "KR-20 (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937) measures internal consistency only for dichotomously scored items (right or wrong, yes or no), so it cannot handle 5-point Likert items.", "c": "Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960) measures chance-corrected agreement between two raters on categorical judgements; it is inter-rater reliability, not the internal consistency of test items.", "d": "The Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910) steps a split-half correlation up to full test length, or predicts reliability when a test is lengthened; it is a correction formula, not the internal consistency coefficient for Likert items."}}, {"q": "If a researcher wants to eliminate the impact of speed or time limits on a test to assess an examinee's maximum level of performance on increasingly difficult items, they should construct a :", "concept": "The answer is c: a test that removes the effect of time limits and measures how far a person can go on items of rising difficulty is a power test. Tests are classified by how time and difficulty are combined. A speed test contains easy items of about equal difficulty and a time limit so tight that almost nobody finishes; the score reflects how quickly a person works, as in a clerical checking or letter-cancellation task. A power test allows generous time, or no limit at all, but its items climb steadily in difficulty until some are too hard for anyone. The score shows the hardest level a person can master, that is, their maximum ability, not their pace. Most real ability tests are speeded power tests, a blend of the two. Anne Anastasi (1954) stressed that the distinction also matters for reliability: split-half and internal consistency estimates are falsely inflated on pure speed tests, so they belong with power tests. Raven's Progressive Matrices, devised by John Carlyle Raven (1938) and given untimed in its standard form, is a classic power test. For example, Meera takes an untimed reasoning test whose puzzles grow harder page by page. She stops at puzzle 34 because the later ones are beyond her, not because the clock ran out, so her score reflects her power, the ceiling of her ability. Option c is right because removing time pressure and grading items from easy to hard is the definition of a power test. Exam trap: examiners reverse the definitions. It is power tests that have items of increasing difficulty; speed tests have uniformly easy items. A mastery test sounds similar because it also concerns what a person can do, but it checks whether a fixed standard has been reached, not the maximum level a person can attain.", "traps": {"a": "A speed test does the opposite: it uses easy, uniform items and a strict time limit, so the score depends on how fast a person works, which is exactly the factor the researcher wants to remove.", "b": "A mastery test is a criterion-referenced test (in the tradition of Robert Glaser, 1963) that checks whether a person has reached a preset standard of skill; it is not defined by removing time limits and grading items up to a maximum level.", "d": "A situational judgement test presents realistic work scenarios and asks which response is most effective, a low-fidelity simulation (Stephan Motowidlo, Marvin Dunnette and Gary Carter, 1990); it measures practical judgement, not maximum performance on items of rising difficulty."}}, {"q": "Which ethical principle is violated if a psychologist administers a highly specialized clinical test battery without having advanced training or certification in its interpretation ?", "concept": "The answer is b: giving a specialised clinical test battery without the training to interpret it violates the ethical principle of competence. Competence means that psychologists work only within the limits of their education, training, supervised experience and professional credentials. The American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016) sets this out in Standard 2 (Competence), and its assessment standards add that tests must be used only in ways a psychologist's training supports and must not be handed to unqualified persons. In India, the Rehabilitation Council of India, under the RCI Act (1992), registers clinical psychologists and expects the same. Test publishers enforce competence through user qualification levels. Level A tests, such as achievement tests, need only the manual; Level B tests, such as group intelligence and aptitude tests, need training in test construction and statistics; Level C tests, such as individual intelligence tests, clinical personality inventories and projective techniques, need an advanced degree and supervised experience. The risk is real: an untrained user may score a battery wrongly, misread a profile and label a client with a disorder or brain damage that is not there. For example, Arjun, a counsellor with only a bachelor's degree, buys a neuropsychological battery online and tells a client she has early dementia. Even though she agreed to testing and her results stayed private, Arjun acted outside his competence. Option b is right because the violation lies in the psychologist's lack of training and qualification, the core of the competence standard. Exam trap: informed consent and confidentiality are also central duties in testing, so they look tempting. But neither is breached by lack of training: a psychologist can obtain full consent and protect privacy and still be unfit to interpret the test.", "traps": {"a": "Informed consent means explaining the purpose, nature, fees and limits of confidentiality of testing and obtaining the client's agreement; the stem says nothing about consent, and an untrained psychologist could still obtain it.", "c": "Confidentiality concerns protecting test results and client information from unauthorised release; the problem in the stem is the psychologist's lack of qualification, not a leak of information.", "d": "'None of these' fails because practising outside one's training is the textbook breach of the competence standard (Standard 2 of the APA Ethics Code), offered as option b."}}, {"q": "What psychometric challenge occurs when an individual consistently responds to self-report personality items presenting a favorable, idealized image of themselves rather than reflecting their true behaviour ?", "concept": "The answer is a: consistently answering self-report items so as to present a favourable, idealised image, rather than one's true behaviour, is social desirability bias. Self-report inventories assume that people describe themselves honestly, but answers can be shaped by how people want to look. Lee Joseph Cronbach (1946) showed that habitual ways of answering, called response sets, shift scores regardless of item content. Allen L. Edwards (1957) found that how socially desirable an item sounds strongly predicts whether people endorse it. Delroy Paulhus (1984) later split social desirability into two parts: impression management, a conscious effort to look good to others, and self-deceptive enhancement, an honest but inflated view of oneself. Test makers control it in several ways: forced-choice items matched for desirability, as in the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule; lie scales such as the L scale of the MMPI; and separate measures such as the Marlowe-Crowne Social Desirability Scale of Douglas Crowne and David Marlowe (1960). For example, Riya, applying for a job, fills in a personality questionnaire. She marks 'never' for 'I sometimes lose my temper' and 'always' for 'I finish every task on time', though neither is true. Her answers paint the ideal employee, not the real Riya. Option a is right because the stem describes a favourable, idealised self-presentation on self-report items, the definition of social desirability bias, also called faking good. Exam trap: malingering is the mirror image. Both distort the picture, but malingering is deliberately presenting oneself as worse (faking bad) for an external gain such as compensation or avoiding duty. Acquiescence and extreme responding are also response styles, but they depend on the format of an item (agreeing, or choosing the end points), not on how desirable an answer looks.", "traps": {"b": "Acquiescence (yea-saying), described by Lee Joseph Cronbach (1946), is a tendency to agree with items whatever their content; it can make a person look worse as easily as better, and it is controlled by balanced, reverse-keyed items.", "c": "Malingering is the deliberate exaggeration or invention of symptoms for an external incentive (faking bad), the opposite direction from the idealised self-image in the stem.", "d": "Extreme response bias is a tendency to choose the end points of a rating scale (always 1 or 5) regardless of content; it concerns how the scale is used, not presenting a desirable image."}}, {"q": "Which neuropsychological test is specifically designed to assess executive functioning, abstract reasoning, and set-shifting capabilities by requiring participants to match cards based on changing rules ?", "concept": "The answer is d: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is the neuropsychological test of executive functioning, abstract reasoning and set-shifting that asks people to match cards by rules that change. Executive functions are the higher control processes, centred on the prefrontal cortex, that let us plan, form concepts, hold a goal in mind and switch strategy when circumstances change. The WCST was devised by David Grant and Esta Berg (1948), and Robert Heaton (1981) wrote its standard clinical manual. Four stimulus cards are laid out, differing in colour, shape and number of symbols. The person sorts a deck of response cards under them but is never told the rule; the examiner says only 'right' or 'wrong'. After a run of ten correct sorts the rule changes without warning, say from colour to shape. A healthy adult notices the string of 'wrong' feedback, works out the new rule and shifts. The key score is perseverative errors: going on sorting by the old rule despite being told it is wrong. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral frontal lobe excisions made many perseverative errors, tying the test to the frontal lobes. It is widely used in schizophrenia, head injury and frontal lobe damage. For example, after a road accident Kabir keeps sorting by colour even after hearing 'wrong' ten times in a row. His difficulty in shifting set points to frontal injury. Option d is right because matching cards by changing, unannounced rules is the signature of the WCST. Exam trap: the Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test also sits in neuropsychological batteries, so it is a tempting near miss, but it measures visual-motor integration by copying designs, not set-shifting. Card sorting plus shifting rules always means the WCST.", "traps": {"a": "The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test (Lauretta Bender, 1938) asks the person to copy nine geometric designs; it screens visual-motor integration and organic impairment, not set-shifting by card sorting.", "b": "The Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935) is a projective personality test in which people tell stories about ambiguous pictures; it explores needs and conflicts, not executive function.", "c": "The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (David Wechsler, 1955, growing out of the Wechsler-Bellevue of 1939) measures general intelligence through verbal and performance subtests; it has no card-sorting task with shifting rules."}}, {"q": "Which neuroimaging technique provides exceptional temporal resolution by tracking electrical activity down to the millisecond, but offers relatively limited spatial localization ?", "concept": "The answer is c: electroencephalography (EEG) tracks the brain's electrical activity millisecond by millisecond, giving excellent temporal resolution but poor spatial localisation. Brain-measurement methods are judged on two kinds of resolution. Temporal resolution is precision about when activity happens; spatial resolution is precision about where it happens. The German psychiatrist Hans Berger (1929) first recorded EEG from the human scalp. Electrodes on the scalp pick up the summed electrical activity of millions of cortical neurons as it happens, so changes can be timed to within a millisecond. Averaging the EEG over many repeated stimuli gives event-related potentials, such as the P300 wave that appears about 300 milliseconds after a rare target stimulus. The weakness is location: the signal is blurred by the skull and scalp, and each electrode sums activity over a wide area, so EEG can only say roughly where, usually within centimetres, and it barely detects deep structures. By contrast, fMRI and PET measure blood flow or metabolism, which change over seconds, so they locate activity well but time it poorly. For example, Meera wants to know how quickly the brain tells a happy face from an angry one. She uses EEG, because the difference appears within a few hundred milliseconds; if she wanted to know which brain region does the job, she would choose fMRI. Option c is right because EEG combines millisecond timing with limited spatial precision, exactly as the stem describes. Exam trap: fMRI sounds the most modern and has good spatial resolution, so students choose it, but blood-flow signals lag behind neural events by several seconds. CT is purely structural and shows no activity at all. Magnetoencephalography (MEG) shares EEG's millisecond timing with better localisation, but it is not offered here.", "traps": {"a": "fMRI (the BOLD signal described by Seiji Ogawa, 1990) has good spatial resolution, about 1 to 3 mm, but its temporal resolution is only seconds because blood flow lags neural firing; it is the reverse of the stem's profile.", "b": "PET (positron emission tomography) tracks a radioactive tracer to map glucose metabolism or blood flow; it localises activity reasonably well, but each image takes about a minute or more, so its timing is poor, not millisecond-level.", "d": "Computed tomography (Godfrey Hounsfield, 1972) is a structural X-ray technique that shows anatomy such as bleeding or tumours; it records no electrical activity and has no meaningful temporal resolution."}}, {"q": "An individual is born with an extra copy of chromosome 21, resulting in a total of 47 chromosomes. This specific chromosomal anomaly can be seen in :", "concept": "The answer is a: an extra copy of chromosome 21, giving 47 chromosomes in place of the usual 46, is the karyotype of Down syndrome (trisomy 21). Humans normally carry 23 pairs of chromosomes: 22 pairs of autosomes plus one pair of sex chromosomes (XX or XY). When a pair fails to separate during meiosis (nondisjunction), an egg or sperm carries two copies of a chromosome, and the zygote ends up with three. John Langdon Down (1866) described the clinical picture: intellectual disability, a flat facial profile, upward-slanting eyes, a single palmar crease, low muscle tone, heart defects and an early onset of Alzheimer's disease. Jérôme Lejeune (1959) then showed the extra chromosome 21 under the microscope. About 95 per cent of cases are free trisomy 21; the rest are translocation or mosaic forms. The risk rises steeply with maternal age. Down syndrome is the commonest genetic cause of intellectual disability, while fragile X is the commonest inherited one. Example: Meera's newborn son has low muscle tone and a heart murmur; his karyotype reads 47,XY,+21, which is Down syndrome. Option a is right because only Down syndrome is defined by three copies of chromosome 21. Exam trap: Klinefelter syndrome also has 47 chromosomes, so a student who reads only the number may pick c; the extra chromosome there is an X (47,XXY), not chromosome 21. Turner syndrome is the reverse error, a missing X (45,X), and Huntington's disease has a normal chromosome count with one faulty gene. Always check which chromosome is extra, not just the total.", "traps": {"b": "Turner syndrome is a sex chromosome error in females with one X missing (45,X), so the total is 45, not 47, and chromosome 21 is untouched. It brings short stature and infertility, not trisomy.", "c": "Klinefelter syndrome does give 47 chromosomes, but the extra one is an X chromosome in a male (47,XXY), not chromosome 21. It brings tall stature, small testes and infertility.", "d": "Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant single-gene disorder, a CAG repeat expansion in the HTT gene on chromosome 4, with a normal count of 46. It shows as chorea and dementia around age 40, not as a chromosomal anomaly at birth."}}, {"q": "What physiological change is uniquely a characteristic of REM sleep when compared to slow-wave sleep ?", "concept": "The answer is a: the combination of profound muscle atonia, rapid eye movements and a low-voltage EEG is found only in REM sleep. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered REM sleep by watching sleepers' eyes dart under closed lids, and William Dement and Nathaniel Kleitman (1957) linked these periods to vivid dreaming. Sleep has two broad states. Non-REM sleep runs from light N1 and N2 to N3, slow-wave sleep, where the EEG shows high-amplitude delta waves (0.5 to 4 Hz), the body is still but keeps some muscle tone, and heart rate and breathing are slow and regular. REM sleep is called paradoxical sleep because the brain looks awake while the body is limp: the EEG is fast, low voltage and desynchronised, the eyes move rapidly, and the large skeletal muscles are almost paralysed (REM atonia, produced by circuits in the pons that inhibit spinal motor neurons). Heart rate and breathing become irregular. REM makes up 20 to 25 per cent of adult sleep and lengthens toward morning. Example: Kabir's sleep lab record at 5 a.m. shows a flat chin muscle trace, sawtooth EEG waves and bursts of eye movement; he is in REM. Option a is right because no other state, waking or non-REM, joins atonia with rapid eye movements and a low-voltage EEG. Exam trap: delta waves (b) belong to slow-wave sleep, REM brings irregular rather than stable heart rate and breathing (d), and a desynchronised EEG (c) is real in REM but also marks waking and N1. Key note: Some candidates argue for c, since the REM EEG really is desynchronised compared with slow-wave sleep. But the stem asks what is unique to REM, and only the combination in option a (which already includes the low-voltage EEG) meets that test, so the official key stands.", "traps": {"b": "High-amplitude delta waves (0.5 to 4 Hz) define N3, slow-wave sleep itself, so they are the opposite of the REM pattern, which is fast and low voltage.", "c": "A desynchronised EEG does occur in REM, which is why it is called paradoxical sleep, but the same pattern appears in relaxed waking and N1, so it is not unique to REM. Option a already includes this low-voltage EEG plus the features found nowhere else.", "d": "Heart rate and breathing are most stable in slow-wave sleep; in REM they become irregular and variable, so this option reverses the facts."}}, {"q": "Which subcortical structure acts as the primary sensory relay station, filtering and routing all incoming sensory inputs (except olfaction) to their respective cortical destinations ?", "concept": "The answer is c: the thalamus is the brain's main sensory relay station, passing every sense except smell on to the cortex. The thalamus is a paired, egg-shaped mass of grey matter in the diencephalon, sitting on top of the brainstem on either side of the third ventricle. It is made of separate nuclei, each linked to its own patch of cortex. The lateral geniculate nucleus relays vision to the occipital lobe, the medial geniculate nucleus relays hearing to the temporal lobe (a handy memory aid: lateral for light, medial for music), and the ventral posterior nucleus relays touch, pain, temperature, body position and taste to the parietal cortex. Smell is the exception: the olfactory bulb sends fibres straight to the olfactory cortex, reaching the thalamus only later. The thalamus is not a passive switchboard. Through its reticular nucleus and two-way loops with the cortex it gates which signals pass, so it shapes attention, sleep and consciousness. Damage can cause loss of sensation or severe central pain, as Joseph Dejerine and Gustave Roussy (1906) described in thalamic pain syndrome. Example: when Riya hears her name in a noisy canteen, the sound is relayed through her medial geniculate nucleus before her auditory cortex recognises it. Option c is right because the thalamus is the single structure that receives and routes all the non-olfactory senses. Exam trap: the hypothalamus sits just below the thalamus and has a similar name, but it controls drives, hormones and body temperature rather than relaying sensation.", "traps": {"a": "The hypothalamus lies below the thalamus and regulates hunger, thirst, temperature, sexual behaviour and the pituitary gland; it is a homeostatic and endocrine centre, not the sensory relay.", "b": "The amygdala is a limbic structure in the temporal lobe that tags threats and drives fear learning, as in Joseph LeDoux's (1996) work; it receives sensory input from the thalamus rather than routing it to the cortex.", "d": "The pons is a hindbrain structure that relays signals between the cortex and cerebellum and helps generate REM sleep; it does not distribute the senses to their cortical areas."}}, {"q": "The primary mechanism of action for Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors (SSRIs) involves :", "concept": "The answer is c: SSRIs work by blocking the presynaptic serotonin reuptake transporter, so more serotonin stays in the synaptic cleft. Normally a neuron releases serotonin (5-HT) into the synapse, the transmitter binds to receptors on the next cell, and then a transporter protein on the presynaptic terminal (SERT) pumps it back inside for reuse. This reuptake ends the signal. A selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor such as fluoxetine or sertraline binds to SERT and blocks the pump, so serotonin lingers in the cleft and keeps acting on its receptors. In drug terms an SSRI is an indirect agonist: it boosts serotonin's effect without itself activating a receptor. 'Selective' means it largely spares the noradrenaline and dopamine transporters, which is why SSRIs cause fewer side effects than the older tricyclics. Fluoxetine (Prozac) was described by David Wong and colleagues (1974) at Eli Lilly and approved in the United States in 1987. Although the blockade happens within hours, mood lifts only after 2 to 4 weeks, which suggests the benefit comes from slower changes such as receptor down-regulation and neuroplasticity. Example: Arjun starts sertraline for depression; his sleep and appetite improve in the third week, though the drug blocked reuptake from the first dose. Option c is right because blocking the reuptake transporter is exactly what 'reuptake inhibitor' means. Exam trap: option a describes MAO inhibitors, which also raise serotonin but by stopping its breakdown, not its reuptake; examiners swap the two because both are antidepressants that raise monoamines.", "traps": {"a": "Inhibiting monoamine oxidase is the action of MAO inhibitors such as phenelzine and iproniazid; they block the enzyme that breaks down monoamines, not the reuptake pump, and they carry the tyramine 'cheese reaction'.", "b": "Increasing exocytosis (transmitter release) is how amphetamine-type releasers act; SSRIs leave release roughly unchanged and act after release, at the reuptake step.", "d": "Directly stimulating post-synaptic serotonin receptors describes a direct receptor agonist, such as LSD at 5-HT2A receptors or buspirone at 5-HT1A receptors; an SSRI only raises the amount of serotonin available."}}, {"q": "Which major neurotransmitter is widely implicated as the primary inhibitor within the central nervous system ?", "concept": "The answer is d: GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. A neurotransmitter is inhibitory when it makes the next neuron less likely to fire. GABA does this by opening channels that let negative chloride ions in (the fast GABA-A receptor) or let positive potassium ions out (the slower GABA-B receptor). Either way the inside of the cell becomes more negative, a hyperpolarisation called an inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP), and the neuron moves further from its firing threshold. Eugene Roberts and Sam Frankel (1950) identified GABA in the brain; it is made from glutamate by the enzyme glutamic acid decarboxylase, and perhaps a third of brain synapses use it. The balance between glutamate excitation and GABA inhibition matters clinically. Too little inhibition can cause seizures and anxiety, which is why benzodiazepines, barbiturates and alcohol, all of which enhance the GABA-A receptor, calm anxiety and stop fits. Example: when Meera takes a prescribed benzodiazepine before a dental procedure, it strengthens the effect of her own GABA, and her racing worry quietens. Option d is right because GABA is the chief inhibitory transmitter throughout the central nervous system. Exam trap: glutamate, the main excitatory transmitter, is the classic decoy, since GABA is built from it and the two are always taught together. Note also that glycine, not GABA, is the main inhibitory transmitter of the spinal cord, so a question that asks specifically about the spinal cord has a different answer.", "traps": {"a": "Glutamate is the main excitatory neurotransmitter of the central nervous system, acting at AMPA and NMDA receptors; it is the opposite of an inhibitor, and in excess it kills neurons (excitotoxicity).", "b": "Acetylcholine is the transmitter of the neuromuscular junction and of brain systems for memory, attention and REM sleep; it is mostly excitatory, not the main inhibitor.", "c": "Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is a monoamine from the locus coeruleus that raises arousal and vigilance and drives the sympathetic fight-or-flight response; it is a modulator, not the brain's chief inhibitor."}}, {"q": "Which sensory receptor cells located within the retina are specialized for capturing color vision and responsible for light adaptation ?", "concept": "The answer is a: cones are the retinal photoreceptors that give colour vision and work in bright light, so they carry vision when the eye is light-adapted. The retina has two kinds of light-sensitive receptor. Rods (about 120 million) contain a single pigment, rhodopsin, are extremely sensitive and serve scotopic, night vision in shades of grey. Cones (about 6 to 7 million) are packed into the fovea, need more light and serve photopic vision: sharp detail and colour. Colour depends on three cone types, most sensitive to short, medium and long wavelengths, the idea proposed by Thomas Young (1802) and developed by Hermann von Helmholtz (1852) as the trichromatic theory. Light adaptation is the eye's adjustment when you move from darkness into bright light: the pupil narrows, the rods saturate and drop out, and the cones take over, within about a minute. Dark adaptation is the slower reverse, about 20 to 30 minutes, ending with the rods. Example: when Kabir walks out of a dark cinema into afternoon sun he is dazzled, then within a minute his cones take over and he reads the red and blue signboards clearly. Option a is right because cones are the only receptors that capture colour and the ones that operate in light-adapted vision. Exam trap: rods are the decoy because they are the famous adaptation receptors, but their role is dark adaptation and night vision; reversing rods and cones is the standard trick in this question.", "traps": {"b": "Rods serve dim-light (scotopic) vision with one pigment, rhodopsin, so they see no colour; they are the receptors of dark adaptation, not light adaptation.", "c": "Ganglion cells are the output neurons of the retina whose axons form the optic nerve; apart from a small group of melanopsin cells that help set circadian rhythms, they do not capture light and are not the receptors for colour.", "d": "Bipolar cells are interneurons that pass signals from rods and cones to ganglion cells; they process the signal but do not capture light themselves."}}, {"q": "Which hormone released by the adrenal cortex in response to chronic psychological stress and can impair hippocampal functioning over time ?", "concept": "The answer is a: cortisol, the main glucocorticoid of the adrenal cortex, is released in chronic stress and in excess can damage the hippocampus. The body has two stress systems. The fast one, the sympathetic-adrenal-medullary (SAM) system, sends nerve signals to the adrenal medulla, which releases epinephrine (adrenaline) within seconds. The slower one is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: the hypothalamus releases CRH, the anterior pituitary releases ACTH into the blood, and the adrenal cortex responds with cortisol within minutes. Cortisol raises blood sugar and damps inflammation, and normally switches itself off through negative feedback, partly via receptors in the hippocampus. Hans Selye (1936) described how prolonged stress wears the body down in his general adaptation syndrome. Robert Sapolsky, Lewis Krey and Bruce McEwen (1986) proposed the glucocorticoid cascade hypothesis: long-term high cortisol shrinks hippocampal dendrites and can kill neurons, a damaged hippocampus brakes the HPA axis less well, cortisol climbs further, and memory suffers. Brain scans in Cushing's syndrome, long-standing depression and PTSD often show a smaller hippocampus. Example: Riya, caring for her ill father for two years while working night shifts, has raised cortisol and keeps forgetting appointments. Option a is right because cortisol is the adrenal cortex hormone that rises in chronic stress and harms the hippocampus. Exam trap: epinephrine is also an adrenal stress hormone, but it comes from the adrenal medulla and drives the fast, short-term fight-or-flight response, so the words 'cortex' and 'chronic' rule it out.", "traps": {"b": "Epinephrine (adrenaline) is released by the adrenal medulla, not the cortex, within seconds of acute stress, the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915); its effects are fast and brief.", "c": "Thyroxine comes from the thyroid gland and sets the body's metabolic rate; too little slows thinking and mimics depression, but it is not the adrenal stress hormone.", "d": "Insulin is secreted by the beta cells of the pancreas and lowers blood sugar; it is not made by the adrenal cortex and is not the classic chronic stress hormone."}}, {"q": "According to Kurt Lewin's Field Theory, human behaviour is a dynamic function of the interaction between which two core vectors ?", "concept": "The answer is b: Kurt Lewin's field theory holds that behaviour is a function of the person and the psychological environment, written B = f(P, E). Kurt Lewin (1936), a Gestalt-trained psychologist, set this out in Principles of Topological Psychology. He borrowed the idea of a field from physics: just as many forces in a magnetic field act at once on a particle, many forces act together on a person at a given moment. The person and the environment together form the life space, the totality of facts that determine behaviour at that moment. Crucially, the environment is psychological, the world as the person perceives it, not the objective physical setting. Lewin mapped the life space with regions (possible activities), boundaries (barriers), valences (attractions and repulsions) and vectors (forces pushing towards or away from goals). Because behaviour depends on the present field, he stressed contemporaneity: past events matter only as they exist in the present life space. His approach led on to group dynamics and action research and earned him the title of father of modern social psychology. Example: Arjun freezes during a group discussion at college. Field theory explains this through his anxiety and goals (P) together with how he sees the room, such as a stern-looking professor (E), not through either alone. Option b is right because B = f(P, E) names exactly the person and the psychological environment. Exam trap: option c sounds scientific but brings in reinforcement history, which is behaviourism; Lewin deliberately focused on the present field rather than past learning.", "traps": {"a": "Biological drives and unconscious defence mechanisms belong to the psychoanalytic model of Sigmund Freud (1923), with its id, ego and superego, not to Kurt Lewin's field of present forces.", "c": "Past reinforcement history and future expectations echo behaviourism and Julian Rotter's (1954) social learning theory of expectancy and reinforcement value; Kurt Lewin's principle of contemporaneity looks only at the present field.", "d": "Social norms and institutional constraints describe the sociological level of analysis, such as the social facts of Émile Durkheim (1895); in Kurt Lewin's formula they count only as part of the environment the person perceives, not as one of the two core terms."}}, {"q": "According to the Elaboration Likelihood Model (ELM) of persuasion, under which specific condition will an individual process a message using the central route ?", "concept": "The answer is d: in the Elaboration Likelihood Model, people take the central route when they have both the motivation and the ability to think carefully about a message. Richard Petty and John Cacioppo (1986) proposed the ELM to explain why the same message persuades some people and not others. Elaboration means thinking about the arguments in a message. When elaboration likelihood is high, people take the central route: they scrutinise argument quality, and strong arguments produce attitude change that lasts, resists counter-attack and predicts behaviour. Motivation comes from personal relevance and a high need for cognition; ability needs time, knowledge and freedom from distraction. If either is missing, people take the peripheral route and are swayed by surface cues such as an attractive or famous source, the sheer number of arguments or a good mood; this change is quicker but weaker and short-lived. In a classic study, Richard Petty, John Cacioppo and Rachel Goldman (1981) found that students judged a proposed comprehensive examination on argument quality when it would start next year (high relevance) but on source expertise when it was a decade away. Example: Meera, choosing health insurance for her parents, reads every clause and compares claim ratios: high motivation and ability, so central route. Her cousin picks a plan because a film star endorses it: peripheral route. Option d is right because the central route needs motivation and ability together. Exam trap: options a, b and c each describe a peripheral route condition (distraction, an attractive source, emotional appeal); examiners count on students confusing 'persuasive' with 'central'.", "traps": {"a": "High distraction and low cognitive capacity remove the ability to elaborate, which pushes a person onto the peripheral route; Richard Petty, Gary Wells and Timothy Brock (1976) showed that distraction weakens the impact of strong arguments.", "b": "An attractive or famous source is a textbook peripheral cue in the ELM: it persuades through liking and mental shortcuts when motivation or ability is low, not through scrutiny of the arguments.", "c": "Heavy fear appeals work mainly through emotion and arousal, studied as message factors by Irving Janis and Seymour Feshbach (1953); relying on emotion is not the condition that triggers central route processing."}}, {"q": "According to Harold Kelley's Covariation Model of attribution, an observer will make an external attribution when a target's behavior exhibits :", "concept": "The answer is d: Harold Kelley's covariation model predicts an external attribution when consensus, distinctiveness and consistency are all high. Harold Kelley (1967) asked how we decide whether a behaviour is caused by the person, the stimulus or the circumstances. His answer is that we act like intuitive scientists: across many observations we credit the cause that varies together, or covaries, with the behaviour. Three kinds of information matter. Consensus asks whether other people react to the same stimulus in the same way. Distinctiveness asks whether this person reacts differently to other stimuli. Consistency asks whether this person reacts the same way to this stimulus on other occasions. High consensus, high distinctiveness and high consistency point outward, to the stimulus or situation. Low consensus, low distinctiveness and high consistency point inward, to the person's disposition. Low consistency points to passing circumstances. Leslie McArthur (1972) tested the model with written vignettes and found that people do use all three cues, though they underuse consensus. Example: Kabir laughs at a stand-up comedian. Everyone else in the hall laughs too (high consensus), Kabir rarely laughs at other comedians (high distinctiveness) and he laughs at this one every time (high consistency). We conclude the comedian is genuinely funny: an external cause. Option d is right because only the all-high pattern points to the stimulus. Exam trap: option a is the mirror image, the classic pattern for an internal attribution; consistency is high in both patterns, and examiners rely on students forgetting that consensus and distinctiveness flip.", "traps": {"a": "Low distinctiveness, low consensus and high consistency is Harold Kelley's pattern for an internal (dispositional) attribution: the person reacts this way to everything, nobody else does, and it happens every time.", "b": "High distinctiveness and low consensus with low consistency leads to a circumstance attribution in Harold Kelley's model; low consistency marks the behaviour as a one-off, put down to passing conditions rather than the stimulus.", "c": "Low distinctiveness, high consensus and low consistency is a mixed, ambiguous pattern; with low consistency the observer again leans towards a circumstantial explanation, not a clear external attribution to the stimulus."}}, {"q": "According to Jean Piaget's cognitive development theory, which cognitive limitation explains why a preoperational child believes that a tall, narrow glass holds more liquid than a short, wide glass, even after watching the liquid poured directly from one to the other ?", "concept": "The answer is c: the preoperational child fails liquid conservation because of centration and a lack of reversibility. Jean Piaget (1952), in The Child's Conception of Number, showed that young children do not grasp conservation, the understanding that a quantity stays the same when only its shape or arrangement changes. In the liquid task a child agrees that two identical short, wide glasses hold the same amount of juice. One is then poured, in full view, into a tall, narrow glass. A four- or five-year-old now says the tall glass has more. Two limits of preoperational thought (about 2 to 7 years) explain this. Centration is focusing on one striking feature, here the height of the liquid, while ignoring another, the narrower width. Lack of reversibility is the inability to run the action backwards in the mind and see that pouring the juice back would give equal glasses again. Around 7, in the concrete operational stage, children conserve and justify it with three arguments: identity (nothing was added or taken away), compensation (taller but thinner) and reversibility (pour it back and it is the same). Example: Riya, aged 4, insists her brother's tall glass of lassi has more than hers, though their mother poured both from the same jug; at 8 she laughs at the idea. Option c is right because centration plus irreversibility is Piaget's own explanation of conservation failure. Exam trap: lack of object permanence (b) sounds like losing track of things, but it is an infant limitation, solved by about 2 years, long before this task.", "traps": {"a": "Assimilation is Jean Piaget's term for fitting new information into an existing scheme; preoperational children assimilate constantly, so an inability to assimilate is not why they fail conservation.", "b": "Object permanence, knowing that objects exist when out of sight, is a sensorimotor achievement complete by about 18 to 24 months, so a preoperational child already has it; the juice never leaves view in this task anyway.", "d": "Formal deductive operations belong to the formal operational stage from about 11 or 12; missing them explains a younger child's trouble with abstract hypotheses, not failure to conserve, which concrete operations solve around 7."}}, {"q": "Which of the following developmental milestones is uniquely characteristic of the transition into the formal operational stage of cognitive development ?", "concept": "The answer is a: systematic hypothetico-deductive reasoning and abstract thought are the signature of the formal operational stage. In the account of Bärbel Inhelder and Jean Piaget (1958), cognitive development runs through four stages: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (from about 11 or 12). Concrete thinkers reason logically, but only about real, picturable things. Formal thinkers can reason about possibilities, about abstractions such as justice or infinity, and about propositions whose conclusions follow from their logical form. The core skill is hypothetico-deductive reasoning: starting from a general idea, deducing hypotheses and testing them systematically, one variable at a time. Inhelder and Piaget showed this with the pendulum problem in The Growth of Logical Thinking from Childhood to Adolescence. Children had to find what controls how fast a pendulum swings: string length, weight, height of release or force of push. Concrete operational children changed several things at once and drew muddled conclusions; formal operational adolescents varied one factor while holding the others constant and found that only string length matters. Example: Arjun, 14, tests why his plants are wilting by changing only the watering in one pot while keeping light and soil the same; that is formal operational reasoning. Option a is right because hypothetical, abstract and systematic reasoning first appears at the formal operational stage. Exam trap: conservation (b) is the most famous Piagetian milestone, so students grab it, but it marks the concrete operational stage; formal operations are about thinking beyond the concrete.", "traps": {"b": "Conservation of mass and volume is a concrete operational achievement in Jean Piaget's theory, arriving in steps (horizontal decalage: mass around 7 to 8, weight around 9 to 10, volume around 11 to 12); it is logic about concrete objects, not hypothetical reasoning.", "c": "Basic symbolic play, such as using a block as a phone, emerges at the start of the preoperational stage (around 2) with the symbolic function; it is far too early to mark formal operations.", "d": "Tracking objects out of view is the start of object permanence in the sensorimotor stage of infancy (roughly 8 to 12 months), not an adolescent milestone."}}, {"q": "According to Paul Baltes' framework of life-span developmental psychology, successful aging is optimized through which of the following behavioural management process ?", "concept": "The answer is d: the model of successful ageing in Paul Baltes' framework is selective optimisation with compensation (SOC). Paul Baltes and Margret Baltes (1990) proposed SOC as a general strategy for ageing well. It grew out of the lifespan view Paul Baltes (1987) had set out: development is lifelong, multidirectional and always a mix of gains and losses, and in late life the losses grow. SOC describes three processes for managing this. Selection means narrowing one's goals to the domains that matter most, either by choice or because of a loss. Optimisation means investing time, effort and practice to keep performing well in those chosen domains. Compensation means using new means, such as aids, other people or new strategies, when old means are lost. The Baltes couple's classic illustration was the pianist Arthur Rubinstein, who in his eighties played fewer pieces (selection), practised them more (optimisation) and slowed down before fast passages so they sounded quicker by contrast (compensation). Alexandra Freund and Paul Baltes (1998) found that older adults who reported using SOC strategies had higher well-being. Example: Kabir's grandmother, 78, gives up her large garden but keeps her favourite herb bed (selection), spends more time tending it (optimisation) and uses a kneeling stool and long-handled tools for her stiff knees (compensation). Option d is right because SOC is precisely the behavioural management process Paul Baltes named. Exam trap: option a looks technical because it lists defence mechanisms, but sublimation and projection are psychoanalytic ideas from Sigmund Freud (1894) and Anna Freud (1936), not lifespan psychology.", "traps": {"a": "Sublimation and projection are ego defence mechanisms from Sigmund Freud (1894) and Anna Freud (1936); they describe unconscious protection against anxiety, not a conscious strategy for successful ageing.", "b": "Continuous physical deceleration describes biological decline, the loss side of ageing; Paul Baltes stressed gains as well as losses, and decline is not a strategy that optimises ageing.", "c": "Age-segregated institutionalisation, separating older adults from the rest of society, is closer to the withdrawal in the disengagement theory of Elaine Cumming and William Henry (1961); it is not a behavioural management process for successful ageing."}}, {"q": "According to Urie Bronfenbrenner's ecological systems theory, the direct interaction between a child's family and their school teachers falls under which system layer ?", "concept": "The answer is b: links and interactions between two of a child's microsystems, such as family and school, make up the mesosystem. Urie Bronfenbrenner (1979), in The Ecology of Human Development, pictured the child at the centre of nested environmental systems, like Russian dolls, each influencing the child and influenced in turn. The microsystem is the innermost layer: settings the child takes part in directly and face to face, such as home, classroom, peer group and neighbourhood. The mesosystem is the set of connections between those microsystems, such as parent-teacher meetings, help with homework or ties between a child's friends and parents. It is a relationship between settings, not a setting. The exosystem contains settings the child is not part of that still affect the child, such as a parent's workplace, the school management committee or local health services. The macrosystem is the overarching culture: values, laws, customs and the economic system. Bronfenbrenner later added the chronosystem, the dimension of time and life transitions, and renamed the approach the bioecological model. A strong mesosystem helps: children do better when home and school share expectations and communicate. Example: Meera's parents meet her class teacher every month, and the teacher sends home reading tips they follow; this home-school link is the mesosystem. Option b is right because the stem describes an interaction between two microsystems, which is the definition of the mesosystem. Exam trap: microsystem (a) tempts because the family and the teachers each belong to a microsystem, but the question asks about the link between them; the prefix meso (middle) marks that in-between layer.", "traps": {"a": "The microsystem is each setting the child is directly in, such as the family on its own or the classroom on its own; the interaction between two such settings is one level up, the mesosystem.", "c": "The exosystem holds settings that affect the child without the child being present, such as a parent's workplace or the school board; the child's own family and teachers are not in it.", "d": "The macrosystem is the broad cultural layer of values, laws and economic systems in Urie Bronfenbrenner's (1979) model, far removed from a specific family-teacher interaction."}}, {"q": "The cognitive limitation wherein a young child is unable to take or understand another person's spatial or psychological perspective is called :", "concept": "The answer is c: egocentrism is a young child's inability to take another person's point of view, whether spatial or psychological. In the theory of Jean Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder (1956), egocentrism is the hallmark of the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years). It does not mean selfishness. It means the child assumes that others see, think and feel what the child does, because the child cannot yet separate their own perspective from someone else's. Piaget and Inhelder tested it with the three mountains task. Children sat in front of a model of three mountains, one with snow, one with a house and one with a red cross, and chose the picture showing what a doll seated elsewhere would see. Four-year-olds usually picked their own view; correct choices became common only around 7 to 8. Egocentrism also shows in speech (talking without checking whether the listener understands) and in hiding games, where a toddler covers their own eyes and believes they cannot be seen. Later research, such as the policeman doll task of Martin Hughes (1975), showed that with familiar, meaningful tasks children take others' perspectives much earlier than Piaget thought. Example: Kabir, 3, holds up his drawing to show his grandmother on a video call with the paper facing himself, sure she can see it. Option c is right because failure to take another's spatial or psychological perspective is the definition of egocentrism. Exam trap: centration (a) is a close cousin, since both are preoperational limits, but centration is focusing on one feature of an object or problem, not failing to take another person's viewpoint.", "traps": {"a": "Centration is the preoperational habit of focusing on one salient feature, such as the height of liquid in a glass, while ignoring others; it concerns objects and problems, not other people's viewpoints.", "b": "Animism is the preoperational belief that lifeless things are alive and have feelings, such as a child saying the moon follows her; it is a separate limitation in the account of Jean Piaget (1929), not failure of perspective taking.", "d": "Abstract thought is not a limitation at all but an achievement of the formal operational stage from about 11 or 12, the opposite end of cognitive development."}}, {"q": "The dynamic process of biological growth and development that progresses naturally according to a genetic blueprint over time is called :", "concept": "The answer is d: maturation is the natural unfolding of growth and development according to a genetic timetable, with little dependence on practice. Arnold Gesell (1928) built his maturational theory on this idea. Watching hundreds of children at Yale, he found that motor and other milestones appear in a fixed sequence and at fairly predictable ages, and he published age norms for them. In his view development is driven from within, and teaching cannot push a child past what maturation allows. Readiness is therefore the key idea: learning works best once maturation has prepared the body and brain. Arnold Gesell and Helen Thompson (1929) tested this with the method of co-twin control. One identical twin, T, was trained in stair climbing for six weeks from 46 weeks of age; her sister, C, began training only at 53 weeks, yet caught up within about two weeks. Practice added little before the body was ready. Walking, puberty and the arrival of teeth are classic examples of maturation. Learning, by contrast, is a relatively permanent change in behaviour that results from experience and practice. Example: Riya's parents worry that she is not walking at 10 months, but most babies walk between 9 and 15 months, so the doctor reassures them that her maturational timetable will bring it. Option d is right because the stem describes growth guided by a genetic blueprint, which is the definition of maturation. Exam trap: learning (a) is the usual decoy, since learning and maturation always work together, but learning depends on experience, while maturation depends on biology and time.", "traps": {"a": "Learning is a relatively permanent change in behaviour produced by experience and practice, as studied by Ivan Pavlov (1927) and B. F. Skinner (1938); it is not driven by a genetic blueprint.", "b": "Socialisation is the process of acquiring the norms, values and behaviour of one's society through family, school and peers; it depends on social experience, not genetic timing.", "c": "Habituation is a simple form of learning in which the response to a repeated, harmless stimulus declines, as when a baby stops looking at a familiar picture; it is experience-based, not growth."}}, {"q": "A client presents with an extreme, irrational fear of being evaluated or scrutinized by others in social situations, leading to severe avoidance of public speaking and group meetings. Under the DSM-5, this profile warrants a diagnosis of :", "concept": "The answer is b: an intense, irrational fear of being evaluated or scrutinised by others, with avoidance of speaking and meetings, is social anxiety disorder in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013). Social anxiety disorder, formerly called social phobia, is marked fear or anxiety about one or more social situations in which the person may be watched or judged: conversations, eating or drinking in front of others, giving a speech. The core fear is negative evaluation: being humiliated, embarrassed or rejected, or offending others. The situations almost always provoke fear and are avoided or endured with intense distress, the fear is out of proportion to the real threat, and it typically lasts six months or more with clear impairment. A performance only specifier covers people whose fear is confined to speaking or performing in public. The disorder usually begins in the early teens. David M. Clark and Adrian Wells (1995) explained why it persists: anxious people turn attention inward to monitor how they come across, and use safety behaviours such as avoiding eye contact, which stop them learning that disaster does not happen. Cognitive behavioural therapy with exposure is the first-line treatment, and SSRIs also help. Example: Arjun, a capable engineer, refuses a promotion because it involves presenting to clients; he dreads that others will see his hands shake and think him incompetent. Option b is right because fear of scrutiny and negative evaluation is the defining feature of social anxiety disorder. Exam trap: agoraphobia (a) also involves avoiding crowds and public places, but the agoraphobic fears being unable to escape or get help if panic strikes, not being judged by other people.", "traps": {"a": "Agoraphobia is fear of at least two situations where escape might be difficult or help unavailable, such as public transport, open or enclosed spaces, crowds or being outside alone; the fear is of panic-like symptoms and being trapped, not of being judged.", "c": "Generalised anxiety disorder is excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters (health, money, work) on more days than not for at least six months; it is not centred on social scrutiny.", "d": "Panic disorder is recurrent unexpected panic attacks followed by at least a month of worry about further attacks or change in behaviour; the attacks come out of the blue, not from fear of evaluation."}}, {"q": "The 'cognitive triad' in Beck's Cognitive Therapy for depression consists of a patient's dysfunctional, negative views regarding which three dimensions ?", "concept": "The answer is a: Aaron Beck's cognitive triad is a depressed person's negative view of the self, the world and the future. Aaron Beck (1967), a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, set out the model in Depression: Clinical, Experimental and Theoretical Aspects. Studying depressed patients' dreams and thoughts, he expected to find anger turned inward, as Freudian theory predicted, but found instead themes of loss, defeat and worthlessness. He proposed that depression is maintained by a pattern of negative thinking with three parts. The negative view of the self says 'I am worthless, inadequate, unlovable'. The negative view of the world, or of current experience, says 'everything is against me, nothing works out'. The negative view of the future says 'it will never get better', which links to hopelessness and suicide risk. These views are kept alive by automatic thoughts, by underlying schemas formed in childhood, and by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation and selective abstraction. In cognitive therapy, Aaron Beck and colleagues (1979) taught patients to catch these thoughts, test them against evidence through collaborative empiricism and Socratic questioning, and replace them with balanced ones. Example: after failing one exam, Meera thinks 'I am stupid' (self), 'my teachers are unfair and nobody helps me' (world) and 'I will never get a job' (future): the full triad. Option a is right because self, world and future are the three domains Aaron Beck named. Exam trap: students mix up the triad with past, present and future, or with the CBT triangle of thoughts, feelings and behaviours; the cognitive triad is self, world, future.", "traps": {"b": "Past achievements, current status and biological health is an invented list; Aaron Beck's triad concerns how the person views themselves, their experiences and what lies ahead, not achievements or physical health.", "c": "The mother, the father and the siblings evoke the family triangles of family systems theory, such as those of Murray Bowen (1978); they have nothing to do with Aaron Beck's cognitive model.", "d": "Unconscious desires, societal constraints and ego ideals echo the id, superego and ego ideal in the structural model of Sigmund Freud (1923), a psychodynamic account of personality, not the conscious negative thoughts of Aaron Beck's triad."}}, {"q": "Which therapeutic modality, developed by Fritz Perls, emphasizes immediate, here-and-now awareness, integrating fragmented parts of the self, and using techniques like the 'empty chair' ?", "concept": "The answer is a: the therapy Fritz Perls developed, built on here-and-now awareness, integrating disowned parts of the self and the empty chair, is Gestalt therapy. Frederick Salomon (Fritz) Perls (1893 to 1970), working with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman, set out the approach in Gestalt Therapy (Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman, 1951). It is a humanistic, experiential therapy. Its central idea is that distress comes from unfinished business (old feelings never fully expressed) and from parts of the self that have been split off and disowned. Talking about the past is discouraged; the client is asked to notice, right now, what he feels, does and avoids (\"What are you aware of now?\"). Change comes from awareness and contact, not from the therapist's interpretation. In the empty chair technique the client speaks to an imagined person, or to a disowned part of himself, placed in an empty chair, and may then switch chairs and answer back. In the related two-chair dialogue, the critical 'top dog' and the excuse-making 'underdog' argue until the client owns both sides. For example, Kabir, who never grieved his father, tells the empty chair what he could not say at the funeral and then answers as his father; the anger and sadness he had pushed away become part of him again. Option a is right because every feature in the stem (Perls, here-and-now awareness, integration of fragments, the empty chair) is a signature of Gestalt therapy. Exam trap: do not confuse Gestalt therapy with Gestalt psychology, the school of perception founded by Max Wertheimer (1912); they share only the word Gestalt (whole). Role play with chairs also appears in the psychodrama of Jacob Moreno (1921), but the empty chair as a here-and-now awareness technique is Perls's.", "traps": {"b": "REBT is Albert Ellis's (1955) cognitive therapy that disputes irrational beliefs through the ABC model (Activating event, Belief, Consequence); it is directive and logical, not an awareness-based empty chair method.", "c": "Psychoanalysis is Sigmund Freud's (1896) method of free association, dream analysis and interpretation of transference, focused on the unconscious past rather than here-and-now awareness. Perls trained as an analyst but broke away from it.", "d": "Logotherapy is Viktor Frankl's (1946) existential therapy built on the will to meaning, using paradoxical intention and dereflection; it has no empty chair."}}, {"q": "A client undergoes an intense behaviour therapy procedure where they are continuously exposed to their maximum feared stimulus in a safe setting without any possibility of escape until their anxiety completely subsides. This is an example of :", "concept": "The answer is b: continuous exposure to the most feared stimulus at full intensity, with escape blocked until anxiety subsides, is flooding. Flooding is an exposure-based behaviour therapy. It rests on extinction: in classical conditioning, a conditioned fear weakens when the feared cue keeps appearing without the harm it once predicted. Avoidance normally prevents this, because each escape brings relief, so escape is negatively reinforced and the person never discovers that the fear was false. Flooding removes that exit. The client is exposed, in real life (in vivo) or in imagination, to the top of the fear hierarchy at once, and stays until arousal peaks and falls on its own, often within 30 to 90 minutes. Thomas Stampfl's implosive therapy (1967) is an imaginal version that adds exaggerated, psychodynamic themes. Flooding works quickly but is very distressing, so it needs informed consent and a client able to tolerate it. For example, Meera, who fears lifts, rides a lift with her therapist floor after floor for an hour; her panic rises sharply, then fades, and the lift loses its power over her. Option b is right because the stem gives flooding's three defining marks: the maximum feared stimulus, no possibility of escape, and staying until anxiety subsides. Exam trap: systematic desensitisation, from Joseph Wolpe (1958), also treats phobias through exposure, so examiners use it as the near miss. The difference is the dose and the method: desensitisation climbs a graded hierarchy step by step while the client is relaxed (reciprocal inhibition), whereas flooding starts at the top with no relaxation and no escape.", "traps": {"a": "Systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's (1958) graded method: relaxation is paired with each step of an anxiety hierarchy, from least to most feared, so it never begins with the maximum stimulus.", "c": "Aversive conditioning (aversion therapy) pairs an unwanted behaviour, such as drinking alcohol, with an unpleasant stimulus such as a nausea-producing drug in order to reduce the behaviour; it adds discomfort to a pleasure rather than extinguishing a fear.", "d": "Modelling is Albert Bandura's (1977) observational learning: the client watches someone else approach the feared object safely, often in graded steps, instead of being held in the feared situation himself."}}, {"q": "When a client in psychoanalysis unconsciously directs the intense emotional feelings, conflicts, or expectations they originally held toward their parents onto the therapist, it is called :", "concept": "The answer is c: the unconscious redirection onto the therapist of feelings, conflicts and expectations originally held towards parents is transference. Sigmund Freud (1905) recognised it in the case of Dora, a young patient who broke off treatment abruptly; he realised she had been reacting to him as if he were the men who had hurt her. Transference happens because early relationship patterns live on unconsciously, and the analyst's neutral, fairly anonymous stance gives them a blank screen to land on. It can be positive (affection, idealisation, love) or negative (hostility, suspicion). Freud first saw it as an obstacle, then as the main tool of cure: when the patient's old conflict is relived with the analyst (the transference neurosis), it can be interpreted and worked through in the present. For example, Arjun, whose father was harsh and critical, becomes sure that his calm therapist is secretly disappointed in him and starts arriving late to avoid her 'judgement'; exploring this shows him how he treats every authority figure. Option c is right because the stem describes the client's feelings about parents being directed onto the therapist, which is the textbook definition of transference. Exam trap: countertransference runs the other way: it is the therapist's emotional reaction to the client. Freud (1910) named it as a blind spot arising from the therapist's own unresolved conflicts, and since Paula Heimann (1950) the term also covers all the therapist's feelings, used as clues about the client. If the stem says the client feels something towards the therapist, choose transference; if the therapist does, choose countertransference.", "traps": {"a": "Resistance is the client's unconscious opposition to the work of therapy, such as forgetting sessions or falling silent as painful material nears; transference can be used as resistance, but resistance itself is not the shifting of parental feelings onto the therapist.", "b": "Countertransference is the therapist's emotional reaction to the client, named by Sigmund Freud in 1910; the stem describes feelings flowing from client to therapist, the opposite direction.", "d": "Catharsis is the emotional release of pent-up feeling by reliving a memory, the basis of Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud's cathartic method (1895); it is a discharge of emotion, not its redirection onto the therapist."}}, {"q": "Which of the following describes 'psychological resilience' in clinical psychology ?", "concept": "The answer is d: psychological resilience is the capacity to adapt successfully and keep stable psychological functioning in the face of severe adversity or trauma. Resilience research began with children who did well against the odds. Emmy Werner (1982), with Ruth Smith, followed every child born on the Hawaiian island of Kauai in 1955 for decades; about one third of the high-risk children (poverty, family conflict, birth problems) grew into competent, caring adults. Protective factors included an easy temperament, at least one stable caring adult, good problem-solving skills and support outside the family. Ann Masten (2001) called resilience 'ordinary magic' because it comes from common human resources, not rare gifts. George Bonanno (2004) showed that after loss or trauma the commonest path in adults is resilience: a short dip, then stable healthy functioning, rather than lasting disorder. Resilient people still feel pain and still show stress responses; what marks them out is recovery and adaptation. For example, Riya loses her home in a flood. She grieves, sleeps badly for a few weeks and feels afraid, but she leans on her cousins, returns to college and within months is functioning well again: that is resilience. Option d is right because it captures the two essentials of the definition: significant adversity, and positive adaptation despite it. Exam trap: resilience is not invulnerability or the absence of distress. Options that say 'absolute absence' or 'complete suppression' describe impossible states or unhealthy avoidance; resilience is bending and bouncing back, not never bending. Do not confuse it with hardiness, the commitment, control and challenge of Suzanne Kobasa (1979), a personality style that can support resilience.", "traps": {"a": "An absolute absence of any physiological stress response is neither realistic nor healthy; resilient people do show stress reactions (raised heart rate, cortisol, fear) but recover and adapt, so this describes invulnerability, not resilience.", "b": "Complete suppression of negative emotional memories describes suppression or repression, defence mechanisms described by Sigmund Freud (1894) and Anna Freud (1936); chronic suppression is linked with poorer adjustment, not with resilience.", "c": "Manic defence is Melanie Klein's (1935) psychoanalytic term for warding off sadness and loss through omnipotence, denial and triumph; it is a defensive escape from pain, and a permanent state of it would be maladaptive."}}, {"q": "A client reports experiencing sudden, un-triggered episodes of intense terror, racing heart, chest pain, and shortness of breath, accompanied by a persistent fear of having future attacks. This clinical presentation points to :", "concept": "The answer is b: recurrent, unexpected surges of terror with racing heart, chest pain and breathlessness, plus persistent fear of further attacks, is panic disorder. A panic attack is an abrupt surge of intense fear that peaks within minutes, with at least 4 of 13 symptoms in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013): palpitations, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, choking, chest pain, nausea, dizziness, chills or heat, numbness, derealisation, fear of losing control and fear of dying. Attacks occur in many disorders, so an attack alone is not a diagnosis. Panic disorder needs recurrent unexpected attacks (out of the blue, with no obvious trigger), with at least one followed by 1 month or more of persistent worry about further attacks or their consequences (\"I will have a heart attack\"), or a maladaptive change in behaviour such as avoiding exercise. Donald Klein (1964) helped separate panic from general anxiety when he found that imipramine stopped the attacks but did not ease background worry. David Clark (1986) explained attacks cognitively, as catastrophic misinterpretation of normal bodily sensations. For example, Kabir's heart suddenly pounds while he watches television; he feels he is dying and rushes to hospital, where his ECG is normal. Weeks later he still dreads the next attack. Option b is right because the stem gives both halves of the diagnosis: un-triggered attacks and persistent fear of future attacks. Exam trap: specific phobia and social anxiety disorder can also produce panic attacks, but those attacks are cued by the feared object or situation. 'Un-triggered' points to panic disorder; 'whenever he sees a dog' points to a phobia.", "traps": {"a": "Generalised anxiety disorder is excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters on more days than not for at least 6 months, with tension, fatigue and poor sleep; it is chronic background worry, not sudden discrete attacks.", "c": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves intrusive, unwanted thoughts (obsessions) and repetitive acts or mental rituals (compulsions) that reduce the distress; the stem has no obsessions or rituals.", "d": "Specific phobia is marked fear of a particular object or situation, such as heights or snakes; any panic it causes is triggered by that cue, whereas these attacks are un-triggered."}}, {"q": "According to the Diathesis-Stress Model, a psychological disorder develops when :", "concept": "The answer is a: in the diathesis-stress model a disorder develops when a pre-existing vulnerability, often biological, interacts with environmental stress. A diathesis is a predisposition. It may be genetic (a family history of schizophrenia), biological (an over-reactive stress system) or psychological (a negative thinking style, early loss). Paul Meehl (1962) used the idea to explain schizophrenia: an inherited neural defect (schizotaxia) produces a vulnerable personality, but only certain life experiences tip it into illness. Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) generalised it as the vulnerability-stress model: everyone has a threshold, and illness appears when stress exceeds that person's threshold. The two parts interact, so neither alone is usually enough. A highly vulnerable person may break down under modest stress, while a person with little vulnerability may cope with severe stress. Twin studies fit this: when one identical twin has schizophrenia, the other develops it in only about half of cases, so genes load the gun and the environment pulls the trigger. For example, Arjun and Kabir both lose their jobs in the same month. Arjun, whose mother and grandmother had recurrent depression, falls into a major depressive episode; Kabir, with no such loading, is upset but recovers. Option a is right because it states the model's core: vulnerability and stress acting together. Exam trap: options that make one factor sufficient on its own (stress alone, genes alone, a single life event) describe one-dimensional models, the very views the diathesis-stress model was built to replace. Note too that the diathesis need not be biological; a cognitive vulnerability counts as well.", "traps": {"b": "Stress without any pre-existing vulnerability is a purely environmental or sociocultural account; the model holds that stress produces disorder only in someone who is already vulnerable.", "c": "Genes completely overriding the environment is biological determinism; the model rejects it, since even identical twins are often discordant for disorders such as schizophrenia, showing that environment matters.", "d": "A single high-stress event on its own is a pure life-events account; it leaves out the vulnerability half of the model, and most people who face such an event do not develop a disorder."}}, {"q": "A psychologist coordinates an immediate intervention for a community the following a major natural disaster to stabilize acute trauma symptoms and provide immediate coping resources. This falls under :", "concept": "The answer is c: an immediate, short-term intervention after a disaster to stabilise acute trauma reactions and supply coping resources is crisis intervention. A crisis, as Gerald Caplan (1964) defined it, arises when a person meets an obstacle to important life goals that cannot, for the time being, be overcome by his usual problem-solving, and a period of disorganisation follows. Crises usually resolve within about 4 to 6 weeks, for better or worse, so early help can tip the outcome. The approach grew from Erich Lindemann's (1944) study of the bereaved after the Cocoanut Grove nightclub fire in Boston, which suggested that timely brief help could prevent lasting grief reactions. Crisis intervention is brief, active and directive. It works in the here and now: ensuring safety, calming acute distress, meeting practical needs, restoring problem-solving and linking people to support and services. Its goal is a return to the pre-crisis level of functioning, not a change of personality. After disasters it is often delivered as psychological first aid. For example, after a cyclone in Odisha, a psychologist named Meera sets up relief-camp teams that check who is at risk, teach simple grounding and breathing, reunite families and refer the most distressed people for care. Option c is right because the stem stresses immediacy, a community after a disaster, stabilising acute symptoms and providing coping resources, all defining features of crisis intervention. Exam trap: one-session group 'debriefing' after trauma sounds similar, but it is a single technique, not the umbrella approach, and the review by Suzanna Rose and colleagues (2002) found that single-session debriefing does not prevent PTSD and may even slow recovery.", "traps": {"a": "Traditional psychoanalysis, from Sigmund Freud (1896), is long-term treatment several times a week aimed at unconscious conflicts; it is far too slow and exploratory for acute disaster response.", "b": "Long-term cognitive restructuring is the core of Aaron Beck's (1979) cognitive therapy, changing distorted thoughts over many sessions; it may help some survivors later but is not immediate stabilisation.", "d": "Group debriefing, as in Jeffrey Mitchell's (1983) critical incident stress debriefing, is one specific group technique rather than the overall approach, and single-session debriefing is no longer recommended because it does not prevent later disorder."}}, {"q": "What is the primary diagnostic difference between a manic episode and a hypomanic episode under the DSM-5 criteria ?", "concept": "The answer is c: under DSM-5 the main difference is severity: a manic episode causes marked impairment in social or occupational functioning, needs hospitalisation or has psychotic features, whereas a hypomanic episode does not. DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) gives both episodes the same core: a distinct period of abnormally elevated, expansive or irritable mood with persistently increased activity or energy, plus at least 3 of 7 symptoms (4 if the mood is only irritable). The symptoms are inflated self-esteem or grandiosity, decreased need for sleep, pressured speech, flight of ideas or racing thoughts, distractibility, increased goal-directed activity or agitation, and risky pleasure-seeking. The differences are duration and severity. Mania lasts at least 1 week (any duration if hospitalised); hypomania at least 4 consecutive days. In hypomania the change is clear and observable to others, but not severe enough to cause marked impairment or need hospitalisation, and there are no psychotic features; if any of these appear, the episode is by definition manic. The line matters because one manic episode makes bipolar I disorder, while bipolar II needs hypomania plus a major depressive episode. For example, Riya sleeps 4 hours a night for five days, talks fast and starts three projects, yet keeps up at work: hypomania. Her cousin Meera stops sleeping for a week, spends her savings, believes God has chosen her and is admitted: mania. Option c is right because impairment, hospitalisation and psychosis are the criteria DSM-5 uses to separate the two. Exam trap: do not reverse the durations. Mania needs the longer minimum (7 days) and hypomania the shorter (4 days), and psychotic features always mean mania, never hypomania.", "traps": {"a": "Elevated, expansive or irritable mood is the core feature of both manic and hypomanic episodes, so saying manic episodes lack elevated mood reverses the definition.", "b": "This reverses the duration rules: DSM-5 requires at least 1 week for a manic episode but only 4 consecutive days for a hypomanic one.", "d": "Delusions and hallucinations are excluded from hypomania; if psychotic features are present the episode is, by definition, manic."}}, {"q": "The mental health diagnostic system published by the World Health Organization (WHO) that covers both physical and mental disorders globally is known as the :", "concept": "The answer is b: the World Health Organization's classification that covers both physical and mental disorders worldwide is ICD-11. The International Classification of Diseases (ICD) began as a list of causes of death drawn up by Jacques Bertillon (1893), and WHO took it over in 1948. ICD-6 (1948) was the first edition to include mental disorders. ICD-10 (endorsed 1990, published 1992) placed them in Chapter V (F codes), still common in Indian case files. ICD-11 was adopted by the World Health Assembly in May 2019 and came into effect on 1 January 2022. It covers every kind of health condition, from infections and cancers to injuries, with mental, behavioural and neurodevelopmental disorders in Chapter 6 (codes such as 6A20 for schizophrenia). Its mental disorders come with Clinical Descriptions and Diagnostic Requirements, which are more flexible than DSM checklists, and it is free to use and available in many languages, which suits low-resource countries. For example, when Dr Meera records a patient's diabetes and depression in a government hospital in Delhi, both diagnoses are coded from the same ICD system. Option b is right because ICD is the only option published by WHO and the only one covering physical as well as mental disorders. Exam trap: DSM-5 is the best-known diagnostic manual, so examiners offer it first, but it is published by the American Psychiatric Association (2013; text revision DSM-5-TR, 2022) and covers mental disorders only. Remember the pairing: WHO and all diseases means ICD; APA and mental disorders only means DSM.", "traps": {"a": "DSM-5 is the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders of the American Psychiatric Association (2013); it covers mental disorders only and is not a WHO classification.", "c": "MMPI-2 is not a classification system but a 567-item true-false personality inventory (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989), revising the 1943 MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley.", "d": "PDM-2 is the Psychodynamic Diagnostic Manual, second edition (Vittorio Lingiardi and Nancy McWilliams, 2017), produced by psychoanalytic organisations to describe personality and mental functioning; it is not WHO's and covers no physical disease."}}, {"q": "Who is recognized as the historical founder of psychoanalysis, introducing concepts such as unconscious mind, ego, and defense mechanisms ?", "concept": "The answer is b: Sigmund Freud is recognised as the founder of psychoanalysis, the theory and therapy built on the unconscious mind, the ego and defence mechanisms. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) was a Viennese neurologist. Working with Josef Breuer, he published Studies on Hysteria (1895), based partly on Breuer's patient 'Anna O.', whose symptoms eased when she talked through forgotten memories (the 'talking cure'). Freud first used the word psychoanalysis in 1896 and replaced hypnosis with free association. In The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) he argued that much of mental life is unconscious and that dreams are the 'royal road' to it. In The Ego and the Id (1923) he divided personality into the id (instinctual drives, pleasure principle), the ego (reality principle, the mediator) and the superego (conscience). Defence mechanisms, such as repression and projection, are the ego's unconscious ways of keeping anxiety-provoking impulses out of awareness. For example, when Arjun keeps 'forgetting' his dental appointment, a Freudian would see repression of anxiety at work. Option b is right because Freud founded psychoanalysis and introduced all three concepts named in the stem. Exam trap: his daughter Anna Freud wrote The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936), which listed and systematised the defences, so examiners sometimes credit her; but the idea of defence began with Sigmund Freud's 1894 paper on the neuro-psychoses of defence. Carl Jung (1913) and Alfred Adler (1911) were early colleagues who broke away to found their own schools, so neither is the founder.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) was Freud's early heir who broke with him in 1913 and founded analytical psychology, with the collective unconscious and archetypes; he did not found psychoanalysis.", "c": "Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937) left Freud's circle in 1911 and founded individual psychology, centred on inferiority feelings, striving for superiority and social interest.", "d": "B. F. Skinner (1904 to 1990) was a radical behaviourist who studied operant conditioning and rejected unconscious mental causes altogether, the opposite of psychoanalysis."}}, {"q": "The psychological process of dealing with internal or external demands that are appraised as taxing or exceeding personal resources is known as :", "concept": "The answer is d: the process of managing internal or external demands appraised as taxing or exceeding one's resources is coping. The stem is almost word for word the definition given by Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984): coping is 'constantly changing cognitive and behavioural efforts to manage specific external and/or internal demands that are appraised as taxing or exceeding the resources of the person'. It belongs to their transactional model of stress, in which stress is not just an event but a relationship between person and environment. First comes primary appraisal (is this harmful, threatening or a challenge?), then secondary appraisal (what can I do about it?), and then coping, the actual effort. Lazarus and Folkman divided coping into problem-focused coping (changing the situation: making a plan, seeking information) and emotion-focused coping (managing the feelings: reappraising, seeking comfort, distraction). Problem-focused coping tends to help when the situation can be changed; emotion-focused coping helps when it cannot. Coping is a process, not a fixed trait, and it is defined by effort, not by success. For example, Kabir learns his board exam is in two weeks and he is behind. He sees a threat (primary appraisal), decides he can still revise two chapters a day (secondary appraisal), and then draws up a timetable and calls a friend when he panics: both kinds of coping. Option d is right because coping is the name for the efforts that follow when demands are appraised as exceeding resources. Exam trap: the stem contains the word 'appraised', which tempts students towards 'stress appraisal'. But appraisal is the judgement that comes before coping; coping is what you then do about the demand.", "traps": {"a": "Stress appraisal is the evaluation step in Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman's (1984) model, judging whether a demand is threatening (primary) and whether resources are enough (secondary); it comes before coping rather than being the process of dealing with the demand.", "b": "Homeostasis is Walter Cannon's (1926) term for the body's automatic tendency to keep internal states such as temperature stable; it is physiological self-regulation, not a psychological effort to handle appraised demands.", "c": "Resilience is the capacity to adapt well and recover after adversity; it is an outcome or quality that good coping can produce, not the moment-to-moment process of dealing with demands."}}, {"q": "Which specific source of diagnostic unreliability is primarily minimized by using structured clinical interview for DSM-5 ?", "concept": "The answer is d: a structured clinical interview such as the SCID mainly reduces information variance, the unreliability that arises when clinicians gather different information about the same patient. Robert Spitzer, Jean Endicott and Eli Robins (1975) named five sources of disagreement between diagnosticians. Subject variance: the patient really has different conditions at different times (intoxicated on one visit, sober on the next). Occasion variance: the patient is seen in different stages of the same illness. Information variance: clinicians have different information, because they ask different questions, cover different areas or use different informants. Observation variance: given the same information, clinicians notice or interpret it differently. Criterion variance: clinicians use different rules for turning the same data into a diagnosis. An earlier study by Clyde Ward, Aaron Beck and colleagues (1962) found that criterion variance caused most disagreements. The two problems got different fixes. Explicit operational criteria (the Feighner criteria of John Feighner and colleagues in 1972, then DSM-III in 1980) reduced criterion variance. Structured interviews such as the SCID (Robert Spitzer and colleagues, from the 1980s) reduced information variance by making every clinician cover the same questions in the same order, so no area is skipped. For example, Dr Riya and Dr Kabir see the same patient; Riya asks about past manic spells and Kabir does not, so they disagree. With the SCID, both must work through the mania module. Option d is right because standardising what is asked is exactly what a structured interview does. Exam trap: criterion variance is the near miss. It is cured mainly by explicit diagnostic criteria, the job of the DSM itself; the SCID applies those criteria, but its own contribution is standardising the information gathered.", "traps": {"a": "Subject variance is disagreement because the patient truly has different conditions at different times, such as being intoxicated at one interview; it lies in the patient, so no interview format can remove it.", "b": "Temporal variance (Robert Spitzer's 'occasion variance', 1975) arises when the patient is seen at different stages of an illness; it reflects real change over time, which a structured interview cannot standardise away.", "c": "Criterion variance is clinicians using different rules to turn the same findings into a diagnosis; it is reduced mainly by explicit operational criteria such as those of DSM-III (1980), not by the interview format itself."}}, {"q": "In the context of Rorschach inkblot test, what was the primary objective of John Exner's Comprehensive system ?", "concept": "The answer is d: the main aim of John Exner's Comprehensive System was to standardise the administration and scoring of the Rorschach. Hermann Rorschach (1921) published his ten inkblots but died a year later without a full scoring system. In the United States five rival systems grew up from the 1930s, led by Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Marguerite Hertz, Zygmunt Piotrowski and David Rapaport, each with its own codes and instructions. As a result, research done with one system could not be compared with research done with another, and reliability was poor. John E. Exner (1974) combined the elements of each system that had the best empirical support into one Comprehensive System. It fixed the seating, instructions and inquiry, required at least 14 responses for a valid record, defined each code (location, determinants, form quality, content, special scores) and gathered the codes into a Structural Summary of ratios and indices compared against norms. Examples are Lambda, the Experience Balance (EB) and constellations such as the depression index (DEPI) and the suicide constellation (S-CON). After Exner's death, Gregory Meyer and colleagues built the Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS, 2011) with the same aim. For example, if Dr Meera in Bengaluru and a psychologist in Chicago both score Arjun's protocol with the Comprehensive System, they should reach the same codes and compare them with the same norms. Option d is right because Exner's purpose was a single, standardised, empirically based way to give and score the test. Exam trap: standardising the Rorschach did not change its nature. It is still a projective, performance-based test, so 'eliminating projection' is wrong; Exner stressed the perceptual problem-solving side of responses but did not remove projection.", "traps": {"a": "Projection, the idea that people reveal inner needs when interpreting ambiguous stimuli (Lawrence Frank's projective hypothesis, 1939), remains the basis of the Rorschach; Exner standardised the test but did not try to remove projection.", "b": "Shortening administration was not Exner's aim; his system actually demands at least 14 responses for a valid record, and controlling record length was a later concern of R-PAS (Gregory Meyer and colleagues, 2011).", "c": "Face validity, whether a test looks as if it measures what it claims, is deliberately low in inkblot tests so that responses are hard to fake; Exner sought reliability, norms and empirical validity, not face validity."}}, {"q": "During behavioural observation, the phenomenon where a individual's behaviour changes just because they are being watched is known as :", "concept": "The answer is d: when a person's behaviour changes simply because he knows he is being watched, the effect is called reactivity. In behavioural assessment, a clinician counts and records what a person actually does: in natural settings (home, school, ward), in analogue settings (a simulated situation in the clinic) or through self-monitoring. Each method assumes the record shows typical behaviour, and reactivity threatens that assumption. A child may behave better when a stranger sits in the classroom, or a patient may smoke less on the days he records each cigarette. Reactivity usually fades as the person gets used to the observer, so clinicians allow a settling-in period, observe unobtrusively (one-way mirrors, video, or people already present such as teachers) or use records that need no observer. In research the same problem appears as the Hawthorne effect, named after the Western Electric Hawthorne studies (1924 to 1932) associated with Elton Mayo (1933), where output changed when workers knew they were being studied. Reactivity can even be used on purpose in therapy, since recording a habit often reduces it. For example, Arjun, aged 8, is referred for classroom disruption. On the first two days, with the psychologist sitting at the back, he sits quietly; by the fourth day, used to her presence, he is out of his seat again. Option d is right because reactivity is precisely the change in behaviour caused by the act of observation itself. Exam trap: observer drift sounds similar but concerns the observer, not the observed: over time, observers gradually change how they apply the behaviour definitions, so their records become less accurate. It is corrected by retraining and by checking agreement between observers.", "traps": {"a": "The expectancy effect (Robert Rosenthal, 1966) is when an observer's or experimenter's expectations bias what is recorded or subtly shape the participant's behaviour; the change comes from the observer's beliefs, not from simply being watched.", "b": "Habituation is a decrease in response to a repeated stimulus; in observation it describes the person getting used to the observer, which reduces reactivity rather than being the change itself.", "c": "Observer drift is the observer's gradual, unnoticed shift in how they apply behaviour definitions over time; it is an error in the recorder, not a change in the person being observed."}}, {"q": "Which component of the mental status exam specifically assesses the 'process' of thought rather than the 'content' of thought ?", "concept": "The answer is a: linearity describes how ideas connect, so it belongs to thought process (form of thought), whereas phobias, suicidality and obsessions are all thought content. In the mental status examination (MSE), the clinician separates how a person thinks from what he thinks about. Thought process, also called form of thought, asks whether ideas follow one another logically towards a goal. Normal thinking is described as linear, logical and goal-directed. Disordered process includes circumstantiality (long detours but finally reaching the point), tangentiality (drifting away and never returning), flight of ideas (rapid jumps on chance links, typical of mania), loosening of associations or derailment (ideas without logical links, which Eugen Bleuler (1911) made central to schizophrenia), thought blocking, perseveration and word salad. Thought content covers themes and beliefs: delusions, overvalued ideas, obsessions, phobias, preoccupations, and suicidal or homicidal ideas. The clinician rates process by listening to the structure of speech, and content by asking what occupies the person's mind. For example, Meera answers 'How did you come here today?' with a straight, connected account: her process is linear and goal-directed. Yet she says she cannot stop thinking her hands are contaminated (an obsession) and is terrified of lifts (a phobia): those go under content. Option a is right because linearity describes the flow and connection of ideas, the defining concern of thought process. Exam trap: obsessions feel like a 'process' because they repeat, but the MSE files them under content, since they describe what the person thinks. Likewise suicidal ideation is content, even though it is also assessed separately in risk assessment.", "traps": {"b": "Phobias are irrational fears of particular objects or situations; in the MSE they are recorded under thought content because they describe what the person thinks about, not how ideas are linked.", "c": "Suicidality (suicidal ideas, plans and intent) is recorded under thought content and in risk assessment; it is a theme of thinking, not a feature of its flow.", "d": "Obsessions are intrusive, unwanted thoughts recognised as one's own and resisted; though repetitive, they are classed as thought content, since they describe what occupies the mind."}}, {"q": "There are two girls Rita and Gita who are both five years old. One has an IQ of 140 and the other has an IQ of 80. What are their mental ages ?", "concept": "The answer is a: with a chronological age of 5, an IQ of 140 gives a mental age of 7 and an IQ of 80 gives a mental age of 4. This uses the ratio IQ. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905, revised 1908) grouped test items by the age at which typical children pass them, so a child's mental age (MA) is the age level of the items he or she passes. William Stern (1912) divided mental age by chronological age (CA), and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied the result by 100 in the Stanford-Binet: IQ = (MA ÷ CA) × 100. To find mental age, rearrange it: MA = (IQ × CA) ÷ 100. If Rita is the girl with IQ 140, her mental age is 140 × 5 ÷ 100 = 7, so she performs like an average seven-year-old. If Gita has IQ 80, her mental age is 80 × 5 ÷ 100 = 4, like an average four-year-old. A quick sense check: an IQ above 100 always means MA above CA, and an IQ below 100 always means MA below CA. The ratio IQ works for children, whose abilities grow each year, but fails for adults, whose mental age stops rising in the late teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15). Option a is right because 7 and 4, in the order the IQs are given (140, then 80), is the only pair that satisfies the formula. Exam trap: the usual slip is to invert the formula and divide CA by MA, or to add or subtract years instead of multiplying. Always check by working back: 7 ÷ 5 × 100 = 140 and 4 ÷ 5 × 100 = 80.", "traps": {"b": "Mental ages of 6 and 5 would give ratio IQs of 120 and 100 (6 ÷ 5 × 100 and 5 ÷ 5 × 100), not 140 and 80.", "c": "Mental ages of 8 and 5 would give ratio IQs of 160 and 100, so neither figure matches the stem.", "d": "A mental age of 4 does give an IQ of 80, but a mental age of 6 gives 120, not 140, so the first figure is wrong."}}, {"q": "Arrange the prominent personalities in clinical psychology in ascending order :", "concept": "The answer is c: in chronological (ascending) order the four are Plato, St. Augustine, Johann Weyer and William Harvey. The question asks you to place figures from the early history of ideas about mental illness on a timeline. Plato (about 427 to 347 BC), the Greek philosopher, saw mental disturbance partly as a loss of harmony in the soul, held that the disturbed should not be judged like others for their acts, and urged in the Laws that families care for them at home. St. Augustine of Hippo (AD 354 to 430) wrote the Confessions (about AD 397 to 400), a searching examination of his own feelings, conflicts and anguish that historians treat as a forerunner of introspection and even of psychoanalytic self-analysis. Johann Weyer (1515 to 1588), a Rhineland physician, argued in De praestigiis daemonum (1563) that many women accused of witchcraft were mentally ill and needed treatment, not burning; Gregory Zilboorg (1941) called him the founder of modern psychiatry. William Harvey (1578 to 1657), the English physician who described the circulation of the blood in De motu cordis (1628), also noted that emotions such as fear and anger disturb the heart, an early psychosomatic idea. A memory hook: Greek philosophy (BC), a Church Father of the late Roman Empire, the Renaissance protest against witch hunts (sixteenth century), then the scientific revolution (seventeenth century). For example, Kabir makes a timeline card: fourth century BC, about AD 400, 1563, 1628. Option c is right because it is the only sequence that follows these dates. Exam trap: Weyer and Harvey were both early modern physicians, so options swap them; but Weyer's book (1563) appeared before Harvey was even born (1578).", "traps": {"a": "This puts St. Augustine (AD 354 to 430) before Plato (about 427 to 347 BC), but Plato lived some 750 years earlier, so the first two are reversed.", "b": "This places William Harvey (De motu cordis, 1628) before Johann Weyer (De praestigiis daemonum, 1563); Weyer's work came first, before Harvey was even born.", "d": "This puts Johann Weyer, a sixteenth-century Renaissance physician, before St. Augustine, a Church Father of the fourth and fifth centuries AD, breaking the timeline by more than a thousand years."}}], "gurugram-2026-pdcp": [{"q": "A researcher divides a city into geographical zones and randomly selects three zones. Every household in the selected zones is included in the study. Which sampling technique is being used ?", "concept": "The answer is b: dividing the city into zones, randomly picking three zones and then including every household inside them is cluster sampling. Cluster sampling is a probability method in which the population is first split into naturally occurring groups, called clusters, such as zones, villages, schools or hospital wards. The researcher then randomly selects some whole clusters and studies them, instead of picking individuals from a full list of the population. When every member of each chosen cluster is included, as here, it is one-stage cluster sampling; when individuals are sampled again inside the chosen clusters, it becomes two-stage or multistage sampling, the method used in large surveys such as India's National Family Health Survey. Its main advantage is cost and convenience: no list of every household in the city is needed, only a list of zones, and field workers travel to just a few areas. Its weakness is precision. Households in one zone tend to resemble each other in income, housing and community, so a cluster sample usually carries a larger sampling error than a simple random sample of the same size. Example: Meera wants to survey family stress in Pune. She splits the city into 20 zones, draws three by lottery and interviews every household in those three. She never needed a city-wide household list. Option b is right because the unit chosen at random is the whole zone (the cluster), and everyone inside the selected clusters is studied. Exam trap: students confuse clusters with strata. In stratified sampling the population is also divided into groups, but the researcher samples from every group; in cluster sampling only some groups are chosen, and those are taken whole. Strata should be internally similar and different from one another, while each cluster should ideally be a small mixed copy of the whole population.", "traps": {"a": "Stratified sampling divides the population into strata (such as income groups) and draws a random sample from every stratum. Here only three zones were chosen and the rest were left out entirely, so it is not stratified.", "c": "Systematic sampling picks every kth unit from a list after a random start, such as every 10th household on a voters' roll. No list interval is used here; whole zones were chosen.", "d": "Quota sampling is a non-probability method in which the researcher fills set numbers for each category (say 50 men and 50 women) by convenience. Here zones were picked at random and everyone in them was included, so it is a probability method."}}, {"q": "A psychologist investigates whether childhood trauma is associated with adult anxiety by studying individuals who already experienced trauma. This is an example of :", "concept": "The answer is b: studying people who have already experienced childhood trauma, to see whether it is linked to adult anxiety, is ex post facto research. Ex post facto is Latin for 'after the fact'. Fred N. Kerlinger (1964) described it as research in which the independent variable has already occurred, or cannot ethically or practically be manipulated, so the researcher starts with pre-existing groups or with the outcome and looks back for possible causes. It is also called causal-comparative research. Here trauma is the independent variable. No psychologist could ever assign children to experience trauma, so the only route is to compare adults who were traumatised with adults who were not, and measure their anxiety. Example: Arjun, a researcher in Chennai, recruits 60 adults with documented childhood abuse and 60 without, and gives both groups an anxiety inventory. If the first group scores higher, Arjun has found an association, but he cannot be sure that trauma caused it, because the groups may also differ in poverty, parental mental illness or genes. Option b is right because the presumed cause happened before the study began and was not manipulated by the researcher; the researcher simply selected people who already differed on it. The two main weaknesses of the design are self-selection (people are not randomly placed in the groups) and the post hoc fallacy (assuming that whatever came first must be the cause). Its strength is that it lets researchers study causes that could never be ethically manipulated, such as abuse, bereavement, brain injury or smoking. Exam trap: ex post facto studies look like experiments because they compare groups, but there is no manipulation and no random assignment, so they cannot establish cause and effect. Ex post facto research is non-experimental.", "traps": {"a": "An experiment requires the researcher to manipulate the independent variable and randomly assign participants to conditions. Trauma cannot be manipulated or assigned, so this cannot be experimental research.", "c": "Longitudinal research follows the same people repeatedly over a long period, such as from childhood to adulthood. The stem describes a one-time comparison of people who already had trauma, with no repeated measurement.", "d": "'Correlational experiment' is a contradiction in terms: correlational research measures variables as they occur without manipulation, while an experiment manipulates. It is not a recognised design and does not capture the after-the-fact logic of the stem."}}, {"q": "Rejecting a true null hypothesis is known as :", "concept": "The answer is c: rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true is a Type I error, a false positive. Every significance test starts from the null hypothesis (H0), which states that there is no effect or no difference. After testing, the researcher either rejects H0 or fails to reject it. Because the decision rests on a sample, two kinds of mistake are possible. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1928, 1933) framed testing as a decision with fixed error rates and gave us the two error types. A Type I error means claiming an effect that does not exist. Its probability is alpha, the significance level chosen in advance, usually .05; so at alpha .05 a true null will be wrongly rejected about 5 times in 100. A Type II error is the opposite: failing to reject a null hypothesis that is false, so a real effect is missed. Its probability is beta, and the power of a test is 1 minus beta. Example: Kabir tests a new relaxation app on anxious students. In truth the app does nothing, but by chance his sample improves, p comes out at .03, and he announces that the app works. Kabir has made a Type I error. Lowering alpha to .01 reduces Type I errors, but with the same sample size it raises the risk of Type II errors; a larger sample reduces both. Option c is right because 'rejecting a true null' is the textbook definition of a Type I error. Exam trap: the two errors are easy to swap. Remember 'Type I is a false alarm (reject a true H0); Type II is a miss (keep a false H0)'. Examiners also offer 'accepting a false null' as a description of Type I, which is the reverse.", "traps": {"a": "A Type II error is failing to reject a null hypothesis that is false (a missed effect), with probability beta. The stem describes rejecting a true null, which is the opposite mistake.", "b": "Sampling error is the chance difference between a sample statistic and the population parameter, such as a sample mean that differs from the true mean. It is a source of uncertainty, not the name of a wrong decision about H0.", "d": "Measurement error is the gap between an observed score and the true score in classical test theory, caused by imperfect instruments or testing conditions. It concerns the accuracy of scores, not decisions in hypothesis testing."}}, {"q": "The asymptotic tails of a normal distribution indicate that :", "concept": "The answer is a: asymptotic tails mean that the normal curve comes closer and closer to the X-axis (the baseline) as it moves away from the mean, but never actually touches it. An asymptote is a line that a curve approaches without ever meeting. The normal probability curve, first derived by Abraham de Moivre (1733) and later tied to errors of observation by Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809), is bell-shaped, symmetrical and unimodal, with the mean, median and mode at the same central point. In theory it stretches from minus infinity to plus infinity. However far you move from the mean, a tiny probability always remains, so the curve stays just above the baseline. About 68.26 percent of cases lie within 1 standard deviation (SD) of the mean, 95.44 percent within 2 SD and 99.74 percent within 3 SD, which leaves 0.26 percent beyond 3 SD on either side: small, but not zero. Example: Riya's class takes an IQ test with a mean of 100 and an SD of 15. An IQ of 160, 4 SD above the mean, is extremely rare, but the curve says it is not impossible; the tail keeps going. The total area under the curve is 1 (100 percent), so areas can be read directly as probabilities. Option a is right because 'asymptotic' means exactly this: the tails approach the X-axis indefinitely without touching or crossing it. Exam trap: real data sets have a lowest and a highest score, so a drawn histogram does end, but the theoretical normal curve does not. Do not confuse asymptotic tails with skewness either: the normal curve has tails of equal length on both sides, and both are asymptotic.", "traps": {"b": "A curve that cuts the X-axis at two points would have a fixed lowest and highest value, like a bounded or truncated distribution. The theoretical normal curve never reaches the baseline, so it has no such points.", "c": "Positive skew means a longer tail on the right, with the mean pulled above the median. The normal curve is perfectly symmetrical (skewness of zero), and both of its tails are asymptotic.", "d": "The normal curve runs from minus infinity to plus infinity, and z-scores below the mean are negative. Asymptotic tails say nothing about scores being non-negative; that is a property of some raw scales, such as reaction time."}}, {"q": "A distribution with a longer tail on the right side is described as :", "concept": "The answer is b: a distribution whose longer tail stretches to the right, towards the high scores, is positively skewed. Skewness describes how far a distribution departs from symmetry, and the direction of skew is named after the tail, not the hump. In a positively (right) skewed distribution most scores pile up at the low end, and a few very high scores drag the tail out to the right. Those extreme scores pull the mean upwards, so the usual order is mean greater than median greater than mode. In a negatively (left) skewed distribution the tail points towards the low scores and the order reverses: mode greater than median greater than mean. Karl Pearson (1895) gave a simple index of skewness: 3 times (mean minus median), divided by the standard deviation. A positive value means right skew, a negative value left skew. Example: Arjun records monthly family income in a Delhi neighbourhood. Most families earn modest amounts, but two business owners earn many times more. The graph rises steeply on the left and trails off to the right: a positively skewed distribution, in which the median describes a typical family better than the mean. Reaction times and the number of hospital visits per year are other common right-skewed variables, and a very hard test, on which most students score low, is also positively skewed. Option b is right because the side of the longer tail names the skew, and here it is the right, or positive, side. Exam trap: students look at where the scores are bunched (on the left) and call the distribution 'left skewed'. The bunch is on the left precisely because the tail runs to the right. A very easy test gives the opposite pattern, negative skew.", "traps": {"a": "Negative skew is the mirror image: the longer tail points left, towards low scores, as on a very easy test where most students score high. The stem says the tail is on the right.", "c": "A symmetrical distribution, such as the normal curve, has tails of equal length on both sides, with the mean, median and mode together. A longer right tail breaks that symmetry.", "d": "Mesokurtic is a kurtosis term (peakedness and tail weight), describing a distribution as peaked as the normal curve. It says nothing about which side has the longer tail."}}, {"q": "Which of the following represents an interval level of measurement ?", "concept": "The answer is c: temperature measured in degrees Celsius is the classic example of an interval scale. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, remembered as NOIR: nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio. A nominal scale only names categories (gender, blood group). An ordinal scale puts things in order, but the gaps between ranks need not be equal (first, second and third in a race). An interval scale has order and equal distances between adjacent values, but its zero point is arbitrary, not a true absence of the quantity. A ratio scale adds a true zero (height, weight, reaction time, temperature in Kelvin). Celsius fits interval perfectly. The difference between 10 and 20 degrees equals the difference between 30 and 40 degrees, so adding and subtracting make sense. But 0 degrees Celsius is simply the freezing point of water, not 'no heat', so ratios do not make sense: 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20 degrees. Example: Meera notes that Delhi was at 40 degrees and Shimla at 20 degrees. She can say that Delhi was 20 degrees warmer, but not that it was twice as hot. In psychology, IQ scores and summed Likert-scale scores are usually treated as interval data, which is why means, standard deviations and t-tests are used with them. Option c is right because Celsius has equal units but an arbitrary zero, the defining pair of features of interval measurement. Exam trap: examiners offer 'rank' or 'socio-economic status' because they involve numbers or order. Order alone makes a scale ordinal; equal intervals are what make it interval, and a true zero is what would make it ratio.", "traps": {"a": "Gender is a nominal variable: its categories are names with no order or quantity. Numbers given to them (1 for male, 2 for female) are labels only.", "b": "Socio-economic status, as usually classified (lower, middle, upper), is ordinal: the classes are ordered, but the gap between lower and middle need not equal the gap between middle and upper.", "d": "Rank in a competition is ordinal: it shows who came first or second, but not by how much. The winner may beat the runner-up by one second and the third-placed person by ten minutes."}}, {"q": "A researcher measures the stress levels of the same participants before and after a mindfulness program. Which t-test is appropriate ?", "concept": "The answer is c: when the same participants are measured twice, before and after a programme, the right test is the matched (paired, related or dependent) samples t-test. The t-test was worked out by William Sealy Gosset (1908), a chemist at the Guinness brewery who published under the pen name 'Student', to compare means from small samples. It has three forms. The one-sample t-test compares one sample mean with a known or hypothesised value. The independent samples t-test compares the means of two separate groups of people. The paired samples t-test compares two sets of scores that come in linked pairs: the same person tested twice, or two people deliberately matched (twins, or patients matched on age and severity). The paired test works on each person's difference score (after minus before) and asks whether the mean difference is significantly different from zero, with degrees of freedom equal to the number of pairs minus 1. Because each person serves as his or her own control, stable individual differences (some people are always more stressed than others) are removed, so the paired test is usually more powerful than an independent test on the same number of scores. Example: Kabir runs an eight-week mindfulness course for 25 nurses in Bengaluru and measures each nurse's stress before and after. He computes 25 difference scores and tests their mean with 24 degrees of freedom. Option c is right because the before and after scores belong to the same people, so the samples are related, not independent. Exam trap: two sets of scores tempt students to pick the independent samples test. The deciding question is not 'how many sets of scores?' but 'do they come from the same or matched people?'. If yes, use the paired test; its non-parametric alternative is the signed-rank test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945).", "traps": {"a": "The independent samples t-test compares the means of two different, unrelated groups, such as a mindfulness group and a separate control group. Here the same people give both scores, so the samples are not independent.", "b": "The one-sample t-test compares a single sample mean with a known or hypothesised population value, such as a published norm. The stem compares two sets of scores from the same people.", "d": "The z-test is used when the population standard deviation is known, or with very large samples. The population SD of these stress scores is not known, and a plain z-test ignores the linked before and after pairs."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a primary method of data collection ?", "concept": "The answer is d: standard deviation is not a method of data collection at all; it is a descriptive statistic used after the data have been collected. Primary data are gathered first-hand by the researcher for the present study. The main primary methods are observation (watching and recording behaviour as it happens, in natural or controlled settings), the interview (face-to-face or telephone questioning, which may be structured, semi-structured or unstructured), the questionnaire (a written set of questions that respondents answer on their own, on paper or online) and psychological tests. Secondary data, by contrast, were collected by someone else for another purpose and are re-used: census records, hospital files, archives and published surveys. The standard deviation (SD), a term coined by Karl Pearson (1894), measures how widely scores spread around the mean; it is the square root of the average squared deviation from the mean. It is a tool of analysis, used to summarise data that observation, interviews or questionnaires have already produced. Example: Riya studies screen time among teenagers in Jaipur. She observes students in the school canteen, interviews ten parents and gives a questionnaire to 200 students. Those three are her methods of data collection. Later she calculates that mean screen time is 4 hours a day with an SD of 1.5 hours; the SD describes her data but collected none of it. Option d is right because observation, interview and questionnaire are all primary data-collection methods, while standard deviation is a measure of variability. Exam trap: in 'not' questions, examiners often slip in an item from a different stage of research, here analysis rather than collection. Ask which option belongs to a different category, not which method seems weakest.", "traps": {"a": "Observation is a primary method: the researcher records behaviour first-hand, as in naturalistic or participant observation. It is a true method of data collection, so it cannot be the answer.", "b": "The interview is a primary method: the researcher questions the respondent directly, in structured, semi-structured or unstructured form. It collects first-hand data, so it is not the odd one out.", "c": "The questionnaire is a primary method: a written set of questions answered by respondents themselves, widely used in surveys. It gathers new data for the study, so it belongs in the list."}}, {"q": "A histogram is most appropriate for displaying :", "concept": "The answer is b: a histogram is the graph designed for a continuous frequency distribution, such as test scores grouped into class intervals. A frequency distribution shows how often each score, or each class interval of scores, occurs. A histogram displays it as a set of adjoining rectangles: the class intervals run along the X-axis, frequency runs up the Y-axis, and the area of each bar is proportional to the frequency of its interval. The term was introduced by Karl Pearson (1891). The bars touch because the variable is continuous: one interval ends exactly where the next begins, so there is no gap on the scale. If you join the midpoints of the bar tops and bring the line down to the baseline at both ends, you get a frequency polygon, a related graph for the same kind of data. The shape of a histogram also shows at a glance whether a distribution is symmetrical, skewed or bimodal. Example: Arjun gives an anxiety scale to 120 college students in Kolkata and groups the scores into intervals of 10 to 19, 20 to 29, 30 to 39 and so on. He plots a histogram with touching bars and sees that most students cluster in the middle, with a long tail to the right. Option b is right because the histogram is built for continuous data grouped into intervals, which is exactly a continuous frequency distribution. Exam trap: the bar graph looks similar, but it is meant for nominal or categorical data (religion, diagnosis, gender), and its bars are separated by gaps to show that the categories are distinct. Touching bars mean a continuous scale; separated bars mean categories.", "traps": {"a": "Nominal data (categories such as gender or diagnosis) are shown with a bar graph that has gaps between the bars, or with a pie chart. A histogram's touching bars would wrongly suggest a continuous scale.", "c": "Correlation coefficients summarise relationships between pairs of variables; they are set out in a correlation matrix, and the relationship itself is drawn as a scatter diagram, not a histogram.", "d": "Factor loadings, the correlations of variables with factors in factor analysis, are presented in a factor matrix or loading plot, and eigenvalues in a scree plot. A histogram is not used for them."}}, {"q": "In criterion-related validity, the measure against which a test is compared is called the :", "concept": "The answer is b: in criterion-related validity the test is compared with an external criterion, an independent measure of the behaviour the test is meant to predict or reflect. Validity asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure. Criterion-related validity answers by correlating test scores with a criterion: a direct, outside measure of the same quality, such as job performance ratings, examination marks, a psychiatric diagnosis or later success in training. The resulting correlation is called the validity coefficient. There are two kinds. In concurrent validity the criterion is measured at about the same time as the test, as when a new depression scale is checked against a psychiatrist's diagnosis made the same week. In predictive validity the criterion comes later, as when an entrance test is checked against first-year university marks. The criterion must be external because a test cannot validate itself: agreement among its own items shows only consistency. A good criterion is relevant, reliable, free from bias and available at reasonable cost. If the people rating the criterion already know the test scores, the correlation is inflated artificially, a flaw called criterion contamination. Example: Kabir designs a clerical aptitude test for a bank in Mumbai. To check its predictive validity he correlates applicants' test scores with their supervisors' ratings after six months on the job. Those supervisor ratings are the external criterion. Option b is right because the yardstick lies outside the test itself and measures the real-world behaviour of interest. Exam trap: 'internal criterion' is a real term, but it belongs to item analysis and internal consistency, where each item is checked against the total score of the same test. That shows homogeneity, not criterion-related validity.", "traps": {"a": "An internal criterion is the total score of the same test, used in item analysis to see whether each item agrees with the rest (internal consistency). It cannot show that the test predicts anything outside itself.", "c": "'Parallel criterion' is not a standard term. It echoes parallel (alternate) forms, equivalent versions of a test that are used to estimate reliability, not criterion-related validity.", "d": "'Construct criterion' is not a standard term. Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), checks a test against a theory through convergent and discriminant evidence rather than against one outside criterion."}}, {"q": "Which of the following correlation coefficients indicates the strongest linear relationship, irrespective of direction ?", "concept": "The answer is b: a correlation of -0.92 shows the strongest linear relationship of the four, because strength depends on the size of the coefficient, not its sign. The Pearson product-moment correlation coefficient, r, developed by Karl Pearson (1896), runs from -1.00 to +1.00. Its sign gives the direction: positive means the two variables rise together, negative means one rises as the other falls. Its absolute value (the number with the sign ignored) gives the strength: 0 means no linear relationship, and 1 means that all the points fall exactly on a straight line. So rank the options by absolute value: 0.92 is larger than 0.76, 0.58 and 0.45. Squaring r gives the coefficient of determination, the proportion of variance the two variables share. -0.92 squared is about 0.85, so about 85 percent of the variation in one variable is accounted for by the other, against about 58 percent for +0.76 and only 20 percent for +0.45. Example: Meera, a counsellor in Lucknow, finds that among her clients the hours of sleep per night correlate -0.92 with scores on a fatigue scale. As sleep goes up, fatigue goes down, and very consistently. That is a stronger relationship than a +0.76 correlation between study hours and marks. Option b is right because, irrespective of direction, -0.92 is the closest of the four to 1 in absolute value. Exam trap: many students read the negative sign as 'weaker' or 'less', and choose the largest positive number (+0.76). The sign only tells direction; -0.92 is exactly as strong as +0.92. Remember also that r measures only linear relationships: a strong curved relationship can give an r near zero.", "traps": {"a": "+0.45 is a moderate positive correlation: the variables rise together, but only about 20 percent of the variance is shared. In absolute size it is the weakest of the four.", "c": "+0.76 is a strong positive correlation and the largest positive value offered, which is why it tempts. But 0.76 in absolute value is smaller than 0.92, so it is not the strongest.", "d": "-0.58 is a moderate negative correlation, sharing about 34 percent of the variance. Its sign is negative like the answer, but its absolute value (0.58) is much smaller than 0.92."}}, {"q": "The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula is primarily used to estimate :", "concept": "The answer is b: the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula estimates how the reliability of a test changes when the test is made longer or shorter. Charles Spearman (1910) and William Brown (1910) published it independently in the same issue of the British Journal of Psychology. Its logic is that a longer test samples behaviour more fully, so random errors tend to cancel out and reliability rises, provided the added items are of the same quality as the old ones. The general formula is: new reliability = nr divided by (1 + (n minus 1)r), where r is the present reliability and n is the factor by which the length changes (2 for doubling, 0.5 for halving). Its commonest use is in split-half reliability. The test is split into two halves, often odd and even items, and the halves are correlated. That half-test correlation underestimates the reliability of the full test, so the formula with n = 2 corrects it: full reliability = 2r divided by (1 + r). Example: Arjun splits his 40-item self-esteem scale into odd and even items and finds that the halves correlate 0.60. Corrected, the full-test reliability is 1.20 divided by 1.60, which is 0.75. If he doubled the scale to 80 similar items, the same formula predicts a reliability of about 0.86. It can also be run backwards to find how many items are needed to reach a target reliability. Option b is right because the 'prophecy' is exactly a prediction of reliability after the length of the test is changed. Exam trap: the formula concerns reliability, not validity, item quality or the standard error. Students also confuse it with the formula 20 of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) and the alpha of Lee Cronbach (1951), which estimate internal consistency directly without splitting.", "traps": {"a": "Item validity is judged in item analysis by correlating each item with a criterion or with the total score (item discrimination). The Spearman-Brown formula works on whole-test reliability, not on single items.", "c": "The standard error of measurement is computed from the test's SD and its reliability (SD times the square root of 1 minus r). It gives the error band around one person's score; Spearman-Brown does not compute it.", "d": "Predictive validity is the correlation of test scores with a criterion measured later, such as job performance. Lengthening a test can affect validity, but the Spearman-Brown formula predicts reliability."}}, {"q": "The nomothetic approach emphasizes :", "concept": "The answer is b: the nomothetic approach looks for universal or general laws of behaviour that hold for people in general. The terms come from the German philosopher Wilhelm Windelband (1894), who divided sciences into nomothetic ones (from the Greek nomos, law), which seek general laws, and idiographic ones (from idios, one's own), which seek to understand a single, unique case. Gordon Allport (1937) brought the pair into personality psychology. A nomothetic researcher studies many people on the same dimensions, using large samples, standardised tests and statistics, and places each person against group norms. The factor-analytic trait work of Raymond Cattell (1946), the dimensions of Hans Eysenck (1947) and the Big Five are classic nomothetic research, as are experiments that seek laws of learning and memory true of everyone. The idiographic approach instead studies one person in depth, through case studies, diaries, letters and open interviews, to grasp his or her unique pattern. Allport preferred it, famously analysing 301 letters written by one woman in Letters from Jenny (1965). Example: Kabir wants to know whether extraversion predicts the size of friendship networks among Indian college students, so he tests 500 students on a standard scale and computes a correlation. That is nomothetic. If instead he spent months studying the life story of his friend Rohan, that would be idiographic. Option b is right because nomothetic research aims at general, law-like statements about behaviour. Exam trap: the two words are often swapped. Link 'nomo' with norms and numbers (many people, general laws) and 'idio' with the individual. Also, Allport did not coin the terms; Windelband did, and Allport applied them to personality.", "traps": {"a": "Individual uniqueness is the focus of the idiographic approach, which studies one person in depth and was favoured by Gordon Allport. It is the opposite of the nomothetic approach.", "c": "Clinical case studies are the main tool of the idiographic approach (the intensive study of one person), not of the large-sample nomothetic search for general laws.", "d": "Projective assessment, such as the Rorschach or the TAT, is often read idiographically, looking at the unique meaning of one person's responses. It is a technique, not what the nomothetic approach emphasises."}}, {"q": "Who introduced the first Intelligence Quotient (IQ) formula based on Mental Age and Chronological Age ?", "concept": "The answer is c: the IQ formula based on mental age and chronological age came into testing through the Stanford-Binet, which is what 'Binet Stanford' refers to. The story has three steps. First, Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) published the first practical intelligence test in Paris, to identify children who needed special help at school. In the 1908 revision they grouped items by age level, so a child's performance could be expressed as a mental age (MA): the age level of the items the child passes. Second, the German psychologist William Stern (1912) divided mental age by chronological age (CA), giving a 'mental quotient'. Third, Lewis Terman (1916) at Stanford University revised and standardised the Binet scale as the Stanford-Binet, multiplied the quotient by 100 to remove decimals, and made the term intelligence quotient, or IQ, famous: IQ = (MA divided by CA) multiplied by 100. Example: Riya is 10 years old (CA 10) and passes items up to the 12-year level (MA 12). Her ratio IQ is 12 divided by 10, multiplied by 100, which is 120. If her MA were 8, her IQ would be 80. The ratio IQ was later replaced by the deviation IQ of David Wechsler (1939), because mental age stops growing in adulthood. Option c is right because the MA/CA formula reached the world through the 1916 Stanford-Binet. Exam trap: students pick 'Binet' because the test carries his name, but Binet worked with mental level only and never computed a quotient. Key note: strictly, William Stern proposed the mental quotient MA/CA in 1912, and Lewis Terman multiplied it by 100 and made 'IQ' famous in the 1916 Stanford-Binet, which is what 'Binet Stanford' points to. Alfred Binet himself never used a quotient, so a and b are wrong, and Stern is not offered; the official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Alfred Binet (with Theodore Simon) built the first intelligence test in 1905 and introduced age levels in 1908, but he never divided mental age by chronological age, so 'Binet' alone did not give the IQ formula.", "b": "The Binet-Simon scale (1905, 1908, 1911) supplied the idea of mental age, not the quotient. The ratio of MA to CA came later, from William Stern (1912) and the Stanford-Binet (1916).", "d": "Stanford is the university where Lewis Terman worked, not a person or a test on its own. The formula belongs to the Stanford-Binet revision, so 'Stanford' alone is incomplete."}}, {"q": "The Army Alpha Test was first developed during :", "concept": "The answer is b: the Army Alpha test was developed during World War I, in 1917, when the United States entered the war. The US Army needed a quick way to sort about 1.75 million recruits by mental ability, to screen out the unfit and pick out likely officers. Individual tests like the Binet scale took about an hour per person with a trained examiner, which was impossible on that scale. A committee of the American Psychological Association, chaired by its president Robert Yerkes (1917), built the first mass group intelligence tests, drawing heavily on the group items of Arthur Otis (1917), a student of Lewis Terman (1916). Two forms were made. The Army Alpha was a verbal, paper-and-pencil test for literate, English-speaking recruits, with subtests such as following directions, arithmetic problems, analogies, number series and general information. The Army Beta was a non-verbal, pictorial test for recruits who could not read or did not know English, given largely through gestures and demonstrations. Example: Arjun, revising testing history, fixes the order as the Binet-Simon scale in 1905 (an individual test), the Stanford-Binet in 1916, then Army Alpha and Beta in 1917 (the first large group tests). After the war the Army tests were adapted for schools and industry, and group testing spread rapidly in the 1920s. Option b is right because Army Alpha and Beta were created in 1917 for the American army in World War I. Exam trap: the best-known army test of World War II was a different one, the Army General Classification Test (1940). Do not confuse Army Alpha either with the Personal Data Sheet of Robert Woodworth (1917), the first personality inventory, which was made in the same war to screen recruits for emotional instability.", "traps": {"a": "World War II (1939 to 1945) brought the Army General Classification Test (1940) and wide use of psychologists in selection, but Army Alpha had been built more than two decades earlier.", "c": "The Korean War (1950 to 1953) is linked with the Armed Forces Qualification Test (1950), a later military screening test, not with Army Alpha.", "d": "The Vietnam War era saw the Armed Services Vocational Aptitude Battery (1968). By then Army Alpha was about 50 years old."}}, {"q": "The General Aptitude Test Battery (GATB) was developed primarily to assess :", "concept": "The answer is b: the General Aptitude Test Battery (GATB) was built to assess occupational aptitudes, so that job seekers could be matched to suitable kinds of work. An aptitude is a capacity to learn a skill with training, as opposed to achievement, which is what has already been learned. A multiple aptitude battery measures several such capacities in one package, giving a profile of strengths and weaknesses instead of a single IQ. The GATB was developed by the United States Employment Service and released in 1947, after more than a decade of research, for use in public employment offices. Its 12 tests (8 paper-and-pencil and 4 apparatus tests) yield nine aptitudes: general learning ability (G), verbal aptitude (V), numerical aptitude (N), spatial aptitude (S), form perception (P), clerical perception (Q), motor coordination (K), finger dexterity (F) and manual dexterity (M). Each occupation was given a pattern of minimum aptitude scores, so a counsellor could see which jobs suited a person's profile. Example: Kabir, aged 19, visits an employment counsellor in Hyderabad. On a GATB-type battery he scores high on spatial aptitude, form perception and manual dexterity but low on clerical perception. The counsellor suggests training as a draughtsman or technician rather than an office clerk. A school-focused relative is the Differential Aptitude Tests of George Bennett, Harold Seashore and Alexander Wesman (1947), with eight subtests for educational and vocational guidance. Option b is right because the GATB was made for vocational counselling and job placement, which rest on occupational aptitudes. Exam trap: the GATB has nine aptitudes and the DAT eight subtests, and students mix the two numbers. Both measure aptitudes, not personality, interests or clinical problems.", "traps": {"a": "Personality traits are measured by inventories such as the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire of Raymond Cattell (1949) or the MMPI. The GATB measures abilities to learn job skills, not traits.", "c": "Emotional intelligence, defined by Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990), is measured by tools such as the MSCEIT. The GATB predates the concept by decades and contains no emotional items.", "d": "Clinical symptoms are assessed with tools such as the MMPI, the Beck Depression Inventory or clinician rating scales. The GATB is a vocational test used in employment offices, not a clinical one."}}, {"q": "The Wechsler Deterioration Quotient is primarily used to estimate :", "concept": "The answer is b: the Wechsler Deterioration Quotient (also called the deterioration index) estimates intellectual decline, that is, how much cognitive ability a person has lost through ageing, brain disease or severe psychiatric illness. David Wechsler (1939), who built the Wechsler-Bellevue scale and later the WAIS, noticed that some subtests resist ageing and brain damage while others drop sharply. The resistant ones he called 'hold' tests: on the WAIS, Vocabulary, Information, Picture Completion and Object Assembly, which rely on long-stored knowledge. The sensitive ones he called 'don't hold' tests: Digit Span, Similarities, Digit Symbol and Block Design, which need speed, working memory and fresh problem solving. The hold tests give an estimate of the person's earlier, premorbid level; the don't hold tests show current functioning. The quotient is (hold minus don't hold) divided by hold, usually multiplied by 100 to give a percentage loss. Wechsler treated a loss beyond about 10 percent as possible deterioration and beyond about 20 percent as definite deterioration, over and above what is expected for age. Example: Meera, a 60-year-old retired teacher in Kochi, still has an excellent vocabulary and general knowledge, but she has become slow on Digit Symbol and Block Design. Her hold scores are far above her don't hold scores, giving a high quotient that suggests decline and calls for a full neuropsychological work-up. Option b is right because the whole point of comparing hold and don't hold subtests is to measure loss from a former level. Exam trap: the quotient is not a measure of current IQ or achievement. Today it is largely replaced by better premorbid estimates, such as the National Adult Reading Test of Hazel Nelson (1982), and by full neuropsychological batteries.", "traps": {"a": "Academic achievement is measured by achievement tests, such as the Wide Range Achievement Test, which assess what has been learned at school. The quotient compares Wechsler subtests to detect loss, not school learning.", "c": "Emotional maturity is a quality of personality and adjustment, measured with separate self-report maturity scales. The deterioration quotient is built purely from cognitive subtests and says nothing about emotional growth.", "d": "Occupational aptitude is measured by batteries such as the General Aptitude Test Battery (1947) or the Differential Aptitude Tests. The deterioration quotient looks back at decline, not forward at job potential."}}, {"q": "According to Murray's theory used in the TAT, hunger and thirst are examples of :", "concept": "The answer is b: in Henry Murray's theory, hunger and thirst are viscerogenic needs, the primary needs that arise from the body's tissues and organs. Henry Murray (1938), in Explorations in Personality, defined a need as a force in the brain that organises perception, thinking and action to change an unsatisfying situation. He divided needs into two classes. Viscerogenic (primary) needs come from bodily processes and lead to physical satisfaction: the needs for food, water, air, sex, urination, and the avoidance of heat, cold and harm. Psychogenic (secondary) needs are psychological and learned, not tied to any organ: achievement, affiliation, dominance, autonomy, aggression, nurturance, order and many more. Murray added press, the forces in the environment that help or block a need. The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), published by Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935), grew out of this theory. The person tells stories about ambiguous pictures, and the examiner identifies the hero, the hero's needs and the press the hero faces, assuming that storytellers project their own needs onto the hero. David McClelland (1953) later used TAT stories to score the need for achievement. Example: Arjun tells a TAT story in which a boy skips lunch to finish a model plane so he can win a science prize. The skipped lunch touches a viscerogenic need (hunger), but the main theme, striving to win, reveals the psychogenic need for achievement. Option b is right because Murray placed the needs for food and water in the viscerogenic class, as they arise from the body. Exam trap: TAT scoring centres on psychogenic needs, so students assume that all of Murray's needs are psychogenic. Remember that viscera means the internal organs, so viscerogenic means 'born of the body'.", "traps": {"a": "Psychogenic needs, in Murray's scheme, are the learned psychological needs such as achievement, affiliation and dominance. Hunger and thirst arise from the body, so they are not psychogenic.", "c": "Social motives (achievement, affiliation, power) are learned motives studied by David McClelland and others. They grow out of experience, whereas hunger and thirst are unlearned bodily needs.", "d": "Ego needs is a common label for the esteem needs, such as status and recognition, in the hierarchy of Abraham Maslow (1943). It is not Murray's category, and hunger and thirst are physiological, not esteem, needs."}}, {"q": "The Stanford Prison Experiment demonstrated that :", "concept": "The answer is b: the Stanford Prison Experiment is the classic demonstration that social roles can strongly shape behaviour, often overriding personality. Philip Zimbardo, Craig Haney and Curtis Banks (1971) built a mock prison in the basement of the psychology building at Stanford University. They screened volunteers, chose 24 healthy, stable male college students and randomly assigned them to be guards or prisoners. Prisoners were 'arrested' at home, given smocks and known only by numbers; guards wore uniforms and mirrored sunglasses and carried batons. Within days many guards became harsh and humiliating, while several prisoners became passive, distressed and helpless, and some had to be released early. The study, planned for two weeks, was stopped after only six days, after Christina Maslach (1971), then a graduate student, objected to the conditions. Because the participants had been screened as normal and were randomly assigned, Zimbardo argued that their behaviour came from the roles and the prison setting, not from 'bad apples'. He later applied this idea to the abuse at Abu Ghraib in The Lucifer Effect (2007). Example: Kabir, a mild and polite student, is made a hostel warden for a week. Within days he is shouting at juniors and enforcing petty rules, behaviour that fades once the role ends. Option b is right because random assignment to the guard or prisoner role, not prior personality, predicted how people behaved. Exam trap: this study is about the power of roles and situations. It is not about obedience to one authority figure (Stanley Milgram, 1963) or conformity to a group (Solomon Asch, 1951). It has also been heavily criticised for its ethics and for possible coaching of guards, and the BBC Prison Study of Stephen Reicher and Alexander Haslam (2006) found that guards did not turn tyrannical on their own.", "traps": {"a": "The study did not measure intelligence or link it to aggression. Guards and prisoners were similar screened students, and their aggression or submission followed the role they were given.", "c": "The finding points the other way: participants were screened as psychologically normal, yet randomly assigned roles changed their behaviour, which stresses the situation over fixed personality.", "d": "Leadership was not studied. The idea that leaders are born with special traits such as intelligence is the great man or trait theory of leadership, which the experiment did not address."}}, {"q": "The Johari Window is mainly used to improve :", "concept": "The answer is b: the Johari Window is a model for improving self-awareness and interpersonal communication, by mapping how much of ourselves is known to us and to others. It was created by Joseph Luft and Harrington Ingham (1955), and its name joins their first names, Joe and Harry. The window has four panes, made by crossing two questions: is this known to me, and is it known to others? The open area (arena) holds what both I and others know, such as my job or my cheerful manner. The blind area holds what others see but I do not, such as a habit of interrupting. The hidden area (facade) holds what I know but keep from others, such as private fears. The unknown area holds what neither I nor others know yet, such as untapped talents or buried feelings. Trust and communication grow as the open area widens. It widens through self-disclosure, which moves hidden material into the open, and through asking for and accepting feedback, which shrinks the blind area; shared discovery can also shrink the unknown area. The model is used in counselling, group work, team building and interpersonal skills training. Example: in a group session, Riya learns from feedback that she often sounds dismissive in meetings, which she had never noticed, so her blind area shrinks. She also shares that she is anxious about public speaking, so her hidden area shrinks. Her open area grows, and her team understands her better. Option b is right because the whole purpose of the model is to increase self-awareness and improve communication through disclosure and feedback. Exam trap: the Johari Window is often used in leadership training, which makes 'Leadership only' tempting, but the word 'only' is the trap: its core aim is self-knowledge and communication in any relationship.", "traps": {"a": "Memory is explained by models such as the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968). The Johari Window says nothing about encoding, storage or recall.", "c": "Intelligence is measured by tests such as the Stanford-Binet or the Wechsler scales. The Johari Window is a model of self-knowledge and communication, not a way to raise or assess intelligence.", "d": "The model is used in leadership and management training, but 'only' makes this wrong: it applies to any relationship, from friendship and therapy to work teams, and its core aim is self-awareness and communication."}}, {"q": "Edward T. Hall introduced the concept of :", "concept": "The answer is b: Edward T. Hall introduced proxemics, the study of how people use physical space and interpersonal distance when they communicate. Edward T. Hall (1963), an American anthropologist, coined the word and developed it in The Hidden Dimension (1966). He argued that distance is a silent channel of nonverbal communication, as meaningful as gaze or tone of voice, and that every culture has its own unwritten rules for it. Among middle-class Americans he described four zones. Intimate distance (touching to about 45 cm) is kept for partners, children and close friends. Personal distance (about 45 cm to 1.2 m) suits friends in conversation. Social distance (about 1.2 to 3.6 m) is used for formal and business dealings. Public distance (beyond about 3.6 m) is the space of lectures and speeches. Hall noted that contact cultures, such as Arab and Latin American ones, stand closer than northern European ones, so the same distance can feel warm to one person and pushy to another. In Beyond Culture (1976) he added the contrast between high-context cultures, where much of the meaning lies in the setting and the relationship (India, Japan), and low-context cultures, where meaning is carried by explicit words. For example, when a stranger sits right beside Meera on an almost empty bus bench, she feels uneasy and shifts away: her personal zone has been invaded. Option b is right because proxemics is the term Hall himself created and the idea his name is tied to in every textbook. Exam trap: other famous Edwards and Halls sit nearby in the syllabus. Edward C. Tolman (1948) gave us cognitive maps, and G. Stanley Hall (1892) founded the American Psychological Association; do not let a shared first name or surname pull you towards option d.", "traps": {"a": "Social facilitation is the improvement of performance on simple tasks in the presence of others, first shown by Norman Triplett (1898) with cyclists and explained by Robert Zajonc (1965) as arousal; it concerns audiences, not personal space.", "c": "Attribution theory, the study of how people explain behaviour by causes in the person or the situation, began with Fritz Heider (1958) and was developed by Harold Kelley (1967) and Bernard Weiner (1979), not by Hall.", "d": "Cognitive mapping comes from Edward C. Tolman (1948), whose rats learned the layout of a maze without reward; the shared first name Edward is the trap."}}, {"q": "Self-serving bias refers to the tendency to :", "concept": "The answer is a: self-serving bias is the tendency to attribute our own successes to internal causes (our ability or effort) and our failures to external causes (bad luck, an unfair task, other people). It is one of the classic attributional biases, the systematic tilts in how people explain behaviour. Attribution theory began with Fritz Heider (1958), who separated dispositional (internal) from situational (external) causes. Dale Miller and Michael Ross (1975) reviewed the early evidence and asked whether the bias was real or a by-product of people's expectations; later work, including a large meta-analysis by Amy Mezulis and colleagues (2004), confirmed that it is strong and found in almost every group studied, though weaker in collectivist cultures such as Japan. The usual explanation is motivational: crediting ourselves for success and blaming circumstances for failure protects self-esteem. A cognitive account adds that we expect to succeed, so success feels caused by us while failure feels like a surprise from outside. For example, when Arjun tops his statistics test he says, 'I worked really hard', but when he fails the next one he says, 'The paper was unfair and the hall was noisy'. Option a is right because it captures both halves of the bias: internal causes for success, external causes for failure. Exam trap: do not confuse it with the fundamental attribution error of Lee Ross (1977), which is about explaining other people's behaviour by their dispositions while underrating the situation, or with the actor-observer effect of Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett (1971). The self-serving bias is about our own outcomes, and the direction of the attribution flips depending on whether the outcome was good or bad.", "traps": {"b": "Blaming oneself for all failures is close to the opposite pattern, the internal, stable and global attributional style that Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) linked to depression.", "c": "Crediting success to luck is an external attribution for success, seen in modest or depressed people and in the impostor phenomenon (Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes, 1978); the self-serving bias does the reverse.", "d": "Ignoring situational influences when judging others is the fundamental attribution error (Lee Ross, 1977), a bias about other people's behaviour, not about how we explain our own successes and failures."}}, {"q": "Night terrors are most commonly observed during :", "concept": "The answer is c: night terrors (sleep terrors) arise from deep non-REM sleep, Stage 3 or N3, also called slow-wave sleep. Sleep runs in cycles of about 90 minutes. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered REM sleep, and the non-REM stages are now labelled N1, N2 and N3 by the American Academy of Sleep Medicine (2007), which merged the old Stages 3 and 4 into N3. N3 is dominated by slow, high-amplitude delta waves, is the hardest stage to wake from, and is concentrated in the first third of the night. Roger Broughton (1968) showed that sleep terrors, sleepwalking and confusional arousals are 'disorders of arousal': the sleeper is partly jolted out of N3, so the body and the emotion systems wake while awareness stays asleep. In a sleep terror the person, usually a child, sits up suddenly, screams, sweats, has a racing heart and wide pupils, is hard to console, and remembers little or nothing next morning. For example, about two hours after falling asleep, five-year-old Kabir screams and stares in terror, does not recognise his mother, then settles back to sleep; at breakfast he recalls nothing. Option c is right because the timing (early in the night), the poor recall and the difficulty waking all point to deep slow-wave sleep. Exam trap: nightmares and night terrors are different. Nightmares are frightening dreams from REM sleep, mostly late in the night, and the dreamer wakes fully and recalls the story vividly. Because the body is paralysed in REM (REM atonia), screaming, sitting up and walking are not normal REM events; acting out dreams in REM is a separate condition, REM sleep behaviour disorder.", "traps": {"a": "REM sleep is where nightmares occur, with vivid recall on waking; the muscles are paralysed in REM, so the thrashing, screaming arousal of a night terror does not come from it.", "b": "Stage 1 (N1) is light, transitional sleep with theta waves and hypnic jerks; sleepers wake easily from it, whereas night terrors come from the deepest stage.", "d": "Dream sleep is simply another name for REM sleep, the stage of nightmares, so it fails for the same reason as option a."}}, {"q": "The amygdala plays a crucial role in :", "concept": "The answer is b: the amygdala, an almond-shaped cluster of nuclei deep in each temporal lobe, is central to emotional processing, especially the detection of threat and the learning and expression of fear. It belongs to the limbic system, named by Paul MacLean (1952). The first clue came from Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy (1937, 1939): after removal of both temporal lobes, including the amygdalae, their monkeys became tame and fearless, approached snakes and put objects in their mouths, the Klüver-Bucy syndrome. Joseph LeDoux (1996) traced fear conditioning in rats and described two routes from the thalamus: a fast, crude 'low road' straight to the amygdala, which triggers freezing and a racing heart within milliseconds, and a slower 'high road' through the cortex that checks what the stimulus really is. In humans, patient S.M., whose amygdalae were destroyed by Urbach-Wiethe disease, could not recognise fear in faces (Ralph Adolphs and colleagues, 1994) and showed almost no fear of snakes, spiders or horror films. The amygdala also makes emotional events more memorable, working with stress hormones such as noradrenaline. For example, when Riya jumps back from what looks like a snake on a dark path before she has consciously seen that it is only a rope, her amygdala's low road has fired first. Option b is right because fear and threat processing is the amygdala's best established role. Exam trap: the hippocampus sits right beside it, and examiners swap the two. The hippocampus stores new facts and events, as the amnesia of Henry Molaison (H.M., operated on in 1953) showed; the amygdala adds emotional colour to them. Also, the limbic system as a whole is the classic 'seat of emotion', while the amygdala is its fear hub.", "traps": {"a": "Balance and motor coordination depend mainly on the cerebellum and the vestibular system of the inner ear, not on the amygdala.", "c": "Speech production depends on Broca's area in the left inferior frontal lobe, described by Paul Broca (1861) from his patient 'Tan'.", "d": "Visual perception is the work of the occipital lobe and its primary visual cortex; the amygdala only receives visual input to judge whether something is threatening."}}, {"q": "The occipital lobe is primarily responsible for :", "concept": "The answer is b: the occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, is primarily responsible for vision. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes: frontal, parietal, temporal and occipital. The occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or area 17 on the map of Korbinian Brodmann, 1909), which lines the calcarine sulcus on the inner surface of the lobe. Signals travel from the retina along the optic nerve, through the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, and along the optic radiations to V1. The map is retinotopic: neighbouring points on the retina project to neighbouring points of cortex, and the left half of the visual field is processed by the right occipital lobe. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959, 1962) recorded single cells in the visual cortex of cats and found simple and complex cells tuned to lines of a particular orientation, work that won them the Nobel Prize in 1981. From V1, information flows forward along two streams described by Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982): a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe for recognising objects and faces, and a dorsal 'where' stream into the parietal lobe for location and movement. Damage to V1 causes cortical blindness in the matching part of the visual field, even though the eyes are healthy. For example, after a stroke in her right occipital lobe, Meera keeps bumping into things on her left, because she has lost the left half of her visual field. Option b is right because vision is the defining function of the occipital lobe. Exam trap: each primary sense has its own cortex, and examiners rotate them. Hearing is temporal, touch is parietal and taste is in the insula; only vision belongs to the occipital lobe.", "traps": {"a": "Hearing is processed in the primary auditory cortex of the temporal lobe (Heschl's gyrus, Brodmann areas 41 and 42), not in the occipital lobe.", "c": "Taste goes to the primary gustatory cortex in the insula and frontal operculum, deep in the lateral fissure, not to the occipital lobe.", "d": "Touch is processed in the primary somatosensory cortex of the parietal lobe, on the postcentral gyrus just behind the central sulcus."}}, {"q": "Which of the following correctly represents the sequence of events during an action potential ?", "concept": "The answer is b: an action potential runs resting, threshold, depolarisation, repolarisation, hyperpolarisation, and back to resting. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked out this sequence by recording from the giant axon of the squid, work that won them the Nobel Prize in 1963. At rest the inside of a neuron is about -70 mV, held there by the sodium-potassium pump and by potassium leaking out. Incoming excitatory signals nudge the membrane upward; if their sum reaches threshold (about -55 mV in most texts, about -65 mV in some), voltage-gated sodium channels fly open. Sodium rushes in and the membrane depolarises sharply, overshooting to about +40 or +50 mV. The sodium channels then inactivate while voltage-gated potassium channels open, so potassium flows out and the membrane repolarises. These potassium channels close slowly, so the membrane briefly dips below its resting level, the hyperpolarisation or undershoot, before the pump and leak channels restore -70 mV. The whole spike lasts about 1 millisecond and obeys the all-or-none law: below threshold nothing fires, above it the spike is always the same size. During the absolute refractory period no stimulus can fire another spike; during the relative refractory period, which overlaps the hyperpolarisation, only a stronger stimulus can. For example, when Kabir touches a hot pan, each sensory neuron in his finger runs this exact cycle many times a second. Option b is right because it is the only order in which each step causes the next: reaching threshold opens sodium channels, sodium entry depolarises, potassium exit repolarises, and slow potassium channels cause the undershoot. Exam trap: hyperpolarisation comes last, after repolarisation, never before depolarisation; and threshold is a point on the rising phase reached from rest, not a starting state that comes before rest.", "traps": {"a": "This puts repolarisation before depolarisation, which is impossible: the membrane cannot return towards rest before it has first moved away from it.", "c": "This starts at threshold and places resting second, but a neuron begins at its resting potential and is pushed up to threshold; hyperpolarisation also cannot come before depolarisation.", "d": "This skips threshold and puts hyperpolarisation first; hyperpolarisation is the after-effect of slow potassium channels at the end of the spike, and a hyperpolarising input makes firing less likely, not more."}}, {"q": "The myelin sheath surrounding many axons is primarily responsible for :", "concept": "The answer is c: the myelin sheath is a fatty insulating wrap around many axons, and its main job is to increase the speed of nerve impulse conduction. Myelin is made by glial cells: oligodendrocytes in the brain and spinal cord, and Schwann cells (named after Theodor Schwann, 1839) in the peripheral nerves. It is laid down in segments with small gaps between them, the nodes of Ranvier, described by Louis-Antoine Ranvier (1878). Because myelin blocks ion flow across the membrane, the action potential can be regenerated only at the nodes, where voltage-gated sodium channels are packed. Current spreads quickly and passively under the myelin, so the spike seems to leap from node to node: saltatory conduction, from the Latin saltare, to jump. Ichiji Tasaki (1939) demonstrated this jumping conduction in single frog nerve fibres. An unmyelinated axon must regenerate the spike at every point along its length, so it is slow, about 1 m per second in thin fibres, while large myelinated axons conduct at up to about 100 m per second. Myelin also saves energy, because fewer ions need pumping back. Its fat makes it white, which is why tracts of myelinated axons form the brain's white matter. When myelin is destroyed, as in multiple sclerosis, conduction slows or fails, causing weakness, numbness and blurred vision. For example, Arjun pulls his hand off a hot stove almost before he feels the heat, because the myelinated fibres of his reflex arc carry the signal so fast. Option c is right because faster, more efficient conduction is the defining function of myelin. Exam trap: myelin speeds conduction but does not create the action potential; the spike starts at the axon hillock (initial segment), not at the dendrites, and transmitters are made by the neuron itself, not by its sheath.", "traps": {"a": "Neurotransmitters are synthesised in the neuron's cell body or axon terminals and stored in synaptic vesicles; the myelin sheath, made by glial cells, does not produce them.", "b": "Hormone secretion is regulated by the endocrine system, chiefly the hypothalamus and the pituitary gland; myelin plays no part in it.", "d": "Action potentials begin at the axon hillock (initial segment), where inputs from the dendrites are summed; dendrites mostly carry graded potentials, and myelin covers the axon, not the dendrites."}}, {"q": "Which of the following neurotransmitters primarily functions as the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the central nervous system ?", "concept": "The answer is c: GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter of the central nervous system. It is an amino acid transmitter, made from glutamate in a single step by the enzyme glutamic acid decarboxylase, and it was identified in the brain by Eugene Roberts and Sam Frankel (1950). Up to about a third of brain synapses may use it. GABA acts on two main receptor types. The GABA-A receptor is ionotropic: it is a chloride channel, and when GABA binds, chloride ions flow in and hyperpolarise the neuron, producing a fast inhibitory postsynaptic potential that makes firing less likely. The GABA-B receptor is metabotropic, working through G proteins to open potassium channels. Inhibition is not a minor job: it keeps excitation in check, shapes the timing of neural activity and stops runaway firing. Too little GABA activity is linked to seizures and anxiety. Many calming drugs act by enhancing the GABA-A receptor: benzodiazepines such as diazepam, barbiturates and alcohol. This is why they reduce anxiety, cause sleepiness and can stop seizures, and why mixing them is dangerous. For example, when Meera is given lorazepam before surgery to calm her panic, the drug boosts GABA's inhibitory effect and her racing worry quietens. Option c is right because GABA is the brain's principal inhibitory transmitter. Exam trap: glycine is the main inhibitory transmitter of the spinal cord and brainstem, so if a question says 'spinal cord', choose glycine; if it says 'brain' or 'central nervous system' in general, choose GABA. Also remember that glutamate and GABA are partners: glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter and is the raw material from which GABA is made.", "traps": {"a": "Dopamine is a catecholamine involved in reward, motivation and movement; its effect depends on the receptor (D1 mostly excitatory, D2 mostly inhibitory), so it is not the brain's main inhibitory transmitter.", "b": "Glutamate is the opposite: the brain's main excitatory transmitter, acting on AMPA and NMDA receptors, and the precursor from which GABA is made.", "d": "Acetylcholine excites skeletal muscle at the neuromuscular junction and supports attention, memory and REM sleep in the brain; it is not the main central inhibitory transmitter."}}, {"q": "Glutamate primarily functions as a(n) :", "concept": "The answer is b: glutamate is primarily an excitatory neurotransmitter, in fact the most common excitatory transmitter in the brain and spinal cord. It is an amino acid. Takashi Hayashi (1954) found that glutamate applied to the brain caused convulsions, and David Curtis and Jeffrey Watkins (1960) showed that it excites spinal neurons directly. When glutamate binds its ionotropic receptors, positive ions (sodium, and for some receptors calcium) flow in, depolarising the next neuron and making it more likely to fire. The main ionotropic receptors are AMPA, kainate and NMDA; there are also metabotropic glutamate receptors that act more slowly through G proteins. The NMDA receptor is special: it opens only when glutamate binds and the cell is already depolarised, so it detects coincident activity, and the calcium it lets in strengthens the synapse. This is the basis of long-term potentiation, discovered by Tim Bliss and Terje Lømo (1973) in the rabbit hippocampus and widely seen as a cellular model of learning and memory. Too much glutamate is harmful: in a stroke, glutamate floods out and over-excites neurons until they die, a process called excitotoxicity. For example, as Riya practises a new raga every day, glutamate synapses in her hippocampus and cortex are repeatedly activated and strengthened. Option b is right because glutamate's defining action is to depolarise and excite the neurons it reaches. Exam trap: glutamate and GABA are opposites but chemical relatives. GABA, the main inhibitory transmitter, is made from glutamate in one enzyme step, so a question about the precursor of GABA also wants glutamate; do not let the family link tempt you into calling glutamate inhibitory.", "traps": {"a": "Inhibitory transmitter describes GABA in the brain and glycine in the spinal cord, which hyperpolarise neurons; glutamate does the reverse.", "c": "Hormones are released by endocrine glands into the blood to act on distant targets; glutamate is released at synapses and acts locally on receptors across a tiny cleft.", "d": "Glutamate does have slower, modulating effects through its metabotropic receptors, but the word 'only' makes this wrong, since its main role is fast, direct excitation."}}, {"q": "Dopamine is strongly associated with :", "concept": "The answer is b: dopamine is strongly associated with reward and motivation, as well as with movement. Dopamine is a catecholamine made from the amino acid tyrosine by way of L-dopa. Its reward role runs mainly through the mesolimbic pathway, from the ventral tegmental area (VTA) of the midbrain to the nucleus accumbens, with links to the prefrontal cortex through the mesocortical pathway. The story began with James Olds and Peter Milner (1954), whose rats pressed a lever thousands of times an hour to receive electrical stimulation along the medial forebrain bundle, a route that carries dopamine fibres: the so-called pleasure centres. Later, Wolfram Schultz (1997) recorded VTA dopamine neurons in monkeys and found that they fire for unexpected rewards, stay quiet for fully expected ones, and dip when a promised reward fails to arrive: a reward prediction error signal that drives learning. Kent Berridge and Terry Robinson (1998) argued that dopamine creates 'wanting' (incentive salience, the urge to pursue a reward) more than 'liking' (the pleasure itself). Almost every addictive drug raises dopamine in the nucleus accumbens; cocaine blocks its reuptake and amphetamine increases its release. A separate pathway, the nigrostriatal one, controls movement, and its loss causes Parkinson's disease. For example, each notification on Kabir's phone gives a small dopamine burst that keeps him checking the app, even when he does not particularly enjoy what he finds. Option b is right because reward, motivation and reinforcement are dopamine's best known functions. Exam trap: dopamine is about wanting and about learning what predicts reward, not simply about pleasure; and too much mesolimbic dopamine is linked to the positive symptoms of schizophrenia, while too little nigrostriatal dopamine causes the tremor and rigidity of Parkinson's disease.", "traps": {"a": "Hearing depends on the cochlea, the auditory nerve and the auditory cortex of the temporal lobe; dopamine has no defining role in it.", "c": "Digestion is regulated by the enteric nervous system, parasympathetic (acetylcholine) activity and gut hormones, not mainly by brain dopamine.", "d": "Blood clotting depends on platelets and clotting factors in the blood; it is not a function of any brain neurotransmitter system."}}, {"q": "An end plate potential occurs at the :", "concept": "The answer is b: an end plate potential (EPP) occurs at the neuromuscular junction, the synapse between a motor neuron and a skeletal muscle fibre. The muscle side of this synapse is a folded patch of membrane called the motor end plate, which gives the potential its name. When an action potential reaches the motor neuron's terminal, calcium enters and triggers the release of acetylcholine (ACh). ACh crosses the narrow cleft and binds nicotinic receptors on the end plate, which are ion channels. Sodium flows in and the end plate depolarises: this local, graded depolarisation is the end plate potential. Unlike a typical excitatory postsynaptic potential between neurons, which is tiny and must be summed with many others, the EPP is very large, so a single nerve impulse almost always pushes the muscle fibre past threshold. This built-in margin is called the safety factor. The muscle then fires its own action potential and contracts, while the enzyme acetylcholinesterase quickly breaks ACh down and ends the signal. Paul Fatt and Bernard Katz (1951, 1952) recorded EPPs in frog muscle and found tiny spontaneous 'miniature end plate potentials', each caused by one packet of ACh. This led to the quantal theory of transmitter release, and Katz shared the Nobel Prize in 1970. Curare blocks nicotinic receptors and paralyses muscle; in myasthenia gravis, antibodies attack the receptors, so EPPs shrink and muscles tire quickly. For example, when Arjun decides to lift his pen, each motor neuron impulse produces an EPP that makes his finger muscles contract. Option b is right because the EPP is, by definition, the postsynaptic potential of the neuromuscular junction. Exam trap: the term is specific; the equivalent potentials between two neurons are called EPSPs and IPSPs, not end plate potentials.", "traps": {"a": "A synapse between two neurons produces excitatory or inhibitory postsynaptic potentials (EPSPs and IPSPs), which are small and must be summed; 'end plate' refers specifically to the muscle membrane.", "c": "The retina's photoreceptors produce graded receptor potentials (rods and cones actually hyperpolarise to light), not end plate potentials.", "d": "The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance through ordinary neuron-to-neuron synapses; the end plate potential arises further down the line, where motor neurons meet muscle."}}, {"q": "Lev Vygotsky's concept of the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) refers to :", "concept": "The answer is c: Lev Vygotsky's zone of proximal development (ZPD) is the gap between what a learner can do independently and what the learner can achieve with guidance from an adult or a more capable peer. Lev Vygotsky (1934), the Russian psychologist, set out the idea in his last lectures and in Thought and Language, and it reached most English readers through Mind in Society (1978). His sociocultural theory holds that higher mental functions first appear between people, in shared activity, and only later inside the child. He illustrated the ZPD with two children who both solve problems set for eight-year-olds when working alone. With hints, one solves problems set for twelve-year-olds, the other only those for nine-year-olds. Their actual developmental level is the same, but their zones differ, so a single test score hides real differences in readiness to learn. Teaching works best inside the zone: on tasks the child cannot yet do alone but can do with help. David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) later called this kind of adjustable support scaffolding: the helper gives hints, models steps or breaks the task down, then withdraws support as competence grows. For example, Meera cannot solve long division alone, but when her older brother asks guiding questions she gets each step right; long division sits inside her ZPD, while algebra is still beyond it. Option c is right because it states the definition exactly: the distance between independent and assisted performance. Exam trap: the ZPD is often confused with the actual developmental level, which is what the child does alone (option b); and the word scaffolding belongs to Wood, Bruner and Ross, not to Vygotsky himself, although it describes how to teach within his zone.", "traps": {"a": "Comparing mental age with chronological age comes from Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon (1908) and William Stern (1912), whose ratio became the IQ; it describes test level, not learning potential with help.", "b": "Performance without help is what Vygotsky called the actual developmental level; it is the lower edge of the zone, not the zone itself.", "d": "A period of especially rapid or easy language growth refers to the critical (sensitive) period hypothesis of Eric Lenneberg (1967), not to the ZPD."}}, {"q": "According to Lawrence Kohlberg's theory, an individual who obeys laws because they maintain social order is functioning at which stage of moral development ?", "concept": "The answer is d: obeying laws because they maintain social order is Stage 4, the law-and-order orientation, in Lawrence Kohlberg's theory. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) built his theory by presenting boys with moral dilemmas, the best known being the Heinz dilemma: should a man steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife? He scored not the yes or no answer but the reasoning behind it, and found three levels, each with two stages. At the preconventional level, right is judged by the consequences to oneself: Stage 1, the punishment-and-obedience orientation (avoid punishment), and Stage 2, the instrumental purpose orientation (what is in it for me, fair deals). At the conventional level, reached by most adolescents and where most adults stay, right means conforming to social rules: Stage 3, good interpersonal relationships, or the 'good boy, nice girl' orientation (win the approval of people close to you), and Stage 4, law and order (do your duty, respect authority, keep society running). At the postconventional level, reached by a minority, laws are judged against principles: Stage 5, social contract, and Stage 6, universal ethical principles. For example, Kabir says Heinz should not steal because 'if everyone broke the law whenever they felt it was justified, society would fall apart'; his concern is the whole social system, so he is reasoning at Stage 4. Option d is right because the stem's reason, keeping social order, is the hallmark of Stage 4. Exam trap: Stage 1 also involves obeying, but out of fear of punishment, not respect for the social system; and Stage 3 involves conformity too, but to please family and friends, not to preserve society as a whole. Ask why the person obeys, not whether they obey.", "traps": {"a": "The punishment-and-obedience orientation is Kohlberg's Stage 1 (preconventional): rules are obeyed to avoid punishment, not to maintain social order.", "b": "The instrumental purpose (exchange) orientation is Kohlberg's Stage 2 (preconventional): right is whatever serves one's own interests or makes a fair deal.", "c": "Good interpersonal relationships is Kohlberg's Stage 3 (conventional): the person conforms to win the approval of close others, a narrower aim than keeping society as a whole in order."}}, {"q": "According to Erikson, the major psychosocial crisis during adolescence is :", "concept": "The answer is b: in Erik Erikson's theory, the major psychosocial crisis of adolescence is identity versus role confusion. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, extended the psychosexual stages of Sigmund Freud (1905) into eight psychosocial stages that span the whole lifespan. Each stage poses a crisis, a turning point between two outcomes, shaped by the person's widening social world, and a good balance yields a basic strength or virtue. The eight are: trust versus mistrust (infancy, hope), autonomy versus shame and doubt (about 1 to 3, will), initiative versus guilt (about 3 to 6, purpose), industry versus inferiority (6 to puberty, competence), identity versus role confusion (about 12 to 18, fidelity), intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood, love), generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood, care) and ego integrity versus despair (late adulthood, wisdom). In adolescence, puberty, new thinking skills and looming choices push the young person to fuse childhood identifications, values, skills and future roles into one answer to 'Who am I?'. Erikson described a psychosocial moratorium, a socially permitted delay in which adolescents try out roles before committing. James Marcia (1966) later turned this into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. For example, Riya, 16, switches between wanting to be a doctor, a musician and a lawyer, and argues with her parents about her clothes and friends; Erikson would see her exploring an identity, which is healthy if it leads to commitment. Option b is right because identity versus role confusion is the fifth stage, the one Erikson tied to adolescence. Exam trap: industry versus inferiority (school age, roughly 6 to 12) sits just before adolescence and is the usual near-miss, while intimacy versus isolation, the stage just after, belongs to young adults, not teenagers.", "traps": {"a": "Trust versus mistrust is Erikson's first stage, infancy (birth to about 1 year), when the baby learns whether caregivers can be relied on.", "c": "Industry versus inferiority is Erikson's fourth stage, the school years from about 6 to puberty, when children build competence through tasks and comparison with peers.", "d": "Integrity versus despair is Erikson's eighth and final stage, late adulthood from about 65, when people look back over their lives."}}, {"q": "A zygote is formed immediately after :", "concept": "The answer is b: a zygote is formed immediately after fertilisation, the union of a sperm and an ovum. Each sex cell (gamete) is made by meiosis and carries 23 chromosomes; when a sperm penetrates the ovum, usually in the fallopian tube, the two nuclei fuse into a single cell with the full 46 chromosomes, in 23 pairs. That single fertilised cell is the zygote, the starting point of prenatal development. Developmental psychologists divide prenatal life into three periods. The germinal period covers about the first two weeks: the zygote begins dividing by mitosis (cleavage) as it travels down the tube, becomes a solid ball of cells, the morula (about day 4), then a hollow blastocyst (about day 5), and finally implants in the wall of the uterus at about 6 to 10 days. The embryonic period, weeks 3 to 8, opens with gastrulation, when the inner cells form three germ layers: ectoderm (skin and nervous system), mesoderm (muscle, bone and blood) and endoderm (gut and lungs). This is when the organs form and when teratogens do most damage. The fetal period runs from week 9 to birth. Since every ovum carries an X chromosome, the father's sperm (X or Y) decides the baby's sex, and if a zygote splits in two the result is identical (monozygotic) twins. For example, if an ovum is fertilised by a Y-bearing sperm, the zygote is XY from that moment and will develop as a boy, as happened when Aditi's baby brother was conceived. Option b is right because fertilisation is the event that creates the zygote; every other option happens to it afterwards. Exam trap: the order matters. Fertilisation comes first, then cleavage, then implantation, then gastrulation; each distractor is a real stage, just a later one.", "traps": {"a": "Implantation is the burrowing of the blastocyst into the uterine wall at about 6 to 10 days, which ends the germinal period; by then the zygote has long since formed and divided.", "c": "Gastrulation happens in the third week, when the embryo forms the three germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm); it opens the embryonic period.", "d": "Cleavage is the series of mitotic divisions the zygote undergoes after it forms, turning it into a morula and then a blastocyst, so it comes after the zygote, not before."}}, {"q": "An employee was called in by their employer for submitting the project assignment later than the submission day. The employee starts throwing a temper tantrum in front of their boss, and bangs the door on their way out. What is the defense mechanism being shown here ?", "concept": "The answer is b: throwing a temper tantrum and banging the door is regression, a retreat to the behaviour of an earlier, more childlike stage of development when faced with stress or frustration. Defence mechanisms are unconscious strategies the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety. Sigmund Freud (1894) introduced the idea of defence, and Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, catalogued the defences, regression among them. In psychoanalytic theory, a person under threat slips back towards a stage where they once felt safe or where part of their libido stayed fixated. Tantrums, sulking, crying to get one's way, thumb sucking or baby talk in an adult are classic signs. A famous study by Roger Barker, Tamara Dembo and Kurt Lewin (1941) let children play with attractive toys, then put the toys behind a wire screen where they could be seen but not reached. Frustrated, the children played in a less mature way, much like children about a year and a half younger. In the stem, an adult employee is criticised for missing a deadline. Instead of an adult response, such as explaining or apologising, the employee shouts, stamps and slams the door like a small child: that is regression. Similarly, when Kabir is scolded by his manager and responds by sulking and refusing to speak to anyone all day, he too is regressing. Option b is right because the employee's behaviour is a return to a childlike way of handling frustration. Exam trap: do not confuse regression with displacement (taking anger out on a safer target, such as shouting at a younger sibling after a bad day) or acting out (expressing an impulse in action to avoid feeling it). Neither is offered here, so the childlike tantrum points to regression.", "traps": {"a": "Reaction formation is turning an unacceptable impulse into its exaggerated opposite, such as being extra sweet to a disliked boss; the employee here shows anger openly, not its opposite.", "c": "Suppression is the conscious, deliberate choice to set a worry aside for now, which George Vaillant (1977) counts as a mature defence; the employee does the reverse and loses control.", "d": "Denial is refusing to accept a painful reality, such as insisting the project was never late; the employee is not denying the lateness but reacting to the criticism like a child."}}, {"q": "A psychologist concludes that a client's depressive symptoms are maintained by dysfunctional core beliefs. This formulation is most consistent with :", "concept": "The answer is b: a formulation in which depression is maintained by dysfunctional core beliefs is the hallmark of cognitive therapy, developed by Aaron T. Beck (1967), whose treatment manual Cognitive Therapy of Depression appeared in 1979. Beck, a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that his depressed patients' thinking was full of themes of loss and failure that showed up as conscious, automatic thoughts. He proposed the cognitive model: it is not events themselves that cause emotion, but the way they are interpreted. Cognition works at three levels. Core beliefs, or schemas, are deep, rigid, global beliefs about the self, others and the world, usually formed in childhood ('I am unlovable', 'I am a failure'). From them grow intermediate beliefs: rules and assumptions ('If I am not perfect, I will be rejected'). When a situation activates these, negative automatic thoughts pop up ('She has not replied; she must hate me') and drive low mood. In depression they produce the cognitive triad: negative views of the self, the world and the future. Therapy is structured and collaborative: the client learns to catch automatic thoughts, test them against evidence (Socratic questioning, thought records, behavioural experiments) and later to modify the core beliefs beneath them, for example with the downward arrow technique. Judith S. Beck (1995) mapped this in her cognitive conceptualisation diagram. For example, Arjun, who believes deep down 'I am incompetent', reads every small mistake at work as proof and feels hopeless; his therapist helps him gather evidence against the belief. Option b is right because core beliefs and the distorted thinking they feed are the central concepts of Beck's cognitive therapy. Exam trap: Albert Ellis (1962), in rational emotive behaviour therapy, also targets beliefs, but he calls them irrational beliefs and uses the ABC model; the words 'core beliefs' and 'schemas' point to Beck.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalytic therapy, from Sigmund Freud (1917), explains depression through unconscious conflict and early loss, with anger turned inward, not through conscious, testable beliefs.", "c": "Behaviour therapy, from Joseph Wolpe (1958) and the operant work of B. F. Skinner (1953), explains symptoms through learning and reinforcement, such as a loss of rewarding activity, rather than through beliefs.", "d": "Humanistic therapy, such as the client-centred therapy of Carl Rogers (1951), sees distress as incongruence between self-concept and experience, treated through empathy and unconditional positive regard."}}, {"q": "According to the diathesis-stress model, psychological disorders develop because :", "concept": "The answer is c: the diathesis-stress model holds that psychological disorders develop when a pre-existing vulnerability (the diathesis) interacts with environmental stressors; usually neither is enough alone. The diathesis may be genetic, biological (such as a birth complication or neurotransmitter dysregulation), psychological (such as a negative thinking style) or social (early adversity). The stress may be a major life event, such as bereavement or migration, or the build-up of daily hassles. Paul Meehl (1962) first proposed a version for schizophrenia: an inherited neural defect he called schizotaxia would lead to clinical illness only in combination with a harmful environment. Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) broadened it into the vulnerability-stress model: everyone has some threshold, and illness appears when stress exceeds a person's level of vulnerability. A highly vulnerable person may break down under mild stress, while a person with low vulnerability may need extreme stress. Twin studies support the model: even the identical twin of a person with schizophrenia has only about a 50 percent chance of developing it, so genes alone cannot be the whole explanation. For example, Kabir and his cousin both lose their jobs in the same layoff. Kabir, whose mother and grandfather had depression and who tends to blame himself, slides into a major depressive episode; his cousin, with no family history and good support, feels low for a few weeks and recovers. Option c is right because the word 'interacts' captures the model's core: vulnerability and stress acting together. Exam trap: options that say 'alone is sufficient' or 'completely explains' describe single-cause models, and the diathesis-stress model was defined against them. A later refinement, differential susceptibility (Jay Belsky, 1997), adds that the same sensitivity can also help people thrive in good environments.", "traps": {"a": "Genetic vulnerability alone is a purely biological, single-cause view; twin concordance for schizophrenia of only about 50 percent shows that genes are not sufficient.", "b": "Environmental stress alone is a purely environmental view; most people who face severe stress do not develop a disorder, which is why a diathesis is needed.", "d": "A purely learned explanation is the strict behavioural model of John B. Watson (1920) and B. F. Skinner (1953); it leaves out biological vulnerability altogether."}}, {"q": "A therapist repeatedly reinforces adaptive behaviours while ignoring maladaptive behaviours. This intervention is based on :", "concept": "The answer is b: reinforcing adaptive behaviours while ignoring maladaptive ones is an operant conditioning intervention, based on learning through consequences. Edward Thorndike (1898) first stated the law of effect: responses followed by satisfying consequences become more likely, and those followed by discomfort less likely. B. F. Skinner (1938) developed this into operant conditioning, in which behaviour operates on the environment and is shaped by what follows it. Reinforcement, positive (adding something pleasant, such as praise) or negative (removing something unpleasant), increases a behaviour. Extinction means withholding the reinforcer that has been maintaining a behaviour, so the behaviour gradually fades; planned ignoring of attention-seeking misbehaviour is extinction when attention was the reinforcer. Therapists often combine the two in differential reinforcement: the problem behaviour is put on extinction while a better alternative is reinforced (differential reinforcement of alternative or incompatible behaviour, DRA or DRI). These techniques are used in parent training, classrooms, token economies (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) and applied behaviour analysis for autism. Therapists warn parents about the extinction burst: when reinforcement stops, the behaviour first gets worse before it fades, and giving in at that point teaches the child that bigger tantrums pay. For example, a therapist advises Meera's parents to praise her each time she asks calmly for a toy and to stop responding when she whines; within weeks calm requests rise and whining drops. Option b is right because the procedure works purely through consequences: reinforcement to strengthen one behaviour and extinction to weaken another. Exam trap: social learning theory also uses reinforcement, but its key mechanism is learning by watching a model (Albert Bandura, 1977), and classical conditioning pairs stimuli before a response rather than changing the consequences that follow it.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning, from Ivan Pavlov (1927), links a neutral stimulus with one that already triggers a reflex; it works by pairing stimuli before a response, not through consequences such as praise and ignoring.", "c": "Social learning theory, from Albert Bandura (1977), stresses learning by observing models and vicarious reinforcement; the stem has no model, only direct consequences for the client's own behaviour.", "d": "Cognitive restructuring is a technique of the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (1979) that identifies and challenges distorted thoughts; it targets beliefs, not the reinforcement of behaviour."}}, {"q": "Which feature best distinguishes dementia from delirium ?", "concept": "The answer is b: gradual onset and progressive cognitive decline best distinguish dementia from delirium. Delirium is an acute confusional state: a disturbance of attention and awareness (clouded consciousness) that develops over hours to days, fluctuates through the day, often worsens in the evening, and brings disorientation, memory problems and perceptual disturbances such as visual hallucinations. It always has a physical cause, such as infection, drugs, alcohol withdrawal, a metabolic upset or surgery, and it is often reversible once the cause is treated. Zbigniew Lipowski (1980) did much to define it as acute brain failure, a medical emergency rather than a psychiatric curiosity. Dementia, which DSM-5 (2013) calls major neurocognitive disorder, is a decline from a previous level in memory, language, executive function and other domains, serious enough to cost independence. Its commonest form, Alzheimer's disease, first described by Alois Alzheimer (1906), begins insidiously over months or years and worsens steadily, while consciousness and attention stay fairly clear until the later stages. For example, Arjun's grandfather has been slowly forgetting names and getting lost in his own colony over two years: that pattern suggests dementia. If instead he became confused and agitated overnight after a urinary infection, seeing insects on the wall and alert one hour but drowsy the next, that would suggest delirium. Option b is right because onset and course, slow and progressive versus sudden and fluctuating, are the clearest dividing line. Exam trap: memory impairment, impaired attention and hallucinations can all occur in both conditions, so none of them separates the two; dementia also raises the risk of delirium, so they often coexist. Dementia with Lewy bodies has fluctuations and vivid visual hallucinations too, which is why onset and course are the safer guide.", "traps": {"a": "Memory impairment is present in both: delirium disrupts registration and recall because attention fails, and dementia erodes memory gradually, so it does not separate them.", "c": "Impaired attention is the core feature of delirium, not of early dementia, and attention also fails in late dementia, so it is not the feature that marks out dementia.", "d": "Hallucinations, usually visual, are common in delirium and also occur in dementia with Lewy bodies and later Alzheimer's disease, so their presence cannot tell the two apart."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is classified as a Specific Phobia rather than an Anxiety Disorder involving social situations ?", "concept": "The answer is c: acrophobia, an intense fear of heights, is a specific phobia, not an anxiety disorder centred on social or public situations. A phobia is a marked, persistent fear of a particular object or situation that is out of proportion to the real danger, is avoided or endured with dread, and in DSM-5 (2013) usually lasts 6 months or more. Phobia names join a Greek word to phobos (fear): akron means peak or height, so acrophobia is fear of heights. DSM-5 groups specific phobias into five types: animal, natural environment (heights, storms, water), blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, flights, enclosed places) and other. Acrophobia belongs to the natural environment type. Martin Seligman (1970) explained why fears of heights, snakes and darkness are so common: through preparedness, evolution has readied us to learn fears of things that once threatened survival. Example: Kabir happily speaks on stage and travels alone, but on a glass-floored viewing deck his legs shake and he crawls back to the lift. His fear is tied to one cue, height, and has nothing to do with being watched or judged, so it is a specific phobia. Option c is right because acrophobia is defined by a single, circumscribed trigger, while the other three are separate DSM-5 anxiety diagnoses: social anxiety disorder (fear of scrutiny), agoraphobia (fear of places where escape is hard) and generalised anxiety disorder (diffuse worry). Exam trap: acrophobia and agoraphobia look alike on the page. Agoraphobia, literally fear of the marketplace, is not a specific phobia in DSM-5; it is its own diagnosis needing fear in two or more of five situations, such as crowds and public transport. Acrophobia is simply the fear of heights.", "traps": {"a": "Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) is fear of being scrutinised or embarrassed in social or performance situations, such as speaking in class. It is exactly the social-situation disorder the stem contrasts with a specific phobia.", "b": "Agoraphobia is fear of public transport, open or enclosed spaces, queues or crowds and being out alone, because escape may be hard; since DSM-5 (2013) it is a separate anxiety disorder, not a specific phobia. Its likeness to the word acrophobia is the trap.", "d": "Generalised anxiety disorder is excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters for at least 6 months. It has no single feared object, so it is not a phobia of any kind."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best distinguishes stress from anxiety ?", "concept": "The answer is b: stress is usually a reaction to an identifiable external demand, while anxiety can arise even when no immediate external threat is present. Stress has been defined as a stimulus, as a response and as a transaction. Hans Selye (1936) described the general adaptation syndrome, a body-wide response to any demand that moves from alarm to resistance to exhaustion. Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984) added the transactional model: stress arises when a person appraises a demand (the stressor) as exceeding their resources. Either way, stress is tied to a stressor that can usually be named: an exam, a move, an illness. Anxiety is a state of apprehension, tension and worry about possible future danger. DSM-5 (2013) contrasts fear, the response to a threat that is present now, with anxiety, the anticipation of a threat that may come. Because anxiety is future-oriented and generated from within, it can persist after the stressor has gone, or appear with no clear cause at all, as in generalised anxiety disorder. Example: Arjun feels tense and sleepless in the week before his board exams, and the feeling lifts once the exams end; that is stress. His cousin Meera wakes with a racing heart and a sense of dread on ordinary days when nothing is wrong; that is anxiety. Option b is right because the presence of an identifiable external stressor is the textbook dividing line between the two states. Exam trap: neither state is automatically pathological or automatically normal. Selye (1974) later coined eustress for helpful stress, and anxiety becomes a disorder only when it is excessive, persistent and impairing, so absolute words such as \"always\" signal a wrong option.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses and overstates both terms. Hans Selye (1974) separated healthy eustress from harmful distress, and anxiety becomes pathological only when it is disproportionate and impairing, so neither is 'always' one thing.", "c": "The two overlap in bodily arousal but are not synonyms: stress is anchored to a stressor, whereas anxiety is apprehension about a possible future threat, which DSM-5 (2013) also separates from present-tense fear.", "d": "There is no fixed order. Stress often triggers anxiety, and anxiety can exist without any stressor at all, so 'always precedes' has no support."}}, {"q": "From a psychodynamic perspective, individuals with PTSD often protect themselves from overwhelming traumatic memories through :", "concept": "The answer is b: psychodynamic theory holds that people with PTSD shield themselves from overwhelming traumatic memories mainly through dissociation and repression. A defence mechanism is an unconscious manoeuvre of the ego that keeps unbearable thoughts and feelings out of awareness. Pierre Janet (1889) first proposed that memories too terrible to integrate are split off from ordinary consciousness, a process he called dissociation; they live on as fragments that return as flashbacks and nightmares. Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer (1895), in Studies on Hysteria, described repression, the pushing of painful memories into the unconscious. Mardi Horowitz (1976) built on both in his stress response model: the survivor swings between intrusion (the memory breaking through) and denial or numbing (the defences shutting it out), and recovery comes as the memory is gradually worked through. Modern diagnosis reflects this. DSM-5 (2013) lists inability to recall an important part of the trauma, typically due to dissociative amnesia, as a PTSD symptom, and offers a dissociative subtype with depersonalisation and derealisation. Example: after a bus accident, Riya cannot remember the minutes between the crash and the ambulance, and when reminded of it she feels as if she is watching herself from outside her body. The blank is repression and dissociative amnesia; the unreal feeling is dissociation. Option b is right because both defences keep the trauma out of conscious memory, which is exactly what the stem describes. Exam trap: examiners offer mature defences such as sublimation and humour, which George Vaillant (1977) linked to good adjustment, or add the word \"only\" to make a partly plausible defence too narrow. Trauma calls up the primitive, memory-blocking defences.", "traps": {"a": "Intellectualisation, described by Anna Freud (1936), is talking about a painful event in detached, abstract terms to avoid feeling it. Some survivors do this, but 'only' makes the option far too narrow, and it does not block the memory itself.", "c": "Sublimation (channelling impulses into valued activity) and humour are mature defences in George Vaillant's (1977) hierarchy, linked to healthy coping. They are signs of adaptation, not the way PTSD keeps overwhelming memories out of awareness.", "d": "Reaction formation (turning an impulse into its opposite) is typical of obsessional styles, and compensation is Alfred Adler's (1907) idea of making up for an inferiority. Neither keeps traumatic memories out of consciousness."}}, {"q": "World Mental Health Day is observed every year on :", "concept": "The answer is c: World Mental Health Day is observed every year on 10 October. The day was started in 1992 by the World Federation for Mental Health, an international non-governmental body founded in 1948, to raise awareness of mental health and to fight stigma. The World Health Organization supports it, and since 1994 each year has carried a theme; the 2024 theme, for example, was mental health at work. Schools, hospitals and universities use the day for awareness talks, free screening camps and campaigns that make help-seeking normal. India has used the date for policy too: Tele-MANAS, the national tele-mental health helpline (14416), was launched on 10 October 2022. Several other days fall close to it, and examiners like to mix them up. 10 September is World Suicide Prevention Day, organised since 2003 by the International Association for Suicide Prevention with the World Health Organization. 5 October is World Teachers' Day, set by UNESCO in 1994. 10 November is UNESCO's World Science Day for Peace and Development. A quick memory aid is \"10/10\": the tenth day of the tenth month. Option c is right because 10 October is the date that has been World Mental Health Day since its first observance in 1992. Exam trap: World Suicide Prevention Day (10 September) shares the number 10 and the mental health theme, so it is the near miss most students pick. Keep the two apart by month: suicide prevention in September, mental health in October.", "traps": {"a": "10 September is World Suicide Prevention Day, run since 2003 by the International Association for Suicide Prevention with the World Health Organization. It is a related mental health date, but not World Mental Health Day.", "b": "5 October is World Teachers' Day, marked by UNESCO since 1994; it has no link to the World Federation for Mental Health's awareness day.", "d": "10 November is UNESCO's World Science Day for Peace and Development. It borrows the number 10 but sits in the wrong month."}}, {"q": "Difficulty in handwriting, spelling, punctuation and written expression is most commonly associated with :", "concept": "The answer is b: difficulty in handwriting, spelling, punctuation and written expression is the picture of dysgraphia. Dysgraphia is a specific learning difficulty in writing. DSM-5 (2013) folded the old separate learning disorders into one diagnosis, specific learning disorder, and asks the clinician to specify the area: impairment in reading, in written expression or in mathematics. The written expression specifier covers spelling accuracy, grammar and punctuation accuracy, and clarity or organisation of written work; dysgraphia is the everyday name for it. Like every specific learning disorder, it needs difficulties lasting at least 6 months despite targeted help, achievement well below age level on standardised tests, onset in the school years, and no better explanation in intellectual disability, sensory loss or poor teaching. Intelligence is typically average. In India, the RPwD Act 2016 lists dysgraphia among specific learning disabilities, which are certified from age 8. Example: Arjun, aged 9, explains a science chapter fluently aloud, but his written answers are cramped and hard to read, words like \"becuase\" recur, full stops are missing and his ideas come out in a jumble. His reading and arithmetic are fine. Option b is right because the stem names writing on every count: the motor act (handwriting), the mechanics (spelling and punctuation) and the composition (written expression). Exam trap: spelling problems also appear in dyslexia, so examiners hope you will grab the more famous term. Dyslexia is defined by trouble reading words accurately and fluently; when the list is all about producing written text, the answer is dysgraphia. Do not confuse it with dyspraxia or apraxia, which are problems of motor planning.", "traps": {"a": "Dyslexia, first described as 'word blindness' by W. Pringle Morgan (1896), is a specific learning disorder in reading: slow, inaccurate word decoding and poor fluency. It can bring weak spelling, but handwriting, punctuation and written composition point to dysgraphia.", "c": "Dyscalculia is a specific learning disorder in mathematics: poor number sense, weak memory for arithmetic facts and inaccurate calculation. It does not explain problems with handwriting or punctuation.", "d": "Apraxia, described by Hugo Liepmann (1900), is an inability to carry out learned, purposeful movements despite intact strength and understanding, usually after brain damage. It is a motor planning disorder, not a learning disorder of written language."}}, {"q": "Which sequence correctly represents the WHO classification of Intellectual Disability from least severe to most severe ?", "concept": "The answer is b: the WHO grades intellectual disability, from least to most severe, as mild, moderate, severe and profound. Intellectual disability means significant limitation in intellectual functioning and in adaptive behaviour, beginning in the developmental period. ICD-10 (1992), the WHO classification most exam questions draw on, sets the four levels by IQ: mild 50 to 69, moderate 35 to 49, severe 20 to 34 and profound below 20. ICD-11 (2019) renamed the condition disorders of intellectual development and kept the same four levels, now defined by standard deviations below the mean on intelligence and adaptive behaviour: mild about 2 to 3 SD below, moderate about 3 to 4, and severe and profound beyond 4, told apart by adaptive skills. DSM-5 (2013) also keeps the four labels but grades them by adaptive functioning, since that decides how much support a person needs. About 85 percent of cases are mild and are often first noticed at school; severe and profound levels usually show as delayed milestones in the first two years. Example: Meera, aged 10, has an IQ of 45; she can dress and feed herself, speaks in short sentences and needs supervision outside home, so she falls in the moderate band. Her classmate with an IQ of 62, who reads simple text, sits in the mild band. Option b is right because it is the only option that rises steadily in severity: mild, then moderate, then severe, then profound. Exam trap: d is the same list reversed (most to least severe), and a and c scramble the middle of the scale. Read the direction asked for, and remember that moderate always sits between mild and severe.", "traps": {"a": "This puts moderate before mild, so it starts at the second level. In ICD-10, mild (IQ 50 to 69) is the least severe band and moderate (35 to 49) comes next.", "c": "This swaps moderate and severe. Severe (IQ 20 to 34) is a deeper level than moderate (35 to 49), so the sequence does not rise in order.", "d": "These are the correct four levels listed backwards, from most to least severe. The stem asks for least to most severe, so the order is reversed."}}, {"q": "Which neuroimaging technique is preferred for repeated brain imaging in research participants because it does not expose them to ionizing radiation ?", "concept": "The answer is c: MRI is preferred for repeated scans of research participants because it uses no ionising radiation. Ionising radiation, as in X-rays and radioactive tracers, carries enough energy to damage DNA, and the risk adds up with each exposure, so ethics committees limit how often healthy volunteers can be scanned with it. Magnetic resonance imaging works differently. The person lies in a strong magnetic field that lines up the hydrogen nuclei in the body's water; pulses of radio waves knock them out of line, and as they relax they give off signals that differ between grey matter, white matter and fluid. A computer turns these signals into detailed slices with excellent spatial resolution. Paul Lauterbur (1973) made the first MR images, and he shared the 2003 Nobel Prize with Peter Mansfield. Functional MRI adds the BOLD signal described by Seiji Ogawa (1990), which tracks oxygenated blood flowing to active regions, so the same scanner shows activity as well as structure. Example: Kabir joins a two-year study of brain development; he can be scanned every 6 months, structurally and with fMRI, with no cumulative dose. The main cautions are metal implants, pacemakers and claustrophobia, not radiation. Option c is right because MRI relies only on magnetism and radio waves, so repeat imaging carries no radiation risk. Exam trap: PET and SPECT are functional scans, as is fMRI, so students sometimes assume all functional methods are alike. PET and SPECT need injected radioactive tracers and CT uses X-rays; only MRI and fMRI avoid ionising radiation.", "traps": {"a": "Positron emission tomography (PET) maps metabolism by injecting a radioactive, positron-emitting tracer such as FDG. Each scan gives a radiation dose, so it is not the choice for repeated imaging of healthy volunteers.", "b": "Computed tomography (CT), introduced by Godfrey Hounsfield (1971), builds slices from X-rays passed through the head at many angles. It is fast and good for fresh bleeding, but it exposes the person to ionising radiation.", "d": "Single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) maps blood flow with a gamma-emitting radioactive tracer. Like PET it involves ionising radiation, so repeat research scans are limited."}}, {"q": "Which of the following correctly matches the substance with its characteristic hallucination ?", "concept": "The answer is c: of the four pairings offered, cannabis with visual hallucinations is the one that matches. A hallucination is a perception without an external stimulus, experienced as real. Different drugs tend to produce hallucinations in different senses, and clinicians use the sense involved as a clue. Cannabis acts through delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) on cannabinoid receptors. At ordinary doses it brings euphoria, slowed time and sharpened colours and sounds; at high doses, or with potent forms such as charas, it can cause visual distortions and frank visual hallucinations, and DSM-5 (2013) has a specifier, cannabis intoxication with perceptual disturbances. The other pairings are mismatched. Cocaine and amphetamines are known for tactile hallucinations: formication, the feeling of insects crawling under the skin (\"cocaine bugs\"). LSD, whose effects Albert Hofmann (1943) discovered, is the classic visual hallucinogen, giving colours, trails and geometric patterns in clear consciousness. Alcohol withdrawal delirium (delirium tremens) brings vivid visual hallucinations of small animals or insects, often with tactile ones, while gustatory hallucinations suggest temporal lobe epilepsy. Example: after a strong bhang lassi at Holi, Arjun sees the coloured powder leave glowing trails and the walls seem to ripple. Option c is right because visual perceptual disturbance is a recognised effect of high-dose cannabis, while each other option links a substance to the wrong sense. Exam trap: options a and b swap the hallmarks of cocaine (tactile) and LSD (visual), so read each pairing carefully. Key note: cannabis more often distorts perception than causes true hallucinations, and cocaine can sometimes cause visual ones, so c is best read as the least wrong pairing rather than a textbook hallmark. The official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Cocaine's characteristic hallucination is tactile: formication, the 'cocaine bugs' felt crawling under the skin. Visual hallucinations can occur in stimulant psychosis but are not its hallmark.", "b": "LSD (lysergic acid diethylamide), whose effects Albert Hofmann (1943) discovered, is the classic visual hallucinogen. Pairing it with tactile hallucinations swaps it with cocaine.", "d": "Alcohol withdrawal delirium (delirium tremens) is marked by visual hallucinations, often of small animals, together with tactile ones. Gustatory hallucinations point instead to temporal lobe epilepsy."}}, {"q": "Phenylketonuria (PKU) primarily results from :", "concept": "The answer is b: phenylketonuria results from an inability to metabolise the amino acid phenylalanine. Ivar Asbjørn Følling (1934), a Norwegian physician, discovered PKU when he found phenylpyruvic acid in the urine of two intellectually disabled siblings. PKU is an autosomal recessive single-gene disorder: the child inherits a faulty copy of the gene for the liver enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase from each parent. Without the enzyme, phenylalanine from dietary protein cannot be converted into tyrosine. Phenylalanine and its by-products build up in the blood and damage the developing brain, causing intellectual disability, seizures, a musty body odour and pale skin and hair (tyrosine is needed to make melanin). Carriers with one faulty copy are healthy, and two carrier parents face a one in four risk in each pregnancy. PKU is the classic example of a genetic disorder whose outcome depends on the environment. Robert Guthrie (1963) introduced the heel-prick blood test that made newborn screening possible, and a low-phenylalanine diet started in the first weeks prevents almost all of the brain damage. Example: Meera's newborn daughter shows a raised phenylalanine level on her heel-prick screen; she is started on a special formula, later avoids high-protein foods and the sweetener aspartame, and grows up with normal intelligence. Option b is right because the core defect is metabolic: the body cannot break down phenylalanine. Exam trap: PKU is genetic but it is not chromosomal like Down syndrome (trisomy 21); it is a single recessive gene causing an enzyme deficiency. Remember the chain: phenylalanine hydroxylase missing, phenylalanine high, tyrosine low.", "traps": {"a": "Excess insulin (hyperinsulinism, as from an insulinoma) lowers blood sugar and can cause hypoglycaemic seizures, but it has nothing to do with amino acid metabolism. PKU involves no problem with insulin.", "c": "Iron deficiency causes anaemia and, in infancy, can impair attention and development; it is also linked with pica. It is a nutritional deficiency, not an inherited enzyme defect like PKU.", "d": "Vitamin D deficiency causes rickets in children (soft, bowed bones) and osteomalacia in adults. It concerns calcium and bone, not the breakdown of phenylalanine."}}, {"q": "In psychopathology, obsessions are primarily considered a disorder of :", "concept": "The answer is b: obsessions are classed as a disorder of thought content. Descriptive psychopathology, the method Karl Jaspers (1913) laid down in General Psychopathology, describes thinking under four headings: stream (speed and flow, as in pressure of thought), form (how ideas link, as in flight of ideas or derailment), content (what the person thinks about) and possession (whether thoughts feel like one's own). Content covers delusions, overvalued ideas, preoccupations, phobias and obsessions. An obsession is a recurrent, intrusive and unwanted thought, image or urge that the person recognises as a product of their own mind, finds senseless or distressing, and tries to resist. Aubrey Lewis (1936) stressed this inner resistance as the mark that separates an obsession from a delusion, which is held with conviction and without struggle. Obsessions are ego-dystonic, meaning they clash with the person's values, and in OCD they usually drive compulsions that briefly lower the anxiety. Example: Meera keeps thinking \"my hands are covered in germs\" after touching a door handle. She knows the thought is her own and excessive, tries to push it away, and washes her hands for twenty minutes. The theme of the thought, contamination, is a problem of content. Option b is right because an obsession is defined by what is thought, a fixed intrusive theme, rather than by how perception, intellect or attention works. Exam trap: some Indian case formats file obsessions under possession of thought, because control of thinking is lost, and thought insertion is a possession disorder in which the thought feels alien. Possession is not offered here, so content is the answer, and the patient's insight is what separates an obsession from a delusion.", "traps": {"a": "Disorders of perception are hallucinations, illusions and pseudohallucinations, that is, false or distorted sensory experiences. An obsession is a thought, not a perception.", "c": "Disorders of intelligence are intellectual disability and the loss of reasoning in dementia. People with OCD typically have normal intelligence; their problem lies in the content of intrusive thoughts.", "d": "Disorders of attention, such as distractibility in mania or ADHD, concern focusing and holding attention. Obsessions may capture attention, but they are classified by their content."}}, {"q": "The hallmark feature of anorexia nervosa is :", "concept": "The answer is b: the hallmark of anorexia nervosa is an intense fear of gaining weight even while the person is underweight. William Gull (1873) in London named the illness anorexia nervosa, and Ernest-Charles Lasègue (1873) in Paris described the same picture as anorexie hystérique. The name misleads: anorexia means loss of appetite, but most patients are hungry and are driven by a fear of fatness. DSM-5 (2013) sets three criteria: restriction of energy intake leading to a significantly low body weight; intense fear of gaining weight or becoming fat, or persistent behaviour that blocks weight gain, even at a low weight; and a disturbance in how weight or shape is experienced, with self-worth resting on them or denial of how serious the low weight is. There are two subtypes, restricting and binge-eating/purging, and adult severity is graded by body mass index, from mild (17 or above) to extreme (below 15). Hilde Bruch (1978), in The Golden Cage, described the typical patient as a compliant, high-achieving girl who feels ineffective and finds in control of her body the one thing that is truly hers. Anorexia has one of the highest death rates of any psychiatric disorder. Example: Riya, 16, weighs far less than she should for her height, counts every calorie, runs for hours and still pinches her stomach in the mirror, saying she is fat. Option b is right because fear of weight gain despite low weight is the core criterion that defines the disorder. Exam trap: DSM-5 dropped amenorrhoea as a criterion, and purging does not rule out anorexia; it defines a subtype. Excessive eating belongs to bulimia nervosa and binge-eating disorder, not anorexia.", "traps": {"a": "Excessive eating with loss of control is the core of binge-eating disorder and of bulimia nervosa, which Gerald Russell (1979) described. In anorexia, intake is restricted, even though a binge-eating/purging subtype exists.", "c": "Frequent unexpected panic attacks define panic disorder, an anxiety disorder. Anxiety can accompany anorexia, but panic is not its defining feature.", "d": "Persistent hallucinations point to psychotic disorders such as schizophrenia. The distorted body image in anorexia is an overvalued idea, not a hallucination."}}, {"q": "A therapist helps a client identify irrational beliefs such as \"I always fail\" and replace them with more realistic thoughts. This approach reflects :", "concept": "The answer is b: helping a client identify irrational thoughts such as \"I always fail\" and replace them with realistic ones is cognitive therapy. Cognitive therapy rests on the idea that it is not events themselves but the way we interpret them that shapes how we feel and act. Aaron Beck (1967) developed it after noticing that his depressed patients had a stream of quick, negative automatic thoughts about themselves, the world and the future, which he called the cognitive triad. These thoughts carry cognitive distortions: \"I always fail\" is overgeneralisation, drawing a sweeping rule from a few events. In therapy, client and therapist treat such thoughts as hypotheses to be tested, an approach called collaborative empiricism. Socratic questions (\"What is the evidence? Is there another way to see this?\") and the dysfunctional thought record help the client spot the thought, weigh the evidence and write a balanced alternative; this is cognitive restructuring. Albert Ellis (1955) built a parallel approach, rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), which disputes irrational beliefs such as \"I must succeed at everything\". Example: Kabir fails one mathematics test and tells himself, \"I always fail.\" His therapist asks him to list his recent results; he sees he passed four of five, and rewrites the thought as \"I failed this test, and I can prepare differently for the next one.\" His low mood eases. Option b is right because the stem describes changing thoughts, the central method of cognitive therapy, not changing behaviour directly or exploring the unconscious. Exam trap: \"irrational beliefs\" is Ellis's phrase, which can make students hesitate, but REBT is itself a cognitive therapy, so the cognitive option is still correct. Behaviour therapy targets actions, not beliefs.", "traps": {"a": "Behaviour therapy, built on the conditioning work of Joseph Wolpe (1958) and B. F. Skinner (1938), changes behaviour directly through systematic desensitisation, exposure and reinforcement. It does not centre on disputing beliefs.", "c": "Client-centred therapy, founded by Carl Rogers (1951), is non-directive: the therapist offers empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness rather than challenging the client's thoughts.", "d": "Psychoanalysis, developed by Sigmund Freud (1896), uses free association, dream analysis and interpretation of transference to uncover unconscious conflicts. It does not teach clients to replace conscious thoughts with realistic ones."}}, {"q": "The memory disorder characterized by severe anterograde amnesia and confabulation due to thiamine deficiency is most commonly seen in individuals with chronic :", "concept": "The answer is b: the thiamine-deficiency memory disorder with severe anterograde amnesia and confabulation, Korsakoff's syndrome, is most commonly seen in chronic alcohol dependence. Thiamine (vitamin B1) is needed for brain cells to use glucose. Long-term heavy drinkers run short of it for three reasons: poor diet, poor absorption from the gut and poor storage in a damaged liver. Carl Wernicke (1881) described the acute stage, Wernicke's encephalopathy, with a triad of confusion, ataxia (unsteady gait) and eye movement problems such as nystagmus; it is an emergency treated with thiamine. Untreated, many patients pass into the chronic stage described by Sergei Korsakoff (1887): a dense inability to form new memories (anterograde amnesia), patchy loss of older memories (retrograde amnesia) and confabulation, filling memory gaps with invented stories told without any intent to deceive. Attention and general intelligence are relatively spared. Together the two stages form the Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, and post-mortem studies show damage to the mammillary bodies and the dorsomedial thalamus. Example: Rakesh, 58, has drunk heavily for thirty years. On the ward he greets the doctor warmly, cannot recall meeting her an hour earlier, and says he spent the morning at the bank, though he has not left his bed. Option b is right because chronic alcohol use is by far the commonest cause of the thiamine deficiency behind this syndrome. Exam trap: do not confuse Wernicke's encephalopathy (alcohol, thiamine, eye signs) with Wernicke's aphasia (fluent but meaningless speech after damage to the left temporal lobe). Also, giving glucose before thiamine can tip a vulnerable drinker into Wernicke's encephalopathy.", "traps": {"a": "Opioid dependence brings pinpoint pupils, constipation and the risk of respiratory depression in overdose, with a flu-like withdrawal. It is not a typical cause of thiamine deficiency or Korsakoff's syndrome.", "c": "Nicotine dependence does heavy medical harm, mainly to the lungs and heart, but it does not cause thiamine deficiency or an amnestic syndrome with confabulation.", "d": "Sedative and hypnotic dependence can impair memory, and long-term use is linked with lasting cognitive problems, but not with thiamine deficiency. The thiamine-linked amnestic syndrome is the hallmark of chronic alcohol use."}}, {"q": "In the Bollywood movie 'Bhool Bhulaiya', what is the disorder of the main character ?", "concept": "The answer is c: in Bhool Bhulaiyaa (2007), the main character's disorder is dissociative identity disorder. In the film, directed by Priyadarshan and remade from the Malayalam Manichitrathazhu (1993), Avni, played by Vidya Balan, moves into an old palace and begins to take on the identity of Manjulika, a dancer from the palace's legend. As Manjulika she speaks and dances differently and turns violent; as Avni she has no memory of these episodes. The psychiatrist, Dr Aditya, played by Akshay Kumar, explains this as a split personality. DSM-5 (2013) defines dissociative identity disorder (DID), called multiple personality disorder until DSM-IV (1994), by two core criteria. First, a disruption of identity into two or more distinct personality states, or an experience of possession, with changes in behaviour, memory and sense of self. Second, recurrent gaps in memory for everyday events or personal information beyond ordinary forgetting. The usual identity is the host, the others are alters, and changing between them is a switch. Corbett Thigpen and Hervey Cleckley (1957) made the disorder famous with The Three Faces of Eve. DSM-5 also notes that in many cultures the alternate identity appears as a possessing spirit or person, which is exactly how Manjulika presents. Option c is right because the film shows separate identities with amnesia between them, the core of DID, not a single episode of flight or a bodily symptom. Exam trap: \"dissociative hysteria\" sounds plausible because older Indian texts used hysteria for dissociative states, but it is not a current diagnosis, and a fugue involves sudden travel with loss of identity, not alternating identities.", "traps": {"a": "Dissociative fugue, now a specifier of dissociative amnesia in DSM-5 (2013), is sudden travel or wandering with loss of memory for one's identity. Avni does not flee; she alternates between two identities.", "b": "Conversion disorder, Sigmund Freud's (1894) term for distress converted into bodily symptoms, presents as paralysis, blindness or seizures with no neurological cause. It involves no separate identity.", "d": "Dissociative hysteria is an outdated umbrella label (DSM-II's 1968 'hysterical neurosis, dissociative type') for dissociative states. It is not a current diagnosis and does not name the specific picture of alternating identities."}}, {"q": "The cocktail party phenomenon primarily illustrates :", "concept": "The answer is b: the cocktail party phenomenon illustrates selective attention. Selective attention is the ability to focus on one source of information while filtering out competing ones. Colin Cherry (1953), a British engineer, named the cocktail party problem: how do we follow one conversation in a room full of voices? He tested it with dichotic listening, playing a different message to each ear and asking people to repeat one aloud, which is called shadowing. They recalled almost nothing of the meaning of the unattended message but noticed physical changes, such as a male voice turning female. Neville Moray (1959) then showed that about a third of listeners noticed their own name in the ignored ear, the effect most people mean by the cocktail party phenomenon. This troubled Donald Broadbent's (1958) filter model, which blocked unattended input completely, and led Anne Treisman (1964) to propose an attenuator that only turns ignored input down, so highly relevant words such as one's name can still break through. Example: at a crowded sangeet, Riya listens to her cousin's story and hears the music only as noise, yet she turns at once when someone across the room says \"Riya\". Option b is right because the phenomenon shows us picking one stream out of many and filtering the rest, which is the definition of selective attention. Exam trap: sustained attention is about duration and span of attention is about how many items we take in at once; neither involves picking one voice from many. Key note: some textbooks, notably Michael Eysenck and Mark Keane's Cognitive Psychology (1990), use \"focused attention\" as another name for selective attention and discuss the cocktail party problem under that heading, so a has a defence. Selective attention remains the standard label, and the official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "In clinical models such as McKay Sohlberg and Catherine Mateer's (1987) hierarchy, focused attention is the most basic level, simply responding to a specific stimulus. It does not capture filtering one voice out of many, though some texts use the term loosely as a synonym (see the Key note).", "c": "Sustained attention, or vigilance, is keeping attention on a task over a long period, as in Norman Mackworth's (1948) clock test for radar operators. The cocktail party effect is about choosing among inputs, not endurance.", "d": "Span of attention is the number of items a person can take in at a single glance, roughly 4 to 7. It concerns capacity, not filtering one message from competing ones."}}, {"q": "Piaget's Three-Mountain Task was primarily designed to assess :", "concept": "The answer is b: the three mountains task of Jean Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder (1956) was designed to assess egocentrism. They described it in The Child's Conception of Space. A child sat in front of a model of three mountains, one capped with snow, one with a house and one with a red cross. A doll was placed at another side of the table, and the child chose, from a set of pictures, the view the doll would see. Four-year-olds usually picked the picture of their own view, and correct choices became common only around 7 to 8 years. Piaget read this as egocentrism: the preoperational child (about 2 to 7) cannot yet take another person's point of view and assumes everyone sees the world as they do. Egocentrism also shows in collective monologues, in which children talk past each other. Later work softened the finding. Martin Hughes (1975) set a game in which a boy doll had to hide from policemen dolls; about 90 percent of children aged 3.5 to 5 succeeded, because the task made human sense. Margaret Donaldson (1978) argued that Piaget's tasks underestimated young children. Example: four-year-old Arjun, asked what his grandmother sees from across the table, points to the picture of the mountain with the house, the one facing him. Option b is right because the task tests whether a child can take another person's visual perspective, and failing it is the classic sign of egocentrism. Exam trap: do not confuse it with conservation tasks, such as the beakers of water, which come from the same preoperational stage but test the understanding that quantity stays the same when appearance changes.", "traps": {"a": "Conservation, from Jean Piaget's (1941) work, is understanding that quantity stays the same despite changes in appearance, tested with beakers of water or rows of coins. It is a different preoperational limit from perspective taking.", "c": "Object permanence, the knowledge that objects exist when out of sight, is tested in the sensorimotor stage by hiding a toy under a cloth, as Jean Piaget (1937) described. The three mountains task is given to older, preschool children.", "d": "Moral reasoning was studied by Jean Piaget (1932) with story pairs about clumsy and naughty children, and later by Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) with dilemmas. The mountains model tests visual perspective, not moral judgement."}}, {"q": "Which of the following theorist - animal pairs is incorrectly matched ?", "concept": "The answer is d: Edward Chace Tolman (1930) studied rats, not chimpanzees; the chimpanzee belongs to Wolfgang Köhler (1917). This is a \"not\" question, so three pairings are true and one is false. Ivan Pavlov (1897) used dogs: by measuring their salivation he found that a neutral sound paired with food came to trigger salivation on its own, the basis of classical conditioning. Edward Lee Thorndike (1898) used hungry cats in puzzle boxes: escape times fell gradually across trials, which he explained by the law of effect, the idea that rewarded responses are stamped in. Tolman used rats in mazes: in the latent learning study with Charles Honzik (1930), rats that explored a maze unrewarded for ten days ran it as well as rewarded rats the day after food appeared, showing they had formed a cognitive map. So a, b and c are correct. The chimpanzee studies belong to Köhler, a Gestalt psychologist working on Tenerife. His chimpanzee Sultan joined two sticks to reach a banana outside the cage, solving the problem suddenly, which Köhler called insight learning. Example: Meera remembers the pairs as a small zoo: Pavlov's dog salivates, Thorndike's cat escapes the box, Tolman's rat maps the maze, and Köhler's chimp stacks boxes for the banana. Option d is right because Tolman never based his theory on chimpanzees; the pairing mixes up Tolman's rats with Köhler's apes. Exam trap: when the same theorist appears in two options, one of the two must be the wrong pairing, so ask which animal he really used. Also do not confuse Köhler with Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), the moral development theorist, or with Harry Harlow (1949), whose monkeys showed learning sets.", "traps": {"a": "This pairing is correct: Edward Chace Tolman (1930) ran rats through mazes and, with Charles Honzik, showed latent learning and the cognitive map. So it cannot be the incorrect match.", "b": "This pairing is correct: Edward Lee Thorndike (1898) placed cats in puzzle boxes and derived the law of effect from their gradually falling escape times.", "c": "This pairing is correct: Ivan Pavlov (1897) studied salivation in dogs and discovered classical conditioning; his Nobel Prize of 1904 was for work on digestion."}}, {"q": "Which of the following forms of intelligence is most likely to decrease with normal aging, while accumulated knowledge and vocabulary remain relatively stable ?", "concept": "The answer is a: fluid intelligence is the ability most likely to decline with normal ageing, while accumulated knowledge and vocabulary stay stable. Raymond Cattell (1963) split general intelligence into two parts, and his student John Horn (1966) developed the idea with him. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve new problems on the spot, independent of what one has learned: seeing patterns, holding information in mind and working out rules. Raven's Progressive Matrices is its classic test. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills built up through education and experience, measured by vocabulary and general information tests. The two follow different paths across the lifespan. Fluid ability peaks in the early twenties and then falls steadily, partly because of what Timothy Salthouse (1996) called a general slowing of processing speed. Crystallised ability keeps growing or holds steady into the sixties and seventies. K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) showed that cross-sectional studies exaggerate the decline, yet the pattern of fluid loss and crystallised stability remains. Example: Arjun's grandfather, 72, struggles to learn a new phone app and is slow on a puzzle he has never seen, yet he still beats the family at crosswords and knows the meaning of rare words. Option a is right because fluid reasoning and speed are the abilities that age most, while the knowledge and vocabulary the stem calls stable are crystallised. Exam trap: students often reverse the two; crystallised is the stable one. Also, Raymond Cattell, who proposed fluid and crystallised intelligence, is not James McKeen Cattell (1890), who coined the term \"mental test\".", "traps": {"b": "Crystallised intelligence, Raymond Cattell's (1963) store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills, is the ability that stays stable or grows with age. The stem describes it as the part that remains, so it cannot be the one that declines.", "c": "Spatial intelligence is one of Howard Gardner's (1983) multiple intelligences. Some spatial tasks do slow with age, but it is not the classic contrast with accumulated knowledge that the stem describes.", "d": "Emotional intelligence, defined by Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) and popularised by Daniel Goleman (1995), tends to hold or even improve with age as people get better at regulating emotion."}}, {"q": "In the context of problem solving, what is the moment when the problem's solution comes to us suddenly ?", "concept": "The answer is b: the moment when a solution suddenly comes to mind is called illumination. Graham Wallas (1926), in The Art of Thought, described four stages of creative problem solving. In preparation the person defines the problem, gathers information and works on it consciously, often without success. In incubation the problem is set aside, and work is thought to go on outside awareness. In illumination the answer arrives suddenly, the familiar \"aha\" or \"eureka\" moment, often while the person is doing something else. In verification the idea is checked, tested and refined. Wallas drew partly on the mathematician Henri Poincaré (1908), who described the solution to a problem that had defeated him arriving as he stepped onto a bus. The same sudden restructuring was seen in Wolfgang Köhler's (1917) chimpanzees and is called insight. Janet Metcalfe and David Wiebe (1987) showed that on insight problems, people's ratings of how close they are to a solution stay flat until just before the answer appears, unlike the steady rise on routine algebra. Example: Meera spends an evening stuck on a logic puzzle and gives up; the next morning, while brushing her teeth, the whole answer flashes into her mind. Option b is right because illumination is the stage defined by the sudden arrival of the solution. Exam trap: incubation, the stage just before, is when the problem is set aside, and examiners may offer it in place of illumination. The order is preparation, incubation, illumination, verification; remember \"Please Incubate Ideas Very carefully\".", "traps": {"a": "Orientation is the opening step of understanding and framing the problem, as in Alex Osborn's (1953) seven-step creative problem-solving process. It happens at the start, not at the moment of sudden insight.", "c": "Elaboration is the adding of detail: a creativity score for Joy Paul Guilford (1950) and Ellis Paul Torrance (1966), and the final working-out stage in Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi's (1996) model. It comes after the insight, not with it.", "d": "Action orientation is a personality style in Julius Kuhl's (1984) action control theory, the readiness to turn intentions into action rather than dwell on one's state. It is not a stage of problem solving."}}, {"q": "A small child is unable to think of various ways in which a screwdriver can be used. What is the mental set being shown here ?", "concept": "The answer is a: being unable to think of other ways to use a screwdriver is functional fixedness. Functional fixedness is the tendency to see an object only in terms of its usual function, which blocks creative uses in problem solving. It is a specific kind of mental set, a readiness to approach a problem in one fixed way. Karl Duncker (1945) showed it with the candle problem: people given a candle, matches and a box of drawing pins had to fix the candle to a wall. Few thought of emptying the box and pinning it up as a shelf, but many more did when the box was handed over empty, because then it no longer looked like a container. Abraham Luchins (1942) showed the broader mental set, or Einstellung, with water jar problems: people drilled in one long formula kept using it when a far simpler one would do. Example: six-year-old Kabir needs to open a tin of paint and is handed a screwdriver; he insists it is only for screws and never thinks of using it as a lever, a stirrer or a paperweight. Interestingly, Tim German and Margaret Defeyter (2000) found that 5-year-olds were less fixed than 6 to 7-year-olds, because the idea of what an object is for develops with age. Option a is right because the child's block is about seeing only one use for an object, which is the definition of functional fixedness. Exam trap: the stem says \"mental set\", which may send students looking for Einstellung, but functional fixedness is the form of mental set that concerns objects, and it is the only option here that blocks problem solving at all.", "traps": {"b": "The Barnum effect, named by Paul Meehl (1956) and demonstrated by Bertram Forer (1949), is accepting vague, general personality descriptions, as in horoscopes, as accurate about oneself. It has nothing to do with object use.", "c": "Hindsight bias, studied by Baruch Fischhoff (1975), is the 'I knew it all along' feeling after an outcome is known. It distorts memory of past predictions, not ideas about how an object can be used.", "d": "The availability heuristic, described by Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1973), is judging how common something is by how easily examples come to mind. It is a judgement shortcut, not a block on seeing new uses."}}]});
